  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa ch ph) th) ta) et) ze) si) rh) ps $;

--| Deduction adding 8 conjuncts to antecedent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem ad8antr (ph ps ch th ta et ze si rh mu: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa ph ch) th) ta) et) ze) si) rh) mu) ps $;

--| Deduction adding 8 conjuncts to antecedent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2017.)
theorem ad8antlr (ph ps ch th ta et ze si rh mu: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa ch ph) th) ta) et) ze) si) rh) mu) ps $;

--| Deduction adding 9 conjuncts to antecedent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem ad9antr (ph ps ch th ta et ze si rh mu la: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa ph ch) th) ta) et) ze) si) rh) mu) la) ps $;

--| Deduction adding 9 conjuncts to antecedent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2017.)
theorem ad9antlr (ph ps ch th ta et ze si rh mu la: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa ch ph) th) ta) et) ze) si) rh) mu) la) ps $;

--| Deduction adding 10 conjuncts to antecedent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jan-2017.)
theorem ad10antr (ph ps ch th ta et ze si rh mu la ka: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa ph ch) th) ta) et) ze) si) rh) mu) la) ka) ps $;

--| Deduction adding 10 conjuncts to antecedent. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jan-2017.)
theorem ad10antlr (ph ps ch th ta et ze si rh mu la ka: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa (wa ch ph) th) ta) et) ze) si) rh) mu) la) ka) ps $;

--| Deduction adding two conjuncts to antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jan-2006.)
theorem ad2ant2l (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa th ph) (wa ta ps)) ch $;

--| Deduction adding two conjuncts to antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jan-2006.)
theorem ad2ant2r (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph th) (wa ps ta)) ch $;

--| Deduction adding two conjuncts to antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 23-Nov-2007.)
theorem ad2ant2lr (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa th ph) (wa ps ta)) ch $;

--| Deduction adding two conjuncts to antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 24-Nov-2007.)
theorem ad2ant2rl (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph th) (wa ta ps)) ch $;

--| Simplification of a conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-2007.)
theorem simpll (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) ph $;

--| Simplification of a conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 20-Mar-2007.)
theorem simplr (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) ps $;

--| Simplification of a conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 21-Mar-2007.)
theorem simprl (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) ps $;

--| Simplification of a conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 21-Mar-2007.)
theorem simprr (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) ch $;

--| Simplification of a conjunction. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jul-2009.)
theorem simplll (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th) ph $;

--| Simplification of a conjunction. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jul-2009.)
theorem simpllr (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th) ps $;

--| Simplification of a conjunction. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jul-2009.)
theorem simplrl (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th) ps $;

--| Simplification of a conjunction. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jul-2009.)
theorem simplrr (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th) ch $;

--| Simplification of a conjunction. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jul-2009.)
theorem simprll (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wa ps ch) th)) ps $;

--| Simplification of a conjunction. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jul-2009.)
theorem simprlr (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wa ps ch) th)) ch $;

--| Simplification of a conjunction. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jul-2009.)
theorem simprrl (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps (wa ch th))) ch $;

--| Simplification of a conjunction. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 28-Jul-2009.)
theorem simprrr (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps (wa ch th))) th $;

--| Theorem *4.87 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 122. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Eric Schmidt, 26-Oct-2006.)
theorem pm4_87 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wa (wa (wb (wi (wa ph ps) ch) (wi ph (wi ps ch))) (wb (wi ph (wi ps ch)) (wi ps (wi ph ch)))) (wb (wi ps (wi ph ch)) (wi (wa ps ph) ch)) $;

--| Introduce one conjunct as an antecedent to the other. "abai" stands for "and, biconditional, and, implication". (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 7-Dec-2012.)
theorem abai (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph ps) (wa ph (wi ph ps)) $;

--| Swap two conjuncts. Note that the first digit (1) in the label refers to the outer conjunct position, and the next digit (2) to the inner conjunct position. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1995.)
theorem an12 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph (wa ps ch)) (wa ps (wa ph ch)) $;

--| A rearrangement of conjuncts. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-Dec-2012.)
theorem an32 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) ch) (wa (wa ph ch) ps) $;

--| A rearrangement of conjuncts. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jun-2012.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 31-Dec-2012.)
theorem an13 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph (wa ps ch)) (wa ch (wa ps ph)) $;

--| A rearrangement of conjuncts. (Contributed by NM, 24-Jun-2012.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 31-Dec-2012.)
theorem an31 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) ch) (wa (wa ch ps) ph) $;

--| Swap two conjuncts in antecedent. The label suffix "s" means that ~ an12 is combined with ~ syl (or a variant). (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-1996.)
theorem an12s (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ps (wa ph ch)) th $;

--| Inference commuting a nested conjunction in antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 24-May-2006.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2012.)
theorem ancom2s (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ch ps)) th $;

--| Swap two conjuncts in antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 31-May-2006.)
theorem an13s (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ch (wa ps ph)) th $;

--| Swap two conjuncts in antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-1996.)
theorem an32s (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ch) ps) th $;

--| Inference commuting a nested conjunction in antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 24-May-2006.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2012.)
theorem ancom1s (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ps ph) ch) th $;

--| Swap two conjuncts in antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 31-May-2006.)
theorem an31s (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) th $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ch ps) ph) th $;

--| Commutative-associative law for conjunction in an antecedent. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 19-Jun-2011.)
theorem anass1rs (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) th $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ch) ps) th $;

--| Absorption into embedded conjunct. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 16-Nov-2013.)
theorem anabs1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) ph) (wa ph ps) $;

--| Absorption into embedded conjunct. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-1996.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 9-Dec-2012.)
theorem anabs5 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph (wa ph ps)) (wa ph ps) $;

--| Absorption into embedded conjunct. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-1996.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 17-Nov-2013.)
theorem anabs7 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wa ps (wa ph ps)) (wa ph ps) $;

--| Absorption of antecedent with conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 24-Mar-1996.) (Revised by NM, 18-Nov-2013.)
theorem anabsan (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ph) ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent into conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-1996.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 31-Dec-2012.)
theorem anabss1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ph) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent into conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-1996.)
theorem anabss4 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ps ph) ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent into conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 1-Jan-2013.)
theorem anabss5 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ph ps)) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent into conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 11-Jun-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 18-Nov-2013.)
theorem anabsi5 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ph ps) ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent into conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-2000.)
theorem anabsi6 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ph) ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent into conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-1996.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 18-Nov-2013.)
theorem anabsi7 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ps (wi (wa ph ps) ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent into conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 26-Sep-1999.)
theorem anabsi8 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ps (wi (wa ps ph) ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent into conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-1996.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-Nov-2013.)
theorem anabss7 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa ps (wa ph ps)) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent with conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-2004.) (Revised by NM, 1-Jan-2013.)
theorem anabsan2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ps)) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Absorption of antecedent into conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-1996.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 1-Jan-2013.)
theorem anabss3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 conjuncts. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jul-1994.)
theorem an4 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) (wa (wa ph ch) (wa ps th)) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 conjuncts. (Contributed by NM, 7-Feb-1996.)
theorem an42 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) (wa (wa ph ch) (wa th ps)) $;

--| Inference rearranging 4 conjuncts in antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-1995.)
theorem an4s (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ch) (wa ps th)) ta $;

--| Inference rearranging 4 conjuncts in antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-1995.)
theorem an42s (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ch) (wa th ps)) ta $;

--| Distribution of conjunction over conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 14-Aug-1995.)
theorem anandi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph (wa ps ch)) (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ph ch)) $;

--| Distribution of conjunction over conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-1995.)
theorem anandir (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) ch) (wa (wa ph ch) (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Inference that undistributes conjunction in the antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 7-Jun-2004.)
theorem anandis (ph ps ch ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ph ch)) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps ch)) ta $;

--| Inference that undistributes conjunction in the antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 7-Jun-2004.)
theorem anandirs (ph ps ch ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ch) (wa ps ch)) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa ph ps) ch) ta $;

--| Deduce an equivalence from two implications. (Contributed by NM, 17-Feb-2007.)
theorem impbida (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $;

--| Theorem *3.45 (Fact) of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 113. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm3_45 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wa ph ch) (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Deduction joining nested implications to form implication of conjunctions. (Contributed by NM, 29-Feb-1996.)
theorem im2anan9 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wi ta et) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) (wi (wa ps ta) (wa ch et)) $;

--| Deduction joining nested implications to form implication of conjunctions. (Contributed by NM, 29-Feb-1996.)
theorem im2anan9r (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wi ta et) $ >
  $ wi (wa th ph) (wi (wa ps ta) (wa ch et)) $;

--| Conjoin antecedents and consequents in a deduction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2014.)
theorem anim12dan (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa ps th)) (wa ch ta) $;

--| Two propositions are equivalent if they are both true. Theorem *5.1 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 123. (Contributed by NM, 21-May-1994.)
theorem pm5_1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wb ph ps) $;

--| Theorem *3.43 (Comp) of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 113. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Revised by NM, 27-Nov-2013.)
theorem pm3_43 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) (wi ph (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Distributive law for implication over conjunction. Compare Theorem *4.76 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 121. (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 27-Nov-2013.)
theorem jcab (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph (wa ps ch)) (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) $;

--| Theorem *4.76 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 121. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm4_76 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) (wi ph (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Theorem *4.38 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 118. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm4_38 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wb ph ch) (wb ps th)) (wb (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) $;

--| Deduction joining two equivalences to form equivalence of conjunctions. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-1995.)
theorem bi2anan9 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wb ta et) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) (wb (wa ps ta) (wa ch et)) $;

--| Deduction joining two equivalences to form equivalence of conjunctions. (Contributed by NM, 19-Feb-1996.)
theorem bi2anan9r (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wb ta et) $ >
  $ wi (wa th ph) (wb (wa ps ta) (wa ch et)) $;

--| Deduction joining two biconditionals with different antecedents. (Contributed by NM, 12-May-2004.)
theorem bi2bian9 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wb ta et) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) (wb (wb ps ta) (wb ch et)) $;

--| Theorem *5.33 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm5_33 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph (wi ps ch)) (wa ph (wi (wa ph ps) ch)) $;

--| Theorem *5.36 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm5_36 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph (wb ph ps)) (wa ps (wb ph ps)) $;

--| Absorb a hypothesis into the second member of a biconditional. (Contributed by FL, 15-Feb-2007.)
theorem bianabs (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ph ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $;

--| 'Not' introduction. One of the axioms of propositional logic. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
axiom ax_in1 (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wn ph)) (wn ph) $;

--| 'Not' elimination. One of the axioms of propositional logic. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
axiom ax_in2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wi ph ps) $;

--| Reductio ad absurdum. Theorem *2.01 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 100. This is valid intuitionistically (in the terminology of Section 1.2 of [Bauer] p. 482 it is a proof of negation not a proof by contradiction); compare with ~ pm2.18dc which only holds for some propositions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2015.)
theorem pm2_01 (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wn ph)) (wn ph) $;

--| From a wff and its negation, anything is true. Theorem *2.21 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 104. Also called the Duns Scotus law. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2015.)
theorem pm2_21 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wi ph ps) $;

--| Deduction based on reductio ad absurdum. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem pm2_01d (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| A contradiction implies anything. Deduction from ~ pm2.21 . (Contributed by NM, 10-Feb-1996.)
theorem pm2_21d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $;

--| A contradiction implies anything. Deduction from ~ pm2.21 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2017.)
theorem pm2_21dd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Theorem *2.24 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 104. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_24 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ph) ps) $;

--| Deduction version of ~ pm2.24 . (Contributed by NM, 30-Jan-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem pm2_24d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) ch) $;

--| Inference version of ~ pm2.24 . (Contributed by NM, 20-Aug-2001.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem pm2_24i (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) ps $;

--| A contraposition deduction. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-1993.) (Revised by NM, 12-Feb-2013.)
theorem con2d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wn ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch (wn ps)) $;

--| Modus tollens deduction. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jul-1994.)
theorem mt2d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wn ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| A negated syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 1-Dec-1995.) (Revised by NM, 13-Jun-2013.)
theorem nsyl3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ch ps $ >
  $ wi ch (wn ph) $;

--| A contraposition inference. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by O'Cat, 28-Nov-2008.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Jun-2013.)
theorem con2i (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ps (wn ph) $;

--| A negated syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 31-Dec-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Mar-2013.)
theorem nsyl (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ch ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $;

--| Double negation introduction. Theorem *2.12 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 101. This one holds for all propositions, but its converse only holds for decidable propositions (see ~ notnotrdc ). (Contributed by NM, 28-Dec-1992.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 2-Mar-2013.)
theorem notnot (ph: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn (wn ph)) $;

--| Deduction associated with ~ notnot and ~ notnoti . (Contributed by Jarvin Udandy, 2-Sep-2016.) Avoid biconditional. (Revised by Wolf Lammen, 27-Mar-2021.)
theorem notnotd (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wn ps)) $;

--| A contraposition deduction. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Revised by NM, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem con3d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ch) (wn ps)) $;

--| A contraposition inference. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 20-Jun-2013.)
theorem con3i (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) (wn ph) $;

--| Rotate through consequent right. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem con3rr3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wn ch) (wi ph (wn ps)) $;

--| Variant of ~ con3d with importation. (Contributed by Jonathan Ben-Naim, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem con3and (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wn ch)) (wn ps) $;

--| Deduction based on reductio ad absurdum. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2017.)
theorem pm2_01da (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| In classical logic, this is just a restatement of ~ pm3.2 . In intuitionistic logic, it still holds, but is weaker than pm3.2. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-May-2015.)
theorem pm3_2im (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wn (wi ph (wn ps)))) $;

--| An exportation inference. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by O'Cat, 28-Nov-2008.)
theorem expi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wi ph (wn ps))) ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $;

--| Inference rule for proof by contradiction. (Contributed by NM, 18-May-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Sep-2013.)
theorem pm2_65i (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| A rule similar to modus tollens. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Sep-2013.)
theorem mt2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| Theorem used to justify definition of intuitionistic biconditional ~ df-bi . (Contributed by NM, 24-Nov-2017.)
theorem biijust (ph ps: wff):
  $ wa (wi (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ps ph)) (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ps ph))) (wi (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ps ph)) (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ps ph))) $;

--| Contraposition. Theorem *2.16 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 103. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Feb-2013.)
theorem con3 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wn ps) (wn ph)) $;

--| Contraposition. Theorem *2.03 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 100. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Feb-2013.)
theorem con2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wn ps)) (wi ps (wn ph)) $;

--| Modus tollens inference. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 15-Sep-2012.)
theorem mt2i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wn ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| Infer double negation. (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2008.)
theorem notnoti (ph: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wn (wn ph) $;

--| A contradiction implies anything. Inference from ~ pm2.21 . (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem pm2_21i (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| A contradiction implies anything. Inference from ~ pm2.24 . (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2008.)
theorem pm2_24ii (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ ps $;

--| A negated syllogism deduction. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-2005.)
theorem nsyld (ph ps ch ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wn ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ta ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wn ta)) $;

--| A negated syllogism inference. (Contributed by NM, 3-May-1994.)
theorem nsyli (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th (wn ps)) $;

--| Theorem 8 of [Margaris] p. 60. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Josh Purinton, 29-Dec-2000.)
theorem mth8 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) (wn (wi ph ps))) $;

--| Inference joining the consequents of two premises. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.)
theorem jc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wi ps (wn ch))) $;

--| Theorem *2.51 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_51 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wi ph ps)) (wi ph (wn ps)) $;

--| Theorem *2.52 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem pm2_52 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wi ph ps)) (wi (wn ph) (wn ps)) $;

--| Exportation theorem expressed with primitive connectives. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.)
theorem expt (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wn (wi ph (wn ps))) ch) (wi ph (wi ps ch)) $;

--| Elimination of a nested antecedent. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 10-May-2013.)
theorem jarl (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wi ph ps) ch) (wi (wn ph) ch) $;

--| Theorem *2.65 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. Proof by contradiction. Proofs, such as this one, which assume a proposition, here ` ph ` , derive a contradiction, and therefore conclude ` -. ph ` , are valid intuitionistically (and can be called "proof of negation", for example by Section 1.2 of [Bauer] p. 482). By contrast, proofs which assume ` -. ph ` , derive a contradiction, and conclude ` ph ` , such as ~ condandc , are only valid for decidable propositions. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 8-Mar-2013.)
theorem pm2_65 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ph (wn ps)) (wn ph)) $;

--| Deduction rule for proof by contradiction. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jun-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 26-May-2013.)
theorem pm2_65d (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wn ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| Deduction rule for proof by contradiction. (Contributed by NM, 12-Jun-2014.)
theorem pm2_65da (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| The rule of modus tollens. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Sep-2013.)
theorem mto (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ps $ >
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| Modus tollens deduction. (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 11-Sep-2013.)
theorem mtod (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| Modus tollens inference. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jul-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 15-Sep-2012.)
theorem mtoi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wn ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| A modus tollens deduction. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 19-Aug-2009.)
theorem mtand (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| Deduction negating both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 21-May-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem notbid (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wn ps) (wn ch)) $;

--| Contraposition. Bidirectional version of ~ con2 . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.)
theorem con2b (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph (wn ps)) (wi ps (wn ph)) $;

--| Negate both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem notbii (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wn ph) (wn ps) $;

--| An inference from a biconditional, related to modus tollens. (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-Oct-2012.)
theorem mtbi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wn ps $;

--| An inference from a biconditional, related to modus tollens. (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-Oct-2012.)
theorem mtbir (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ps $ >
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wn ph $;

--| A deduction from a biconditional, similar to modus tollens. (Contributed by NM, 26-Nov-1995.)
theorem mtbid (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $;

--| A deduction from a biconditional, similar to modus tollens. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1994.)
theorem mtbird (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| An inference from a biconditional, similar to modus tollens. (Contributed by NM, 27-Nov-1995.)
theorem mtbii (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wn ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $;

--| An inference from a biconditional, similar to modus tollens. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-1995.)
theorem mtbiri (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wn ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from an implication and a biconditional. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 16-Dec-2013.)
theorem sylnib (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wb ps ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from an implication and a biconditional. Useful for substituting an consequent with a definition. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 16-Dec-2013.)
theorem sylnibr (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wb ch ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from a biconditional and an implication. Useful for substituting an antecedent with a definition. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 16-Dec-2013.)
theorem sylnbi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) ch $;

--| A mixed syllogism inference from a biconditional and an implication. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 16-Dec-2013.)
theorem sylnbir (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ps ph $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) ch $;

--| Replacement of a subexpression by an equivalent one. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 27-Sep-2014.)
theorem xchnxbi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wn ph) ps $ >
  $ wb ph ch $ >
  $ wb (wn ch) ps $;

--| Replacement of a subexpression by an equivalent one. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 27-Sep-2014.)
theorem xchnxbir (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wn ph) ps $ >
  $ wb ch ph $ >
  $ wb (wn ch) ps $;

--| Replacement of a subexpression by an equivalent one. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 27-Sep-2014.)
theorem xchbinx (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wb ps ch $ >
  $ wb ph (wn ch) $;

--| Replacement of a subexpression by an equivalent one. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 27-Sep-2014.)
theorem xchbinxr (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wb ch ps $ >
  $ wb ph (wn ch) $;

--| A false consequent falsifies an antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Nov-2012.)
theorem mt2bi (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wb (wn ps) (wi ps (wn ph)) $;

--| Modus-tollens-like theorem. (Contributed by NM, 7-Apr-2001.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mtt (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wb (wn ps) (wi ps ph)) $;

--| Two propositions are equivalent if they are both false. Theorem *5.21 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 124. (Contributed by NM, 21-May-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem pm5_21 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wn ph) (wn ps)) (wb ph ps) $;

--| Two propositions are equivalent if they are both false. Closed form of ~ 2false . Equivalent to a ~ bi2 -like version of the xor-connective. (Contributed by Wolf Lammen, 13-May-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem pm5_21im (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wi (wn ps) (wb ph ps)) $;

--| The negation of a wff is equivalent to the wff's equivalence to falsehood. (Contributed by Juha Arpiainen, 19-Jan-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem nbn2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wb (wn ps) (wb ph ps)) $;

--| Transfer negation via an equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 3-Oct-2007.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-Jan-2013.)
theorem bibif (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ps) (wb (wb ph ps) (wn ph)) $;

--| The negation of a wff is equivalent to the wff's equivalence to falsehood. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Oct-2013.)
theorem nbn (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wb (wn ps) (wb ps ph) $;

--| Transfer falsehood via equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 11-Sep-2006.)
theorem nbn3 (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wb (wn ps) (wb ps (wn ph)) $;

--| Two falsehoods are equivalent. (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem _2false (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wn ps $ >
  $ wb ph ps $;

--| Two falsehoods are equivalent (deduction rule). (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-2013.)
theorem _2falsed (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $;

--| Two propositions implying a false one are equivalent. (Contributed by NM, 16-Feb-1996.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 19-May-2013.)
theorem pm5_21ni (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch ps $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) (wb ph ch) $;

--| Eliminate an antecedent implied by each side of a biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 21-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem pm5_21nii (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch ps $ >
  $ wi ps (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wb ph ch $;

--| Eliminate an antecedent implied by each side of a biconditional, deduction version. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Nov-2012.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem pm5_21ndd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ch ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wb ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ch th) $;

--| Theorem *5.19 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 124. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem pm5_19 (ph: wff):
  $ wn (wb ph (wn ph)) $;

--| Theorem *4.8 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 122. This one holds for all propositions, but compare with ~ pm4.81dc which requires a decidability condition. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm4_8 (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph (wn ph)) (wn ph) $;

--| Express implication in terms of conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 9-Apr-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-Feb-2015.)
theorem imnan (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph (wn ps)) (wn (wa ph ps)) $;

--| Express implication in terms of conjunction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Sep-2015.)
theorem imnani (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn (wa ph ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| Theorem to move a conjunct in and out of a negation. (Contributed by NM, 9-Nov-2003.)
theorem nan (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph (wn (wa ps ch))) (wi (wa ph ps) (wn ch)) $;

--| Law of noncontradiction. Theorem *3.24 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 111 (who call it the "law of contradiction"). (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Feb-2015.)
theorem pm3_24 (ph: wff):
  $ wn (wa ph (wn ph)) $;

--| Triple negation. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 28-Jul-2018.)
theorem notnotnot (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wn (wn (wn ph))) (wn ph) $;

--| Extend wff definition to include disjunction ('or').
term wo (ph ps: wff): wff;

--| Definition of 'or'. One of the axioms of propositional logic. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
axiom ax_io (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi (wo ph ch) ps) (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ch ps)) $;

--| Disjunction of antecedents. Compare Theorem *4.77 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 121. (Contributed by NM, 30-May-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem jaob (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi (wo ph ch) ps) (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ch ps)) $;

--| Introduction of a disjunct. Axiom *1.3 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 96. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-1993.) (Revised by NM, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem olc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wo ps ph) $;

--| Introduction of a disjunct. Theorem *2.2 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 104. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-1993.) (Revised by NM, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem orc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wo ph ps) $;

--| Slight generalization of Theorem *2.67 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Revised by NM, 9-Dec-2012.)
theorem pm2_67_2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wo ph ch) ps) (wi ph ps) $;

--| Theorem *3.44 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 113. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Oct-2013.)
theorem pm3_44 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wi ps ph) (wi ch ph)) (wi (wo ps ch) ph) $;

--| Inference disjoining the antecedents of two implications. (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-1994.) (Revised by NM, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem jaoi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch ps $ >
  $ wi (wo ph ch) ps $;

--| Deduction disjoining the antecedents of two implications. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1994.) (Revised by NM, 4-Apr-2013.)
theorem jaod (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wo ps th) ch) $;

--| Eliminate a disjunction in a deduction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem mpjaod (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo ps th) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Inference conjoining and disjoining the antecedents of two implications. (Contributed by NM, 30-Sep-1999.)
theorem jaao (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wi ta ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) (wi (wo ps ta) ch) $;

--| Inference disjoining and conjoining the antecedents of two implications. (Contributed by Stefan Allan, 1-Nov-2008.)
theorem jaoa (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi th (wi ta ch) $ >
  $ wi (wo ph th) (wi (wa ps ta) ch) $;

--| Theorem *2.53 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. This holds intuitionistically, although its converse does not (see ~ pm2.54dc ). (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Revised by NM, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem pm2_53 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wo ph ps) (wi (wn ph) ps) $;

--| Infer implication from disjunction. (Contributed by NM, 11-Jun-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem ori (ph ps: wff):
  $ wo ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) ps $;

--| Deduce implication from disjunction. (Contributed by NM, 18-May-1994.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem ord (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wo ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) ch) $;

--| Elimination of disjunction by denial of a disjunct. Theorem *2.55 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 21-Jul-2012.)
theorem orel1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wi (wo ph ps) ps) $;

--| Elimination of disjunction by denial of a disjunct. Theorem *2.56 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 5-Apr-2013.)
theorem orel2 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wi (wo ps ph) ps) $;

--| Axiom *1.4 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 96. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Revised by NM, 15-Nov-2012.)
theorem pm1_4 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wo ph ps) (wo ps ph) $;

--| Commutative law for disjunction. Theorem *4.31 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 118. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 15-Nov-2012.)
theorem orcom (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wo ph ps) (wo ps ph) $;

--| Commutation of disjuncts in consequent. (Contributed by NM, 2-Dec-2010.)
theorem orcomd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wo ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo ch ps) $;

--| Commutation of disjuncts in antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 2-Dec-2012.)
theorem orcoms (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wo ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wo ps ph) ch $;

--| Deduction introducing a disjunct. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem orci (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wo ph ps $;

--| Deduction introducing a disjunct. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem olci (ph ps: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wo ps ph $;

--| Deduction introducing a disjunct. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-2007.)
theorem orcd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wo ps ch) $;

--| Deduction introducing a disjunct. (Contributed by NM, 11-Apr-2008.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Oct-2013.)
theorem olcd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph (wo ch ps) $;

--| Deduction eliminating disjunct. _Notational convention_: We sometimes suffix with "s" the label of an inference that manipulates an antecedent, leaving the consequent unchanged. The "s" means that the inference eliminates the need for a syllogism ( ~ syl ) -type inference in a proof. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1994.)
theorem orcs (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wo ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Deduction eliminating disjunct. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jun-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Oct-2013.)
theorem olcs (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wo ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi ps ch $;

--| Theorem *2.07 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 101. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_07 (ph: wff):
  $ wi ph (wo ph ph) $;

--| Theorem *2.45 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 106. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_45 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wo ph ps)) (wn ph) $;

--| Theorem *2.46 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 106. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_46 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wo ph ps)) (wn ps) $;

--| Theorem *2.47 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_47 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wo ph ps)) (wo (wn ph) ps) $;

--| Theorem *2.48 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_48 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wo ph ps)) (wo ph (wn ps)) $;

--| Theorem *2.49 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_49 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn (wo ph ps)) (wo (wn ph) (wn ps)) $;

--| Theorem *2.67 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Revised by NM, 9-Dec-2012.)
theorem pm2_67 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi (wo ph ps) ps) (wi ph ps) $;

--| A wff is equivalent to its disjunction with falsehood. Theorem *4.74 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 121. (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 18-Nov-2012.)
theorem biorf (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wb ps (wo ph ps)) $;

--| A wff is equivalent to its negated disjunction with falsehood. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jul-2012.)
theorem biortn (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wo (wn ph) ps)) $;

--| A wff is equivalent to its disjunction with falsehood. (Contributed by NM, 23-Mar-1995.)
theorem biorfi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wb ps (wo ps ph) $;

--| Theorem *2.621 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Revised by NM, 13-Dec-2013.)
theorem pm2_621 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wo ph ps) ps) $;

--| Theorem *2.62 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 13-Dec-2013.)
theorem pm2_62 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wo ph ps) (wi (wi ph ps) ps) $;

--| Infer implication from disjunction. (Contributed by Jonathan Ben-Naim, 3-Jun-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem imorri (ph ps: wff):
  $ wo (wn ph) ps $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Negated disjunction in terms of conjunction. This version of DeMorgan's law is a biconditional for all propositions (not just decidable ones), unlike ~ oranim , ~ anordc , or ~ ianordc . Compare Theorem *4.56 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem ioran (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wn (wo ph ps)) (wa (wn ph) (wn ps)) $;

--| Theorem *3.14 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 111. One direction of De Morgan's law). The biconditional holds for decidable propositions as seen at ~ ianordc . The converse holds for decidable propositions, as seen at ~ pm3.13dc . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem pm3_14 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wo (wn ph) (wn ps)) (wn (wa ph ps)) $;

--| Theorem *3.1 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 111. The converse holds for decidable propositions, as seen at ~ anordc . (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem pm3_1 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) (wn (wo (wn ph) (wn ps))) $;

--| Disjunction of antecedents. Compare Theorem *3.44 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 113. (Contributed by NM, 5-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Apr-2013.)
theorem jao (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi ch ps) (wi (wo ph ch) ps)) $;

--| Axiom *1.2 (Taut) of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 96. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Revised by NM, 10-Mar-2013.)
theorem pm1_2 (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wo ph ph) ph $;

--| Idempotent law for disjunction. Theorem *4.25 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 117. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 16-Apr-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Mar-2013.)
theorem oridm (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wo ph ph) ph $;

--| Theorem *4.25 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 117. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm4_25 (ph: wff):
  $ wb ph (wo ph ph) $;

--| Disjoin antecedents and consequents of two premises. (Contributed by NM, 6-Jun-1994.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 25-Jul-2012.)
theorem orim12i (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ch th $ >
  $ wi (wo ph ch) (wo ps th) $;

--| Introduce disjunct to both sides of an implication. (Contributed by NM, 6-Jun-1994.)
theorem orim1i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wo ph ch) (wo ps ch) $;

--| Introduce disjunct to both sides of an implication. (Contributed by NM, 6-Jun-1994.)
theorem orim2i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wo ch ph) (wo ch ps) $;

--| Inference adding a left disjunct to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 12-Dec-2012.)
theorem orbi2i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wo ch ph) (wo ch ps) $;

--| Inference adding a right disjunct to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.)
theorem orbi1i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wo ph ch) (wo ps ch) $;

--| Infer the disjunction of two equivalences. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.)
theorem orbi12i (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb ch th $ >
  $ wb (wo ph ch) (wo ps th) $;

--| Axiom *1.5 (Assoc) of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 96. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm1_5 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wo ph (wo ps ch)) (wo ps (wo ph ch)) $;

--| Swap two disjuncts. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 14-Nov-2012.)
theorem or12 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wo ph (wo ps ch)) (wo ps (wo ph ch)) $;

--| Associative law for disjunction. Theorem *4.33 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 118. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 26-Jun-2011.)
theorem orass (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wo (wo ph ps) ch) (wo ph (wo ps ch)) $;

--| Theorem *2.31 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 104. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_31 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wo ph (wo ps ch)) (wo (wo ph ps) ch) $;

--| Theorem *2.32 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 105. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_32 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wo (wo ph ps) ch) (wo ph (wo ps ch)) $;

--| A rearrangement of disjuncts. (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 26-Jun-2011.)
theorem or32 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wo (wo ph ps) ch) (wo (wo ph ch) ps) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 disjuncts. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1994.)
theorem or4 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wo (wo ph ps) (wo ch th)) (wo (wo ph ch) (wo ps th)) $;

--| Rearrangement of 4 disjuncts. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-2005.)
theorem or42 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wo (wo ph ps) (wo ch th)) (wo (wo ph ch) (wo th ps)) $;

--| Distribution of disjunction over disjunction. (Contributed by NM, 25-Feb-1995.)
theorem orordi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wo ph (wo ps ch)) (wo (wo ph ps) (wo ph ch)) $;

--| Distribution of disjunction over disjunction. (Contributed by NM, 25-Feb-1995.)
theorem orordir (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wo (wo ph ps) ch) (wo (wo ph ch) (wo ps ch)) $;

--| Theorem *2.3 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 104. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wo ph (wo ps ch)) (wo ph (wo ch ps)) $;

--| Theorem *2.41 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 106. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_41 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wo ps (wo ph ps)) (wo ph ps) $;

--| Theorem *2.42 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 106. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_42 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wo (wn ph) (wi ph ps)) (wi ph ps) $;

--| Theorem *2.4 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 106. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_4 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wo ph (wo ph ps)) (wo ph ps) $;

--| Theorem *4.44 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 119. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm4_44 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb ph (wo ph (wa ph ps)) $;

--| A modus tollens deduction involving disjunction. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 15-Jul-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem mtord (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wo ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $;

--| Theorem *4.45 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 119. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm4_45 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa ph (wo ph ps)) $;

--| Theorem *3.48 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 114. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jan-1997.) (Revised by NM, 1-Dec-2012.)
theorem pm3_48 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ch th)) (wi (wo ph ch) (wo ps th)) $;

--| Disjoin antecedents and consequents in a deduction. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1994.)
theorem orim12d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wo ps th) (wo ch ta)) $;

--| Disjoin antecedents and consequents in a deduction. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-1995.)
theorem orim1d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wo ps th) (wo ch th)) $;

--| Disjoin antecedents and consequents in a deduction. (Contributed by NM, 23-Apr-1995.)
theorem orim2d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wo th ps) (wo th ch)) $;

--| Axiom *1.6 (Sum) of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 97. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem orim2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ps ch) (wi (wo ph ps) (wo ph ch)) $;

--| Deduction adding a left disjunct to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem orbi2d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wo th ps) (wo th ch)) $;

--| Deduction adding a right disjunct to both sides of a logical equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.)
theorem orbi1d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wo ps th) (wo ch th)) $;

--| Theorem *4.37 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 118. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem orbi1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wb ph ps) (wb (wo ph ch) (wo ps ch)) $;

--| Deduction joining two equivalences to form equivalence of disjunctions. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.)
theorem orbi12d (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wo ps th) (wo ch ta)) $;

--| Theorem *5.61 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 30-Jun-2013.)
theorem pm5_61 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wo ph ps) (wn ps)) (wa ph (wn ps)) $;

--| Inference disjoining the antecedents of two implications. (Contributed by NM, 23-Oct-2005.)
theorem jaoian (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa th ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa (wo ph th) ps) ch $;

--| Deduction disjoining the antecedents of two implications. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2005.)
theorem jaodan (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wo ps th)) ch $;

--| Eliminate a disjunction in a deduction. A translation of natural deduction rule ` \/ ` E ( ` \/ ` elimination). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem mpjaodan (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wo ps th) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Theorem *4.77 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 121. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm4_77 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wi ps ph) (wi ch ph)) (wi (wo ps ch) ph) $;

--| Theorem *2.63 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_63 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wo ph ps) (wi (wo (wn ph) ps) ps) $;

--| Theorem *2.64 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_64 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wo ph ps) (wi (wo ph (wn ps)) ph) $;

--| Theorem *5.53 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm5_53 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wi (wo (wo ph ps) ch) th) (wa (wa (wi ph th) (wi ps th)) (wi ch th)) $;

--| Theorem *2.38 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 105. (Contributed by NM, 6-Mar-2008.)
theorem pm2_38 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ps ch) (wi (wo ps ph) (wo ch ph)) $;

--| Theorem *2.36 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 105. (Contributed by NM, 6-Mar-2008.)
theorem pm2_36 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ps ch) (wi (wo ph ps) (wo ch ph)) $;

--| Theorem *2.37 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 105. (Contributed by NM, 6-Mar-2008.)
theorem pm2_37 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ps ch) (wi (wo ps ph) (wo ph ch)) $;

--| Theorem *2.73 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 108. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_73 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wo (wo ph ps) ch) (wo ps ch)) $;

--| Theorem *2.74 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 108. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem pm2_74 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ps ph) (wi (wo (wo ph ps) ch) (wo ph ch)) $;

--| Theorem *2.76 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 108. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem pm2_76 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wo ph (wi ps ch)) (wi (wo ph ps) (wo ph ch)) $;

--| Theorem *2.75 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 108. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Jan-2013.)
theorem pm2_75 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wo ph ps) (wi (wo ph (wi ps ch)) (wo ph ch)) $;

--| Theorem *2.8 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 108. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem pm2_8 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wo ph ps) (wi (wo (wn ps) ch) (wo ph ch)) $;

--| Theorem *2.81 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 108. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_81 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wi ps (wi ch th)) (wi (wo ph ps) (wi (wo ph ch) (wo ph th))) $;

--| Theorem *2.82 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 108. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_82 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wo (wo ph ps) ch) (wi (wo (wo ph (wn ch)) th) (wo (wo ph ps) th)) $;

--| Infer negated disjunction of negated premises. (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-1995.)
theorem pm3_2ni (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wn ps $ >
  $ wn (wo ph ps) $;

--| Absorption of redundant internal disjunct. Compare Theorem *4.45 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 119. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 28-Feb-2014.)
theorem orabs (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa (wo ph ps) ph) $;

--| Absorb a disjunct into a conjunct. (Contributed by Roy F. Longton, 23-Jun-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 10-Nov-2013.)
theorem oranabs (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wo ph (wn ps)) ps) (wa ph ps) $;

--| Distributive law for disjunction. Theorem *4.41 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 119. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem ordi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wo ph (wa ps ch)) (wa (wo ph ps) (wo ph ch)) $;

--| Distributive law for disjunction. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1994.)
theorem ordir (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wo (wa ph ps) ch) (wa (wo ph ch) (wo ps ch)) $;

--| Distributive law for conjunction. Theorem *4.4 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 118. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 5-Jan-2013.)
theorem andi (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa ph (wo ps ch)) (wo (wa ph ps) (wa ph ch)) $;

--| Distributive law for conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1994.)
theorem andir (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wo ph ps) ch) (wo (wa ph ch) (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Double distributive law for disjunction. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1994.)
theorem orddi (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wo (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) (wa (wa (wo ph ch) (wo ph th)) (wa (wo ps ch) (wo ps th))) $;

--| Double distributive law for conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-1994.)
theorem anddi (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wo ph ps) (wo ch th)) (wo (wo (wa ph ch) (wa ph th)) (wo (wa ps ch) (wa ps th))) $;

--| Theorem *4.39 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 118. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm4_39 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wb ph ch) (wb ps th)) (wb (wo ph ps) (wo ch th)) $;

--| Implication in terms of biconditional and disjunction. Theorem *4.72 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 121. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 30-Jan-2013.)
theorem pm4_72 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi ph ps) (wb ps (wo ph ps)) $;

--| Theorem *5.16 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 124. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2015.)
theorem pm5_16 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn (wa (wb ph ps) (wb ph (wn ps))) $;

--| A wff is disjoined with truth is true. (Contributed by NM, 23-May-1999.)
theorem biort (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ph (wo ph ps)) $;

--| Extend wff definition to include stability.
term wstab (ph: wff): wff;

--| Propositions where a double-negative can be removed are called stable. See Chapter 2 [Moschovakis] p. 2. Our notation for stability is a connective ` STAB ` which we place before the formula in question. For example, ` STAB x = y ` corresponds to "x = y is stable". (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 13-Aug-2018.)
axiom df_stab (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wstab ph) (wi (wn (wn ph)) ph) $;

--| Every formula of the form ` -. ph ` is stable. Uses ~ notnotnot . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 13-Aug-2018.)
theorem stabnot (ph: wff):
  $ wstab (wn ph) $;

--| Extend wff definition to include decidability.
term wdc (ph: wff): wff;

--| Propositions which are known to be true or false are called decidable. The (classical) Law of the Excluded Middle corresponds to the principle that all propositions are decidable, but even given intuitionistic logic, particular kinds of propositions may be decidable (for example, the proposition that two natural numbers are equal will be decidable under most sets of axioms). Our notation for decidability is a connective ` DECID ` which we place before the formula in question. For example, ` DECID x = y ` corresponds to "x = y is decidable". We could transform intuitionistic logic to classical logic by adding unconditional forms of ~ condc , ~ exmiddc , ~ peircedc , or ~ notnotrdc , any of which would correspond to the assertion that all propositions are decidable. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Mar-2018.)
axiom df_dc (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wdc ph) (wo ph (wn ph)) $;

--| Law of excluded middle, for a decidable proposition. The law of the excluded middle is also called the principle of _tertium non datur_. Theorem *2.11 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 101. It says that something is either true or not true; there are no in-between values of truth. The key way in which intuitionistic logic differs from classical logic is that intuitionistic logic says that excluded middle only holds for some propositions, and classical logic says that it holds for all propositions. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-May-2018.)
theorem exmiddc (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wo ph (wn ph)) $;

--| Commuted law of the excluded middle for a decidable proposition. Based on theorem *2.1 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 101. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 25-Mar-2018.)
theorem pm2_1dc (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wo (wn ph) ph) $;

--| A decidable proposition is decidable when negated. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 25-Mar-2018.)
theorem dcn (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wdc (wn ph)) $;

--| The equivalent of a decidable proposition is decidable. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 28-Mar-2018.)
theorem dcbii (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wdc ph) (wdc ps) $;

--| The equivalent of a decidable proposition is decidable. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 7-Sep-2019.)
theorem dcbid (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wdc ps) (wdc ch)) $;

--| Contraposition of a decidable proposition. This theorem swaps or "transposes" the order of the consequents when negation is removed. An informal example is that the statement "if there are no clouds in the sky, it is not raining" implies the statement "if it is raining, there are clouds in the sky." This theorem (without the decidability condition, of course) is called _Transp_ or "the principle of transposition" in _Principia Mathematica_ (Theorem *2.17 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 103) and is Axiom A3 of [Margaris] p. 49. We will also use the term "contraposition" for this principle, although the reader is advised that in the field of philosophical logic, "contraposition" has a different technical meaning. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 13-Mar-2018.)
theorem condc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wi (wn ph) (wn ps)) (wi ps ph)) $;

--| Proof by contradiction for a decidable proposition. Based on Theorem *2.18 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 103 (also called the Law of Clavius). Intuitionistically it requires a decidability assumption, but compare with ~ pm2.01 which does not. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 24-Mar-2018.)
theorem pm2_18dc (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wi (wn ph) ph) ph) $;

--| Double negation elimination for a decidable proposition. The converse, ~ notnot , holds for all propositions, not just decidable ones. This is Theorem *2.14 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 102, but with a decidability condition added. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Mar-2018.)
theorem notnotrdc (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wn (wn ph)) ph) $;

--| Decidability implies stability. The converse is not necessarily true. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 13-Aug-2018.)
theorem dcimpstab (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wstab ph) $;

--| Contraposition for a decidable proposition. Based on theorem *2.15 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 102. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 29-Mar-2018.)
theorem con1dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wi (wn ph) ps) (wi (wn ps) ph)) $;

--| A contraposition deduction. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 18-May-2018.)
theorem con4biddc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wdc ps) (wi (wdc ch) (wb (wn ps) (wn ch)))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wdc ps) (wi (wdc ch) (wb ps ch))) $;

--| An importation inference for a decidable consequent. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-Apr-2018.)
theorem impidc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ch) (wi ph (wi ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi (wdc ch) (wi (wn (wi ph (wn ps))) ch) $;

--| Simplification given a decidable proposition. Similar to Theorem *3.27 (Simp) of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 112. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-Apr-2018.)
theorem simprimdc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ps) (wi (wn (wi ph (wn ps))) ps) $;

--| Simplification for a decidable proposition. Similar to Theorem *3.26 (Simp) of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 112. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 29-Mar-2018.)
theorem simplimdc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wn (wi ph ps)) ph) $;

--| Deduction eliminating a decidable antecedent. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 4-May-2018.)
theorem pm2_61ddc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wn ps) ch) $ >
  $ wi (wdc ps) (wi ph ch) $;

--| Case elimination for a decidable proposition. Based on theorem *2.6 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 25-Mar-2018.)
theorem pm2_6dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wi (wn ph) ps) (wi (wi ph ps) ps)) $;

--| Inference forming an implication from the antecedents of two premises, where a decidable antecedent is negated. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 25-Mar-2018.)
theorem jadc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wn ph) ch) $ >
  $ wi ps ch $ >
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wi ph ps) ch) $;

--| Deduction forming an implication from the antecedents of two premises, where a decidable antecedent is negated. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 26-Mar-2018.)
theorem jaddc (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wdc ps) (wi (wn ps) th)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wdc ps) (wi (wi ps ch) th)) $;

--| Case elimination for a decidable proposition. Based on theorem *2.61 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 29-Mar-2018.)
theorem pm2_61dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wi ph ps) (wi (wi (wn ph) ps) ps)) $;

--| Negating an implication for a decidable antecedent. Based on theorem *2.5 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 29-Mar-2018.)
theorem pm2_5dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wn (wi ph ps)) (wi (wn ph) ps)) $;

--| Theorem *2.521 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107, but with an additional decidability condition. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 5-May-2018.)
theorem pm2_521dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wn (wi ph ps)) (wi ps ph)) $;

--| Contraposition. Theorem *4.1 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 116, but for a decidable proposition. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 24-Apr-2018.)
theorem con34bdc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ps) (wb (wi ph ps) (wi (wn ps) (wn ph))) $;

--| Double negation equivalence for a decidable proposition. Like Theorem *4.13 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 117, but with a decidability antecendent. The forward direction, ~ notnot , holds for all propositions, not just decidable ones. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 13-Mar-2018.)
theorem notnotbdc (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wb ph (wn (wn ph))) $;

--| Contraposition. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 4-Apr-2018.)
theorem con1biimdc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wb (wn ph) ps) (wb (wn ps) ph)) $;

--| Contraposition. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 17-Apr-2018.)
theorem con1bidc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wb (wb (wn ph) ps) (wb (wn ps) ph))) $;

--| Contraposition. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 17-Apr-2018.)
theorem con2bidc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wb (wb ph (wn ps)) (wb ps (wn ph)))) $;

--| A contraposition deduction. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 4-Apr-2018.)
theorem con1biddc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wdc ps) (wb (wn ps) ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wdc ps) (wb (wn ch) ps)) $;

--| A contraposition inference. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 15-Mar-2018.)
theorem con1biidc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wb (wn ph) ps) $ >
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wb (wn ps) ph) $;

--| Contraposition. Bidirectional version of ~ con1dc . (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.)
theorem con1bdc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wb (wi (wn ph) ps) (wi (wn ps) ph))) $;

--| A contraposition inference. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 15-Mar-2018.)
theorem con2biidc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ps) (wb ph (wn ps)) $ >
  $ wi (wdc ps) (wb ps (wn ph)) $;

--| A contraposition deduction. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Apr-2018.)
theorem con2biddc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wdc ch) (wb ps (wn ch))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wdc ch) (wb ch (wn ps))) $;

--| Proof by contradiction. This only holds for decidable propositions, as it is part of the family of theorems which assume ` -. ps ` , derive a contradiction, and therefore conclude ` ps ` . By contrast, assuming ` ps ` , deriving a contradiction, and therefore concluding ` -. ps ` , as in ~ pm2.65 , is valid for all propositions. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 13-May-2018.)
theorem condandc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wn ps)) ch $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wn ps)) (wn ch) $ >
  $ wi (wdc ps) (wi ph ps) $;

--| Combine antecedents into a single biconditional. This inference is reminiscent of ~ jadc . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 4-May-2018.)
theorem bijadc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wn ph) (wi (wn ps) ch) $ >
  $ wi (wdc ps) (wi (wb ph ps) ch) $;

--| Relationship between an equivalence and an equivalence with some negation, for decidable propositions. Based on theorem *5.18 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 124. Given decidability, we can consider ` -. ( ph <-> -. ps ) ` to represent "negated exclusive-or". (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 4-Apr-2018.)
theorem pm5_18dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wb (wb ph ps) (wn (wb ph (wn ps))))) $;

--| Definition of 'and' in terms of negation and implication, for decidable propositions. The forward direction holds for all propositions, and can (basically) be found at ~ pm3.2im . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-Apr-2018.)
theorem dfandc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wb (wa ph ps) (wn (wi ph (wn ps))))) $;

--| A decidable proposition or its triple negation is true. Theorem *2.13 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 101 with decidability condition added. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 13-May-2018.)
theorem pm2_13dc (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wo ph (wn (wn (wn ph)))) $;

--| Theorem *4.63 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120, for decidable propositions. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 1-May-2018.)
theorem pm4_63dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wb (wn (wi ph (wn ps))) (wa ph ps))) $;

--| Theorem *4.67 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120, for decidable propositions. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 1-May-2018.)
theorem pm4_67dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wb (wn (wi (wn ph) (wn ps))) (wa (wn ph) ps))) $;

--| Express conjunction in terms of implication. One direction of Theorem *4.61 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120. The converse holds for decidable propositions, as can be seen at ~ annimdc . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 24-Dec-2017.)
theorem annimim (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wn ps)) (wn (wi ph ps)) $;

--| One direction of Theorem *4.65 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120. The converse holds in classical logic. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 28-Jul-2018.)
theorem pm4_65r (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wn ph) (wn ps)) (wn (wi (wn ph) ps)) $;

--| An implication between two decidable propositions is decidable. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 28-Mar-2018.)
theorem dcim (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wdc (wi ph ps))) $;

--| Express implication in terms of conjunction. The converse only holds given a decidability condition; see ~ imandc . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 24-Dec-2017.)
theorem imanim (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wn (wa ph (wn ps))) $;

--| Express implication in terms of conjunction. Theorem 3.4(27) of [Stoll] p. 176, with an added decidability condition. The forward direction, ~ imanim , holds for all propositions, not just decidable ones. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 25-Apr-2018.)
theorem imandc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ps) (wb (wi ph ps) (wn (wa ph (wn ps)))) $;

--| Theorem *4.14 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 117, given a decidability condition. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 24-Apr-2018.)
theorem pm4_14dc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ch) (wb (wi (wa ph ps) ch) (wi (wa ph (wn ch)) (wn ps))) $;

--| Theorem *3.37 (Transp) of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 112, given a decidability condition. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 24-Apr-2018.)
theorem pm3_37dc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ch) (wi (wi (wa ph ps) ch) (wi (wa ph (wn ch)) (wn ps))) $;

--| Theorem *4.15 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 117. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 18-Nov-2012.)
theorem pm4_15 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wi (wa ph ps) (wn ch)) (wi (wa ps ch) (wn ph)) $;

--| Deriving disjunction from implication for a decidable proposition. Based on theorem *2.54 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 107. The converse, ~ pm2.53 , holds whether the proposition is decidable or not. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 26-Mar-2018.)
theorem pm2_54dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wi (wn ph) ps) (wo ph ps)) $;

--| Definition of 'or' in terms of negation and implication for a decidable proposition. Based on definition of [Margaris] p. 49. One direction, ~ pm2.53 , holds for all propositions, not just decidable ones. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 26-Mar-2018.)
theorem dfordc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wb (wo ph ps) (wi (wn ph) ps)) $;

--| Elimination of disjunction based on a disjunction, for a decidable proposition. Based on theorem *2.25 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 104. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm2_25dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wo ph (wi (wo ph ps) ps)) $;

--| Concluding disjunction from implication for a decidable proposition. Based on theorem *2.68 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 108. Converse of ~ pm2.62 and one half of ~ dfor2dc . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 27-Mar-2018.)
theorem pm2_68dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wi (wi ph ps) ps) (wo ph ps)) $;

--| Logical 'or' expressed in terms of implication only, for a decidable proposition. Based on theorem *5.25 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 124. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 27-Mar-2018.)
theorem dfor2dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wb (wo ph ps) (wi (wi ph ps) ps)) $;

--| Simplify an implication between implications, for a decidable proposition. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 18-Mar-2018.)
theorem imimorbdc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ps) (wb (wi (wi ps ch) (wi ph ch)) (wi ph (wo ps ch))) $;

--| Implication in terms of disjunction for a decidable proposition. Based on theorem *4.6 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120. The reverse direction, ~ imorr , holds for all propositions. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 20-Apr-2018.)
theorem imordc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wb (wi ph ps) (wo (wn ph) ps)) $;

--| Implication in terms of disjunction. One direction of theorem *4.6 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120. The converse holds for decidable propositions, as seen at ~ imordc . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-Jul-2018.)
theorem imorr (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wo (wn ph) ps) (wi ph ps) $;

--| Implication in terms of disjunction. Like Theorem *4.62 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120, but for a decidable antecedent. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-Apr-2018.)
theorem pm4_62dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wb (wi ph (wn ps)) (wo (wn ph) (wn ps))) $;

--| Negated conjunction in terms of disjunction (DeMorgan's law). Theorem *4.51 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120, but where one proposition is decidable. The reverse direction, ~ pm3.14 , holds for all propositions, but the equivalence only holds where one proposition is decidable. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-Apr-2018.)
theorem ianordc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wb (wn (wa ph ps)) (wo (wn ph) (wn ps))) $;

--| Absorption of disjunction into equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 6-Aug-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem oibabs (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wi (wo ph ps) (wb ph ps)) (wb ph ps) $;

--| Theorem *4.64 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120, given a decidability condition. The reverse direction, ~ pm2.53 , holds for all propositions. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 2-May-2018.)
theorem pm4_64dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wb (wi (wn ph) ps) (wo ph ps)) $;

--| Theorem *4.66 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120, given a decidability condition. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 2-May-2018.)
theorem pm4_66dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wb (wi (wn ph) (wn ps)) (wo ph (wn ps))) $;

--| One direction of theorem *4.52 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120. The converse also holds in classical logic. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 27-Jul-2018.)
theorem pm4_52im (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (wn ps)) (wn (wo (wn ph) ps)) $;

--| One direction of theorem *4.53 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120. The converse also holds in classical logic. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 27-Jul-2018.)
theorem pm4_53r (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wo (wn ph) ps) (wn (wa ph (wn ps))) $;

--| Theorem *4.54 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120, for decidable propositions. One form of DeMorgan's law. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 2-May-2018.)
theorem pm4_54dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wb (wa (wn ph) ps) (wn (wo ph (wn ps))))) $;

--| Theorem *4.56 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm4_56 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wn ph) (wn ps)) (wn (wo ph ps)) $;

--| Disjunction in terms of conjunction (DeMorgan's law). One direction of Theorem *4.57 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120. The converse does not hold intuitionistically but does hold in classical logic. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 25-Jul-2018.)
theorem oranim (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wo ph ps) (wn (wa (wn ph) (wn ps))) $;

--| Implication distributes over disjunction. One direction of Theorem *4.78 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 121. The converse holds in classical logic. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 15-Jan-2018.)
theorem pm4_78i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wo (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) (wi ph (wo ps ch)) $;

--| Equivalence between a disjunction of two implications, and a conjunction and an implication. Based on theorem *4.79 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 121 but with additional decidability antecedents. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 28-Mar-2018.)
theorem pm4_79dc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wb (wo (wi ps ph) (wi ch ph)) (wi (wa ps ch) ph))) $;

--| Two ways of stating exclusive-or which are equivalent for a decidable proposition. Based on theorem *5.17 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 124. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 16-Apr-2018.)
theorem pm5_17dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ps) (wb (wa (wo ph ps) (wn (wa ph ps))) (wb ph (wn ps))) $;

--| Reverse distribution of disjunction over implication, given decidability. Based on theorem *2.85 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 108. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 1-Apr-2018.)
theorem pm2_85dc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wi (wo ph ps) (wo ph ch)) (wo ph (wi ps ch))) $;

--| Disjunction distributes over implication. The forward direction, ~ pm2.76 , is valid intuitionistically. The reverse direction holds if ` ph ` is decidable, as can be seen at ~ pm2.85dc . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 1-Apr-2018.)
theorem orimdidc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wb (wo ph (wi ps ch)) (wi (wo ph ps) (wo ph ch))) $;

--| Decidable proposition version of theorem *2.26 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 104. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 20-Apr-2018.)
theorem pm2_26dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wo (wn ph) (wi (wi ph ps) ps)) $;

--| Theorem *4.81 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 122, for decidable propositions. This one needs a decidability condition, but compare with ~ pm4.8 which holds for all propositions. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 4-Jul-2018.)
theorem pm4_81dc (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wb (wi (wn ph) ph) ph) $;

--| A decidable proposition or its negation implies a second proposition. Based on theorem *5.11 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 123. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 29-Mar-2018.)
theorem pm5_11dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wo (wi ph ps) (wi (wn ph) ps))) $;

--| Excluded middle with antecedents for a decidable consequent. Based on theorem *5.12 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 123. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-Mar-2018.)
theorem pm5_12dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ps) (wo (wi ph ps) (wi ph (wn ps))) $;

--| A decidable proposition is implied by or implies other propositions. Based on theorem *5.14 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 123. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-Mar-2018.)
theorem pm5_14dc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ps) (wo (wi ph ps) (wi ps ch)) $;

--| An implication holds in at least one direction, where one proposition is decidable. Based on theorem *5.13 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 123. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-Mar-2018.)
theorem pm5_13dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ps) (wo (wi ph ps) (wi ps ph)) $;

--| A disjunction is equivalent to one of its disjuncts, given a decidable disjunct. Based on theorem *5.55 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-Mar-2018.)
theorem pm5_55dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wo (wb (wo ph ps) ph) (wb (wo ph ps) ps)) $;

--| Peirce's theorem for a decidable proposition. This odd-looking theorem can be seen as an alternative to ~ exmiddc , ~ condc , or ~ notnotrdc in the sense of expressing the "difference" between an intuitionistic system of propositional calculus and a classical system. In intuitionistic logic, it only holds for decidable propositions. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 3-Jul-2018.)
theorem peircedc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wi (wi ph ps) ph) ph) $;

--| The Inversion Axiom of the infinite-valued sentential logic (L-infinity) of Lukasiewicz, but where one of the propositions is decidable. Using ~ dfor2dc , we can see that this expresses "disjunction commutes." Theorem *2.69 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 108 (plus the decidability condition). (Contributed by NM, 12-Aug-2004.)
theorem looinvdc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wi (wi ph ps) ps) (wi (wi ps ph) ph)) $;

--| A proposition is testable iff its negative or double-negative is true. See Chapter 2 [Moschovakis] p. 2. Our notation for testability is ` DECID -. ` before the formula in question. For example, ` DECID -. x = y ` corresponds to "x = y is testable". (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 13-Aug-2018.)
theorem dftest (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wdc (wn ph)) (wo (wn ph) (wn (wn ph))) $;

--| A proposition is testable iff its negation is testable. See also ~ dcn (which could be read as "Decidability implies testability"). (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 6-Dec-2018.)
theorem testbitestn (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wdc (wn ph)) (wdc (wn (wn ph))) $;

--| "Stable and testable" is equivalent to decidable. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 13-Aug-2018.)
theorem stabtestimpdc (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wstab ph) (wdc (wn ph))) (wdc ph) $;

--| Eliminate an antecedent implied by each side of a biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 20-Nov-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Nov-2013.)
theorem pm5_21nd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) th $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) th $ >
  $ wi th (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $;

--| Theorem *5.35 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm5_35 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ph ch)) (wi ph (wb ps ch)) $;

--| A conjunction is equivalent to one of its conjuncts, given a decidable conjunct. Based on theorem *5.54 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-Mar-2018.)
theorem pm5_54dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wo (wb (wa ph ps) ph) (wb (wa ph ps) ps)) $;

--| Move conjunction outside of biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 13-May-1999.)
theorem baib (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ps (wb ph ch) $;

--| Move conjunction outside of biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 11-Jul-1994.)
theorem baibr (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ps (wb ch ph) $;

--| Move conjunction outside of biconditional. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem rbaib (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ch (wb ph ps) $;

--| Move conjunction outside of biconditional. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem rbaibr (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ch (wb ps ph) $;

--| Move conjunction outside of biconditional. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem baibd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ch th)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph ch) (wb ps th) $;

--| Move conjunction outside of biconditional. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Sep-2015.)
theorem rbaibd (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ch th)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) (wb ps ch) $;

--| Theorem *5.44 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm5_44 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph ps) (wb (wi ph ch) (wi ph (wa ps ch))) $;

--| Conjunction in antecedent versus disjunction in consequent, for a decidable proposition. Theorem *5.6 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125, with decidability condition added. The reverse implication holds for all propositions (see ~ pm5.6r ). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 2-Apr-2018.)
theorem pm5_6dc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ps) (wb (wi (wa ph (wn ps)) ch) (wi ph (wo ps ch))) $;

--| Conjunction in antecedent versus disjunction in consequent. One direction of Theorem *5.6 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. If ` ps ` is decidable, the converse also holds (see ~ pm5.6dc ). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 4-Aug-2018.)
theorem pm5_6r (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wi ph (wo ps ch)) (wi (wa ph (wn ps)) ch) $;

--| Change disjunction in consequent to conjunction in antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jun-1994.)
theorem orcanai (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wo ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wn ps)) ch $;

--| Introduction of conjunct inside of a contradiction. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-1993.)
theorem intnan (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wn (wa ps ph) $;

--| Introduction of conjunct inside of a contradiction. (Contributed by NM, 3-Apr-1995.)
theorem intnanr (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wn (wa ph ps) $;

--| Introduction of conjunct inside of a contradiction. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jul-2005.)
theorem intnand (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wa ch ps)) $;

--| Introduction of conjunct inside of a contradiction. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jul-2005.)
theorem intnanrd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wn (wa ps ch)) $;

--| A conjunction of two decidable propositions is decidable. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-Apr-2018.)
theorem dcan (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wdc (wa ph ps))) $;

--| A disjunction of two decidable propositions is decidable. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-Apr-2018.)
theorem dcor (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wdc (wo ph ps))) $;

--| An equivalence of two decidable propositions is decidable. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-Apr-2018.)
theorem dcbi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wdc (wb ph ps))) $;

--| Express conjunction in terms of implication. The forward direction, ~ annimim , is valid for all propositions, but as an equivalence, it requires a decidability condition. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 25-Apr-2018.)
theorem annimdc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wb (wa ph (wn ps)) (wn (wi ph ps)))) $;

--| Theorem *4.55 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120, for decidable propositions. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 2-May-2018.)
theorem pm4_55dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wb (wn (wa (wn ph) ps)) (wo ph (wn ps)))) $;

--| Detach truth from conjunction in biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-1996.) (Revised by NM, 9-Jan-2015.)
theorem mpbiran (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wb ph ch $;

--| Detach truth from conjunction in biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-1996.) (Revised by NM, 9-Jan-2015.)
theorem mpbiran2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ch $ >
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ wb ph ps $;

--| Detach a conjunction of truths in a biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-2005.) (Revised by NM, 9-Jan-2015.)
theorem mpbir2an (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ps $ >
  $ ch $ >
  $ wb ph (wa ps ch) $ >
  $ ph $;

--| Detach a conjunction of truths in a biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 6-Nov-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2012.)
theorem mpbi2and (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ps $ >
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa ps ch) th) $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| Detach a conjunction of truths in a biconditional. (Contributed by NM, 6-Nov-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 24-Nov-2012.)
theorem mpbir2and (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi ph th $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ch th)) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Theorem *5.62 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125, for a decidable proposition. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-May-2018.)
theorem pm5_62dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ps) (wb (wo (wa ph ps) (wn ps)) (wo ph (wn ps))) $;

--| Theorem *5.63 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125, for a decidable proposition. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-May-2018.)
theorem pm5_63dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wb (wo ph ps) (wo ph (wa (wn ph) ps))) $;

--| A wff conjoined with falsehood is false. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 26-Nov-2012.)
theorem bianfi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wb ph (wa ps ph) $;

--| A wff conjoined with falsehood is false. (Contributed by NM, 27-Mar-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 5-Nov-2013.)
theorem bianfd (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wn ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Theorem *4.43 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 119. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 26-Nov-2012.)
theorem pm4_43 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb ph (wa (wo ph ps) (wo ph (wn ps))) $;

--| Theorem *4.82 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 122. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.)
theorem pm4_82 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wi ph ps) (wi ph (wn ps))) (wn ph) $;

--| Theorem *4.83 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 122, for decidable propositions. As with other case elimination theorems, like ~ pm2.61dc , it only holds for decidable propositions. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-May-2018.)
theorem pm4_83dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wb (wa (wi ph ps) (wi (wn ph) ps)) ps) $;

--| A transitive law of equivalence. Compare Theorem *4.22 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 117. (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-1993.)
theorem biantr (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wb ph ps) (wb ch ps)) (wb ph ch) $;

--| Disjunction distributes over the biconditional, for a decidable proposition. Based on an axiom of system DS in Vladimir Lifschitz, "On calculational proofs" (1998), ~ http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.25.3384 . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 2-Apr-2018.)
theorem orbididc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wb (wo ph (wb ps ch)) (wb (wo ph ps) (wo ph ch))) $;

--| Disjunction distributes over the biconditional, for a decidable proposition. Based on theorem *5.7 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. This theorem is similar to ~ orbididc . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 2-Apr-2018.)
theorem pm5_7dc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ch) (wb (wb (wo ph ch) (wo ps ch)) (wo ch (wb ph ps))) $;

--| Dijkstra-Scholten's Golden Rule for calculational proofs. (Contributed by NM, 10-Jan-2005.)
theorem bigolden (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb (wb (wa ph ps) ph) (wb ps (wo ph ps)) $;

--| Conjunction in terms of disjunction (DeMorgan's law). Theorem *4.5 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 120, but where the propositions are decidable. The forward direction, ~ pm3.1 , holds for all propositions, but the equivalence only holds given decidability. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-Apr-2018.)
theorem anordc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wb (wa ph ps) (wn (wo (wn ph) (wn ps))))) $;

--| Theorem *3.11 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 111, but for decidable propositions. The converse, ~ pm3.1 , holds for all propositions, not just decidable ones. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Apr-2018.)
theorem pm3_11dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wi (wn (wo (wn ph) (wn ps))) (wa ph ps))) $;

--| Theorem *3.12 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 111, but for decidable propositions. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Apr-2018.)
theorem pm3_12dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wo (wo (wn ph) (wn ps)) (wa ph ps))) $;

--| Theorem *3.13 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 111, but for decidable propositions. The converse, ~ pm3.14 , holds for all propositions. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Apr-2018.)
theorem pm3_13dc (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ph) (wi (wdc ps) (wi (wn (wa ph ps)) (wo (wn ph) (wn ps)))) $;

--| DN_1 for decidable propositions. Without the decidability conditions, DN_1 can serve as a single axiom for Boolean algebra. See ~ http://www-unix.mcs.anl.gov/~~mccune/papers/basax/v12.pdf . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Apr-2018.)
theorem dn1dc (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wdc ph) (wa (wdc ps) (wa (wdc ch) (wdc th)))) (wb (wn (wo (wn (wo (wn (wo ph ps)) ch)) (wn (wo ph (wn (wo (wn ch) (wn (wo ch th)))))))) ch) $;

--| Decidable proposition version of theorem *5.71 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 125. (Contributed by Roy F. Longton, 23-Jun-2005.) (Modified for decidability by Jim Kingdon, 19-Apr-2018.)
theorem pm5_71dc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wdc ps) (wi (wi ps (wn ch)) (wb (wa (wo ph ps) ch) (wa ph ch))) $;

--| Theorem *5.75 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 126. (Contributed by NM, 3-Jan-2005.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 7-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 23-Dec-2012.)
theorem pm5_75 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wi ch (wn ps)) (wb ph (wo ps ch))) (wb (wa ph (wn ps)) ch) $;

--| Removal of conjunct from one side of an equivalence. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.)
theorem bimsc1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wa (wi ph ps) (wb ch (wa ps ph))) (wb ch ph) $;

--| Inference for combining cases. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jul-1999.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 6-Jan-2013.)
theorem ccase (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa ch ps) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa ph th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa ch th) ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wo ph ch) (wo ps th)) ta $;

--| Deduction for combining cases. (Contributed by NM, 9-May-2004.)
theorem ccased (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ch) et) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa th ch) et) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa ps ta) et) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa th ta) et) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wa (wo ps th) (wo ch ta)) et) $;

--| Inference for combining cases. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jul-1999.)
theorem ccase2 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ta $ >
  $ wi ch ta $ >
  $ wi th ta $ >
  $ wi (wa (wo ph ch) (wo ps th)) ta $;

--| Miscellaneous inference relating falsehoods. (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-1994.)
theorem niabn (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) (wb (wa ch ps) (wn ph)) $;

--| Alternate version of ~ dedlema . (Contributed by NM, 2-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 7-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 4-Dec-2012.)
theorem dedlem0a (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wi (wi ch ph) (wa ps ph))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ iftrue . (Contributed by NM, 26-Jun-2002.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 7-May-2011.)
theorem dedlema (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wo (wa ps ph) (wa ch (wn ph)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ iffalse . (Contributed by NM, 15-May-1999.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 7-May-2011.)
theorem dedlemb (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (wn ph) (wb ch (wo (wa ps ph) (wa ch (wn ph)))) $;

--| One direction of Theorem *4.42 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 119. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 4-Aug-2018.)
theorem pm4_42r (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wo (wa ph ps) (wa ph (wn ps))) ph $;

--| Miscellaneous inference relating falsehoods. (Contributed by NM, 31-Mar-1994.)
theorem ninba (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wi (wn ps) (wb (wn ph) (wa ch ps)) $;

--| A specialized lemma for set theory (to derive the Axiom of Pairing). (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 5-Jan-2013.)
theorem prlem1 (ph ps ch th ta et: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb et ch) $ >
  $ wi ps (wn th) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ps (wi (wo (wa ps ch) (wa th ta)) et)) $;

--| A specialized lemma for set theory (to derive the Axiom of Pairing). (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-1993.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 9-Dec-2012.)
theorem prlem2 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wo (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) (wa (wo ph ch) (wo (wa ph ps) (wa ch th))) $;

--| A specialized lemma for set theory (ordered pair theorem). (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-1995.) (Proof shortened by Wolf Lammen, 8-Dec-2012.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 2-Feb-2015.)
theorem oplem1 (ph ps ch th ta: wff):
  $ wi ph (wo ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wo th ta) $ >
  $ wb ps th $ >
  $ wi ch (wb th ta) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Lemma used in construction of real numbers. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-1995.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 26-Jun-2011.)
theorem rnlem (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch th)) (wa (wa (wa ph ch) (wa ps th)) (wa (wa ph th) (wa ps ch))) $;

--| Extend wff definition to include 3-way disjunction ('or').
term w3o (ph ps ch: wff): wff;

--| Extend wff definition to include 3-way conjunction ('and').
term w3a (ph ps ch: wff): wff;

--| Define disjunction ('or') of 3 wff's. Definition *2.33 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 105. This abbreviation reduces the number of parentheses and emphasizes that the order of bracketing is not important by virtue of the associative law ~ orass . (Contributed by NM, 8-Apr-1994.)
axiom df_3or (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (w3o ph ps ch) (wo (wo ph ps) ch) $;

--| Define conjunction ('and') of 3 wff.s. Definition *4.34 of [WhiteheadRussell] p. 118. This abbreviation reduces the number of parentheses and emphasizes that the order of bracketing is not important by virtue of the associative law ~ anass . (Contributed by NM, 8-Apr-1994.)
axiom df_3an (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (w3a ph ps ch) (wa (wa ph ps) ch) $;

--| Associative law for triple disjunction. (Contributed by NM, 8-Apr-1994.)
theorem _3orass (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (w3o ph ps ch) (wo ph (wo ps ch)) $;

--| Associative law for triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 8-Apr-1994.)
theorem _3anass (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (w3a ph ps ch) (wa ph (wa ps ch)) $;

--| Rotation law for triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 8-Apr-1994.)
theorem _3anrot (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (w3a ph ps ch) (w3a ps ch ph) $;

--| Rotation law for triple disjunction. (Contributed by NM, 4-Apr-1995.)
theorem _3orrot (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (w3o ph ps ch) (w3o ps ch ph) $;

--| Commutation law for triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-1994.)
theorem _3ancoma (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (w3a ph ps ch) (w3a ps ph ch) $;

--| Commutation law for triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-1994.)
theorem _3ancomb (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (w3a ph ps ch) (w3a ph ch ps) $;

--| Commutation law for triple disjunction. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 20-Apr-2011.)
theorem _3orcomb (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (w3o ph ps ch) (w3o ph ch ps) $;

--| Reversal law for triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-1994.)
theorem _3anrev (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (w3a ph ps ch) (w3a ch ps ph) $;

--| Convert triple conjunction to conjunction, then commute. (Contributed by Jonathan Ben-Naim, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem _3anan32 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (w3a ph ps ch) (wa (wa ph ch) ps) $;

--| Convert triple conjunction to conjunction, then commute. (Contributed by Jonathan Ben-Naim, 3-Jun-2011.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 14-Jun-2011.)
theorem _3anan12 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (w3a ph ps ch) (wa ps (wa ph ch)) $;

--| Distribution of triple conjunction over conjunction. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 4-Nov-2018.)
theorem anandi3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (w3a ph ps ch) (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ph ch)) $;

--| Distribution of triple conjunction over conjunction. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 4-Nov-2018.)
theorem anandi3r (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (w3a ph ps ch) (wa (wa ph ps) (wa ch ps)) $;

--| Negated triple disjunction as triple conjunction. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 19-Apr-2011.)
theorem _3ioran (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wb (wn (w3o ph ps ch)) (w3a (wn ph) (wn ps) (wn ch)) $;

--| Simplification of triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-1994.)
theorem _3simpa (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) (wa ph ps) $;

--| Simplification of triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-1994.)
theorem _3simpb (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) (wa ph ch) $;

--| Simplification of triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-1994.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 13-May-2011.)
theorem _3simpc (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) (wa ps ch) $;

--| Simplification of triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-1994.)
theorem simp1 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) ph $;

--| Simplification of triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-1994.)
theorem simp2 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) ps $;

--| Simplification of triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-1994.)
theorem simp3 (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi (w3a ph ps ch) ch $;

--| Simplification rule. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 17-Nov-2009.)
theorem simpl1 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (w3a ph ps ch) th) ph $;

--| Simplification rule. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 17-Nov-2009.)
theorem simpl2 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (w3a ph ps ch) th) ps $;

--| Simplification rule. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 17-Nov-2009.)
theorem simpl3 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa (w3a ph ps ch) th) ch $;

--| Simplification rule. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 17-Nov-2009.)
theorem simpr1 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a ps ch th)) ps $;

--| Simplification rule. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 17-Nov-2009.)
theorem simpr2 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a ps ch th)) ch $;

--| Simplification rule. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 17-Nov-2009.)
theorem simpr3 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a ps ch th)) th $;

--| Infer a conjunct from a triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 19-Apr-2005.)
theorem simp1i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ w3a ph ps ch $ >
  $ ph $;

--| Infer a conjunct from a triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 19-Apr-2005.)
theorem simp2i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ w3a ph ps ch $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Infer a conjunct from a triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 19-Apr-2005.)
theorem simp3i (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ w3a ph ps ch $ >
  $ ch $;

--| Deduce a conjunct from a triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2005.)
theorem simp1d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (w3a ps ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Deduce a conjunct from a triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2005.)
theorem simp2d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (w3a ps ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Deduce a conjunct from a triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2005.)
theorem simp3d (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph (w3a ps ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| Deduce a conjunct from a triple conjunction. (Contributed by Jonathan Ben-Naim, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem simp1bi (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph (w3a ps ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph ps $;

--| Deduce a conjunct from a triple conjunction. (Contributed by Jonathan Ben-Naim, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem simp2bi (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph (w3a ps ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph ch $;

--| Deduce a conjunct from a triple conjunction. (Contributed by Jonathan Ben-Naim, 3-Jun-2011.)
theorem simp3bi (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wb ph (w3a ps ch th) $ >
  $ wi ph th $;

--| Deduction adding a conjunct to antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 16-Jul-1995.)
theorem _3adant1 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (w3a th ph ps) ch $;

--| Deduction adding a conjunct to antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 16-Jul-1995.)
theorem _3adant2 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph th ps) ch $;

--| Deduction adding a conjunct to antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 16-Jul-1995.)
theorem _3adant3 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (wa ph ps) ch $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps th) ch $;

--| Deduction adding conjuncts to an antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-2005.)
theorem _3ad2ant1 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ps th) ch $;

--| Deduction adding conjuncts to an antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-2005.)
theorem _3ad2ant2 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi (w3a ps ph th) ch $;

--| Deduction adding conjuncts to an antecedent. (Contributed by NM, 21-Apr-2005.)
theorem _3ad2ant3 (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi ph ch $ >
  $ wi (w3a ps th ph) ch $;

--| Simplification of triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 9-Nov-2011.)
theorem simp1l (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (w3a (wa ph ps) ch th) ph $;

--| Simplification of triple conjunction. (Contributed by NM, 9-Nov-2011.)
theorem simp1r (ph ps ch th: wff):
  $ wi (w3a (wa ph ps) ch th) ps $;

