--| Lemma for ~ 4t3e12 and related theorems. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _4t3lem (A2 B2 C D E: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel B2 cn0 $ >
  $ wceq C (co B2 c1 caddc) $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 B2 cmul) D $ >
  $ wceq (co D A2 caddc) E $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 C cmul) E $;

--| 4 times 3 equals 12. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _4t3e12:
  $ wceq (co c4 c3 cmul) (cdc c1 c2) $;

--| 4 times 4 equals 16. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _4t4e16:
  $ wceq (co c4 c4 cmul) (cdc c1 c6) $;

--| 5 times 3 equals 15. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _5t3e15:
  $ wceq (co c5 c3 cmul) (cdc c1 c5) $;

--| 5 times 4 equals 20. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _5t4e20:
  $ wceq (co c5 c4 cmul) (cdc c2 cc0) $;

--| 5 times 5 equals 25. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _5t5e25:
  $ wceq (co c5 c5 cmul) (cdc c2 c5) $;

--| 6 times 2 equals 12. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _6t2e12:
  $ wceq (co c6 c2 cmul) (cdc c1 c2) $;

--| 6 times 3 equals 18. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _6t3e18:
  $ wceq (co c6 c3 cmul) (cdc c1 c8) $;

--| 6 times 4 equals 24. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _6t4e24:
  $ wceq (co c6 c4 cmul) (cdc c2 c4) $;

--| 6 times 5 equals 30. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _6t5e30:
  $ wceq (co c6 c5 cmul) (cdc c3 cc0) $;

--| 6 times 6 equals 36. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _6t6e36:
  $ wceq (co c6 c6 cmul) (cdc c3 c6) $;

--| 7 times 2 equals 14. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _7t2e14:
  $ wceq (co c7 c2 cmul) (cdc c1 c4) $;

--| 7 times 3 equals 21. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _7t3e21:
  $ wceq (co c7 c3 cmul) (cdc c2 c1) $;

--| 7 times 4 equals 28. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _7t4e28:
  $ wceq (co c7 c4 cmul) (cdc c2 c8) $;

--| 7 times 5 equals 35. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _7t5e35:
  $ wceq (co c7 c5 cmul) (cdc c3 c5) $;

--| 7 times 6 equals 42. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _7t6e42:
  $ wceq (co c7 c6 cmul) (cdc c4 c2) $;

--| 7 times 7 equals 49. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _7t7e49:
  $ wceq (co c7 c7 cmul) (cdc c4 c9) $;

--| 8 times 2 equals 16. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _8t2e16:
  $ wceq (co c8 c2 cmul) (cdc c1 c6) $;

--| 8 times 3 equals 24. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _8t3e24:
  $ wceq (co c8 c3 cmul) (cdc c2 c4) $;

--| 8 times 4 equals 32. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _8t4e32:
  $ wceq (co c8 c4 cmul) (cdc c3 c2) $;

--| 8 times 5 equals 40. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _8t5e40:
  $ wceq (co c8 c5 cmul) (cdc c4 cc0) $;

--| 8 times 6 equals 48. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _8t6e48:
  $ wceq (co c8 c6 cmul) (cdc c4 c8) $;

--| 8 times 7 equals 56. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _8t7e56:
  $ wceq (co c8 c7 cmul) (cdc c5 c6) $;

--| 8 times 8 equals 64. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _8t8e64:
  $ wceq (co c8 c8 cmul) (cdc c6 c4) $;

--| 9 times 2 equals 18. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _9t2e18:
  $ wceq (co c9 c2 cmul) (cdc c1 c8) $;

--| 9 times 3 equals 27. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _9t3e27:
  $ wceq (co c9 c3 cmul) (cdc c2 c7) $;

--| 9 times 4 equals 36. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _9t4e36:
  $ wceq (co c9 c4 cmul) (cdc c3 c6) $;

--| 9 times 5 equals 45. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _9t5e45:
  $ wceq (co c9 c5 cmul) (cdc c4 c5) $;

--| 9 times 6 equals 54. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _9t6e54:
  $ wceq (co c9 c6 cmul) (cdc c5 c4) $;

--| 9 times 7 equals 63. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _9t7e63:
  $ wceq (co c9 c7 cmul) (cdc c6 c3) $;

--| 9 times 8 equals 72. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _9t8e72:
  $ wceq (co c9 c8 cmul) (cdc c7 c2) $;

--| 9 times 9 equals 81. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2015.)
theorem _9t9e81:
  $ wceq (co c9 c9 cmul) (cdc c8 c1) $;

--| Decompose base 4 into base 2. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.)
theorem decbin0 (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co c4 A2 cmul) (co c2 (co c2 A2 cmul) cmul) $;

--| Decompose base 4 into base 2. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.)
theorem decbin2 (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co (co c4 A2 cmul) c2 caddc) (co c2 (co (co c2 A2 cmul) c1 caddc) cmul) $;

--| Decompose base 4 into base 2. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Feb-2014.)
theorem decbin3 (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wceq (co (co c4 A2 cmul) c3 caddc) (co (co c2 (co (co c2 A2 cmul) c1 caddc) cmul) c1 caddc) $;

--| Extend class notation with the upper integer function. Read " ` ZZ>= `` M ` " as "the set of integers greater than or equal to ` M ` ."
term cuz: class;

--| Define a function whose value at ` j ` is the semi-infinite set of contiguous integers starting at ` j ` , which we will also call the upper integers starting at ` j ` . Read " ` ZZ>= `` M ` " as "the set of integers greater than or equal to ` M ` ." See ~ uzval for its value, ~ uzssz for its relationship to ` ZZ ` , ~ nnuz and ~ nn0uz for its relationships to ` NN ` and ` NN0 ` , and ~ eluz1 and ~ eluz2 for its membership relations. (Contributed by NM, 5-Sep-2005.)
axiom df_uz {j k: setvar}:
  $ wceq cuz (cmpt j cz (crab k (wbr (cv j) (cv k) cle) cz)) $;

--| The value of the upper integers function. (Contributed by NM, 5-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem uzval {k: setvar} (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wceq (cfv N cuz) (crab k (wbr N (cv k) cle) cz)) $;

--| The domain and range of the upper integers function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Aug-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem uzf:
  $ wf cz (cpw cz) cuz $;

--| Membership in the upper set of integers starting at ` M ` . (Contributed by NM, 5-Sep-2005.)
theorem eluz1 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cz) (wb (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wa (wcel N cz) (wbr M N cle))) $;

--| Implication of membership in an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 6-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem eluzel2 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wcel M cz) $;

--| Membership in an upper set of integers. We use the fact that a function's value (under our function value definition) is empty outside of its domain to show ` M e. ZZ ` . (Contributed by NM, 5-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem eluz2 (M N: class):
  $ wb (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (w3a (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz) (wbr M N cle)) $;

--| Membership in an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 5-Sep-2005.)
theorem eluz1i (M N: class):
  $ wcel M cz $ >
  $ wb (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wa (wcel N cz) (wbr M N cle)) $;

--| An integer in an upper set of integers is an element of an upper set of integers with a smaller bound. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 17-Jun-2018.)
theorem eluzuzle (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 cz) (wbr B2 A2 cle)) (wi (wcel C (cfv A2 cuz)) (wcel C (cfv B2 cuz))) $;

--| A member of an upper set of integers is an integer. (Contributed by NM, 6-Sep-2005.)
theorem eluzelz (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wcel N cz) $;

--| A member of an upper set of integers is a real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Aug-2013.)
theorem eluzelre (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wcel N cr) $;

--| A member of an upper set of integers is a complex number. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 29-Jun-2017.)
theorem eluzelcn (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wcel N cc) $;

--| Implication of membership in an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 6-Sep-2005.)
theorem eluzle (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wbr M N cle) $;

--| Membership in an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-2005.)
theorem eluz (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wbr M N cle)) $;

--| Membership of the least member in an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-2005.)
theorem uzid (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cz) (wcel M (cfv M cuz)) $;

--| The upper integers are all nonempty. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jan-2014.)
theorem uzn0 (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M (crn cuz)) (wne M c0) $;

--| Transitive law for sets of upper integers. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-2005.)
theorem uztrn (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M (cfv K cuz)) (wcel K (cfv N cuz))) (wcel M (cfv N cuz)) $;

--| Transitive law for sets of upper integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Dec-2013.)
theorem uztrn2 (K M N Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv K cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel N Z) (wcel M (cfv N cuz))) (wcel M Z) $;

--| Contraposition law for upper integers. (Contributed by NM, 28-Nov-2005.)
theorem uzneg (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wcel (cneg M) (cfv (cneg N) cuz)) $;

--| An upper set of integers is a subset of all integers. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem uzssz (M: class):
  $ wss (cfv M cuz) cz $;

--| Subset relationship for two sets of upper integers. (Contributed by NM, 5-Sep-2005.)
theorem uzss (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wss (cfv N cuz) (cfv M cuz)) $;

--| Trichotomy of the ordering relation on integers, stated in terms of upper integers. (Contributed by NM, 6-Jul-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 25-Jun-2013.)
theorem uztric (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wo (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wcel M (cfv N cuz))) $;

--| The upper integers function is one-to-one. (Contributed by NM, 12-Dec-2005.)
theorem uz11 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cz) (wb (wceq (cfv M cuz) (cfv N cuz)) (wceq M N)) $;

--| Membership in the next upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2005.)
theorem eluzp1m1 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cuz))) (wcel (co N c1 cmin) (cfv M cuz)) $;

--| Strict ordering implied by membership in the next upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-2005.)
theorem eluzp1l (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cuz))) (wbr M N clt) $;

--| Membership in the next upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 5-Oct-2005.)
theorem eluzp1p1 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wcel (co N c1 caddc) (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cuz)) $;

--| Membership in a later upper set of integers. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 22-Nov-2007.)
theorem eluzaddi (K M N: class):
  $ wcel M cz $ >
  $ wcel K cz $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wcel (co N K caddc) (cfv (co M K caddc) cuz)) $;

--| Membership in an earlier upper set of integers. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 22-Nov-2007.)
theorem eluzsubi (K M N: class):
  $ wcel M cz $ >
  $ wcel K cz $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv (co M K caddc) cuz)) (wcel (co N K cmin) (cfv M cuz)) $;

--| Membership in a later upper set of integers. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem eluzadd (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wcel K cz)) (wcel (co N K caddc) (cfv (co M K caddc) cuz)) $;

--| Membership in an earlier upper set of integers. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem eluzsub (K M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cz) (wcel K cz) (wcel N (cfv (co M K caddc) cuz))) (wcel (co N K cmin) (cfv M cuz)) $;

--| Choices for an element of an upper interval of integers. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem uzm1 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wo (wceq N M) (wcel (co N c1 cmin) (cfv M cuz))) $;

--| The nonnegative difference of integers is a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2005.)
theorem uznn0sub (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wcel (co N M cmin) cn0) $;

--| Intersection of two upper intervals of integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2013.)
theorem uzin (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (cin (cfv M cuz) (cfv N cuz)) (cfv (cif (wbr M N cle) N M) cuz)) $;

--| Choices for an element of an upper interval of integers. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem uzp1 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wo (wceq N M) (wcel N (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cuz))) $;

--| Nonnegative integers expressed as an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-2005.)
theorem nn0uz:
  $ wceq cn0 (cfv cc0 cuz) $;

--| Positive integers expressed as an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-2005.)
theorem nnuz:
  $ wceq cn (cfv c1 cuz) $;

--| A positive integer expressed as a member of an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 6-Jun-2006.)
theorem elnnuz (N: class):
  $ wb (wcel N cn) (wcel N (cfv c1 cuz)) $;

--| A nonnegative integer expressed as a member an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 6-Jun-2006.)
theorem elnn0uz (N: class):
  $ wb (wcel N cn0) (wcel N (cfv cc0 cuz)) $;

--| An integer is greater than or equal to 2 is a positive integer. (Contributed by AV, 3-Nov-2018.)
theorem eluz2nn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel A2 cn) $;

--| If an integer is greater than or equal to 2, then it is a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by AV, 27-Aug-2018.) (Proof shortened by AV, 3-Nov-2018.)
theorem eluzge2nn0 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel N cn0) $;

--| An integer in the upper set of integers starting at 3 is element of the upper set of integers starting at 2. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 17-Sep-2018.)
theorem uzuzle23 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel A2 (cfv c2 cuz)) $;

--| If an integer is greater than 3, then it is a positive integer. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 17-Sep-2018.)
theorem eluzge3nn (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel N cn) $;

--| An integer greater than or equal to 3 decreased by 2 is a positive integer. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 17-Sep-2018.)
theorem uz3m2nn (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel (co N c2 cmin) cn) $;

--| 1 is an integer greater than or equal to 0. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 8-Jun-2018.)
theorem _1eluzge0:
  $ wcel c1 (cfv cc0 cuz) $;

--| 2 is an integer greater than or equal to 0. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 8-Jun-2018.) (Proof shortened by OpenAI, 25-Mar-2020.)
theorem _2eluzge0:
  $ wcel c2 (cfv cc0 cuz) $;

--| 2 is an integer greater than or equal to 0. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 8-Jun-2018.) Obsolete version of ~ 2eluzge0 as of 25-Mar-2020. (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem _2eluzge0OLD:
  $ wcel c2 (cfv cc0 cuz) $;

--| 2 is an integer greater than or equal to 1. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 8-Jun-2018.)
theorem _2eluzge1:
  $ wcel c2 (cfv c1 cuz) $;

--| The upper integers starting from a natural are a subset of the naturals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 29-Jun-2013.)
theorem uznnssnn (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wss (cfv N cuz) cn) $;

--| Restricted universal quantification in an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 9-Sep-2005.)
theorem raluz {n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cz) (wb (wral n ph (cfv M cuz)) (wral n (wi (wbr M (cv n) cle) ph) cz)) $;

--| Restricted universal quantification in an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 9-Sep-2005.)
theorem raluz2 {n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (M: class):
  $ wb (wral n ph (cfv M cuz)) (wi (wcel M cz) (wral n (wi (wbr M (cv n) cle) ph) cz)) $;

--| Restricted existential quantification in an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 9-Sep-2005.)
theorem rexuz {n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cz) (wb (wrex n ph (cfv M cuz)) (wrex n (wa (wbr M (cv n) cle) ph) cz)) $;

--| Restricted existential quantification in an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 9-Sep-2005.)
theorem rexuz2 {n: setvar} (ph: wff n) (M: class):
  $ wb (wrex n ph (cfv M cuz)) (wa (wcel M cz) (wrex n (wa (wbr M (cv n) cle) ph) cz)) $;

--| Double existential quantification in an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 3-Nov-2005.)
theorem _2rexuz {m n: setvar} (ph: wff m n):
  $ wb (wex m (wrex n ph (cfv (cv m) cuz))) (wrex m (wrex n (wa (wbr (cv m) (cv n) cle) ph) cz) cz) $;

--| Second Peano postulate for an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 7-Sep-2005.)
theorem peano2uz (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wcel (co N c1 caddc) (cfv M cuz)) $;

--| Second Peano postulate for an upper set of integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Dec-2013.)
theorem peano2uzs (M N Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N Z) (wcel (co N c1 caddc) Z) $;

--| Reversed second Peano axiom for upper integers. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jan-2006.)
theorem peano2uzr (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cuz))) (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $;

--| Addition closure law for an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jun-2006.)
theorem uzaddcl (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wcel K cn0)) (wcel (co N K caddc) (cfv M cuz)) $;

--| The sum of a nonnegative integer and an integer is an integer greater than or equal to that integer. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 3-Oct-2018.)
theorem nn0pzuz (N Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel Z cz)) (wcel (co N Z caddc) (cfv Z cuz)) $;

--| Induction on the upper set of integers that starts at an integer ` M ` . The first four hypotheses give us the substitution instances we need, and the last two are the basis and the induction step. (Contributed by NM, 7-Sep-2005.)
theorem uzind4 {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j) (ps ch th ta: wff k) (M: class) (N: class k):
  $ wi (wceq (cv j) M) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv j) (cv k)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv j) (co (cv k) c1 caddc)) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv j) N) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cz) ps $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz)) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) ta $;

--| Induction on the upper set of integers that starts at an integer ` M ` . The last four hypotheses give us the substitution instances we need; the first two are the basis and the induction step. Either ~ uzind4 or ~ uzind4ALT may be used; see comment for ~ nnind . (Contributed by NM, 7-Sep-2005.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem uzind4ALT {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j) (ps ch th ta: wff k) (M: class) (N: class k):
  $ wi (wcel M cz) ps $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz)) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv j) M) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv j) (cv k)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv j) (co (cv k) c1 caddc)) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv j) N) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) ta $;

--| Induction on the upper set of integers that starts at an integer ` M ` , using explicit substitution. The hypotheses are the basis and the induction step. (Contributed by NM, 4-Nov-2005.)
theorem uzind4s {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (M: class) (N: class k):
  $ wi (wcel M cz) (wsbc k ph M) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz)) (wi ph (wsbc k ph (co (cv k) c1 caddc))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wsbc k ph N) $;

--| Induction on the upper set of integers that starts at an integer ` M ` , using explicit substitution. The hypotheses are the basis and the induction step. Use this instead of ~ uzind4s when ` j ` and ` k ` must be distinct in ` [. ( k + 1 ) / j ]. ph ` . (Contributed by NM, 16-Nov-2005.)
theorem uzind4s2 {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j) (M: class j) (N: class j k):
  $ wi (wcel M cz) (wsbc j ph M) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz)) (wi (wsbc j ph (cv k)) (wsbc j ph (co (cv k) c1 caddc))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wsbc j ph N) $;

--| Induction on the upper integers that start at ` M ` . The first hypothesis specifies the lower bound, the next four give us the substitution instances we need, and the last two are the basis and the induction step. (Contributed by NM, 4-Sep-2005.)
theorem uzind4i {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j) (ps ch th ta: wff k) (M: class) (N: class k):
  $ wcel M cz $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv j) M) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv j) (cv k)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv j) (co (cv k) c1 caddc)) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv j) N) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ ps $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz)) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) ta $;

--| Strong Mathematical Induction for positive integers (inference schema). (Contributed by NM, 17-Aug-2001.)
theorem indstr {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) cn) (wi (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) clt) ps) cn) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) cn) ph $;

--| Membership in a nonnegative upper set of integers implies membership in ` NN0 ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 22-Jun-2011.)
theorem eluznn0 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel M (cfv N cuz))) (wcel M cn0) $;

--| Membership in a positive upper set of integers implies membership in ` NN ` . (Contributed by JJ, 1-Oct-2018.)
theorem eluznn (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel M (cfv N cuz))) (wcel M cn) $;

--| Two ways to say "an integer greater than or equal to 2." (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Nov-2012.)
theorem eluz2b1 (N: class):
  $ wb (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wa (wcel N cz) (wbr c1 N clt)) $;

--| Two ways to say "an integer greater than or equal to 2." (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Nov-2012.)
theorem eluz2b2 (N: class):
  $ wb (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wa (wcel N cn) (wbr c1 N clt)) $;

--| Two ways to say "an integer greater than or equal to 2." (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 23-Nov-2012.)
theorem eluz2b3 (N: class):
  $ wb (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wa (wcel N cn) (wne N c1)) $;

--| One less than an integer greater than or equal to 2 is a positive integer. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 17-Nov-2012.)
theorem uz2m1nn (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel (co N c1 cmin) cn) $;

--| 1 is not in ` ( ZZ>= `` 2 ) ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Nov-2012.)
theorem _1nuz2:
  $ wn (wcel c1 (cfv c2 cuz)) $;

--| A positive integer is either 1 or greater than or equal to 2. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 17-Nov-2012.)
theorem elnn1uz2 (N: class):
  $ wb (wcel N cn) (wo (wceq N c1) (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz))) $;

--| Closure of multiplication of integers greater than or equal to 2. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 26-Oct-2012.)
theorem uz2mulcl (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz))) (wcel (co M N cmul) (cfv c2 cuz)) $;

--| Strong Mathematical Induction for positive integers (inference schema). The first two hypotheses give us the substitution instances we need; the last two are the basis and the induction step. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Nov-2012.)
theorem indstr2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1) (ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c1) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ ch $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) (cfv c2 cuz)) (wi (wral y1 (wi (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) clt) ps) cn) ph) $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) cn) ph $;

--| Membership of an integer in an upper set of integers is decidable. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 18-Apr-2020.)
theorem eluzdc (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wdc (wcel N (cfv M cuz))) $;

--| The image under negation of a bounded-above set of reals is bounded below. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Mar-2011.)
theorem ublbneg {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) A2) cr) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) (crab z (wcel (cneg (cv z)) A2) cr)) cr) $;

--| Two ways to express the image under negation of a set of integers. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Mar-2011.)
theorem eqreznegel {z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 cz) (wceq (crab z (wcel (cneg (cv z)) A2) cr) (crab z (wcel (cneg (cv z)) A2) cz)) $;

--| The image under negation of an inhabited set of reals is inhabited. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 10-Apr-2020.)
theorem negm {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 cr) (wex x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2))) (wex y1 (wcel (cv y1) (crab z (wcel (cneg (cv z)) A2) cr))) $;

--| If a set of reals is bounded below, it is bounded below by an integer. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Mar-2011.)
theorem lbzbi {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wss A2 cr) (wb (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) A2) cr) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv x3) (cv y1) cle) A2) cz)) $;

--| A (nonnegative) integer between 1 and 3 must be 1, 2 or 3. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 13-Sep-2018.)
theorem nn01to3 (N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel N cn0) (wbr c1 N cle) (wbr N c3 cle)) (w3o (wceq N c1) (wceq N c2) (wceq N c3)) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ nn0ge2m1nn : If a nonnegative integer is greater than or equal to two, the integer decreased by 1 is a positive integer. This version is proved using ~ eluz2 , a theorem for upper sets of integers, which are defined later than the positive and nonnegative integers. This proof is, however, much shorter than the proof of ~ nn0ge2m1nn . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 1-Aug-2018.) (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem nn0ge2m1nnALT (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wbr c2 N cle)) (wcel (co N c1 cmin) cn) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the class of rationals.
term cq: class;

--| Define the set of rational numbers. Based on definition of rationals in [Apostol] p. 22. See ~ elq for the relation "is rational." (Contributed by NM, 8-Jan-2002.)
axiom df_q:
  $ wceq cq (cima cdiv (cxp cz cn)) $;

--| Division restricted to ` ZZ X. NN ` is a function. Given excluded middle, it would be easy to prove this for ` CC X. ( CC \ { 0 } ) ` . The key difference is that an element of ` NN ` is apart from zero, whereas being an element of ` CC \ { 0 } ` implies being not equal to zero. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 19-Mar-2020.)
theorem divfnzn:
  $ wfn (cres cdiv (cxp cz cn)) (cxp cz cn) $;

--| Membership in the set of rationals. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jan-2002.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jan-2014.)
theorem elq {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cq) (wrex x3 (wrex y1 (wceq A2 (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cdiv)) cn) cz) $;

--| If ` A ` is rational, then some integer multiple of it is an integer. (Contributed by NM, 7-Nov-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 22-Jul-2014.)
theorem qmulz {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wrex x3 (wcel (co A2 (cv x3) cmul) cz) cn) $;

--| The ratio of an integer and a positive integer is a rational number. (Contributed by NM, 12-Jan-2002.)
theorem znq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cn)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cdiv) cq) $;

--| A rational number is a real number. (Contributed by NM, 14-Nov-2002.)
theorem qre (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| An integer is a rational number. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jan-2002.)
theorem zq (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cz) (wcel A2 cq) $;

--| The integers are a subset of the rationals. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jan-2002.)
theorem zssq:
  $ wss cz cq $;

--| The nonnegative integers are a subset of the rationals. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-2004.)
theorem nn0ssq:
  $ wss cn0 cq $;

--| The positive integers are a subset of the rationals. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-2004.)
theorem nnssq:
  $ wss cn cq $;

--| The rationals are a subset of the reals. (Contributed by NM, 9-Jan-2002.)
theorem qssre:
  $ wss cq cr $;

--| The rationals are a subset of the complex numbers. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-2004.)
theorem qsscn:
  $ wss cq cc $;

--| The set of rational numbers exists. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jul-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem qex:
  $ wcel cq cvv $;

--| A positive integer is rational. (Contributed by NM, 17-Nov-2004.)
theorem nnq (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wcel A2 cq) $;

--| A rational number is a complex number. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-2004.)
theorem qcn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wcel A2 cc) $;

--| Closure of addition of rationals. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-2004.)
theorem qaddcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cq) (wcel B2 cq)) (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) cq) $;

--| Closure law for the negative of a rational. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem qnegcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wcel (cneg A2) cq) $;

--| Closure of multiplication of rationals. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-2004.)
theorem qmulcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cq) (wcel B2 cq)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) cq) $;

--| Closure of subtraction of rationals. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-2004.)
theorem qsubcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cq) (wcel B2 cq)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmin) cq) $;

--| Apartness is equivalent to not equal for rationals. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 20-Mar-2020.)
theorem qapne (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cq) (wcel B2 cq)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cap) (wne A2 B2)) $;

--| Rational 'Less than' expressed in terms of 'less than or equal to'. Also see ~ ltleap which is a similar result for real numbers. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem qltlen (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cq) (wcel B2 cq)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wne B2 A2))) $;

--| Closure of reciprocal of rationals. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-2004.)
theorem qreccl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cq) (wne A2 cc0)) (wcel (co c1 A2 cdiv) cq) $;

--| Closure of division of rationals. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-2004.)
theorem qdivcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cq) (wcel B2 cq) (wne B2 cc0)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cdiv) cq) $;

--| Reverse closure law for addition of rationals. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-2004.)
theorem qrevaddcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 cq) (wb (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) cq)) (wcel A2 cq)) $;

--| The reciprocal of a positive integer is rational. (Contributed by NM, 17-Nov-2004.)
theorem nnrecq (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wcel (co c1 A2 cdiv) cq) $;

--| The sum of an irrational number and a rational number is irrational. (Contributed by NM, 7-Nov-2008.)
theorem irradd (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (cdif cr cq)) (wcel B2 cq)) (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) (cdif cr cq)) $;

--| The product of a real which is not rational with a nonzero rational is not rational. Note that by "not rational" we mean the negation of "is rational" (whereas "irrational" is often defined to mean apart from any rational number - given excluded middle these two definitions would be equivalent). (Contributed by NM, 7-Nov-2008.)
theorem irrmul (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (cdif cr cq)) (wcel B2 cq) (wne B2 cc0)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) (cdif cr cq)) $;

--| There is a natural one-to-one mapping from ` ( RR X. RR ) ` to ` CC ` , where we map ` <. x , y >. ` to ` ( x + ( _i x. y ) ) ` . In our construction of the complex numbers, this is in fact our _definition_ of ` CC ` (see ~ df-c ), but in the axiomatic treatment we can only show that there is the expected mapping between these two sets. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Feb-2014.)
theorem cnref1o {x3 y1: setvar} (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpt2 x3 y1 cr cr (co (cv x3) (co ci (cv y1) cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wf1o (cxp cr cr) cc F $;

--| Extend class notation to include the class of positive reals.
term crp: class;

--| Define the set of positive reals. Definition of positive numbers in [Apostol] p. 20. (Contributed by NM, 27-Oct-2007.)
axiom df_rp {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq crp (crab x3 (wbr cc0 (cv x3) clt) cr) $;

--| Membership in the set of positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 27-Oct-2007.)
theorem elrp (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 crp) (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) $;

--| Membership in the set of positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 23-Feb-2008.)
theorem elrpii (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wbr cc0 A2 clt $ >
  $ wcel A2 crp $;

--| 1 is a positive real. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 23-Nov-2008.)
theorem _1rp:
  $ wcel c1 crp $;

--| 2 is a positive real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem _2rp:
  $ wcel c2 crp $;

--| A positive real is a real. (Contributed by NM, 27-Oct-2007.)
theorem rpre (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| A positive real is an extended real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Aug-2015.)
theorem rpxr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wcel A2 cxr) $;

--| A positive real is a complex number. (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2008.)
theorem rpcn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wcel A2 cc) $;

--| A positive integer is a positive real. (Contributed by NM, 28-Nov-2008.)
theorem nnrp (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wcel A2 crp) $;

--| The positive reals are a subset of the reals. (Contributed by NM, 24-Feb-2008.)
theorem rpssre:
  $ wss crp cr $;

--| A positive real is greater than zero. (Contributed by FL, 27-Dec-2007.)
theorem rpgt0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wbr cc0 A2 clt) $;

--| A positive real is greater than or equal to zero. (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-2008.)
theorem rpge0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $;

--| A positive real is a positive real number. (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem rpregt0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) $;

--| A positive real is a nonnegative real number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem rprege0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) $;

--| A positive real is nonzero. (Contributed by NM, 18-Jul-2008.)
theorem rpne0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wne A2 cc0) $;

--| A positive real is apart from zero. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Mar-2020.)
theorem rpap0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wbr A2 cc0 cap) $;

--| A positive real is a nonzero real number. (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2008.)
theorem rprene0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wne A2 cc0)) $;

--| A positive real is a real number apart from zero. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Mar-2020.)
theorem rpreap0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr A2 cc0 cap)) $;

--| A positive real is a nonzero complex number. (Contributed by NM, 11-Nov-2008.)
theorem rpcnne0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) $;

--| A positive real is a complex number apart from zero. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Mar-2020.)
theorem rpcnap0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap)) $;

--| Quantification over positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 12-Feb-2008.)
theorem ralrp {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wral x3 ph crp) (wral x3 (wi (wbr cc0 (cv x3) clt) ph) cr) $;

--| Quantification over positive reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2014.)
theorem rexrp {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wb (wrex x3 ph crp) (wrex x3 (wa (wbr cc0 (cv x3) clt) ph) cr) $;

--| Closure law for addition of positive reals. Part of Axiom 7 of [Apostol] p. 20. (Contributed by NM, 27-Oct-2007.)
theorem rpaddcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 crp)) (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) crp) $;

--| Closure law for multiplication of positive reals. Part of Axiom 7 of [Apostol] p. 20. (Contributed by NM, 27-Oct-2007.)
theorem rpmulcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 crp)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) crp) $;

--| Closure law for division of positive reals. (Contributed by FL, 27-Dec-2007.)
theorem rpdivcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 crp)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cdiv) crp) $;

--| Closure law for reciprocation of positive reals. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 23-Nov-2008.)
theorem rpreccl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wcel (co c1 A2 cdiv) crp) $;

--| Closure law for half of a positive real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem rphalfcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wcel (co A2 c2 cdiv) crp) $;

--| A number greater or equal to a positive real is positive real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rpgecl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wcel B2 crp) $;

--| Half of a positive real is less than the original number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2014.)
theorem rphalflt (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wbr (co A2 c2 cdiv) A2 clt) $;

--| Closure law for division of a real by a positive real. (Contributed by NM, 10-Nov-2008.)
theorem rerpdivcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 crp)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cdiv) cr) $;

--| A nonnegative number plus one is a positive number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Oct-2015.)
theorem ge0p1rp (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wcel (co A2 c1 caddc) crp) $;

--| Either a real apart from zero or its negation is a positive real, but not both. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 23-Mar-2020.)
theorem rpnegap (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr A2 cc0 cap)) (wxo (wcel A2 crp) (wcel (cneg A2) crp)) $;

--| Zero is not a positive real. Axiom 9 of [Apostol] p. 20. (Contributed by NM, 27-Oct-2007.)
theorem _0nrp:
  $ wn (wcel cc0 crp) $;

--| Subtracting a positive real from another number decreases it. (Contributed by FL, 27-Dec-2007.)
theorem ltsubrp (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 crp)) (wbr (co A2 B2 cmin) A2 clt) $;

--| Adding a positive number to another number increases it. (Contributed by FL, 27-Dec-2007.)
theorem ltaddrp (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 crp)) (wbr A2 (co A2 B2 caddc) clt) $;

--| Two ways to say one number is less than another. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-May-2014.)
theorem difrp (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wcel (co B2 A2 cmin) crp)) $;

--| Membership in the set of positive reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem elrpd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $;

--| A positive integer is a positive real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem nnrpd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $;

--| A positive real is a real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rpred (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| A positive real is an extended real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rpxrd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $;

--| A positive real is a complex number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rpcnd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $;

--| A positive real is greater than zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rpgt0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 clt) $;

--| A positive real is greater than or equal to zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rpge0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $;

--| A positive real is nonzero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rpne0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $;

--| A positive real is apart from zero. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 28-Jul-2021.)
theorem rpap0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 cap) $;

--| A positive real is real and greater than zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rpregt0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) $;

--| A positive real is real and greater or equal to zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rprege0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) $;

--| A positive real is a nonzero real number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rprene0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wne A2 cc0)) $;

--| A positive real is a nonzero complex number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rpcnne0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wne A2 cc0)) $;

--| Closure law for reciprocation of positive reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rpreccld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co c1 A2 cdiv) crp) $;

--| Closure law for reciprocation of positive reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rprecred (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co c1 A2 cdiv) cr) $;

--| Closure law for half of a positive real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rphalfcld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 c2 cdiv) crp) $;

--| The reciprocal of a positive number less than 1 is greater than 1. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem reclt1d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 c1 clt) (wbr c1 (co c1 A2 cdiv) clt)) $;

--| The reciprocal of a positive number greater than 1 is less than 1. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem recgt1d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr c1 A2 clt) (wbr (co c1 A2 cdiv) c1 clt)) $;

--| Closure law for addition of positive reals. Part of Axiom 7 of [Apostol] p. 20. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rpaddcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) crp) $;

--| Closure law for multiplication of positive reals. Part of Axiom 7 of [Apostol] p. 20. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rpmulcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) crp) $;

--| Closure law for division of positive reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rpdivcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 cdiv) crp) $;

--| The reciprocal of both sides of 'less than'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltrecd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co c1 B2 cdiv) (co c1 A2 cdiv) clt)) $;

--| The reciprocal of both sides of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lerecd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co c1 B2 cdiv) (co c1 A2 cdiv) cle)) $;

--| Reciprocal swap in a 'less than' relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltrec1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co c1 A2 cdiv) B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co c1 B2 cdiv) A2 clt) $;

--| Reciprocal swap in a 'less than or equal to' relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lerec2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 (co c1 B2 cdiv) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 (co c1 A2 cdiv) cle) $;

--| Division of both sides of 'less than or equal to' into a nonnegative number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lediv2ad (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co C B2 cdiv) (co C A2 cdiv) cle) $;

--| Division of a positive number by both sides of 'less than'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltdiv2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co C B2 cdiv) (co C A2 cdiv) clt)) $;

--| Division of a positive number by both sides of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lediv2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co C B2 cdiv) (co C A2 cdiv) cle)) $;

--| Invert ratios of positive numbers and swap their ordering. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ledivdivd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co C D cdiv) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co D C cdiv) (co B2 A2 cdiv) cle) $;

--| The ratio of a number over a smaller positive number is larger than 1. (Contributed by Glauco Siliprandi, 5-Apr-2020.)
theorem divge1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wbr c1 (co B2 A2 cdiv) cle) $;

--| A real number divided by a positive real number is less than 1 iff the real number is less than the positive real number. (Contributed by AV, 25-May-2020.)
theorem divlt1lt (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 crp)) (wb (wbr (co A2 B2 cdiv) c1 clt) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) $;

--| A real number divided by a positive real number is less than or equal to 1 iff the real number is less than or equal to the positive real number. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jun-2021.)
theorem divle1le (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 crp)) (wb (wbr (co A2 B2 cdiv) c1 cle) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) $;

--| If a number is less than or equal to another number, the number divided by a positive number greater than or equal to one is less than or equal to the other number. (Contributed by AV, 29-Jun-2021.)
theorem ledivge1le (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 crp) (wa (wcel C crp) (wbr c1 C cle))) (wi (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) B2 cle)) $;

--| A nonnegative number plus one is a positive number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ge0p1rpd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 c1 caddc) crp) $;

--| Closure law for division of a real by a positive real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rerpdivcld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 B2 cdiv) cr) $;

--| Subtracting a positive real from another number decreases it. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltsubrpd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 B2 cmin) A2 clt) $;

--| Adding a positive number to another number increases it. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltaddrpd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 (co A2 B2 caddc) clt) $;

--| Adding a positive number to another number increases it. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltaddrp2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 (co B2 A2 caddc) clt) $;

--| Multiplication by a number greater than 1. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltmulgt11d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr c1 A2 clt) (wbr B2 (co B2 A2 cmul) clt)) $;

--| Multiplication by a number greater than 1. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltmulgt12d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr c1 A2 clt) (wbr B2 (co A2 B2 cmul) clt)) $;

--| Division of a positive number by a positive number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem gt0divd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr cc0 A2 clt) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cdiv) clt)) $;

--| Division of a nonnegative number by a positive number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ge0divd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cdiv) cle)) $;

--| A number greater or equal to a positive real is positive real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rpgecld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $;

--| The ratio of nonnegative and positive numbers is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem divge0d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (co A2 B2 cdiv) cle) $;

--| The ratio of nonnegative and positive numbers is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltmul1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul) clt)) $;

--| Multiplication of both sides of 'less than' by a positive number. Theorem I.19 of [Apostol] p. 20. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltmul2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co C A2 cmul) (co C B2 cmul) clt)) $;

--| Multiplication of both sides of 'less than or equal to' by a positive number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lemul1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul) cle)) $;

--| Multiplication of both sides of 'less than or equal to' by a positive number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lemul2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co C A2 cmul) (co C B2 cmul) cle)) $;

--| Division of both sides of 'less than' by a positive number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltdiv1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 C cdiv) clt)) $;

--| Division of both sides of a less than or equal to relation by a positive number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lediv1d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 C cdiv) cle)) $;

--| 'Less than' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltmuldivd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (co A2 C cmul) B2 clt) (wbr A2 (co B2 C cdiv) clt)) $;

--| 'Less than' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltmuldiv2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (co C A2 cmul) B2 clt) (wbr A2 (co B2 C cdiv) clt)) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem lemuldivd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (co A2 C cmul) B2 cle) (wbr A2 (co B2 C cdiv) cle)) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem lemuldiv2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (co C A2 cmul) B2 cle) (wbr A2 (co B2 C cdiv) cle)) $;

--| 'Less than' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltdivmuld (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) B2 clt) (wbr A2 (co C B2 cmul) clt)) $;

--| 'Less than' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltdivmul2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) B2 clt) (wbr A2 (co B2 C cmul) clt)) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ledivmuld (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) B2 cle) (wbr A2 (co C B2 cmul) cle)) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' relationship between division and multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ledivmul2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) B2 cle) (wbr A2 (co B2 C cmul) cle)) $;

--| The ratio of nonnegative and positive numbers is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem ltmul1dd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 C cmul) (co B2 C cmul) clt) $;

--| Multiplication of both sides of 'less than' by a positive number. Theorem I.19 of [Apostol] p. 20. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem ltmul2dd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co C A2 cmul) (co C B2 cmul) clt) $;

--| Division of both sides of 'less than' by a positive number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem ltdiv1dd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 C cdiv) clt) $;

--| Division of both sides of a less than or equal to relation by a positive number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2016.)
theorem lediv1dd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) (co B2 C cdiv) cle) $;

--| Comparison of ratio of two nonnegative numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lediv12ad (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C D cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 D cdiv) (co B2 C cdiv) cle) $;

--| Swap denominator with other side of 'less than'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem ltdiv23d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 B2 cdiv) C clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) B2 clt) $;

--| Swap denominator with other side of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lediv23d (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 B2 cdiv) C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 C cdiv) B2 cle) $;

--| The ratio of nonnegative and positive numbers is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lt2mul2divd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (co A2 B2 cmul) (co C D cmul) clt) (wbr (co A2 D cdiv) (co C B2 cdiv) clt)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include the negative of an extended real.
term cxne (A2: class): class;

--| Extend class notation to include addition of extended reals.
term cxad: class;

--| Extend class notation to include multiplication of extended reals.
term cxmu: class;

--| Define the negative of an extended real number. (Contributed by FL, 26-Dec-2011.)
axiom df_xneg (A2: class):
  $ wceq (cxne A2) (cif (wceq A2 cpnf) cmnf (cif (wceq A2 cmnf) cpnf (cneg A2))) $;

--| Define addition over extended real numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
axiom df_xadd {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cxad (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (cif (wceq (cv x3) cpnf) (cif (wceq (cv y1) cmnf) cc0 cpnf) (cif (wceq (cv x3) cmnf) (cif (wceq (cv y1) cpnf) cc0 cmnf) (cif (wceq (cv y1) cpnf) cpnf (cif (wceq (cv y1) cmnf) cmnf (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc)))))) $;

--| Define multiplication over extended real numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
axiom df_xmul {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cxmu (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (cif (wo (wceq (cv x3) cc0) (wceq (cv y1) cc0)) cc0 (cif (wo (wo (wa (wbr cc0 (cv y1) clt) (wceq (cv x3) cpnf)) (wa (wbr (cv y1) cc0 clt) (wceq (cv x3) cmnf))) (wo (wa (wbr cc0 (cv x3) clt) (wceq (cv y1) cpnf)) (wa (wbr (cv x3) cc0 clt) (wceq (cv y1) cmnf)))) cpnf (cif (wo (wo (wa (wbr cc0 (cv y1) clt) (wceq (cv x3) cmnf)) (wa (wbr (cv y1) cc0 clt) (wceq (cv x3) cpnf))) (wo (wa (wbr cc0 (cv x3) clt) (wceq (cv y1) cmnf)) (wa (wbr (cv x3) cc0 clt) (wceq (cv y1) cpnf)))) cmnf (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul))))) $;

--| Plus infinity belongs to the set of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2005.) (Proof shortened by Anthony Hart, 29-Aug-2011.)
theorem pnfxr:
  $ wcel cpnf cxr $;

--| Plus infinity exists (common case). (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem pnfex:
  $ wcel cpnf cvv $;

--| Minus infinity belongs to the set of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 13-Oct-2005.) (Proof shortened by Anthony Hart, 29-Aug-2011.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 19-Nov-2011.)
theorem mnfxr:
  $ wcel cmnf cxr $;

--| The 'less than' binary relation on the set of extended reals. Definition 12-3.1 of [Gleason] p. 173. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2005.)
theorem ltxr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wo (wo (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wbr A2 B2 cltrr)) (wa (wceq A2 cmnf) (wceq B2 cpnf))) (wo (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wceq B2 cpnf)) (wa (wceq A2 cmnf) (wcel B2 cr))))) $;

--| Membership in the set of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2005.)
theorem elxr (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cxr) (w3o (wcel A2 cr) (wceq A2 cpnf) (wceq A2 cmnf)) $;

--| Plus and minus infinity are different elements of ` RR* ` . (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2005.)
theorem pnfnemnf:
  $ wne cpnf cmnf $;

--| Minus and plus infinity are different (common case). (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem mnfnepnf:
  $ wne cmnf cpnf $;

--| An extended real other than minus infinity is real or positive infinite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xrnemnf (A2: class):
  $ wb (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wne A2 cmnf)) (wo (wcel A2 cr) (wceq A2 cpnf)) $;

--| An extended real other than plus infinity is real or negative infinite. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xrnepnf (A2: class):
  $ wb (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wne A2 cpnf)) (wo (wcel A2 cr) (wceq A2 cmnf)) $;

--| The extended real 'less than' is irreflexive. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2005.)
theorem xrltnr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wn (wbr A2 A2 clt)) $;

--| Any (finite) real is less than plus infinity. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2005.)
theorem ltpnf (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wbr A2 cpnf clt) $;

--| Zero is less than plus infinity (common case). (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem _0ltpnf:
  $ wbr cc0 cpnf clt $;

--| Minus infinity is less than any (finite) real. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2005.)
theorem mnflt (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cmnf A2 clt) $;

--| Minus infinity is less than 0 (common case). (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem mnflt0:
  $ wbr cmnf cc0 clt $;

--| Minus infinity is less than plus infinity. (Contributed by NM, 14-Oct-2005.)
theorem mnfltpnf:
  $ wbr cmnf cpnf clt $;

--| Minus infinity is less than an extended real that is either real or plus infinity. (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2006.)
theorem mnfltxr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wo (wcel A2 cr) (wceq A2 cpnf)) (wbr cmnf A2 clt) $;

--| No extended real is greater than plus infinity. (Contributed by NM, 15-Oct-2005.)
theorem pnfnlt (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wn (wbr cpnf A2 clt)) $;

--| No extended real is less than minus infinity. (Contributed by NM, 15-Oct-2005.)
theorem nltmnf (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wn (wbr A2 cmnf clt)) $;

--| Plus infinity is an upper bound for extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jan-2006.)
theorem pnfge (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wbr A2 cpnf cle) $;

--| 0 less than or equal to positive infinity. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem _0lepnf:
  $ wbr cc0 cpnf cle $;

--| If a number is a nonnegative integer or positive infinity, it is greater than or equal to 0. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 6-Jan-2018.)
theorem nn0pnfge0 (N: class):
  $ wi (wo (wcel N cn0) (wceq N cpnf)) (wbr cc0 N cle) $;

--| Minus infinity is less than or equal to any extended real. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jan-2006.)
theorem mnfle (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wbr cmnf A2 cle) $;

--| Ordering on the extended reals is not symmetric. (Contributed by NM, 15-Oct-2005.)
theorem xrltnsym (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wi (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt))) $;

--| 'Less than' is antisymmetric and irreflexive for extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-2007.)
theorem xrltnsym2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wn (wa (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr B2 A2 clt))) $;

--| Ordering on the extended reals is transitive. (Contributed by NM, 15-Oct-2005.)
theorem xrlttr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C cxr)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr B2 C clt)) (wbr A2 C clt)) $;

--| 'Less than' is a weakly linear ordering on the extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 15-Oct-2005.)
theorem xrltso:
  $ wor cxr clt $;

--| Extended real version of ~ lttri3 . (Contributed by NM, 9-Feb-2006.)
theorem xrlttri3 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wb (wceq A2 B2) (wa (wn (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wn (wbr B2 A2 clt)))) $;

--| 'Less than' implies 'less than or equal' for extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jan-2006.)
theorem xrltle (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wi (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) $;

--| 'Less than or equal to' is reflexive for extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 7-Feb-2007.)
theorem xrleid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wbr A2 A2 cle) $;

--| Trichotomy law for extended reals. (Contributed by FL, 2-Aug-2009.)
theorem xrletri3 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wb (wceq A2 B2) (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 A2 cle))) $;

--| Transitive law for ordering on extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jan-2006.)
theorem xrlelttr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C cxr)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 C clt)) (wbr A2 C clt)) $;

--| Transitive law for ordering on extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jan-2006.)
theorem xrltletr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C cxr)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr B2 C cle)) (wbr A2 C clt)) $;

--| Transitive law for ordering on extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 9-Feb-2006.)
theorem xrletr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C cxr)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 C cle)) (wbr A2 C cle)) $;

--| Transitive law for ordering on extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem xrlttrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C clt) $;

--| Transitive law for ordering on extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem xrlelttrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C clt) $;

--| Transitive law for ordering on extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem xrltletrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C clt) $;

--| Transitive law for ordering on extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem xrletrd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 C cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 C cle) $;

--| 'Less than' implies not equal for extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jan-2006.)
theorem xrltne (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wne B2 A2) $;

--| An extended real is not less than plus infinity iff they are equal. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jan-2006.)
theorem nltpnft (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wb (wceq A2 cpnf) (wn (wbr A2 cpnf clt))) $;

--| An extended real is not greater than minus infinity iff they are equal. (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2006.)
theorem ngtmnft (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wb (wceq A2 cmnf) (wn (wbr cmnf A2 clt))) $;

--| An extended real is real iff it is strictly bounded by infinities. (Contributed by NM, 2-Feb-2006.)
theorem xrrebnd (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wb (wcel A2 cr) (wa (wbr cmnf A2 clt) (wbr A2 cpnf clt))) $;

--| A way of proving that an extended real is real. (Contributed by NM, 9-Mar-2006.)
theorem xrre (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wbr cmnf A2 clt) (wbr A2 B2 cle))) (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| An extended real between two others is real. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-2007.)
theorem xrre2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C cxr)) (wa (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr B2 C clt))) (wcel B2 cr) $;

--| A way of proving that an extended real is real. (Contributed by FL, 29-May-2014.)
theorem xrre3 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wbr B2 A2 cle) (wbr A2 cpnf clt))) (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| A nonnegative extended real is greater than negative infinity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem ge0gtmnf (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wbr cmnf A2 clt) $;

--| A nonnegative extended real is greater than negative infinity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem ge0nemnf (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wne A2 cmnf) $;

--| A nonnegative extended real that is less than a real bound is real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xrrege0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr A2 B2 cle))) (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| There exists an integer greater than or equal to any two others. (Contributed by NM, 28-Aug-2005.)
theorem z2ge {k: setvar} (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wrex k (wa (wbr M (cv k) cle) (wbr N (cv k) cle)) cz) $;

--| Equality of two extended numbers with ` -e ` in front of them. (Contributed by FL, 26-Dec-2011.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xnegeq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (cxne A2) (cxne B2)) $;

--| Minus ` +oo ` . Remark of [BourbakiTop1] p. IV.15. (Contributed by FL, 26-Dec-2011.)
theorem xnegpnf:
  $ wceq (cxne cpnf) cmnf $;

--| Minus ` -oo ` . Remark of [BourbakiTop1] p. IV.15. (Contributed by FL, 26-Dec-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xnegmnf:
  $ wceq (cxne cmnf) cpnf $;

--| Minus a real number. Remark [BourbakiTop1] p. IV.15. (Contributed by FL, 26-Dec-2011.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem rexneg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cxne A2) (cneg A2)) $;

--| The negative of zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xneg0:
  $ wceq (cxne cc0) cc0 $;

--| Closure of extended real negative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xnegcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel (cxne A2) cxr) $;

--| Extended real version of ~ negneg . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xnegneg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wceq (cxne (cxne A2)) A2) $;

--| Extended real version of ~ neg11 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xneg11 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wb (wceq (cxne A2) (cxne B2)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Forward direction of ~ xltneg . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xltnegi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wbr (cxne B2) (cxne A2) clt) $;

--| Extended real version of ~ ltneg . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xltneg (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (cxne B2) (cxne A2) clt)) $;

--| Extended real version of ~ leneg . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xleneg (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (cxne B2) (cxne A2) cle)) $;

--| Extended real version of ~ lt0neg1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xlt0neg1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wb (wbr A2 cc0 clt) (wbr cc0 (cxne A2) clt)) $;

--| Extended real version of ~ lt0neg2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Aug-2015.)
theorem xlt0neg2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wb (wbr cc0 A2 clt) (wbr (cxne A2) cc0 clt)) $;

--| Extended real version of ~ le0neg1 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem xle0neg1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wb (wbr A2 cc0 cle) (wbr cc0 (cxne A2) cle)) $;

--| Extended real version of ~ le0neg2 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem xle0neg2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wb (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr (cxne A2) cc0 cle)) $;

--| Closure of extended real negative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem xnegcld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cxr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cxne A2) cxr) $;

--| The set of extended reals exists. (Contributed by NM, 24-Dec-2006.)
theorem xrex:
  $ wcel cxr cvv $;

--| Extend class notation with the set of open intervals of extended reals.
term cioo: class;

--| Extend class notation with the set of open-below, closed-above intervals of extended reals.
term cioc: class;

--| Extend class notation with the set of closed-below, open-above intervals of extended reals.
term cico: class;

--| Extend class notation with the set of closed intervals of extended reals.
term cicc: class;

--| Define the set of open intervals of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 24-Dec-2006.)
axiom df_ioo {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq cioo (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) clt) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) clt)) cxr)) $;

--| Define the set of open-below, closed-above intervals of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 24-Dec-2006.)
axiom df_ioc {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq cioc (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) clt) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) cle)) cxr)) $;

--| Define the set of closed-below, open-above intervals of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 24-Dec-2006.)
axiom df_ico {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq cico (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) cle) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) clt)) cxr)) $;

--| Define the set of closed intervals of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 24-Dec-2006.)
axiom df_icc {x3 y1 z: setvar}:
  $ wceq cicc (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) cle) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) cle)) cxr)) $;

--| Value of the interval function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem ixxval {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2 R S: class) (O: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq O (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) S)) cxr)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wceq (co A2 B2 O) (crab z (wa (wbr A2 (cv z) R) (wbr (cv z) B2 S)) cxr)) $;

--| Membership in an interval of extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem elixx1 {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2 C R S: class) (O: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq O (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) S)) cxr)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 O)) (w3a (wcel C cxr) (wbr A2 C R) (wbr C B2 S))) $;

--| The set of intervals of extended reals maps to subsets of extended reals. (Contributed by FL, 14-Jun-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2013.)
theorem ixxf {x3 y1 z: setvar} (R S: class) (O: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq O (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) S)) cxr)) $ >
  $ wf (cxp cxr cxr) (cpw cxr) O $;

--| The set of intervals of extended reals exists. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 17-Nov-2014.)
theorem ixxex {x3 y1 z: setvar} (R S: class) (O: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq O (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) S)) cxr)) $ >
  $ wcel O cvv $;

--| The set of intervals of extended reals maps to subsets of extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jul-2014.)
theorem ixxssxr {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2 R S O: class):
  $ wceq O (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) S)) cxr)) $ >
  $ wss (co A2 B2 O) cxr $;

--| Membership in a set of open intervals of extended reals. We use the fact that an operation's value is empty outside of its domain to show ` A e. RR* ` and ` B e. RR* ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem elixx3g {x3 y1 z: setvar} (A2 B2 C R S: class) (O: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq O (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) S)) cxr)) $ >
  $ wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 O)) (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C cxr)) (wa (wbr A2 C R) (wbr C B2 S))) $;

--| An interval is a subset of its closure. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 18-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem ixxssixx {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (P: class x3 y1 z) (R S T U: class w) (O: class y1 z):
  $ wceq O (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) S)) cxr)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) T) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) U)) cxr)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel (cv w) cxr)) (wi (wbr A2 (cv w) R) (wbr A2 (cv w) T)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv w) cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wi (wbr (cv w) B2 S) (wbr (cv w) B2 U)) $ >
  $ wss (co A2 B2 O) (co A2 B2 P) $;

--| Split an interval into disjoint pieces. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2014.)
theorem ixxdisj {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (P: class x3 y1 z) (R S T U: class w) (O: class y1 z):
  $ wceq O (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) S)) cxr)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) T) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) U)) cxr)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel (cv w) cxr)) (wb (wbr B2 (cv w) T) (wn (wbr (cv w) B2 S))) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C cxr)) (wceq (cin (co A2 B2 O) (co B2 C P)) c0) $;

--| Subset relationship for intervals of extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem ixxss1 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (P: class x3 y1 z) (R S T: class w) (O: class y1 z) (W: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq O (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) S)) cxr)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) T) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) S)) cxr)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel (cv w) cxr)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 B2 W) (wbr B2 (cv w) T)) (wbr A2 (cv w) R)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wbr A2 B2 W)) (wss (co B2 C P) (co A2 C O)) $;

--| Subset relationship for intervals of extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem ixxss2 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (P: class x3 y1 z) (R S T: class w) (O: class y1 z) (W: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq O (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) S)) cxr)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) T)) cxr)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv w) cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C cxr)) (wi (wa (wbr (cv w) B2 T) (wbr B2 C W)) (wbr (cv w) C S)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel C cxr) (wbr B2 C W)) (wss (co A2 B2 P) (co A2 C O)) $;

--| Subset relationship for intervals of extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Feb-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem ixxss12 {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (A2 B2 C D: class) (P: class x3 y1 z) (R S T U: class w) (O: class y1 z) (W X: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wceq O (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) R) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) S)) cxr)) $ >
  $ wceq P (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab z (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv z) T) (wbr (cv z) (cv y1) U)) cxr)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel C cxr) (wcel (cv w) cxr)) (wi (wa (wbr A2 C W) (wbr C (cv w) T)) (wbr A2 (cv w) R)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel (cv w) cxr) (wcel D cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wi (wa (wbr (cv w) D U) (wbr D B2 X)) (wbr (cv w) B2 S)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wa (wbr A2 C W) (wbr D B2 X))) (wss (co C D P) (co A2 B2 O)) $;

--| The set of open intervals of extended reals exists. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem iooex:
  $ wcel cioo cvv $;

--| Value of the open interval function. (Contributed by NM, 24-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem iooval {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cioo) (crab x3 (wa (wbr A2 (cv x3) clt) (wbr (cv x3) B2 clt)) cxr)) $;

--| An open interval with identical lower and upper bounds is empty. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 29-Mar-2020.)
theorem iooidg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wceq (co A2 A2 cioo) c0) $;

--| Membership in a set of open intervals of extended reals. We use the fact that an operation's value is empty outside of its domain to show ` A e. RR* ` and ` B e. RR* ` . (Contributed by NM, 24-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem elioo3g (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo)) (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C cxr)) (wa (wbr A2 C clt) (wbr C B2 clt))) $;

--| Membership in an open interval of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 24-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem elioo1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo)) (w3a (wcel C cxr) (wbr A2 C clt) (wbr C B2 clt))) $;

--| A member of an open interval of reals is a real. (Contributed by NM, 17-Aug-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem elioore (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co B2 C cioo)) (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| An open interval does not contain its left endpoint. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-Mar-2020.)
theorem lbioog (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wn (wcel A2 (co A2 B2 cioo))) $;

--| An open interval does not contain its right endpoint. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-Mar-2020.)
theorem ubioog (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wn (wcel B2 (co A2 B2 cioo))) $;

--| Value of the open interval function. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem iooval2 {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cioo) (crab x3 (wa (wbr A2 (cv x3) clt) (wbr (cv x3) B2 clt)) cr)) $;

--| Subset relationship for open intervals of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 7-Feb-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Feb-2015.)
theorem iooss1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wss (co B2 C cioo) (co A2 C cioo)) $;

--| Subset relationship for open intervals of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 7-Feb-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem iooss2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C cxr) (wbr B2 C cle)) (wss (co A2 B2 cioo) (co A2 C cioo)) $;

--| Value of the open-below, closed-above interval function. (Contributed by NM, 24-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem iocval {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cioc) (crab x3 (wa (wbr A2 (cv x3) clt) (wbr (cv x3) B2 cle)) cxr)) $;

--| Value of the closed-below, open-above interval function. (Contributed by NM, 24-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem icoval {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cico) (crab x3 (wa (wbr A2 (cv x3) cle) (wbr (cv x3) B2 clt)) cxr)) $;

--| Value of the closed interval function. (Contributed by NM, 24-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem iccval {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cicc) (crab x3 (wa (wbr A2 (cv x3) cle) (wbr (cv x3) B2 cle)) cxr)) $;

--| Membership in an open interval of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-2007.)
theorem elioo2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo)) (w3a (wcel C cr) (wbr A2 C clt) (wbr C B2 clt))) $;

--| Membership in an open-below, closed-above interval of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 24-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem elioc1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioc)) (w3a (wcel C cxr) (wbr A2 C clt) (wbr C B2 cle))) $;

--| Membership in a closed-below, open-above interval of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 24-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem elico1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cico)) (w3a (wcel C cxr) (wbr A2 C cle) (wbr C B2 clt))) $;

--| Membership in a closed interval of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 24-Dec-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem elicc1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc)) (w3a (wcel C cxr) (wbr A2 C cle) (wbr C B2 cle))) $;

--| A closed interval with identical lower and upper bounds is a singleton. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 13-Jul-2009.)
theorem iccid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wceq (co A2 A2 cicc) (csn A2)) $;

--| An empty closed interval of extended reals. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-Mar-2020.)
theorem icc0r (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wi (wbr B2 A2 clt) (wceq (co A2 B2 cicc) c0)) $;

--| An inhabited open interval spans an interval of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 17-Aug-2008.)
theorem eliooxr (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co B2 C cioo)) (wa (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C cxr)) $;

--| Ordering implied by a member of an open interval of reals. (Contributed by NM, 17-Aug-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2014.)
theorem eliooord (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co B2 C cioo)) (wa (wbr B2 A2 clt) (wbr A2 C clt)) $;

--| The upper bound belongs to an open-below, closed-above interval. See ~ ubicc2 . (Contributed by FL, 29-May-2014.)
theorem ubioc1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wcel B2 (co A2 B2 cioc)) $;

--| The lower bound belongs to a closed-below, open-above interval. See ~ lbicc2 . (Contributed by FL, 29-May-2014.)
theorem lbico1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wcel A2 (co A2 B2 cico)) $;

--| An element of a closed interval is less than or equal to its upper bound. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 14-Jul-2009.)
theorem iccleub (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc))) (wbr C B2 cle) $;

--| An element of a closed interval is more than or equal to its lower bound (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 23-Dec-2016.)
theorem iccgelb (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc))) (wbr A2 C cle) $;

--| Membership in an open interval of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 17-Aug-2008.)
theorem elioo5 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C cxr)) (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo)) (wa (wbr A2 C clt) (wbr C B2 clt))) $;

--| Membership in an open interval of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jun-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem elioo4g (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo)) (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C cr)) (wa (wbr A2 C clt) (wbr C B2 clt))) $;

--| An open interval is a set of reals. (Contributed by NM, 31-May-2007.)
theorem ioossre (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (co A2 B2 cioo) cr $;

--| Membership in an open-below, closed-above real interval. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 30-Dec-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2014.)
theorem elioc2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioc)) (w3a (wcel C cr) (wbr A2 C clt) (wbr C B2 cle))) $;

--| Membership in a closed-below, open-above real interval. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2014.)
theorem elico2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cico)) (w3a (wcel C cr) (wbr A2 C cle) (wbr C B2 clt))) $;

--| Membership in a closed real interval. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Sep-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2014.)
theorem elicc2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc)) (w3a (wcel C cr) (wbr A2 C cle) (wbr C B2 cle))) $;

--| Inference for membership in a closed interval. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 3-Jun-2013.)
theorem elicc2i (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc)) (w3a (wcel C cr) (wbr A2 C cle) (wbr C B2 cle)) $;

--| Membership in a closed real interval. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jan-2017.)
theorem elicc4 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C cxr)) (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wa (wbr A2 C cle) (wbr C B2 cle))) $;

--| Condition for a closed interval to be a subset of another closed interval. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 20-Feb-2015.)
theorem iccss (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wbr A2 C cle) (wbr D B2 cle))) (wss (co C D cicc) (co A2 B2 cicc)) $;

--| Condition for a closed interval to be a subset of an open interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Feb-2015.)
theorem iccssioo (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wa (wbr A2 C clt) (wbr D B2 clt))) (wss (co C D cicc) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $;

--| Condition for a closed-below, open-above interval to be a subset of a closed-below, open-above interval. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 21-Sep-2017.)
theorem icossico (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wa (wbr A2 C cle) (wbr D B2 cle))) (wss (co C D cico) (co A2 B2 cico)) $;

--| Condition for a closed interval to be a subset of another closed interval. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem iccss2 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wcel D (co A2 B2 cicc))) (wss (co C D cicc) (co A2 B2 cicc)) $;

--| Condition for a closed interval to be a subset of a half-open interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem iccssico (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wa (wbr A2 C cle) (wbr D B2 clt))) (wss (co C D cicc) (co A2 B2 cico)) $;

--| Condition for a closed interval to be a subset of an open interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Feb-2015.)
theorem iccssioo2 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo)) (wcel D (co A2 B2 cioo))) (wss (co C D cicc) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $;

--| Condition for a closed interval to be a subset of a closed-below, open-above interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 20-Feb-2015.)
theorem iccssico2 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel C (co A2 B2 cico)) (wcel D (co A2 B2 cico))) (wss (co C D cicc) (co A2 B2 cico)) $;

--| The open interval from minus to plus infinity. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-2007.)
theorem ioomax:
  $ wceq (co cmnf cpnf cioo) cr $;

--| The closed interval from minus to plus infinity. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jul-2014.)
theorem iccmax:
  $ wceq (co cmnf cpnf cicc) cxr $;

--| The set of positive reals expressed as an open interval. (Contributed by NM, 7-May-2007.)
theorem ioopos {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq (co cc0 cpnf cioo) (crab x3 (wbr cc0 (cv x3) clt) cr) $;

--| The set of positive reals expressed as an open interval. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 25-Nov-2007.)
theorem ioorp:
  $ wceq (co cc0 cpnf cioo) crp $;

--| Shift the arguments of the open interval function. (Contributed by NM, 17-Aug-2008.)
theorem iooshf (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wcel C cr) (wcel D cr))) (wb (wcel (co A2 B2 cmin) (co C D cioo)) (wcel A2 (co (co C B2 caddc) (co D B2 caddc) cioo))) $;

--| A closed-above interval with real upper bound is a set of reals. (Contributed by FL, 29-May-2014.)
theorem iocssre (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wss (co A2 B2 cioc) cr) $;

--| A closed-below interval with real lower bound is a set of reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jun-2014.)
theorem icossre (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wss (co A2 B2 cico) cr) $;

--| A closed real interval is a set of reals. (Contributed by FL, 6-Jun-2007.) (Proof shortened by Paul Chapman, 21-Jan-2008.)
theorem iccssre (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wss (co A2 B2 cicc) cr) $;

--| A closed interval is a set of extended reals. (Contributed by FL, 28-Jul-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jul-2014.)
theorem iccssxr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (co A2 B2 cicc) cxr $;

--| An open-below, closed-above interval is a subset of the extended reals. (Contributed by FL, 29-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jul-2014.)
theorem iocssxr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (co A2 B2 cioc) cxr $;

--| A closed-below, open-above interval is a subset of the extended reals. (Contributed by FL, 29-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jul-2014.)
theorem icossxr (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (co A2 B2 cico) cxr $;

--| An open interval is a subset of its closure. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 18-Oct-2007.)
theorem ioossicc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (co A2 B2 cioo) (co A2 B2 cicc) $;

--| A closed-below, open-above interval is a subset of its closure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 25-Oct-2016.)
theorem icossicc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (co A2 B2 cico) (co A2 B2 cicc) $;

--| A closed-above, open-below interval is a subset of its closure. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 1-Apr-2017.)
theorem iocssicc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (co A2 B2 cioc) (co A2 B2 cicc) $;

--| An open interval is a subset of its closure-below. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 3-Mar-2017.)
theorem ioossico (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (co A2 B2 cioo) (co A2 B2 cico) $;

--| Condition for a closed interval to be a subset of an open interval. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Mar-2017.)
theorem iocssioo (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wa (wbr A2 C cle) (wbr D B2 clt))) (wss (co C D cioc) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $;

--| Condition for a closed interval to be a subset of an open interval. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 29-Mar-2017.)
theorem icossioo (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wa (wbr A2 C clt) (wbr D B2 cle))) (wss (co C D cico) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $;

--| Condition for an open interval to be a subset of an open interval. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 26-Sep-2017.)
theorem ioossioo (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wa (wbr A2 C cle) (wbr D B2 cle))) (wss (co C D cioo) (co A2 B2 cioo)) $;

--| A nonempty subset of a closed real interval satisfies the conditions for the existence of its supremum. To be useful without excluded middle, we'll probably need to change not equal to apart, and perhaps make other changes, but the theorem does hold as stated here. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Jan-2008.)
theorem iccsupr {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3) (B2: class) (C: class x3 y1) (S: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wss S (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wcel C S)) (w3a (wss S cr) (wne S c0) (wrex x3 (wral y1 (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) S) cr)) $;

--| Membership in an unbounded interval of extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem elioopnf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wb (wcel B2 (co A2 cpnf cioo)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 clt))) $;

--| Membership in an unbounded interval of extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem elioomnf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cxr) (wb (wcel B2 (co cmnf A2 cioo)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr B2 A2 clt))) $;

--| Membership in a closed unbounded interval of reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Sep-2014.)
theorem elicopnf (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wb (wcel B2 (co A2 cpnf cico)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 cle))) $;

--| Two ways of saying that a real number is positive. (Contributed by NM, 7-May-2007.)
theorem repos (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpnf cioo)) (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) $;

--| The set of open intervals of extended reals maps to subsets of reals. (Contributed by NM, 7-Feb-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2013.)
theorem ioof:
  $ wf (cxp cxr cxr) (cpw cr) cioo $;

--| The set of closed intervals of extended reals maps to subsets of extended reals. (Contributed by FL, 14-Jun-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem iccf:
  $ wf (cxp cxr cxr) (cpw cxr) cicc $;

--| The union of the range of the open interval function. (Contributed by NM, 7-May-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Jan-2014.)
theorem unirnioo:
  $ wceq cr (cuni (crn cioo)) $;

--| Alternate definition of the set of open intervals of extended reals. (Contributed by NM, 1-Mar-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2015.)
theorem dfioo2 {x3 y1 w: setvar}:
  $ wceq cioo (cmpt2 x3 y1 cxr cxr (crab w (wa (wbr (cv x3) (cv w) clt) (wbr (cv w) (cv y1) clt)) cr)) $;

--| Open intervals are elements of the set of all open intervals. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 4-Apr-2020.)
theorem ioorebasg (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cioo) (crn cioo)) $;

--| The predicate "is a nonnegative real". (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2014.)
theorem elrege0 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpnf cico)) (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) $;

--| Nonnegative real numbers are real numbers. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 9-Sep-2018.) (Proof shortened by AV, 8-Sep-2019.)
theorem rge0ssre:
  $ wss (co cc0 cpnf cico) cr $;

--| Elementhood in the set of nonnegative extended reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-Jun-2014.)
theorem elxrge0 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpnf cicc)) (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) $;

--| 0 is a member of ` ( 0 [,) +oo ) ` (common case). (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem _0e0icopnf:
  $ wcel cc0 (co cc0 cpnf cico) $;

--| 0 is a member of ` ( 0 [,] +oo ) ` (common case). (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem _0e0iccpnf:
  $ wcel cc0 (co cc0 cpnf cicc) $;

--| The nonnegative reals are closed under addition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2014.)
theorem ge0addcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpnf cico)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cico))) (wcel (co A2 B2 caddc) (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $;

--| The nonnegative reals are closed under multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 19-Jun-2014.)
theorem ge0mulcl (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co cc0 cpnf cico)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 cpnf cico))) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmul) (co cc0 cpnf cico)) $;

--| The lower bound of a closed interval is a member of it. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 26-Nov-2007.) (Revised by FL, 29-May-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2015.)
theorem lbicc2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wcel A2 (co A2 B2 cicc)) $;

--| The upper bound of a closed interval is a member of it. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 26-Nov-2007.) (Revised by FL, 29-May-2014.)
theorem ubicc2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wcel B2 (co A2 B2 cicc)) $;

--| Zero is an element of the closed unit. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Jun-2013.)
theorem _0elunit:
  $ wcel cc0 (co cc0 c1 cicc) $;

--| One is an element of the closed unit. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Jun-2013.)
theorem _1elunit:
  $ wcel c1 (co cc0 c1 cicc) $;

--| Membership in a negated open real interval. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 26-Nov-2007.)
theorem iooneg (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cioo)) (wcel (cneg C) (co (cneg B2) (cneg A2) cioo))) $;

--| Membership in a negated closed real interval. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 26-Nov-2007.)
theorem iccneg (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wcel (cneg C) (co (cneg B2) (cneg A2) cicc))) $;

--| A shifted real is a member of a shifted, closed-below, open-above real interval. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Mar-2008.)
theorem icoshft (A2 B2 C X: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wi (wcel X (co A2 B2 cico)) (wcel (co X C caddc) (co (co A2 C caddc) (co B2 C caddc) cico))) $;

--| Shifting a closed-below, open-above interval is one-to-one onto. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 25-Mar-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 1-Sep-2015.)
theorem icoshftf1o {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co A2 B2 cico) (co (cv x3) C caddc)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wf1o (co A2 B2 cico) (co (co A2 C caddc) (co B2 C caddc) cico) F) $;

--| End-to-end closed-below, open-above real intervals are disjoint. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 16-Jun-2014.)
theorem icodisj (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr) (wcel C cxr)) (wceq (cin (co A2 B2 cico) (co B2 C cico)) c0) $;

--| If the upper bound of one open interval is less than or equal to the lower bound of the other, the intervals are disjoint. (Contributed by Jeff Hankins, 13-Jul-2009.)
theorem ioodisj (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wa (wcel A2 cxr) (wcel B2 cxr)) (wa (wcel C cxr) (wcel D cxr))) (wbr B2 C cle)) (wceq (cin (co A2 B2 cioo) (co C D cioo)) c0) $;

--| Membership in a shifted interval. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem iccshftr (A2 B2 C D R X: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 R caddc) C $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 R caddc) D $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wcel X cr) (wcel R cr))) (wb (wcel X (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wcel (co X R caddc) (co C D cicc))) $;

--| Membership in a shifted interval. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem iccshftri (A2 B2 C D R X: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel R cr $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 R caddc) C $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 R caddc) D $ >
  $ wi (wcel X (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wcel (co X R caddc) (co C D cicc)) $;

--| Membership in a shifted interval. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem iccshftl (A2 B2 C D R X: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 R cmin) C $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 R cmin) D $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wcel X cr) (wcel R cr))) (wb (wcel X (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wcel (co X R cmin) (co C D cicc))) $;

--| Membership in a shifted interval. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem iccshftli (A2 B2 C D R X: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel R cr $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 R cmin) C $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 R cmin) D $ >
  $ wi (wcel X (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wcel (co X R cmin) (co C D cicc)) $;

--| Membership in a dilated interval. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem iccdil (A2 B2 C D R X: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 R cmul) C $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 R cmul) D $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wcel X cr) (wcel R crp))) (wb (wcel X (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wcel (co X R cmul) (co C D cicc))) $;

--| Membership in a dilated interval. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem iccdili (A2 B2 C D R X: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel R crp $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 R cmul) C $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 R cmul) D $ >
  $ wi (wcel X (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wcel (co X R cmul) (co C D cicc)) $;

--| Membership in a contracted interval. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem icccntr (A2 B2 C D R X: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 R cdiv) C $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 R cdiv) D $ >
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wcel X cr) (wcel R crp))) (wb (wcel X (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wcel (co X R cdiv) (co C D cicc))) $;

--| Membership in a contracted interval. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem icccntri (A2 B2 C D R X: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wcel R crp $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 R cdiv) C $ >
  $ wceq (co B2 R cdiv) D $ >
  $ wi (wcel X (co A2 B2 cicc)) (wcel (co X R cdiv) (co C D cicc)) $;

--| A condition for a ratio to be a member of the closed unit. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Jun-2013.)
theorem divelunit (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 clt))) (wb (wcel (co A2 B2 cdiv) (co cc0 c1 cicc)) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) $;

--| A linear combination of two reals which lies in the interval between them. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2015.)
theorem lincmb01cmp (A2 B2 T: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wcel T (co cc0 c1 cicc))) (wcel (co (co (co c1 T cmin) A2 cmul) (co T B2 cmul) caddc) (co A2 B2 cicc)) $;

--| Describe a bijection from ` [ 0 , 1 ] ` to an arbitrary nontrivial closed interval ` [ A , B ] ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Sep-2015.)
theorem iccf1o {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpt x3 (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co (co (cv x3) B2 cmul) (co (co c1 (cv x3) cmin) A2 cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wa (wf1o (co cc0 c1 cicc) (co A2 B2 cicc) F) (wceq (ccnv F) (cmpt y1 (co A2 B2 cicc) (co (co (cv y1) A2 cmin) (co B2 A2 cmin) cdiv)))) $;

--| ` ( 0 [,] 1 ) ` is a subset of the reals. (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem unitssre:
  $ wss (co cc0 c1 cicc) cr $;

--| Extend class notation to include the notation for a contiguous finite set of integers. Read " ` M ... N ` " as "the set of integers from ` M ` to ` N ` inclusive."
term cfz: class;

--| Define an operation that produces a finite set of sequential integers. Read " ` M ... N ` " as "the set of integers from ` M ` to ` N ` inclusive." See ~ fzval for its value and additional comments. (Contributed by NM, 6-Sep-2005.)
axiom df_fz {k m n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfz (cmpt2 m n cz cz (crab k (wa (wbr (cv m) (cv k) cle) (wbr (cv k) (cv n) cle)) cz)) $;

--| The value of a finite set of sequential integers. E.g., ` 2 ... 5 ` means the set ` { 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 } ` . A special case of this definition (starting at 1) appears as Definition 11-2.1 of [Gleason] p. 141, where ` NN ` _k means our ` 1 ... k ` ; he calls these sets _segments_ of the integers. (Contributed by NM, 6-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem fzval {k: setvar} (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (co M N cfz) (crab k (wa (wbr M (cv k) cle) (wbr (cv k) N cle)) cz)) $;

--| An alternative way of expressing a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem fzval2 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (co M N cfz) (cin (co M N cicc) cz)) $;

--| Establish the domain and codomain of the finite integer sequence function. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 8-Aug-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Nov-2013.)
theorem fzf:
  $ wf (cxp cz cz) (cpw cz) cfz $;

--| Membership in a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jul-2005.)
theorem elfz1 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (w3a (wcel K cz) (wbr M K cle) (wbr K N cle))) $;

--| Membership in a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 29-Sep-2005.)
theorem elfz (K M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cz) (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wa (wbr M K cle) (wbr K N cle))) $;

--| Membership in a finite set of sequential integers. We use the fact that an operation's value is empty outside of its domain to show ` M e. ZZ ` and ` N e. ZZ ` . (Contributed by NM, 6-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem elfz2 (K M N: class):
  $ wb (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wa (w3a (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz) (wcel K cz)) (wa (wbr M K cle) (wbr K N cle))) $;

--| Membership in a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 26-Dec-2005.)
theorem elfz5 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel K (cfv M cuz)) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wbr K N cle)) $;

--| Membership in a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jul-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem elfz4 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz) (wcel K cz)) (wa (wbr M K cle) (wbr K N cle))) (wcel K (co M N cfz)) $;

--| Membership in a finite set of sequential integers in terms of sets of upper integers. (Contributed by NM, 18-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem elfzuzb (K M N: class):
  $ wb (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wa (wcel K (cfv M cuz)) (wcel N (cfv K cuz))) $;

--| Membership in a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem eluzfz (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel K (cfv M cuz)) (wcel N (cfv K cuz))) (wcel K (co M N cfz)) $;

--| A member of a finite set of sequential integers belongs to an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 17-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem elfzuz (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wcel K (cfv M cuz)) $;

--| Membership in a finite set of sequential integers implies membership in an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem elfzuz3 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wcel N (cfv K cuz)) $;

--| Membership in a finite set of sequential integer implies the upper bound is an integer. (Contributed by NM, 6-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem elfzel2 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wcel N cz) $;

--| Membership in a finite set of sequential integer implies the lower bound is an integer. (Contributed by NM, 6-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem elfzel1 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wcel M cz) $;

--| A member of a finite set of sequential integer is an integer. (Contributed by NM, 6-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem elfzelz (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wcel K cz) $;

--| A member of a finite set of sequential integer is greater than or equal to the lower bound. (Contributed by NM, 6-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem elfzle1 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wbr M K cle) $;

--| A member of a finite set of sequential integer is less than or equal to the upper bound. (Contributed by NM, 6-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem elfzle2 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wbr K N cle) $;

--| Implication of membership in a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 20-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem elfzuz2 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $;

--| Membership in a finite set of sequential integer implies the bounds are comparable. (Contributed by NM, 18-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem elfzle3 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wbr M N cle) $;

--| Membership in a finite set of sequential integers - special case. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jul-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem eluzfz1 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wcel M (co M N cfz)) $;

--| Membership in a finite set of sequential integers - special case. (Contributed by NM, 13-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem eluzfz2 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wcel N (co M N cfz)) $;

--| Membership in a finite set of sequential integers - special case. (Contributed by NM, 14-Sep-2005.)
theorem eluzfz2b (M N: class):
  $ wb (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wcel N (co M N cfz)) $;

--| Membership in a finite set of sequential integers containing one integer. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jul-2005.)
theorem elfz3 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wcel N (co N N cfz)) $;

--| Membership in a finite set of sequential integers containing one integer. (Contributed by NM, 19-Sep-2005.)
theorem elfz1eq (K N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co N N cfz)) (wceq K N) $;

--| If there is a member in a finite set of sequential integers, the upper bound is also a member of this finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 31-May-2018.)
theorem elfzubelfz (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wcel N (co M N cfz)) $;

--| A Peano-postulate-like theorem for downward closure of a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2014.)
theorem peano2fzr (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel K (cfv M cuz)) (wcel (co K c1 caddc) (co M N cfz))) (wcel K (co M N cfz)) $;

--| Properties of a finite interval of integers which is inhabited. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 15-Apr-2020.)
theorem fzm {x3: setvar} (M N: class):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wcel (cv x3) (co M N cfz))) (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $;

--| Trichotomy in terms of a finite interval of integers. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 1-Jun-2020.)
theorem fztri3or (K M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cz) (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (w3o (wbr K M clt) (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wbr N K clt)) $;

--| Decidability of membership in a finite interval of integers. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 1-Jun-2020.)
theorem fzdcel (K M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cz) (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wdc (wcel K (co M N cfz))) $;

--| A finite set of sequential integers is empty if the bounds are reversed. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 16-Apr-2020.)
theorem fznlem (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wi (wbr N M clt) (wceq (co M N cfz) c0)) $;

--| A finite set of sequential integers is empty if the bounds are reversed. (Contributed by NM, 22-Aug-2005.)
theorem fzn (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wbr N M clt) (wceq (co M N cfz) c0)) $;

--| A shifted finite set of sequential integers is equinumerous to the original set. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 11-Apr-2009.)
theorem fzen (K M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz) (wcel K cz)) (wbr (co M N cfz) (co (co M K caddc) (co N K caddc) cfz) cen) $;

--| A 1-based finite set of sequential integers is empty iff it ends at index ` 0 ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 22-Jun-2011.)
theorem fz1n (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wb (wceq (co c1 N cfz) c0) (wceq N cc0)) $;

--| Two ways to say a finite 1-based sequence is empty. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 26-Oct-2012.)
theorem _0fz1 (F N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wfn F (co c1 N cfz))) (wb (wceq F c0) (wceq N cc0)) $;

--| There are no integers between 1 and 0. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 16-Jun-2010.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem fz10:
  $ wceq (co c1 cc0 cfz) c0 $;

--| Membership of an integer greater than L decreased by ( L - M ) in an M based finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 14-Sep-2018.)
theorem uzsubsubfz (L M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel L (cfv M cuz)) (wcel N (cfv L cuz))) (wcel (co N (co L M cmin) cmin) (co M N cfz)) $;

--| Membership of an integer greater than L decreased by ( L - 1 ) in a 1 based finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 14-Sep-2018.)
theorem uzsubsubfz1 (L N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel L cn) (wcel N (cfv L cuz))) (wcel (co N (co L c1 cmin) cmin) (co c1 N cfz)) $;

--| Membership of an integer greater than 2 decreased by 2 in a 1 based finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 14-Sep-2018.)
theorem ige3m2fz (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c3 cuz)) (wcel (co N c2 cmin) (co c1 N cfz)) $;

--| Split a finite interval of integers into two parts. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Apr-2016.)
theorem fzsplit2 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel (co K c1 caddc) (cfv M cuz)) (wcel N (cfv K cuz))) (wceq (co M N cfz) (cun (co M K cfz) (co (co K c1 caddc) N cfz))) $;

--| Split a finite interval of integers into two parts. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 17-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Apr-2016.)
theorem fzsplit (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wceq (co M N cfz) (cun (co M K cfz) (co (co K c1 caddc) N cfz))) $;

--| Condition for two finite intervals of integers to be disjoint. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 17-Jun-2010.)
theorem fzdisj (J K M N: class):
  $ wi (wbr K M clt) (wceq (cin (co J K cfz) (co M N cfz)) c0) $;

--| 0-based and 1-based finite sets of sequential integers are equinumerous. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 11-Apr-2009.)
theorem fz01en (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wbr (co cc0 (co N c1 cmin) cfz) (co c1 N cfz) cen) $;

--| A member of a finite set of sequential integers starting at 1 is a positive integer. (Contributed by NM, 24-Aug-2005.)
theorem elfznn (K N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co c1 N cfz)) (wcel K cn) $;

--| A nonempty finite range of integers contains its end point. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 10-Oct-2014.)
theorem elfz1end (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 cn) (wcel A2 (co c1 A2 cfz)) $;

--| Subtraction closure for a member of a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem fznn0sub (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wcel (co N K cmin) cn0) $;

--| Subtracting the difference of a member of a finite range of integers and the lower bound of the range from the difference of the upper bound and the lower bound of the range results in the difference of the upper bound of the range and the member. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 27-May-2018.)
theorem fzmmmeqm (L M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel M (co L N cfz)) (wceq (co (co N L cmin) (co M L cmin) cmin) (co N M cmin)) $;

--| Membership of a sum in a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jul-2005.)
theorem fzaddel (J K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wa (wcel J cz) (wcel K cz))) (wb (wcel J (co M N cfz)) (wcel (co J K caddc) (co (co M K caddc) (co N K caddc) cfz))) $;

--| Membership of a difference in a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jul-2005.)
theorem fzsubel (J K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wa (wcel J cz) (wcel K cz))) (wb (wcel J (co M N cfz)) (wcel (co J K cmin) (co (co M K cmin) (co N K cmin) cfz))) $;

--| A finite set of sequential integers can represent an ordered pair. (Contributed by NM, 31-Oct-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem fzopth (J K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wb (wceq (co M N cfz) (co J K cfz)) (wa (wceq M J) (wceq N K))) $;

--| Two ways to express a nondecreasing sequence of four integers. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzass4 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wb (wa (wcel B2 (co A2 D cfz)) (wcel C (co B2 D cfz))) (wa (wcel B2 (co A2 C cfz)) (wcel C (co A2 D cfz))) $;

--| Subset relationship for finite sets of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 28-Sep-2005.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem fzss1 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (cfv M cuz)) (wss (co K N cfz) (co M N cfz)) $;

--| Subset relationship for finite sets of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 4-Oct-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 30-Apr-2015.)
theorem fzss2 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv K cuz)) (wss (co M K cfz) (co M N cfz)) $;

--| A finite set of sequential integers is a subset of an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 28-Oct-2005.)
theorem fzssuz (M N: class):
  $ wss (co M N cfz) (cfv M cuz) $;

--| A finite interval of integers with one element. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem fzsn (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cz) (wceq (co M M cfz) (csn M)) $;

--| Subset relationship for finite sets of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jul-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem fzssp1 (M N: class):
  $ wss (co M N cfz) (co M (co N c1 caddc) cfz) $;

--| Join a successor to the end of a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jul-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem fzsuc (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wceq (co M (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (cun (co M N cfz) (csn (co N c1 caddc)))) $;

--| Join a predecessor to the beginning of a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by AV, 24-Aug-2019.)
theorem fzpred (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wceq (co M N cfz) (cun (csn M) (co (co M c1 caddc) N cfz))) $;

--| A finite set of sequential integers is disjoint with its predecessor. (Contributed by AV, 24-Aug-2019.)
theorem fzpreddisj (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wceq (cin (csn M) (co (co M c1 caddc) N cfz)) c0) $;

--| Append an element to a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 19-Sep-2005.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem elfzp1 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wb (wcel K (co M (co N c1 caddc) cfz)) (wo (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wceq K (co N c1 caddc)))) $;

--| Subset relationship for finite sets of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 26-Jul-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem fzp1ss (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cz) (wss (co (co M c1 caddc) N cfz) (co M N cfz)) $;

--| Membership in a set of sequential integers with an appended element. (Contributed by NM, 7-Dec-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem fzelp1 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wcel K (co M (co N c1 caddc) cfz)) $;

--| Add one to an element of a finite set of integers. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 6-Jun-2010.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem fzp1elp1 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wcel (co K c1 caddc) (co M (co N c1 caddc) cfz)) $;

--| Shift membership in a finite sequence of naturals. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Jul-2013.)
theorem fznatpl1 (I N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel I (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz))) (wcel (co I c1 caddc) (co c1 N cfz)) $;

--| A finite interval of integers with two elements. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem fzpr (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cz) (wceq (co M (co M c1 caddc) cfz) (cpr M (co M c1 caddc))) $;

--| A finite interval of integers with three elements. (Contributed by NM, 13-Sep-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2014.)
theorem fztp (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cz) (wceq (co M (co M c2 caddc) cfz) (ctp M (co M c1 caddc) (co M c2 caddc))) $;

--| Join a successor to the end of a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2014.)
theorem fzsuc2 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N (cfv (co M c1 cmin) cuz))) (wceq (co M (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (cun (co M N cfz) (csn (co N c1 caddc)))) $;

--| ` ( M ... ( N + 1 ) ) ` is the disjoint union of ` ( M ... N ) ` with ` { ( N + 1 ) } ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2014.)
theorem fzp1disj (M N: class):
  $ wceq (cin (co M N cfz) (csn (co N c1 caddc))) c0 $;

--| Remove a successor from the end of a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2019.)
theorem fzdifsuc (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wceq (co M N cfz) (cdif (co M (co N c1 caddc) cfz) (csn (co N c1 caddc)))) $;

--| Two ways of defining the first two values of a sequence on ` NN ` . (Contributed by NM, 5-Sep-2011.)
theorem fzprval {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wb (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cif (wceq (cv x3) c1) A2 B2)) (co c1 c2 cfz)) (wa (wceq (cfv c1 F) A2) (wceq (cfv c2 F) B2)) $;

--| Two ways of defining the first three values of a sequence on ` NN ` . (Contributed by NM, 13-Sep-2011.)
theorem fztpval {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wb (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (cif (wceq (cv x3) c1) A2 (cif (wceq (cv x3) c2) B2 C))) (co c1 c3 cfz)) (w3a (wceq (cfv c1 F) A2) (wceq (cfv c2 F) B2) (wceq (cfv c3 F) C)) $;

--| Reversal of start and end of a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 25-Nov-2005.)
theorem fzrev (J K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wa (wcel J cz) (wcel K cz))) (wb (wcel K (co (co J N cmin) (co J M cmin) cfz)) (wcel (co J K cmin) (co M N cfz))) $;

--| Reversal of start and end of a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 25-Nov-2005.)
theorem fzrev2 (J K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wa (wcel J cz) (wcel K cz))) (wb (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wcel (co J K cmin) (co (co J N cmin) (co J M cmin) cfz))) $;

--| Reversal of start and end of a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 25-Nov-2005.)
theorem fzrev2i (J K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel J cz) (wcel K (co M N cfz))) (wcel (co J K cmin) (co (co J N cmin) (co J M cmin) cfz)) $;

--| The "complement" of a member of a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 20-Nov-2005.)
theorem fzrev3 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K cz) (wb (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wcel (co (co M N caddc) K cmin) (co M N cfz))) $;

--| The "complement" of a member of a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 20-Nov-2005.)
theorem fzrev3i (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wcel (co (co M N caddc) K cmin) (co M N cfz)) $;

--| Finite set of sequential integers starting at 1. (Contributed by NM, 31-Aug-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2015.)
theorem fznn (K N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wb (wcel K (co c1 N cfz)) (wa (wcel K cn) (wbr K N cle))) $;

--| Membership in a 1 based finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by AV, 30-Oct-2018.)
theorem elfz1b (M N: class):
  $ wb (wcel N (co c1 M cfz)) (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel M cn) (wbr N M cle)) $;

--| Membership in a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Mar-2011.)
theorem elfzm11 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wcel K (co M (co N c1 cmin) cfz)) (w3a (wcel K cz) (wbr M K cle) (wbr K N clt))) $;

--| Express an upper integer set as the disjoint (see ~ uzdisj ) union of the first ` N ` values and the rest. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Apr-2014.)
theorem uzsplit (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wceq (cfv M cuz) (cun (co M (co N c1 cmin) cfz) (cfv N cuz))) $;

--| The first ` N ` elements of an upper integer set are distinct from any later members. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Apr-2014.)
theorem uzdisj (M N: class):
  $ wceq (cin (co M (co N c1 cmin) cfz) (cfv N cuz)) c0 $;

--| Add/remove an item to/from the end of a finite sequence. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 17-Nov-2012.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Mar-2014.)
theorem fseq1p1m1 (A2 B2 F G H N: class):
  $ wceq H (csn (cop (co N c1 caddc) B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wb (w3a (wf (co c1 N cfz) A2 F) (wcel B2 A2) (wceq G (cun F H))) (w3a (wf (co c1 (co N c1 caddc) cfz) A2 G) (wceq (cfv (co N c1 caddc) G) B2) (wceq F (cres G (co c1 N cfz))))) $;

--| Add/remove an item to/from the end of a finite sequence. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 17-Nov-2012.)
theorem fseq1m1p1 (A2 B2 F G H N: class):
  $ wceq H (csn (cop N B2)) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wb (w3a (wf (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz) A2 F) (wcel B2 A2) (wceq G (cun F H))) (w3a (wf (co c1 N cfz) A2 G) (wceq (cfv N G) B2) (wceq F (cres G (co c1 (co N c1 cmin) cfz))))) $;

--| Quantification over a one-member finite set of sequential integers in terms of substitution. (Contributed by NM, 28-Nov-2005.)
theorem fz1sbc {k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wb (wral k ph (co N N cfz)) (wsbc k ph N)) $;

--| An integer is a member of a 0-based finite set of sequential integers iff its successor is a member of the corresponding 1-based set. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 22-Jun-2011.)
theorem elfzp1b (K N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cz) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wcel K (co cc0 (co N c1 cmin) cfz)) (wcel (co K c1 caddc) (co c1 N cfz))) $;

--| An integer is a member of a 1-based finite set of sequential integers iff its predecessor is a member of the corresponding 0-based set. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 22-Jun-2011.)
theorem elfzm1b (K N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cz) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wcel K (co c1 N cfz)) (wcel (co K c1 cmin) (co cc0 (co N c1 cmin) cfz))) $;

--| Options for membership in a finite interval of integers. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 18-Jun-2010.)
theorem elfzp12 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wb (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wo (wceq K M) (wcel K (co (co M c1 caddc) N cfz)))) $;

--| Choices for an element of a finite interval of integers. (Contributed by Jeff Madsen, 2-Sep-2009.)
theorem fzm1 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wb (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wo (wcel K (co M (co N c1 cmin) cfz)) (wceq K N))) $;

--| No finite set of sequential integers equals an upper set of integers. (Contributed by NM, 11-Dec-2005.)
theorem fzneuz (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wcel K cz)) (wn (wceq (co M N cfz) (cfv K cuz))) $;

--| Disjointness of the upper integers and a finite sequence. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-Jun-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2013.)
theorem fznuz (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wn (wcel K (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cuz))) $;

--| Disjointness of the upper integers and a finite sequence. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Aug-2013.)
theorem uznfz (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (cfv N cuz)) (wn (wcel K (co M (co N c1 cmin) cfz))) $;

--| One plus the upper bound of a finite set of integers is not a member of that set. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Dec-2017.)
theorem fzp1nel (M N: class):
  $ wn (wcel (co N c1 caddc) (co M N cfz)) $;

--| Reversal of scanning order inside of a quantification over a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 25-Nov-2005.)
theorem fzrevral {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j) (K M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz) (wcel K cz)) (wb (wral j ph (co M N cfz)) (wral k (wsbc j ph (co K (cv k) cmin)) (co (co K N cmin) (co K M cmin) cfz))) $;

--| Reversal of scanning order inside of a quantification over a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 25-Nov-2005.)
theorem fzrevral2 {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j) (K M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz) (wcel K cz)) (wb (wral j ph (co (co K N cmin) (co K M cmin) cfz)) (wral k (wsbc j ph (co K (cv k) cmin)) (co M N cfz))) $;

--| Reversal of scanning order inside of a quantification over a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 20-Nov-2005.)
theorem fzrevral3 {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j) (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wral j ph (co M N cfz)) (wral k (wsbc j ph (co (co M N caddc) (cv k) cmin)) (co M N cfz))) $;

--| Shift the scanning order inside of a quantification over a finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by NM, 27-Nov-2005.)
theorem fzshftral {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j) (K M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz) (wcel K cz)) (wb (wral j ph (co M N cfz)) (wral k (wsbc j ph (co (cv k) K cmin)) (co (co M K caddc) (co N K caddc) cfz))) $;

--| Membership of an integer greater than 1 decreased by 1 in a 1 based finite set of sequential integers (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 14-Sep-2018.)
theorem ige2m1fz1 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel (co N c1 cmin) (co c1 N cfz)) $;

--| Membership in a 0 based finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 18-Jun-2018.) (Proof shortened by Alexander van der Vekens, 15-Sep-2018.)
theorem ige2m1fz (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wbr c2 N cle)) (wcel (co N c1 cmin) (co cc0 N cfz)) $;

--| Membership in a finite set of sequential nonnegative integers. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem elfz2nn0 (K N: class):
  $ wb (wcel K (co cc0 N cfz)) (w3a (wcel K cn0) (wcel N cn0) (wbr K N cle)) $;

--| Characterization of a finite set of sequential nonnegative integers. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-2005.)
theorem fznn0 (K N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wb (wcel K (co cc0 N cfz)) (wa (wcel K cn0) (wbr K N cle))) $;

--| A member of a finite set of sequential nonnegative integers is a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by NM, 5-Aug-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem elfznn0 (K N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co cc0 N cfz)) (wcel K cn0) $;

--| The upper bound of a nonempty finite set of sequential nonnegative integers is a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by NM, 16-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem elfz3nn0 (K N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co cc0 N cfz)) (wcel N cn0) $;

--| 0 is an element of a finite set of sequential nonnegative integers with a nonnegative integer as upper bound. (Contributed by AV, 6-Apr-2018.)
theorem _0elfz (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wcel cc0 (co cc0 N cfz)) $;

--| A nonnegative integer is always part of the finite set of sequential nonnegative integers with this integer as upper bound. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 21-Mar-2018.)
theorem nn0fz0 (N: class):
  $ wb (wcel N cn0) (wcel N (co cc0 N cfz)) $;

--| An element of a finite set of sequential nonnegative integers is an element of an extended finite set of sequential nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 28-Mar-2018.) (Proof shortened by OpenAI, 25-Mar-2020.)
theorem elfz0add (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn0) (wcel B2 cn0)) (wi (wcel N (co cc0 A2 cfz)) (wcel N (co cc0 (co A2 B2 caddc) cfz))) $;

--| An element of a finite set of sequential nonnegative integers is an element of an extended finite set of sequential nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 28-Mar-2018.) Obsolete version of ~ elfz0add as of 25-Mar-2020. (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem elfz0addOLD (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn0) (wcel B2 cn0)) (wi (wcel N (co cc0 A2 cfz)) (wcel N (co cc0 (co A2 B2 caddc) cfz))) $;

--| An integer range from 0 to 2 is an unordered triple. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 1-Feb-2018.)
theorem fz0tp:
  $ wceq (co cc0 c2 cfz) (ctp cc0 c1 c2) $;

--| An element of a finite set of sequential nonnegative integers is an element of a finite set of sequential nonnegative integers with the upper bound being an element of the finite set of sequential nonnegative integers with the same lower bound as for the first interval and the element under consideration as upper bound. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 3-Apr-2018.)
theorem elfz0ubfz0 (K L N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel K (co cc0 N cfz)) (wcel L (co K N cfz))) (wcel K (co cc0 L cfz)) $;

--| A member of a finite set of sequential nonnegative integers is a member of a finite set of sequential nonnegative integers with a member of a finite set of sequential nonnegative integers starting at the upper bound of the first interval. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 27-May-2018.)
theorem elfz0fzfz0 (L M N X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M (co cc0 L cfz)) (wcel N (co L X cfz))) (wcel M (co cc0 N cfz)) $;

--| If a member of a finite set of sequential integers with a lower bound being a member of a finite set of sequential nonnegative integers with the same upper bound, this member is also a member of the finite set of sequential nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 21-Apr-2018.)
theorem fz0fzelfz0 (R M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N (co cc0 R cfz)) (wcel M (co N R cfz))) (wcel M (co cc0 R cfz)) $;

--| Subtraction closure for a member of a finite set of sequential nonnegative integers. (Contributed by NM, 26-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem fznn0sub2 (K N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co cc0 N cfz)) (wcel (co N K cmin) (co cc0 N cfz)) $;

--| Membership of an integer greater than L decreased by L in a finite set of sequential nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 16-Sep-2018.)
theorem uzsubfz0 (L N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel L cn0) (wcel N (cfv L cuz))) (wcel (co N L cmin) (co cc0 N cfz)) $;

--| The difference of an integer in a finite set of sequential nonnegative integers and and an integer of a finite set of sequential integers with the same upper bound and the nonnegative integer as lower bound is a member of the finite set of sequential nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 6-Jun-2018.)
theorem fz0fzdiffz0 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M (co cc0 N cfz)) (wcel K (co M N cfz))) (wcel (co K M cmin) (co cc0 N cfz)) $;

--| Subtracting the lower bound of a finite set of sequential integers from an element of this set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 29-Mar-2018.) (Proof shortened by OpenAI, 25-Mar-2020.)
theorem elfzmlbm (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wcel (co K M cmin) (co cc0 (co N M cmin) cfz)) $;

--| Subtracting the lower bound of a finite set of sequential integers from an element of this set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 29-Mar-2018.) Obsolete version of ~ elfzmlbm as of 25-Mar-2020. (New usage is discouraged.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem elfzmlbmOLD (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) (wcel (co K M cmin) (co cc0 (co N M cmin) cfz)) $;

--| Subtracting the lower bound of a finite set of sequential integers from an element of this set. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 29-Mar-2018.)
theorem elfzmlbp (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cz) (wcel K (co M (co M N caddc) cfz))) (wcel (co K M cmin) (co cc0 N cfz)) $;

--| Lemma for theorems about the central binomial coefficient. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem fzctr (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wcel N (co cc0 (co c2 N cmul) cfz)) $;

--| The difference of two integers from a finite set of sequential nonnegative integers is also element of this finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 12-Jun-2018.)
theorem difelfzle (K M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K (co cc0 N cfz)) (wcel M (co cc0 N cfz)) (wbr K M cle)) (wcel (co M K cmin) (co cc0 N cfz)) $;

--| The difference of two integers from a finite set of sequential nonnegative integers increased by the upper bound is also element of this finite set of sequential integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 12-Jun-2018.)
theorem difelfznle (K M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K (co cc0 N cfz)) (wcel M (co cc0 N cfz)) (wn (wbr K M cle))) (wcel (co (co M N caddc) K cmin) (co cc0 N cfz)) $;

--| Express the set of nonnegative integers as the disjoint (see ~ nn0disj ) union of the first ` N + 1 ` values and the rest. (Contributed by AV, 8-Nov-2019.)
theorem nn0split (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wceq cn0 (cun (co cc0 N cfz) (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cuz))) $;

--| The first ` N + 1 ` elements of the set of nonnegative integers are distinct from any later members. (Contributed by AV, 8-Nov-2019.)
theorem nn0disj (N: class):
  $ wceq (cin (co cc0 N cfz) (cfv (co N c1 caddc) cuz)) c0 $;

--| A one value function. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 3-Dec-2017.)
theorem _1fv (P N V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N V) (wceq P (csn (cop cc0 N)))) (wa (wf (co cc0 cc0 cfz) V P) (wceq (cfv cc0 P) N)) $;

--| The first four function values of a word of length at least 4. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 18-Nov-2017.)
theorem _4fvwrd4 {a b c d: setvar} (P: class) (L: class a b c d) (V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel L (cfv c3 cuz)) (wf (co cc0 L cfz) V P)) (wrex a (wrex b (wrex c (wrex d (wa (wa (wceq (cfv cc0 P) (cv a)) (wceq (cfv c1 P) (cv b))) (wa (wceq (cfv c2 P) (cv c)) (wceq (cfv c3 P) (cv d)))) V) V) V) V) $;

--| Two functions over 0 based finite set of sequential integers are equal if and only if their domains have the same length and the function values are the same at each position. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 30-Jun-2018.)
theorem _2ffzeq {i: setvar} (P F M: class) (N X Y: class i):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cn0) (wf (co cc0 M cfz) X F) (wf (co cc0 N cfz) Y P)) (wb (wceq F P) (wa (wceq M N) (wral i (wceq (cfv (cv i) F) (cfv (cv i) P)) (co cc0 M cfz)))) $;

--| Syntax for half-open integer ranges.
term cfzo: class;

--| Define a function generating sets of integers using a _half-open_ range. Read ` ( M ..^ N ) ` as the integers from ` M ` up to, but not including, ` N ` ; contrast with ` ( M ... N ) ` ~ df-fz , which includes ` N ` . Not including the endpoint simplifies a number of formulae related to cardinality and splitting; contrast ~ fzosplit with ~ fzsplit , for instance. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Aug-2015.)
axiom df_fzo {m n: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfzo (cmpt2 m n cz cz (co (cv m) (co (cv n) c1 cmin) cfz)) $;

--| Functionality of the half-open integer set function. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzof:
  $ wf (cxp cz cz) (cpw cz) cfzo $;

--| Reverse closure for half-open integer sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem elfzoel1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co B2 C cfzo)) (wcel B2 cz) $;

--| Reverse closure for half-open integer sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem elfzoel2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co B2 C cfzo)) (wcel C cz) $;

--| Reverse closure for half-open integer sets. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem elfzoelz (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co B2 C cfzo)) (wcel A2 cz) $;

--| Value of the half-open integer set in terms of the closed integer set. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzoval (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wceq (co M N cfzo) (co M (co N c1 cmin) cfz)) $;

--| Membership in a half-open finite set of integers. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem elfzo (K M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cz) (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wcel K (co M N cfzo)) (wa (wbr M K cle) (wbr K N clt))) $;

--| Membership in a half-open integer interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.)
theorem elfzo2 (K M N: class):
  $ wb (wcel K (co M N cfzo)) (w3a (wcel K (cfv M cuz)) (wcel N cz) (wbr K N clt)) $;

--| Membership in a half-open integer interval. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.)
theorem elfzouz (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfzo)) (wcel K (cfv M cuz)) $;

--| The left endpoint of a half-open integer interval is in the set iff the two arguments are integers with ` M < N ` . This provides an alternative notation for the "strict upper integer" predicate by analogy to the "weak upper integer" predicate ` M e. ( ZZ>= `` N ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.)
theorem fzolb (M N: class):
  $ wb (wcel M (co M N cfzo)) (w3a (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz) (wbr M N clt)) $;

--| The left endpoint of a half-open integer interval is in the set iff the two arguments are integers with ` M < N ` . This provides an alternative notation for the "strict upper integer" predicate by analogy to the "weak upper integer" predicate ` M e. ( ZZ>= `` N ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.)
theorem fzolb2 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wcel M (co M N cfzo)) (wbr M N clt)) $;

--| A member in a half-open integer interval is greater than or equal to the lower bound. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem elfzole1 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfzo)) (wbr M K cle) $;

--| A member in a half-open integer interval is less than the upper bound. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem elfzolt2 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfzo)) (wbr K N clt) $;

--| Membership in a half-open integer interval implies that the bounds are unequal. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem elfzolt3 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfzo)) (wbr M N clt) $;

--| A member in a half-open integer interval is less than the upper bound. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.)
theorem elfzolt2b (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfzo)) (wcel K (co K N cfzo)) $;

--| Membership in a half-open integer interval implies that the bounds are unequal. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.)
theorem elfzolt3b (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfzo)) (wcel M (co M N cfzo)) $;

--| A half-open range does not contain its right endpoint. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 25-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzonel (A2 B2: class):
  $ wn (wcel B2 (co A2 B2 cfzo)) $;

--| The upper bound of a half-open range is greater or equal to an element of the range. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.)
theorem elfzouz2 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfzo)) (wcel N (cfv K cuz)) $;

--| A half-open range is contained in the corresponding closed range. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem elfzofz (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfzo)) (wcel K (co M N cfz)) $;

--| Express membership in a half-open integer interval in terms of the "less than or equal" and "less than" predicates on integers, resp. ` K e. ( ZZ>= `` M ) <-> M <_ K ` , ` K e. ( K ..^ N ) <-> K < N ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.)
theorem elfzo3 (K M N: class):
  $ wb (wcel K (co M N cfzo)) (wa (wcel K (cfv M cuz)) (wcel K (co K N cfzo))) $;

--| A half-open integer interval is inhabited iff it contains its left endpoint. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 20-Apr-2020.)
theorem fzom {x3: setvar} (M N: class):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wcel (cv x3) (co M N cfzo))) (wcel M (co M N cfzo)) $;

--| A half-open range is contained in the corresponding closed range. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.)
theorem fzossfz (A2 B2: class):
  $ wss (co A2 B2 cfzo) (co A2 B2 cfz) $;

--| A half-open set of sequential integers is empty if the bounds are equal or reversed. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 30-Oct-2017.)
theorem fzon (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wbr N M cle) (wceq (co M N cfzo) c0)) $;

--| A half-open integer range is empty if the bounds are equal or reversed. (Contributed by AV, 20-Oct-2018.)
theorem fzonlt0 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wn (wbr M N clt)) (wceq (co M N cfzo) c0)) $;

--| Half-open sets with equal endpoints are empty. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.)
theorem fzo0 (A2: class):
  $ wceq (co A2 A2 cfzo) c0 $;

--| If ` K < N ` then ` N - K ` is a positive integer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jan-2017.)
theorem fzonnsub (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfzo)) (wcel (co N K cmin) cn) $;

--| If ` M < N ` then ` N - M ` is a positive integer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Jan-2017.)
theorem fzonnsub2 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfzo)) (wcel (co N M cmin) cn) $;

--| Subset relationship for half-open sequences of integers. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.)
theorem fzoss1 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (cfv M cuz)) (wss (co K N cfzo) (co M N cfzo)) $;

--| Subset relationship for half-open sequences of integers. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.)
theorem fzoss2 (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv K cuz)) (wss (co M K cfzo) (co M N cfzo)) $;

--| Subset of a half open range. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 1-Nov-2017.)
theorem fzossrbm1 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wss (co cc0 (co N c1 cmin) cfzo) (co cc0 N cfzo)) $;

--| Subset relationship for half-open integer ranges with lower bounds 0 and 1. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 18-Mar-2018.)
theorem fzo0ss1 (N: class):
  $ wss (co c1 N cfzo) (co cc0 N cfzo) $;

--| A half-open integer range starting at a nonnegative integer is a subset of the nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 13-May-2018.)
theorem fzossnn0 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cn0) (wss (co M N cfzo) cn0) $;

--| One direction of splitting a half-open integer range in half. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzospliti (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co B2 C cfzo)) (wcel D cz)) (wo (wcel A2 (co B2 D cfzo)) (wcel A2 (co D C cfzo))) $;

--| Split a half-open integer range in half. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzosplit (B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wcel D (co B2 C cfz)) (wceq (co B2 C cfzo) (cun (co B2 D cfzo) (co D C cfzo))) $;

--| Abutting half-open integer ranges are disjoint. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 14-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzodisj (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wceq (cin (co A2 B2 cfzo) (co B2 C cfzo)) c0 $;

--| Split an upper integer set into a half-open integer range and another upper integer set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2016.)
theorem fzouzsplit (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cfv A2 cuz)) (wceq (cfv A2 cuz) (cun (co A2 B2 cfzo) (cfv B2 cuz))) $;

--| A half-open integer range does not overlap the upper integer range starting at the endpoint of the first range. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 21-Sep-2016.)
theorem fzouzdisj (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq (cin (co A2 B2 cfzo) (cfv B2 cuz)) c0 $;

--| An integer is strictly greater than zero iff it is a member of ` NN ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.)
theorem lbfzo0 (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel cc0 (co cc0 A2 cfzo)) (wcel A2 cn) $;

--| Membership in a half-open integer range based at 0. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.)
theorem elfzo0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (co cc0 B2 cfzo)) (w3a (wcel A2 cn0) (wcel B2 cn) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) $;

--| An integer between 1 and an upper bound of a half-open integer range is not 0 and between 0 and the upper bound of the half-open integer range. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 21-Mar-2018.)
theorem fzo1fzo0n0 (K N: class):
  $ wb (wcel K (co c1 N cfzo)) (wa (wcel K (co cc0 N cfzo)) (wne K cc0)) $;

--| Membership in a half-open range of nonnegative integers, generalization of ~ elfzo0 requiring the upper bound to be an integer only. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 23-Sep-2018.)
theorem elfzo0z (A2 B2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 (co cc0 B2 cfzo)) (w3a (wcel A2 cn0) (wcel B2 cz) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) $;

--| A member in a half-open range of nonnegative integers is less than or equal to the upper bound of the range. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 23-Sep-2018.)
theorem elfzo0le (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co cc0 B2 cfzo)) (wbr A2 B2 cle) $;

--| A member of a half-open range of nonnegative integers is a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 21-May-2018.)
theorem elfzonn0 (K N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co cc0 N cfzo)) (wcel K cn0) $;

--| The result of subtracting a nonnegative integer from a positive integer and adding another nonnegative integer which is less than the first one is less then the positive integer. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 19-May-2018.)
theorem fzonmapblen (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (co cc0 N cfzo)) (wcel B2 (co cc0 N cfzo)) (wbr B2 A2 clt)) (wbr (co B2 (co N A2 cmin) caddc) N clt) $;

--| If a nonnegative integer in a finite interval of integers is not the upper bound of the interval, it is contained in the corresponding half-open integer range. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 15-Jun-2018.)
theorem fzofzim (K M: class):
  $ wi (wa (wne K M) (wcel K (co cc0 M cfz))) (wcel K (co cc0 M cfzo)) $;

--| Half-open integer ranges starting with 1 are subsets of NN. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Dec-2016.)
theorem fzossnn (N: class):
  $ wss (co c1 N cfzo) cn $;

--| Membership in a half-open integer range based at 1. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 14-Feb-2017.)
theorem elfzo1 (M N: class):
  $ wb (wcel N (co c1 M cfzo)) (w3a (wcel N cn) (wcel M cn) (wbr N M clt)) $;

--| A half-open integer range based at 0 is inhabited precisely if the upper bound is a positive integer. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 20-Apr-2020.)
theorem fzo0m {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wex x3 (wcel (cv x3) (co cc0 A2 cfzo))) (wcel A2 cn) $;

--| Translate membership in a half-open integer range. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzoaddel (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co B2 C cfzo)) (wcel D cz)) (wcel (co A2 D caddc) (co (co B2 D caddc) (co C D caddc) cfzo)) $;

--| Translate membership in a shifted-down half-open integer range. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzoaddel2 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 (co cc0 (co B2 C cmin) cfzo)) (wcel B2 cz) (wcel C cz)) (wcel (co A2 C caddc) (co C B2 cfzo)) $;

--| Translate membership in a half-open integer range. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzosubel (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co B2 C cfzo)) (wcel D cz)) (wcel (co A2 D cmin) (co (co B2 D cmin) (co C D cmin) cfzo)) $;

--| Membership in a translated half-open integer range implies translated membership in the original range. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzosubel2 (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co (co B2 C caddc) (co B2 D caddc) cfzo)) (w3a (wcel B2 cz) (wcel C cz) (wcel D cz))) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmin) (co C D cfzo)) $;

--| Membership in a translated half-open integer range when the original range is zero-based. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzosubel3 (A2 B2 D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co B2 (co B2 D caddc) cfzo)) (wcel D cz)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmin) (co cc0 D cfzo)) $;

--| Membership of the difference of integers in a half-open range of nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 17-Sep-2018.)
theorem eluzgtdifelfzo (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz)) (wi (wa (wcel N (cfv A2 cuz)) (wbr B2 A2 clt)) (wcel (co N A2 cmin) (co cc0 (co N B2 cmin) cfzo))) $;

--| Membership of an integer greater than 1 decreased by 2 in a half-open range of nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 3-Oct-2018.)
theorem ige2m2fzo (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel (co N c2 cmin) (co cc0 (co N c1 cmin) cfzo)) $;

--| Translate membership in a half-open integer range. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 28-Sep-2018.)
theorem fzocatel (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 (co cc0 (co B2 C caddc) cfzo)) (wn (wcel A2 (co cc0 B2 cfzo)))) (wa (wcel B2 cz) (wcel C cz))) (wcel (co A2 B2 cmin) (co cc0 C cfzo)) $;

--| If an integer in a 1 based finite set of sequential integers is subtracted from the upper bound of this finite set of sequential integers, the result is contained in a half-open range of nonnegative integers with the same upper bound. (Contributed by AV, 18-Mar-2018.) (Revised by AV, 30-Oct-2018.)
theorem ubmelfzo (K N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co c1 N cfz)) (wcel (co N K cmin) (co cc0 N cfzo)) $;

--| If an integer is in a half-open range of nonnegative integers with a difference as upper bound, the sum of the integer with the subtrahend of the difference is in the a half-open range of nonnegative integers containing the minuend of the difference. (Contributed by AV, 13-Nov-2018.)
theorem elfzodifsumelfzo (P I M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M (co cc0 N cfz)) (wcel N (co cc0 P cfz))) (wi (wcel I (co cc0 (co N M cmin) cfzo)) (wcel (co I M caddc) (co cc0 P cfzo))) $;

--| Membership of an integer incremented by one in a half-open range of nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 24-Jun-2018.) (Proof shortened by AV, 5-Jan-2020.)
theorem elfzom1elp1fzo (I N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cz) (wcel I (co cc0 (co N c1 cmin) cfzo))) (wcel (co I c1 caddc) (co cc0 N cfzo)) $;

--| Membership in a half-open range of nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 18-Jun-2018.)
theorem elfzom1elfzo (I N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cz) (wcel I (co cc0 (co N c1 cmin) cfzo))) (wcel I (co cc0 N cfzo)) $;

--| Expressing a closed integer range as a half-open integer range. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzval3 (M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wceq (co M N cfz) (co M (co N c1 caddc) cfzo)) $;

--| Expressing a singleton as a half-open range. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzosn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cz) (wceq (co A2 (co A2 c1 caddc) cfzo) (csn A2)) $;

--| Membership of an integer in the smallest open range of integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 22-Sep-2018.)
theorem elfzomin (Z: class):
  $ wi (wcel Z cz) (wcel Z (co Z (co Z c1 caddc) cfzo)) $;

--| Membership of an integer increased by a nonnegative integer in a half- open integer range. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 22-Sep-2018.)
theorem zpnn0elfzo (N Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel Z cz) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel (co Z N caddc) (co Z (co (co Z N caddc) c1 caddc) cfzo)) $;

--| Membership of an integer increased by a nonnegative integer in a half- open integer range. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 22-Sep-2018.)
theorem zpnn0elfzo1 (N Z: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel Z cz) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel (co Z N caddc) (co Z (co Z (co N c1 caddc) caddc) cfzo)) $;

--| Removing a singleton from a half-open integer range at the end. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 23-Mar-2018.)
theorem fzosplitsnm1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 (cfv (co A2 c1 caddc) cuz))) (wceq (co A2 B2 cfzo) (cun (co A2 (co B2 c1 cmin) cfzo) (csn (co B2 c1 cmin)))) $;

--| If an element of a half-open integer range is not less than the upper bound of the range decreased by 1, it must be equal to the upper bound of the range decreased by 1. (Contributed by AV, 3-Nov-2018.)
theorem elfzonlteqm1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co cc0 B2 cfzo)) (wn (wbr A2 (co B2 c1 cmin) clt))) (wceq A2 (co B2 c1 cmin)) $;

--| A nonnegative integer is element of the half-open range of nonnegative integers with the element increased by one as an upper bound. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 5-Aug-2018.)
theorem fzonn0p1 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wcel N (co cc0 (co N c1 caddc) cfzo)) $;

--| A half-open range of nonnegative integers is a subset of a half-open range of nonnegative integers with the upper bound increased by one. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 5-Aug-2018.)
theorem fzossfzop1 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wss (co cc0 N cfzo) (co cc0 (co N c1 caddc) cfzo)) $;

--| If a nonnegative integer is element of a half-open range of nonnegative integers, increasing this integer by one results in an element of a half- open range of nonnegative integers with the upper bound increased by one. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 5-Aug-2018.)
theorem fzonn0p1p1 (I N: class):
  $ wi (wcel I (co cc0 N cfzo)) (wcel (co I c1 caddc) (co cc0 (co N c1 caddc) cfzo)) $;

--| Increasing an element of a half-open range of nonnegative integers by 1 results in an element of the half-open range of nonnegative integers with an upper bound increased by 1. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 1-Aug-2018.)
theorem elfzom1p1elfzo (N X: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn) (wcel X (co cc0 (co N c1 cmin) cfzo))) (wcel (co X c1 caddc) (co cc0 N cfzo)) $;

--| Half-open integer ranges starting with 0 are subsets of NN0. (Contributed by Thierry Arnoux, 8-Oct-2018.)
theorem fzo0ssnn0 (N: class):
  $ wss (co cc0 N cfzo) cn0 $;

--| Expressing the singleton of ` 0 ` as a half-open integer range. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzo01:
  $ wceq (co cc0 c1 cfzo) (csn cc0) $;

--| A 1-based half-open integer interval up to, but not including, 2 is a singleton. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 31-Jan-2018.)
theorem fzo12sn:
  $ wceq (co c1 c2 cfzo) (csn c1) $;

--| A half-open integer range from 0 to 2 is an unordered pair. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 4-Dec-2017.)
theorem fzo0to2pr:
  $ wceq (co cc0 c2 cfzo) (cpr cc0 c1) $;

--| A half-open integer range from 0 to 3 is an unordered triple. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 9-Nov-2017.)
theorem fzo0to3tp:
  $ wceq (co cc0 c3 cfzo) (ctp cc0 c1 c2) $;

--| A half-open integer range from 0 to 4 is a union of two unordered pairs. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 17-Nov-2017.)
theorem fzo0to42pr:
  $ wceq (co cc0 c4 cfzo) (cun (cpr cc0 c1) (cpr c2 c3)) $;

--| A half-open range of nonnegative integers is the union of the singleton set containing 0 and a half-open range of positive integers. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 18-May-2018.)
theorem fzo0sn0fzo1 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (co cc0 N cfzo) (cun (csn cc0) (co c1 N cfzo))) $;

--| The endpoint of a half-open integer range. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.)
theorem fzoend (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co A2 B2 cfzo)) (wcel (co B2 c1 cmin) (co A2 B2 cfzo)) $;

--| The endpoint of a zero-based half-open range. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 27-Aug-2015.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Sep-2015.)
theorem fzo0end (B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 cn) (wcel (co B2 c1 cmin) (co cc0 B2 cfzo)) $;

--| Subset relationship for half-open integer ranges. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 16-Mar-2018.)
theorem ssfzo12 (K L M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cz) (wcel L cz) (wbr K L clt)) (wi (wss (co K L cfzo) (co M N cfzo)) (wa (wbr M K cle) (wbr L N cle))) $;

--| Subset relationship for half-open integer ranges. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 5-Nov-2018.)
theorem ssfzo12bi (K L M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel K cz) (wcel L cz)) (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wbr K L clt)) (wb (wss (co K L cfzo) (co M N cfzo)) (wa (wbr M K cle) (wbr L N cle))) $;

--| The result of subtracting 1 and an integer of a half-open range of nonnegative integers from the upper bound of this range is contained in this range. (Contributed by AV, 23-Mar-2018.) (Revised by AV, 30-Oct-2018.)
theorem ubmelm1fzo (K N: class):
  $ wi (wcel K (co cc0 N cfzo)) (wcel (co (co N K cmin) c1 cmin) (co cc0 N cfzo)) $;

--| If a point is in a half-open range, the next point is in the closed range. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzofzp1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C (co A2 B2 cfzo)) (wcel (co C c1 caddc) (co A2 B2 cfz)) $;

--| If a point is in a half-open range, the next point is in the closed range. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem fzofzp1b (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel C (cfv A2 cuz)) (wb (wcel C (co A2 B2 cfzo)) (wcel (co C c1 caddc) (co A2 B2 cfz))) $;

--| An integer is a member of a 1-based finite set of sequential integers iff its predecessor is a member of the corresponding 0-based set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 27-Sep-2015.)
theorem elfzom1b (K N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cz) (wcel N cz)) (wb (wcel K (co c1 N cfzo)) (wcel (co K c1 cmin) (co cc0 (co N c1 cmin) cfzo))) $;

--| If an element of a half-open integer range is not contained in the lower subrange, it must be in the upper subrange. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 30-Mar-2018.)
theorem elfzonelfzo (R K M N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wi (wa (wcel K (co M R cfzo)) (wn (wcel K (co M N cfzo)))) (wcel K (co N R cfzo))) $;

--| An integer increased by another integer is an element of a half-open integer range if and only if the integer is contained in the half-open integer range with bounds decreased by the other integer. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 30-Mar-2018.)
theorem elfzomelpfzo (K L M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wa (wcel K cz) (wcel L cz))) (wb (wcel K (co (co M L cmin) (co N L cmin) cfzo)) (wcel (co K L caddc) (co M N cfzo))) $;

--| A Peano-postulate-like theorem for downward closure of a half-open integer range. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 1-Oct-2015.)
theorem peano2fzor (K M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel K (cfv M cuz)) (wcel (co K c1 caddc) (co M N cfzo))) (wcel K (co M N cfzo)) $;

--| Extending a half-open range by a singleton on the end. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzosplitsn (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cfv A2 cuz)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 c1 caddc) cfzo) (cun (co A2 B2 cfzo) (csn B2))) $;

--| Extending a half-open integer range by an unordered pair at the end. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 22-Sep-2018.)
theorem fzosplitprm1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) (wceq (co A2 (co B2 c1 caddc) cfzo) (cun (co A2 (co B2 c1 cmin) cfzo) (cpr (co B2 c1 cmin) B2))) $;

--| Membership in a half-open range extended by a singleton. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzosplitsni (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cfv A2 cuz)) (wb (wcel C (co A2 (co B2 c1 caddc) cfzo)) (wo (wcel C (co A2 B2 cfzo)) (wceq C B2))) $;

--| A finite interval of integers as union of a half-open integer range and a singleton. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 15-Jun-2018.)
theorem fzisfzounsn (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel B2 (cfv A2 cuz)) (wceq (co A2 B2 cfz) (cun (co A2 B2 cfzo) (csn B2))) $;

--| Two possibilities for a number one greater than a number in a half-open range. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 23-Aug-2015.)
theorem fzostep1 (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 (co B2 C cfzo)) (wo (wcel (co A2 c1 caddc) (co B2 C cfzo)) (wceq (co A2 c1 caddc) C)) $;

--| Shift the scanning order inside of a quantification over a half-open integer range, analogous to ~ fzshftral . (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 23-Sep-2018.)
theorem fzoshftral {j k: setvar} (ph: wff j) (K M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz) (wcel K cz)) (wb (wral j ph (co M N cfzo)) (wral k (wsbc j ph (co (cv k) K cmin)) (co (co M K caddc) (co N K caddc) cfzo))) $;

--| Induction on the integers from ` M ` to ` N ` inclusive. The first four hypotheses give us the substitution instances we need; the last two are the basis and the induction step. Version of ~ fzind using integer range definitions. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Feb-2016.)
theorem fzind2 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch th ta: wff y1) (K: class y1) (M N: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) M) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) c1 caddc)) (wb ph th) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) K) (wb ph ta) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) ps $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv y1) (co M N cfzo)) (wi ch th) $ >
  $ wi (wcel K (co M N cfz)) ta $;

--| The function values for the borders of a finite interval of integers, which is the domain of the function, are not in the image of the interior of the interval iff the intersection of the images of the interior and the borders is empty. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 31-Oct-2017.) (Revised by AV, 5-Feb-2021.)
theorem fvinim0ffz (F K V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wf (co cc0 K cfz) V F) (wcel K cn0)) (wb (wceq (cin (cima F (cpr cc0 K)) (cima F (co c1 K cfzo))) c0) (wa (wnel (cfv cc0 F) (cima F (co c1 K cfzo))) (wnel (cfv K F) (cima F (co c1 K cfzo))))) $;

--| The difference between two elements in a half-open range of nonnegative integers is greater than the negation of the upper bound and less than the upper bound of the range. (Contributed by AV, 20-Mar-2021.)
theorem subfzo0 (I J N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel I (co cc0 N cfzo)) (wcel J (co cc0 N cfzo))) (wa (wbr (cneg N) (co I J cmin) clt) (wbr (co I J cmin) N clt)) $;

--| Rational trichotomy. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 6-Oct-2021.)
theorem qtri3or (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cq) (wcel N cq)) (w3o (wbr M N clt) (wceq M N) (wbr N M clt)) $;

--| Rational trichotomy. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 6-Oct-2021.)
theorem qletric (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cq) (wcel B2 cq)) (wo (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 A2 cle)) $;

--| Rational trichotomy. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 7-Oct-2021.)
theorem qlelttric (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cq) (wcel B2 cq)) (wo (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr B2 A2 clt)) $;

--| 'Less than' expressed in terms of 'less than or equal to'. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem qltnle (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cq) (wcel B2 cq)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wn (wbr B2 A2 cle))) $;

--| Equality of rationals is decidable. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem qdceq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cq) (wcel B2 cq)) (wdc (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ qbtwnz . Induction step. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem qbtwnzlemstep {m: setvar} (A2 K: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cn) (wcel A2 cq) (wrex m (wa (wbr (cv m) A2 cle) (wbr A2 (co (cv m) (co K c1 caddc) caddc) clt)) cz)) (wrex m (wa (wbr (cv m) A2 cle) (wbr A2 (co (cv m) K caddc) clt)) cz) $;

--| Lemma for ~ qbtwnz . Shrinking the range around the given rational number. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem qbtwnzlemshrink {x3 m: setvar} (A2: class) (J: class x3):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cq) (wcel J cn) (wrex m (wa (wbr (cv m) A2 cle) (wbr A2 (co (cv m) J caddc) clt)) cz)) (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cv x3) A2 cle) (wbr A2 (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) clt)) cz) $;

--| Lemma for ~ qbtwnz . Existence of the integer. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem qbtwnzlemex {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wrex x3 (wa (wbr (cv x3) A2 cle) (wbr A2 (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) clt)) cz) $;

--| There is a unique greatest integer less than or equal to a rational number. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem qbtwnz {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wreu x3 (wa (wbr (cv x3) A2 cle) (wbr A2 (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) clt)) cz) $;

--| Extend class notation with floor (greatest integer) function.
term cfl: class;

--| Extend class notation to include the ceiling function.
term cceil: class;

--| Define the floor (greatest integer less than or equal to) function. See ~ flval for its value, ~ flqlelt for its basic property, and ~ flqcl for its closure. For example, ` ( |_ `` ( 3 / 2 ) ) = 1 ` while ` ( |_ `` -u ( 3 / 2 ) ) = -u 2 ` ( ~ ex-fl ). Although we define this on real numbers so that notations are similar to the Metamath Proof Explorer, in the absence of excluded middle few theorems will be possible beyond the rationals. Imagine a real number which is around 2.99995 or 3.00001 . In order to determine whether its floor is 2 or 3, it would be necessary to compute the number to arbitrary precision. The term "floor" was coined by Ken Iverson. He also invented a mathematical notation for floor, consisting of an L-shaped left bracket and its reflection as a right bracket. In APL, the left-bracket alone is used, and we borrow this idea. (Thanks to Paul Chapman for this information.) (Contributed by NM, 14-Nov-2004.)
axiom df_fl {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cfl (cmpt x3 cr (crio y1 (wa (wbr (cv y1) (cv x3) cle) (wbr (cv x3) (co (cv y1) c1 caddc) clt)) cz)) $;

--| The ceiling (least integer greater than or equal to) function. Defined in ISO 80000-2:2009(E) operation 2-9.18 and the "NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions" , front introduction, "Common Notations and Definitions" section at ~ http://dlmf.nist.gov/front/introduction#Sx4 . See ~ ceilqval for its value, ~ ceilqge and ~ ceilqm1lt for its basic properties, and ~ ceilqcl for its closure. For example, ` ( |^ `` ( 3 / 2 ) ) = 2 ` while ` ( |^ `` -u ( 3 / 2 ) ) = -u 1 ` ( ~ ex-ceil ). As described in ~ df-fl most theorems are only for rationals, not reals. The symbol ` |^ ` is inspired by the gamma shaped left bracket of the usual notation. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 19-May-2015.)
axiom df_ceil {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cceil (cmpt x3 cr (cneg (cfv (cneg (cv x3)) cfl))) $;

--| Value of the floor (greatest integer) function. The floor of ` A ` is the (unique) integer less than or equal to ` A ` whose successor is strictly greater than ` A ` . (Contributed by NM, 14-Nov-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Nov-2013.)
theorem flval {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 cfl) (crio x3 (wa (wbr (cv x3) A2 cle) (wbr A2 (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) clt)) cz)) $;

--| The floor (greatest integer) function yields an integer when applied to a rational (closure law). It would presumably be possible to prove a similar result for some real numbers (for example, those apart from any integer), but not real numbers in general. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wcel (cfv A2 cfl) cz) $;

--| A basic property of the floor (greatest integer) function. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqlelt (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wa (wbr (cfv A2 cfl) A2 cle) (wbr A2 (co (cfv A2 cfl) c1 caddc) clt)) $;

--| The floor (greatest integer) function is an integer (closure law). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqcld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cq) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 cfl) cz) $;

--| A basic property of the floor (greatest integer) function. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqle (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wbr (cfv A2 cfl) A2 cle) $;

--| A basic property of the floor (greatest integer) function. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqltp1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wbr A2 (co (cfv A2 cfl) c1 caddc) clt) $;

--| The fractional part of a rational number is less than one. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem qfraclt1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wbr (co A2 (cfv A2 cfl) cmin) c1 clt) $;

--| The fractional part of a rational number is nonnegative. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem qfracge0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wbr cc0 (co A2 (cfv A2 cfl) cmin) cle) $;

--| The floor function value is the greatest integer less than or equal to its argument. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqge (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cq) (wcel B2 cz)) (wb (wbr B2 A2 cle) (wbr B2 (cfv A2 cfl) cle)) $;

--| The floor function value is less than the next integer. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqlt (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cq) (wcel B2 cz)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (cfv A2 cfl) B2 clt)) $;

--| An integer is its own floor. (Contributed by NM, 15-Nov-2004.)
theorem flid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cz) (wceq (cfv A2 cfl) A2) $;

--| The floor function is idempotent. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqidm (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 cfl) cfl) (cfv A2 cfl)) $;

--| A rational number equals its floor iff it is an integer. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 9-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqidz (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 cfl) A2) (wcel A2 cz)) $;

--| If A is not an integer, then the floor of A is less than A. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 9-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqltnz (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cq) (wn (wcel A2 cz))) (wbr (cfv A2 cfl) A2 clt) $;

--| Ordering relationship for the greatest integer function. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 9-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqwordi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cq) (wcel B2 cq) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wbr (cfv A2 cfl) (cfv B2 cfl) cle) $;

--| Ordering relationship for the greatest integer function. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 9-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqword2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cq) (wcel B2 cq) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wcel (cfv B2 cfl) (cfv (cfv A2 cfl) cuz)) $;

--| A condition equivalent to floor. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 9-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqbi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cq) (wcel B2 cz)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 cfl) B2) (wa (wbr B2 A2 cle) (wbr A2 (co B2 c1 caddc) clt))) $;

--| A condition equivalent to floor. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 9-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqbi2 (F N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cz) (wcel F cq)) (wb (wceq (cfv (co N F caddc) cfl) N) (wa (wbr cc0 F cle) (wbr F c1 clt))) $;

--| The floor of a sum of an integer and a fraction is equal to the integer iff the denominator of the fraction is less than the numerator. (Contributed by AV, 14-Jul-2021.)
theorem adddivflid (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cn0) (wcel C cn)) (wb (wbr B2 C clt) (wceq (cfv (co A2 (co B2 C cdiv) caddc) cfl) A2)) $;

--| The floor of a number greater than or equal to 0 is a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqge0nn0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cq) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wcel (cfv A2 cfl) cn0) $;

--| The floor of a number greater than or equal to 1 is a positive integer. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqge1nn (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cq) (wbr c1 A2 cle)) (wcel (cfv A2 cfl) cn) $;

--| The floor function of a division of a nonnegative integer by a positive integer is a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 14-Apr-2018.)
theorem fldivnn0 (K L: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cn0) (wcel L cn)) (wcel (cfv (co K L cdiv) cfl) cn0) $;

--| The floor of a fraction is 0 iff the denominator is less than the numerator. (Contributed by AV, 8-Jul-2021.)
theorem divfl0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn0) (wcel B2 cn)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cdiv) cfl) cc0)) $;

--| An integer can be moved in and out of the floor of a sum. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqaddz (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cq) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 N caddc) cfl) (co (cfv A2 cfl) N caddc)) $;

--| An integer can be moved in and out of the floor of a sum. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqzadd (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cz) (wcel A2 cq)) (wceq (cfv (co N A2 caddc) cfl) (co N (cfv A2 cfl) caddc)) $;

--| Move a nonnegative integer in and out of a floor. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqmulnn0 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cn0) (wcel A2 cq)) (wbr (co N (cfv A2 cfl) cmul) (cfv (co N A2 cmul) cfl) cle) $;

--| A real bounded between an integer and its successor is nonnegative iff the integer is nonnegative. Second half of Lemma 13-4.1 of [Gleason] p. 217. (Contributed by NM, 12-Mar-2005.)
theorem btwnzge0 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel N cz)) (wa (wbr N A2 cle) (wbr A2 (co N c1 caddc) clt))) (wb (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 N cle)) $;

--| Two times an integer plus one is not negative iff the integer is not negative. (Contributed by AV, 19-Jun-2021.)
theorem _2tnp1ge0ge0 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wb (wbr cc0 (co (co c2 N cmul) c1 caddc) cle) (wbr cc0 N cle)) $;

--| Ordering relation for the floor of half of an integer. (Contributed by NM, 1-Jan-2006.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 7-Jun-2016.)
theorem flhalf (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wbr N (co c2 (cfv (co (co N c1 caddc) c2 cdiv) cfl) cmul) cle) $;

--| The floor function of a division of a nonnegative integer by a positive integer is less than or equal to the division. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 14-Apr-2018.)
theorem fldivnn0le (K L: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel K cn0) (wcel L cn)) (wbr (cfv (co K L cdiv) cfl) (co K L cdiv) cle) $;

--| The floor function of a division of a nonnegative integer by a positive integer is less than the division of a greater dividend by the same positive integer. (Contributed by Alexander van der Vekens, 14-Apr-2018.)
theorem flltdivnn0lt (K L N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel K cn0) (wcel N cn0) (wcel L cn)) (wi (wbr K N clt) (wbr (cfv (co K L cdiv) cfl) (co N L cdiv) clt)) $;

--| The floor of an integer equal to 3 or greater than 4, increased by 1, is less than or equal to the half of the integer minus 1. (Contributed by AV, 8-Jul-2021.)
theorem fldiv4p1lem1div2 (N: class):
  $ wi (wo (wceq N c3) (wcel N (cfv c5 cuz))) (wbr (co (cfv (co N c4 cdiv) cfl) c1 caddc) (co (co N c1 cmin) c2 cdiv) cle) $;

--| The value of the ceiling function. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 10-Oct-2021.)
theorem ceilqval (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wceq (cfv A2 cceil) (cneg (cfv (cneg A2) cfl))) $;

--| The ceiling function returns an integer (closure law). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem ceiqcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wcel (cneg (cfv (cneg A2) cfl)) cz) $;

--| Closure of the ceiling function. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem ceilqcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wcel (cfv A2 cceil) cz) $;

--| The ceiling of a real number is greater than or equal to that number. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem ceiqge (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wbr A2 (cneg (cfv (cneg A2) cfl)) cle) $;

--| The ceiling of a real number is greater than or equal to that number. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem ceilqge (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wbr A2 (cfv A2 cceil) cle) $;

--| One less than the ceiling of a real number is strictly less than that number. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem ceiqm1l (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wbr (co (cneg (cfv (cneg A2) cfl)) c1 cmin) A2 clt) $;

--| One less than the ceiling of a real number is strictly less than that number. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem ceilqm1lt (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wbr (co (cfv A2 cceil) c1 cmin) A2 clt) $;

--| The ceiling of a real number is the smallest integer greater than or equal to it. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem ceiqle (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cq) (wcel B2 cz) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wbr (cneg (cfv (cneg A2) cfl)) B2 cle) $;

--| The ceiling of a real number is the smallest integer greater than or equal to it. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem ceilqle (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cq) (wcel B2 cz) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wbr (cfv A2 cceil) B2 cle) $;

--| An integer is its own ceiling. (Contributed by AV, 30-Nov-2018.)
theorem ceilid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cz) (wceq (cfv A2 cceil) A2) $;

--| A rational number equals its ceiling iff it is an integer. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem ceilqidz (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wb (wcel A2 cz) (wceq (cfv A2 cceil) A2)) $;

--| The floor of a rational number is less than or equal to its ceiling. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqleceil (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wbr (cfv A2 cfl) (cfv A2 cceil) cle) $;

--| A rational number is an integer iff its floor equals its ceiling. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Oct-2021.)
theorem flqeqceilz (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wb (wcel A2 cz) (wceq (cfv A2 cfl) (cfv A2 cceil))) $;

--| The mapping ` G ` is a one-to-one mapping from ` _om ` onto upper integers that will be used to construct a recursive definition generator. Ordinal natural number 0 maps to complex number ` C ` (normally 0 for the upper integers ` NN0 ` or 1 for the upper integers ` NN ` ), 1 maps to ` C ` + 1, etc. This theorem shows the value of ` G ` at ordinal natural number zero. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 16-May-2020.)
theorem frec2uz0d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (C: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c0 G) C) $;

--| The value of ` G ` (see ~ frec2uz0d ) is an integer. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 16-May-2020.)
theorem frec2uzzd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3) (C: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 G) cz) $;

--| The value of ` G ` (see ~ frec2uz0d ) at a successor. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 16-May-2020.)
theorem frec2uzsucd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3) (C: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (csuc A2) G) (co (cfv A2 G) c1 caddc)) $;

--| The value ` G ` (see ~ frec2uz0d ) at an ordinal natural number is in the upper integers. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 16-May-2020.)
theorem frec2uzuzd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3) (C: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 G) (cfv C cuz)) $;

--| Less-than relation for ` G ` (see ~ frec2uz0d ). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 16-May-2020.)
theorem frec2uzltd {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3) (C: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wcel A2 B2) (wbr (cfv A2 G) (cfv B2 G) clt)) $;

--| The mapping ` G ` (see ~ frec2uz0d ) preserves order. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 16-May-2020.)
theorem frec2uzlt2d {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3) (C: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel A2 B2) (wbr (cfv A2 G) (cfv B2 G) clt)) $;

--| Range of ` G ` (see ~ frec2uz0d ). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 17-May-2020.)
theorem frec2uzrand {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (C: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (crn G) (cfv C cuz)) $;

--| ` G ` (see ~ frec2uz0d ) is a one-to-one onto mapping. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 17-May-2020.)
theorem frec2uzf1od {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (C: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf1o com (cfv C cuz) G) $;

--| ` G ` (see ~ frec2uz0d ) is an isomorphism from natural ordinals to upper integers. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 17-May-2020.)
theorem frec2uzisod {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (C: class) (G: class x3):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wiso com (cfv C cuz) cep clt G) $;

--| The function ` R ` (used in the definition of the recursive definition generator on upper integers) yields ordered pairs of integers and elements of ` S ` . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 27-May-2020.)
theorem frecuzrdgrrn {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (C: class) (D R: class x3 y1) (S F: class) (G: class x3) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cfv C cuz)) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F) S) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfrec (cmpt2 x3 y1 (cfv C cuz) S (cop (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F))) (cop C A2)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel D com)) (wcel (cfv D R) (cxp (cfv C cuz) S)) $;

--| A helper lemma for the value of a recursive definition generator on upper integers (typically either ` NN ` or ` NN0 ` ) with characteristic function ` F ( x , y ) ` and initial value ` A ` . This lemma shows that evaluating ` R ` at an element of ` _om ` gives an ordered pair whose first element is the index (translated from ` _om ` to ` ( ZZ>= `` C ) ` ). See comment in ~ frec2uz0d which describes ` G ` and the index translation. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 24-May-2020.)
theorem frec2uzrdg {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2: class x3 y1) (C: class) (R: class x3 y1) (S F: class) (G: class x3) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cfv C cuz)) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F) S) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfrec (cmpt2 x3 y1 (cfv C cuz) S (cop (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F))) (cop C A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 com) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv B2 R) (cop (cfv B2 G) (cfv (cfv B2 R) c2nd))) $;

--| The function ` R ` (used in the definition of the recursive definition generator on upper integers) is a function defined for all natural numbers. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 26-May-2020.)
theorem frecuzrdgrom {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (C: class) (R: class x3 y1) (S F: class) (G: class x3) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cfv C cuz)) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F) S) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfrec (cmpt2 x3 y1 (cfv C cuz) S (cop (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F))) (cop C A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn R com) $;

--| A helper lemma for the value of a recursive definition generator on upper integers. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 26-May-2020.)
theorem frecuzrdglem {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2 C: class) (R: class x3 y1) (S F: class) (G: class x3) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cfv C cuz)) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F) S) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfrec (cmpt2 x3 y1 (cfv C cuz) S (cop (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F))) (cop C A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 (cfv C cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cop B2 (cfv (cfv (cfv B2 (ccnv G)) R) c2nd)) (crn R)) $;

--| The recursive definition generator on upper integers is a function. See comment in ~ frec2uz0d for the description of ` G ` as the mapping from ` _om ` to ` ( ZZ>= `` C ) ` . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 26-May-2020.)
theorem frecuzrdgfn {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (C: class) (R: class x3 y1) (S: class) (T: class x3 y1) (F: class) (G: class x3) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cfv C cuz)) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F) S) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfrec (cmpt2 x3 y1 (cfv C cuz) S (cop (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F))) (cop C A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq T (crn R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn T (cfv C cuz)) $;

--| Closure law for the recursive definition generator on upper integers. See comment in ~ frec2uz0d for the description of ` G ` as the mapping from ` _om ` to ` ( ZZ>= `` C ) ` . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 31-May-2020.)
theorem frecuzrdgcl {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2 C: class) (R: class x3 y1) (S: class) (T: class x3 y1) (F: class) (G: class x3) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cfv C cuz)) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F) S) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfrec (cmpt2 x3 y1 (cfv C cuz) S (cop (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F))) (cop C A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq T (crn R)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel B2 (cfv C cuz))) (wcel (cfv B2 T) S) $;

--| Initial value of a recursive definition generator on upper integers. See comment in ~ frec2uz0d for the description of ` G ` as the mapping from ` _om ` to ` ( ZZ>= `` C ) ` . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 27-May-2020.)
theorem frecuzrdg0 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (C: class) (R: class x3 y1) (S: class) (T: class x3 y1) (F: class) (G: class x3) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cfv C cuz)) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F) S) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfrec (cmpt2 x3 y1 (cfv C cuz) S (cop (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F))) (cop C A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq T (crn R)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv C T) A2) $;

--| Successor value of a recursive definition generator on upper integers. See comment in ~ frec2uz0d for the description of ` G ` as the mapping from ` _om ` to ` ( ZZ>= `` C ) ` . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 28-May-2020.)
theorem frecuzrdgsuc {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3) (B2 C: class) (R: class x3 y1) (S: class) (T: class x3 y1) (F: class) (G: class x3) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel C cz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cfv C cuz)) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F) S) $ >
  $ wceq R (cfrec (cmpt2 x3 y1 (cfv C cuz) S (cop (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) F))) (cop C A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq T (crn R)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel B2 (cfv C cuz))) (wceq (cfv (co B2 c1 caddc) T) (co B2 (cfv B2 T) F)) $;

--| An upper integer set is denumerable. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Oct-2015.)
theorem uzenom (M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cz) (wbr Z com cen) $;

--| The cardinality of a finite set of sequential integers. (See ~ frec2uz0d for a description of the hypothesis.) (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 18-May-2020.)
theorem frecfzennn {x3: setvar} (G N: class x3):
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N cn0) (wbr (co c1 N cfz) (cfv N (ccnv G)) cen) $;

--| The cardinality of a finite set of sequential integers with arbitrary endpoints. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 18-May-2020.)
theorem frecfzen2 {x3: setvar} (G M N: class x3):
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) cc0) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) (wbr (co M N cfz) (cfv (co (co N c1 caddc) M cmin) (ccnv G)) cen) $;

--| ` G ` maps ` _om ` one-to-one onto ` NN0 ` . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 19-May-2020.)
theorem frechashgf1o {x3: setvar} (G: class x3):
  $ wceq G (cfrec (cmpt x3 cz (co (cv x3) c1 caddc)) cc0) $ >
  $ wf1o com cn0 G $;

--| A finite interval of integers is finite. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 19-May-2020.)
theorem fzfig (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wcel (co M N cfz) cfn) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ fzfig . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-May-2020.)
theorem fzfigd (ph: wff) (M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co M N cfz) cfn) $;

--| Half-open integer sets are finite. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-May-2020.)
theorem fzofig (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wcel (co M N cfzo) cfn) $;

--| The nonnegative integers are equinumerous to the positive integers. (Contributed by NM, 19-Jul-2004.)
theorem nn0ennn:
  $ wbr cn0 cn cen $;

--| The set of positive integers (as a subset of complex numbers) is equinumerous to omega (the set of finite ordinal numbers). (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 15-Sep-2013.)
theorem nnenom:
  $ wbr cn com cen $;

--| Extend class notation with recursive sequence builder.
term cseq (c_pl S F M: class): class;

--| Define a general-purpose operation that builds a recursive sequence (i.e. a function on the positive integers ` NN ` or some other upper integer set) whose value at an index is a function of its previous value and the value of an input sequence at that index. This definition is complicated, but fortunately it is not intended to be used directly. Instead, the only purpose of this definition is to provide us with an object that has the properties expressed by ~ iseq1 and ~ iseqp1 . Typically, those are the main theorems that would be used in practice. The first operand in the parentheses is the operation that is applied to the previous value and the value of the input sequence (second operand). The operand to the left of the parenthesis is the integer to start from. For example, for the operation ` + ` , an input sequence ` F ` with values 1, 1/2, 1/4, 1/8,... would be transformed into the output sequence ` seq 1 ( + , F , QQ ) ` with values 1, 3/2, 7/4, 15/8,.., so that ` ( seq 1 ( + , F , QQ ) `` 1 ) = 1 ` , ` ( seq 1 ( + , F , QQ ) `` 2 ) = ` 3/2, etc. In other words, ` seq M ( + , F , QQ ) ` transforms a sequence ` F ` into an infinite series. Internally, the ` frec ` function generates as its values a set of ordered pairs starting at ` <. M , ( F `` M ) >. ` , with the first member of each pair incremented by one in each successive value. So, the range of ` frec ` is exactly the sequence we want, and we just extract the range and throw away the domain. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 29-May-2020.)
axiom df_iseq {x3 y1: setvar} (c_pl S F M: class):
  $ wceq (cseq c_pl S F M) (crn (cfrec (cmpt2 x3 y1 (cfv M cuz) S (cop (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co (cv y1) (cfv (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) F) c_pl))) (cop M (cfv M F)))) $;

--| Existence of the sequence builder operation. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 20-Aug-2021.)
theorem iseqex (c_pl S F M: class):
  $ wcel (cseq c_pl S F M) cvv $;

--| Equality theorem for the sequence builder operation. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-May-2020.)
theorem iseqeq1 (c_pl S F M N: class):
  $ wi (wceq M N) (wceq (cseq c_pl S F M) (cseq c_pl S F N)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the sequence builder operation. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-May-2020.)
theorem iseqeq2 (c_pl Q S F M: class):
  $ wi (wceq c_pl Q) (wceq (cseq c_pl S F M) (cseq Q S F M)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the sequence builder operation. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-May-2020.)
theorem iseqeq3 (c_pl S F G M: class):
  $ wi (wceq F G) (wceq (cseq c_pl S F M) (cseq c_pl S G M)) $;

--| Equality theorem for the sequence builder operation. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-May-2020.)
theorem iseqeq4 (c_pl S T F M: class):
  $ wi (wceq S T) (wceq (cseq c_pl S F M) (cseq c_pl T F M)) $;

--| Hypothesis builder for the sequence builder operation. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-May-2020.)
theorem nfiseq {x3: setvar} (c_pl S F M: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 M $ >
  $ wnfc x3 c_pl $ >
  $ wnfc x3 F $ >
  $ wnfc x3 S $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (cseq c_pl S F M) $;

--| Closure of a function used in proving sequence builder theorems. This can be thought of as a lemma for the small number of sequence builder theorems which need it. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 31-May-2020.)
theorem iseqovex {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl S F: class) (M: class y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz)) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cmpt2 z w (cfv M cuz) S (co (cv w) (cfv (co (cv z) c1 caddc) F) c_pl))) S) $;

--| Value of the sequence builder function. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-May-2020.)
theorem iseqval {x3 y1 z w: setvar} (ph: wff z w) (c_pl: class) (R: class x3 y1 z w) (S F M: class):
  $ wceq R (cfrec (cmpt2 x3 y1 (cfv M cuz) S (cop (co (cv x3) c1 caddc) (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (cmpt2 z w (cfv M cuz) S (co (cv w) (cfv (co (cv z) c1 caddc) F) c_pl))))) (cop M (cfv M F))) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cseq c_pl S F M) (crn R)) $;

--| The sequence builder function is a function. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 30-May-2020.)
theorem iseqfn {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl S F M: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wfn (cseq c_pl S F M) (cfv M cuz)) $;

--| Value of the sequence builder function at its initial value. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 31-May-2020.)
theorem iseq1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl S F M: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv M (cseq c_pl S F M)) (cfv M F)) $;

--| Closure properties of the recursive sequence builder. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 1-Jun-2020.)
theorem iseqcl {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl S F M N: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv N (cseq c_pl S F M)) S) $;

--| Range of the recursive sequence builder. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 23-Jul-2021.)
theorem iseqf {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl S F M: class) (V: class x3 y1) (Z: class y1):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z S (cseq c_pl S F M)) $;

--| Value of the sequence builder function at a successor. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 31-May-2020.)
theorem iseqp1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl S F M N: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co N c1 caddc) (cseq c_pl S F M)) (co (cfv N (cseq c_pl S F M)) (cfv (co N c1 caddc) F) c_pl)) $;

--| Specifying a larger universe for ` seq ` . As long as ` F ` and ` .+ ` are closed over ` S ` , then any set which contains ` S ` can be used as the last argument to ` seq ` . This theorem does not allow ` T ` to be a proper class, however. It also currently requires that ` .+ ` be closed over ` T ` (as well as ` S ` ). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 18-Aug-2021.)
theorem iseqss {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl S T F M: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel T V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss S T) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) T) (wcel (cv y1) T))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) T) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cseq c_pl S F M) (cseq c_pl T F M)) $;

--| Value of the sequence builder function at a successor. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Jun-2013.)
theorem iseqm1 {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl S F M N: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (cseq c_pl S F M)) (co (cfv (co N c1 cmin) (cseq c_pl S F M)) (cfv N F) c_pl)) $;

--| Equality of sequences. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 3-Jun-2020.)
theorem iseqfveq2 {x3 y1 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl S F G K M N: class) (V: class x3 y1 k):
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K (cseq c_pl S F M)) (cfv K G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv K cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) G) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv K cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co (co K c1 caddc) N cfz))) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (cseq c_pl S F M)) (cfv N (cseq c_pl S G K))) $;

--| Equality of sequences. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 3-Jun-2020.)
theorem iseqfeq2 {x3 y1 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl S F G K M: class) (V: class x3 y1 k):
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv K (cseq c_pl S F M)) (cfv K G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv K cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) G) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv (co K c1 caddc) cuz))) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cres (cseq c_pl S F M) (cfv K cuz)) (cseq c_pl S G K)) $;

--| Equality of sequences. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 4-Jun-2020.)
theorem iseqfveq {x3 y1 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl S F G M N: class) (V: class x3 y1 k):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M N cfz))) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) G) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (cseq c_pl S F M)) (cfv N (cseq c_pl S G M))) $;

--| Equality of sequences. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 15-Aug-2021.)
theorem iseqfeq {x3 y1 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl S F G M: class) (V: class x3 y1 k):
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cseq c_pl S F M) (cseq c_pl S G M)) $;

--| Shifting the index set of a sequence. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 15-Aug-2021.)
theorem iseqshft2 {x3 y1 k: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl S: class k) (F G K M N: class) (V: class x3 y1 k):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M N cfz))) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (co (cv k) K caddc) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv (co M K caddc) cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) G) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (cseq c_pl S F M)) (cfv (co N K caddc) (cseq c_pl S G (co M K caddc)))) $;

--| An infinite series of complex terms is a function from ` NN ` to ` CC ` . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 15-Aug-2021.)
theorem iserf {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cc (cseq caddc cc F M)) $;

--| An infinite series of real numbers is a function from ` NN ` to ` RR ` . (Contributed by NM, 18-Apr-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2014.)
theorem iserfre {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z cr (cseq caddc cr F M)) $;

--| Ordering relation for a monotonic sequence, increasing case. (Contributed by NM, 13-Mar-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2014.)
theorem monoord {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M N cfz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M (co N c1 cmin) cfz))) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv M F) (cfv N F) cle) $;

--| Ordering relation for a monotonic sequence, decreasing case. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jul-2014.)
theorem monoord2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M N cfz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (co M (co N c1 cmin) cfz))) (wbr (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv k) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv N F) (cfv M F) cle) $;

--| The partial sums in an infinite series of positive terms form a monotonic sequence. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 15-Aug-2021.)
theorem isermono {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (F K M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel K (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv K cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co (co K c1 caddc) N cfz))) (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv x3) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv K (cseq caddc cr F M)) (cfv N (cseq caddc cr F M)) cle) $;

--| Split a sequence into two sequences. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 16-Aug-2021.)
theorem iseqsplit {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl S F K M N: class) (V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S) (wcel (cv z) S))) (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M (cfv K cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv K cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (cseq c_pl S F K)) (co (cfv M (cseq c_pl S F K)) (cfv N (cseq c_pl S F (co M c1 caddc))) c_pl)) $;

--| Removing the first term from a sequence. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 16-Aug-2021.)
theorem iseq1p {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl S F M N: class) (V: class x3 y1 z):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S) (wcel (cv z) S))) (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv (co M c1 caddc) cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (cseq c_pl S F M)) (co (cfv M F) (cfv N (cseq c_pl S F (co M c1 caddc))) c_pl)) $;

--| Lemma for iseqcaopr2 . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 16-Aug-2021.)
theorem iseqcaopr3 {x3 y1 k n: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl: class k) (Q S F G H M N: class) (V: class x3 y1 k n):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) Q) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wceq (cfv (cv k) H) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) Q)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv n) (co M N cfzo))) (wceq (co (co (cfv (cv n) (cseq c_pl S F M)) (cfv (cv n) (cseq c_pl S G M)) Q) (co (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) G) Q) c_pl) (co (co (cfv (cv n) (cseq c_pl S F M)) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) F) c_pl) (co (cfv (cv n) (cseq c_pl S G M)) (cfv (co (cv n) c1 caddc) G) c_pl) Q)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (cseq c_pl S H M)) (co (cfv N (cseq c_pl S F M)) (cfv N (cseq c_pl S G M)) Q)) $;

--| The sum of two infinite series (generalized to an arbitrary commutative and associative operation). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 30-May-2014.)
theorem iseqcaopr2 {x3 y1 z w k: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl: class k) (Q S F G: class) (H: class w) (M: class) (N: class w) (V: class x3 y1 z w k):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) Q) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S)) (wa (wcel (cv z) S) (wcel (cv w) S)))) (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv z) Q) (co (cv y1) (cv w) Q) c_pl) (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (co (cv z) (cv w) c_pl) Q)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wceq (cfv (cv k) H) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) Q)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (cseq c_pl S H M)) (co (cfv N (cseq c_pl S F M)) (cfv N (cseq c_pl S G M)) Q)) $;

--| The sum of two infinite series (generalized to an arbitrary commutative and associative operation). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 17-Aug-2021.)
theorem iseqcaopr {x3 y1 z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl S: class) (F G H M N: class x3 y1 z) (V: class x3 y1 z k):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wceq (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (w3a (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S) (wcel (cv z) S))) (wceq (co (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) (cv z) c_pl) (co (cv x3) (co (cv y1) (cv z) c_pl) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wceq (cfv (cv k) H) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (cseq c_pl S H M)) (co (cfv N (cseq c_pl S F M)) (cfv N (cseq c_pl S G M)) c_pl)) $;

--| The sum of two infinite series. (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-May-2014.)
theorem iseradd {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G H M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wceq (cfv (cv k) H) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (cseq caddc cc H M)) (co (cfv N (cseq caddc cc F M)) (cfv N (cseq caddc cc G M)) caddc)) $;

--| The difference of two infinite series. (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2014.)
theorem isersub {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G H M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wceq (cfv (cv k) H) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (cseq caddc cc H M)) (co (cfv N (cseq caddc cc F M)) (cfv N (cseq caddc cc G M)) cmin)) $;

--| A sequence that consists entirely of zeroes (or whatever the identity ` Z ` is for operation ` .+ ` ) sums to zero. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 18-Aug-2021.)
theorem iseqid3 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl F M N: class) (V: class x3) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (co Z Z c_pl) Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (cseq c_pl (csn Z) F M)) Z) $;

--| A sequence that consists of zeroes up to ` N ` sums to zero at ` N ` . In this case by "zero" we mean whatever the identity ` Z ` is for the operation ` .+ ` ). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 18-Aug-2021.)
theorem iseqid3s {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl S F M N: class) (V: class x3 y1) (Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq (co Z Z c_pl) Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co M N cfz))) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (cseq c_pl S F M)) Z) $;

--| Discard the first few terms of a sequence that starts with all zeroes (or whatever the identity ` Z ` is for operation ` .+ ` ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jul-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-May-2014.)
theorem iseqid {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl S F M N: class) (V: class x3 y1) (Z: class):
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) S)) (wceq (co Z (cv x3) c_pl) (cv x3)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv N F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (co M (co N c1 cmin) cfz))) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cres (cseq c_pl S F M) (cfv N cuz)) (cseq c_pl S F N)) $;

--| Apply a homomorphism to a sequence. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-Aug-2021.)
theorem iseqhomo {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (c_pl Q S F G H M N: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) H) (co (cfv (cv x3) H) (cfv (cv y1) H) Q)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) H) (cfv (cv x3) G)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) G) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) Q) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv N (cseq c_pl S F M)) H) (cfv N (cseq Q S G M))) $;

--| The distributive property for series. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-Aug-2021.)
theorem iseqdistr {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff) (C c_pl S T F G M N: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wceq (co C (co (cv x3) (cv y1) c_pl) T) (co (co C (cv x3) T) (co C (cv y1) T) c_pl)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) G) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wceq (cfv (cv x3) F) (co C (cfv (cv x3) G) T)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) T) S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) F) S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N (cseq c_pl S F M)) (co C (cfv N (cseq c_pl S G M)) T)) $;

--| The value of the partial sums in a zero-valued infinite series. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 19-Aug-2021.)
theorem iser0 (M N Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N Z) (wceq (cfv N (cseq caddc cc (cxp Z (csn cc0)) M)) cc0) $;

--| A zero-valued infinite series is equal to the constant zero function. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 19-Aug-2021.)
theorem iser0f (M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cz) (wceq (cseq caddc cc (cxp Z (csn cc0)) M) (cxp Z (csn cc0))) $;

--| A finite sum of nonnegative terms is nonnegative. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Aug-2021.)
theorem serige0 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv k) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (cfv N (cseq caddc cc F M)) cle) $;

--| Comparison of partial sums of two infinite series of reals. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Aug-2021.)
theorem serile {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) (cfv M cuz))) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv N (cseq caddc cc F M)) (cfv N (cseq caddc cc G M)) cle) $;

--| Extend class notation to include exponentiation of a complex number to an integer power.
term cexp: class;

--| Define exponentiation to nonnegative integer powers. This definition is not meant to be used directly; instead, ~ exp0 and ~ expp1 provide the standard recursive definition. The up-arrow notation is used by Donald Knuth for iterated exponentiation (_Science_ 194, 1235-1242, 1976) and is convenient for us since we don't have superscripts. 10-Jun-2005: The definition was extended to include zero exponents, so that ` 0 ^ 0 = 1 ` per the convention of Definition 10-4.1 of [Gleason] p. 134. 4-Jun-2014: The definition was extended to include negative integer exponents. The case ` x = 0 , y < 0 ` gives the value ` ( 1 / 0 ) ` , so we will avoid this case in our theorems. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 7-Jun-2020.)
axiom df_iexp {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq cexp (cmpt2 x3 y1 cc cz (cif (wceq (cv y1) cc0) c1 (cif (wbr cc0 (cv y1) clt) (cfv (cv y1) (cseq cmul cc (cxp cn (csn (cv x3))) c1)) (co c1 (cfv (cneg (cv y1)) (cseq cmul cc (cxp cn (csn (cv x3))) c1)) cdiv)))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ expival . If we take a complex number apart from zero and raise it to a positive integer power, the result is apart from zero. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 7-Jun-2020.)
theorem expivallem (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap) (wcel N cn)) (wbr (cfv N (cseq cmul cc (cxp cn (csn A2)) c1)) cc0 cap) $;

--| Value of exponentiation to integer powers. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 7-Jun-2020.)
theorem expival (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel N cz) (wo (wbr A2 cc0 cap) (wbr cc0 N cle))) (wceq (co A2 N cexp) (cif (wceq N cc0) c1 (cif (wbr cc0 N clt) (cfv N (cseq cmul cc (cxp cn (csn A2)) c1)) (co c1 (cfv (cneg N) (cseq cmul cc (cxp cn (csn A2)) c1)) cdiv)))) $;

--| Value of exponentiation to positive integer powers. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Jun-2020.)
theorem expinnval (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel N cn)) (wceq (co A2 N cexp) (cfv N (cseq cmul cc (cxp cn (csn A2)) c1))) $;

--| Value of a complex number raised to the 0th power. Note that under our definition, ` 0 ^ 0 = 1 ` , following the convention used by Gleason. Part of Definition 10-4.1 of [Gleason] p. 134. (Contributed by NM, 20-May-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2014.)
theorem exp0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co A2 cc0 cexp) c1) $;

--| ` 0 ^ 0 = 1 ` (common case). This is our convention. It follows the convention used by Gleason; see Part of Definition 10-4.1 of [Gleason] p. 134. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem _0exp0e1:
  $ wceq (co cc0 cc0 cexp) c1 $;

--| Value of a complex number raised to the first power. (Contributed by NM, 20-Oct-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jul-2013.)
theorem exp1 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co A2 c1 cexp) A2) $;

--| Value of a complex number raised to a nonnegative integer power plus one. Part of Definition 10-4.1 of [Gleason] p. 134. (Contributed by NM, 20-May-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jul-2013.)
theorem expp1 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (co A2 (co N c1 caddc) cexp) (co (co A2 N cexp) A2 cmul)) $;

--| Value of a complex number raised to a negative integer power. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Jun-2020.)
theorem expnegap0 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (co A2 (cneg N) cexp) (co c1 (co A2 N cexp) cdiv)) $;

--| Value of a complex number raised to a negative integer power. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Jun-2020.)
theorem expineg2 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap)) (wa (wcel N cc) (wcel (cneg N) cn0))) (wceq (co A2 N cexp) (co c1 (co A2 (cneg N) cexp) cdiv)) $;

--| A number to the negative one power is the reciprocal. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Jun-2020.)
theorem expn1ap0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap)) (wceq (co A2 (cneg c1) cexp) (co c1 A2 cdiv)) $;

--| Lemma for proving nonnegative integer exponentiation closure laws. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2005.)
theorem expcllem {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class y1) (F: class):
  $ wss F cc $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) F) (wcel (cv y1) F)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul) F) $ >
  $ wcel c1 F $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 F) (wcel B2 cn0)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cexp) F) $;

--| Lemma for proving integer exponentiation closure laws. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Jun-2020.)
theorem expcl2lemap {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class y1) (F: class):
  $ wss F cc $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) F) (wcel (cv y1) F)) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmul) F) $ >
  $ wcel c1 F $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv x3) F) (wbr (cv x3) cc0 cap)) (wcel (co c1 (cv x3) cdiv) F) $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 F) (wbr A2 cc0 cap) (wcel B2 cz)) (wcel (co A2 B2 cexp) F) $;

--| Closure of exponentiation of nonnegative integers. (Contributed by NM, 16-Dec-2005.)
theorem nnexpcl (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel (co A2 N cexp) cn) $;

--| Closure of exponentiation of nonnegative integers. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2005.)
theorem nn0expcl (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cn0) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel (co A2 N cexp) cn0) $;

--| Closure of exponentiation of integers. (Contributed by NM, 16-Dec-2005.)
theorem zexpcl (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel (co A2 N cexp) cz) $;

--| Closure of exponentiation of rationals. (Contributed by NM, 16-Dec-2005.)
theorem qexpcl (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cq) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel (co A2 N cexp) cq) $;

--| Closure of exponentiation of reals. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2005.)
theorem reexpcl (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel (co A2 N cexp) cr) $;

--| Closure law for nonnegative integer exponentiation. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-2005.)
theorem expcl (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel N cn0)) (wcel (co A2 N cexp) cc) $;

--| Closure law for exponentiation of positive reals. (Contributed by NM, 24-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2014.)
theorem rpexpcl (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 crp) (wcel N cz)) (wcel (co A2 N cexp) crp) $;

--| Closure of exponentiation of reals. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 9-Jun-2020.)
theorem reexpclzap (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wbr A2 cc0 cap) (wcel N cz)) (wcel (co A2 N cexp) cr) $;

--| Closure of exponentiation of rational numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Sep-2014.)
theorem qexpclz (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cq) (wne A2 cc0) (wcel N cz)) (wcel (co A2 N cexp) cq) $;

--| Closure of exponentiation of negative one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2015.)
theorem m1expcl2 (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wcel (co (cneg c1) N cexp) (cpr (cneg c1) c1)) $;

--| Closure of exponentiation of negative one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2015.)
theorem m1expcl (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wcel (co (cneg c1) N cexp) cz) $;

--| Closure law for integer exponentiation. Lemma for ~ expclzap and ~ expap0i . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 9-Jun-2020.)
theorem expclzaplem {z: setvar} (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap) (wcel N cz)) (wcel (co A2 N cexp) (crab z (wbr (cv z) cc0 cap) cc)) $;

--| Closure law for integer exponentiation. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 9-Jun-2020.)
theorem expclzap (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap) (wcel N cz)) (wcel (co A2 N cexp) cc) $;

--| Closure of exponentiation of nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 17-Apr-2015.)
theorem nn0expcli (A2 N: class):
  $ wcel A2 cn0 $ >
  $ wcel N cn0 $ >
  $ wcel (co A2 N cexp) cn0 $;

--| The square of a nonnegative integer is a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Oct-2014.)
theorem nn0sqcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn0) (wcel (co A2 c2 cexp) cn0) $;

--| Exponentiation in terms of predecessor exponent. (Contributed by NM, 19-Dec-2005.)
theorem expm1t (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel N cn)) (wceq (co A2 N cexp) (co (co A2 (co N c1 cmin) cexp) A2 cmul)) $;

--| Value of one raised to a nonnegative integer power. (Contributed by NM, 15-Dec-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2014.)
theorem _1exp (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wceq (co c1 N cexp) c1) $;

--| Positive integer exponentiation is apart from zero iff its mantissa is apart from zero. That it is easier to prove this first, and then prove ~ expeq0 in terms of it, rather than the other way around, is perhaps an illustration of the maxim "In constructive analysis, the apartness is more basic [ than ] equality." ([Geuvers], p. 1). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 10-Jun-2020.)
theorem expap0 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel N cn)) (wb (wbr (co A2 N cexp) cc0 cap) (wbr A2 cc0 cap)) $;

--| Positive integer exponentiation is 0 iff its mantissa is 0. (Contributed by NM, 23-Feb-2005.)
theorem expeq0 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel N cn)) (wb (wceq (co A2 N cexp) cc0) (wceq A2 cc0)) $;

--| Integer exponentiation is apart from zero if its mantissa is apart from zero. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 10-Jun-2020.)
theorem expap0i (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap) (wcel N cz)) (wbr (co A2 N cexp) cc0 cap) $;

--| Nonnegative integer exponentiation with a positive mantissa is positive. (Contributed by NM, 16-Dec-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2014.)
theorem expgt0 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel N cz) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wbr cc0 (co A2 N cexp) clt) $;

--| Value of a complex number raised to a negative power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2014.)
theorem expnegzap (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (co A2 (cneg N) cexp) (co c1 (co A2 N cexp) cdiv)) $;

--| Value of zero raised to a positive integer power. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-2004.)
theorem _0exp (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wceq (co cc0 N cexp) cc0) $;

--| Nonnegative integer exponentiation with a nonnegative mantissa is nonnegative. (Contributed by NM, 16-Dec-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2014.)
theorem expge0 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel N cn0) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wbr cc0 (co A2 N cexp) cle) $;

--| Nonnegative integer exponentiation with a mantissa greater than or equal to 1 is greater than or equal to 1. (Contributed by NM, 21-Feb-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2014.)
theorem expge1 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel N cn0) (wbr c1 A2 cle)) (wbr c1 (co A2 N cexp) cle) $;

--| Positive integer exponentiation with a mantissa greater than 1 is greater than 1. (Contributed by NM, 13-Feb-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2014.)
theorem expgt1 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel N cn) (wbr c1 A2 clt)) (wbr c1 (co A2 N cexp) clt) $;

--| Positive integer exponentiation of a product. Proposition 10-4.2(c) of [Gleason] p. 135, restricted to nonnegative integer exponents. (Contributed by NM, 13-Feb-2005.)
theorem mulexp (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) N cexp) (co (co A2 N cexp) (co B2 N cexp) cmul)) $;

--| Integer exponentiation of a product. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 10-Jun-2020.)
theorem mulexpzap (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap)) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wbr B2 cc0 cap)) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) N cexp) (co (co A2 N cexp) (co B2 N cexp) cmul)) $;

--| Nonnegative integer exponentiation of a reciprocal. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 10-Jun-2020.)
theorem exprecap (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (co (co c1 A2 cdiv) N cexp) (co c1 (co A2 N cexp) cdiv)) $;

--| Sum of exponents law for nonnegative integer exponentiation. Proposition 10-4.2(a) of [Gleason] p. 135. (Contributed by NM, 30-Nov-2004.)
theorem expadd (A2 M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel M cn0) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (co A2 (co M N caddc) cexp) (co (co A2 M cexp) (co A2 N cexp) cmul)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ expaddzap . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 10-Jun-2020.)
theorem expaddzaplem (A2 M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap)) (wa (wcel M cr) (wcel (cneg M) cn)) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (co A2 (co M N caddc) cexp) (co (co A2 M cexp) (co A2 N cexp) cmul)) $;

--| Sum of exponents law for integer exponentiation. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 10-Jun-2020.)
theorem expaddzap (A2 M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap)) (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz))) (wceq (co A2 (co M N caddc) cexp) (co (co A2 M cexp) (co A2 N cexp) cmul)) $;

--| Product of exponents law for positive integer exponentiation. Proposition 10-4.2(b) of [Gleason] p. 135, restricted to nonnegative integer exponents. (Contributed by NM, 4-Jan-2006.)
theorem expmul (A2 M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel M cn0) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (co A2 (co M N cmul) cexp) (co (co A2 M cexp) N cexp)) $;

--| Product of exponents law for integer exponentiation. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Jun-2020.)
theorem expmulzap (A2 M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap)) (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz))) (wceq (co A2 (co M N cmul) cexp) (co (co A2 M cexp) N cexp)) $;

--| Exponent subtraction law for nonnegative integer exponentiation. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Jun-2020.)
theorem expsubap (A2 M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap)) (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz))) (wceq (co A2 (co M N cmin) cexp) (co (co A2 M cexp) (co A2 N cexp) cdiv)) $;

--| Value of a nonzero complex number raised to an integer power plus one. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Jun-2020.)
theorem expp1zap (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (co A2 (co N c1 caddc) cexp) (co (co A2 N cexp) A2 cmul)) $;

--| Value of a complex number raised to an integer power minus one. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Jun-2020.)
theorem expm1ap (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (co A2 (co N c1 cmin) cexp) (co (co A2 N cexp) A2 cdiv)) $;

--| Nonnegative integer exponentiation of a quotient. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Jun-2020.)
theorem expdivap (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wbr B2 cc0 cap)) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) N cexp) (co (co A2 N cexp) (co B2 N cexp) cdiv)) $;

--| Ordering relationship for exponentiation. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Jun-2014.)
theorem ltexp2a (A2 M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel M cz) (wcel N cz)) (wa (wbr c1 A2 clt) (wbr M N clt))) (wbr (co A2 M cexp) (co A2 N cexp) clt) $;

--| Weak ordering relationship for exponentiation. (Contributed by NM, 14-Dec-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jun-2014.)
theorem leexp2a (A2 M N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wbr c1 A2 cle) (wcel N (cfv M cuz))) (wbr (co A2 M cexp) (co A2 N cexp) cle) $;

--| Weak ordering relationship for exponentiation. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 14-Jan-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-Apr-2014.)
theorem leexp2r (A2 M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel M cn0) (wcel N (cfv M cuz))) (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr A2 c1 cle))) (wbr (co A2 N cexp) (co A2 M cexp) cle) $;

--| Weak mantissa ordering relationship for exponentiation. (Contributed by NM, 18-Dec-2005.)
theorem leexp1a (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel N cn0)) (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr A2 B2 cle))) (wbr (co A2 N cexp) (co B2 N cexp) cle) $;

--| Nonnegative integer exponentiation with a mantissa between 0 and 1 inclusive is less than or equal to 1. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 29-Dec-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jun-2014.)
theorem exple1 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr A2 c1 cle)) (wcel N cn0)) (wbr (co A2 N cexp) c1 cle) $;

--| An upper bound on ` A ^ N ` when ` 2 <_ A ` . (Contributed by NM, 19-Dec-2005.)
theorem expubnd (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel N cn0) (wbr c2 A2 cle)) (wbr (co A2 N cexp) (co (co c2 N cexp) (co (co A2 c1 cmin) N cexp) cmul) cle) $;

--| Value of the square of a complex number. (Contributed by Raph Levien, 10-Apr-2004.)
theorem sqval (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co A2 c2 cexp) (co A2 A2 cmul)) $;

--| The square of the negative of a number.) (Contributed by NM, 15-Jan-2006.)
theorem sqneg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co (cneg A2) c2 cexp) (co A2 c2 cexp)) $;

--| Swap the order of subtraction in a square. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 10-Jun-2013.)
theorem sqsubswap (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) c2 cexp) (co (co B2 A2 cmin) c2 cexp)) $;

--| Closure of square. (Contributed by NM, 10-Aug-1999.)
theorem sqcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (co A2 c2 cexp) cc) $;

--| Distribution of square over multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 21-Mar-2008.)
theorem sqmul (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) c2 cexp) (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) cmul)) $;

--| A number is zero iff its square is zero. (Contributed by NM, 11-Mar-2006.)
theorem sqeq0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wb (wceq (co A2 c2 cexp) cc0) (wceq A2 cc0)) $;

--| Distribution of square over division. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Jun-2020.)
theorem sqdivap (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wbr B2 cc0 cap)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) c2 cexp) (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) cdiv)) $;

--| A number is nonzero iff its square is nonzero. See also ~ sqap0 which is the same but with not equal changed to apart. (Contributed by NM, 11-Mar-2006.)
theorem sqne0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wb (wne (co A2 c2 cexp) cc0) (wne A2 cc0)) $;

--| A number is apart from zero iff its square is apart from zero. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 13-Aug-2021.)
theorem sqap0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wb (wbr (co A2 c2 cexp) cc0 cap) (wbr A2 cc0 cap)) $;

--| Closure of the square of a real number. (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-1999.)
theorem resqcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (co A2 c2 cexp) cr) $;

--| The square of a nonzero real is positive. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Jun-2020.)
theorem sqgt0ap (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr A2 cc0 cap)) (wbr cc0 (co A2 c2 cexp) clt) $;

--| The naturals are closed under squaring. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 29-Mar-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem nnsqcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cn) (wcel (co A2 c2 cexp) cn) $;

--| Integers are closed under squaring. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 19-Apr-2014.)
theorem zsqcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cz) (wcel (co A2 c2 cexp) cz) $;

--| The square of a rational is rational. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 15-Sep-2014.)
theorem qsqcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cq) (wcel (co A2 c2 cexp) cq) $;

--| The square function is one-to-one for nonnegative reals. Also see ~ sq11ap which would easily follow from this given excluded middle, but which for us is proved another way. (Contributed by NM, 8-Apr-2001.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem sq11 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle))) (wb (wceq (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| The square function on nonnegative reals is strictly monotonic. (Contributed by NM, 24-Feb-2006.)
theorem lt2sq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) clt)) $;

--| The square function on nonnegative reals is monotonic. (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-1999.)
theorem le2sq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) cle)) $;

--| The square of a 'less than or equal to' ordering. (Contributed by NM, 21-Mar-2008.)
theorem le2sq2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 cle))) (wbr (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) cle) $;

--| A square of a real is nonnegative. (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-1999.)
theorem sqge0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 (co A2 c2 cexp) cle) $;

--| The square of an integer is a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Apr-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem zsqcl2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cz) (wcel (co A2 c2 cexp) cn0) $;

--| Two real numbers are equal to 0 iff their Euclidean norm is. (Contributed by NM, 29-Apr-2005.) (Revised by Stefan O'Rear, 5-Oct-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem sumsqeq0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wa (wceq A2 cc0) (wceq B2 cc0)) (wceq (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc) cc0)) $;

--| Value of square. Inference version. (Contributed by NM, 1-Aug-1999.)
theorem sqvali (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 c2 cexp) (co A2 A2 cmul) $;

--| Closure of square. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-1999.)
theorem sqcli (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel (co A2 c2 cexp) cc $;

--| A number is zero iff its square is zero. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.)
theorem sqeq0i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wb (wceq (co A2 c2 cexp) cc0) (wceq A2 cc0) $;

--| Distribution of square over multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 3-Sep-1999.)
theorem sqmuli (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) c2 cexp) (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) cmul) $;

--| Distribution of square over division. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-Jun-2020.)
theorem sqdivapi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wbr B2 cc0 cap $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) c2 cexp) (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) cdiv) $;

--| Closure of square in reals. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-1999.)
theorem resqcli (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel (co A2 c2 cexp) cr $;

--| The square of a nonzero real is positive. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-Jun-2020.)
theorem sqgt0api (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr A2 cc0 cap) (wbr cc0 (co A2 c2 cexp) clt) $;

--| A square of a real is nonnegative. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-1999.)
theorem sqge0i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wbr cc0 (co A2 c2 cexp) cle $;

--| The square function on nonnegative reals is strictly monotonic. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-1999.)
theorem lt2sqi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) clt)) $;

--| The square function on nonnegative reals is monotonic. (Contributed by NM, 12-Sep-1999.)
theorem le2sqi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) cle)) $;

--| The square function is one-to-one for nonnegative reals. (Contributed by NM, 27-Oct-1999.)
theorem sq11i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) (wb (wceq (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| The square of 0 is 0. (Contributed by NM, 6-Jun-2006.)
theorem sq0:
  $ wceq (co cc0 c2 cexp) cc0 $;

--| If a number is zero, its square is zero. (Contributed by FL, 10-Dec-2006.)
theorem sq0i (A2: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 cc0) (wceq (co A2 c2 cexp) cc0) $;

--| If a number is zero, its square is zero. Deduction form of ~ sq0i . Converse of ~ sqeq0d . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem sq0id (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 c2 cexp) cc0) $;

--| The square of 1 is 1. (Contributed by NM, 22-Aug-1999.)
theorem sq1:
  $ wceq (co c1 c2 cexp) c1 $;

--| ` -u 1 ` squared is 1 (common case). (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 8-Dec-2018.)
theorem neg1sqe1:
  $ wceq (co (cneg c1) c2 cexp) c1 $;

--| The square of 2 is 4. (Contributed by NM, 22-Aug-1999.)
theorem sq2:
  $ wceq (co c2 c2 cexp) c4 $;

--| The square of 3 is 9. (Contributed by NM, 26-Apr-2006.)
theorem sq3:
  $ wceq (co c3 c2 cexp) c9 $;

--| The cube of 2 is 8. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-2004.)
theorem cu2:
  $ wceq (co c2 c3 cexp) c8 $;

--| The reciprocal of ` _i ` . (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-1999.)
theorem irec:
  $ wceq (co c1 ci cdiv) (cneg ci) $;

--| ` _i ` squared. (Contributed by NM, 6-May-1999.)
theorem i2:
  $ wceq (co ci c2 cexp) (cneg c1) $;

--| ` _i ` cubed. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jan-2007.)
theorem i3:
  $ wceq (co ci c3 cexp) (cneg ci) $;

--| ` _i ` to the fourth power. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jan-2007.)
theorem i4:
  $ wceq (co ci c4 cexp) c1 $;

--| A positive integer is less than or equal to its square. (Contributed by NM, 15-Sep-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem nnlesq (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wbr N (co N c2 cexp) cle) $;

--| A counterexample showing that exponentiation is not associative. (Contributed by Stefan Allan and G&eacute;rard Lang, 21-Sep-2010.)
theorem expnass:
  $ wbr (co (co c3 c3 cexp) c3 cexp) (co c3 (co c3 c3 cexp) cexp) clt $;

--| Factor the difference of two squares. (Contributed by NM, 21-Feb-2008.)
theorem subsq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) cmin) (co (co A2 B2 caddc) (co A2 B2 cmin) cmul)) $;

--| Express the difference of the squares of two numbers as a polynomial in the difference of the numbers. (Contributed by NM, 21-Feb-2008.)
theorem subsq2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) cmin) (co (co (co A2 B2 cmin) c2 cexp) (co (co c2 B2 cmul) (co A2 B2 cmin) cmul) caddc)) $;

--| The square of a binomial. (Contributed by NM, 11-Aug-1999.)
theorem binom2i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) c2 cexp) (co (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co c2 (co A2 B2 cmul) cmul) caddc) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc) $;

--| Factor the difference of two squares. (Contributed by NM, 7-Feb-2005.)
theorem subsqi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) cmin) (co (co A2 B2 caddc) (co A2 B2 cmin) cmul) $;

--| The square of a binomial. (Contributed by FL, 10-Dec-2006.)
theorem binom2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) c2 cexp) (co (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co c2 (co A2 B2 cmul) cmul) caddc) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc)) $;

--| Special case of ~ binom2 where ` B = 1 ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-May-2014.)
theorem binom21 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co (co A2 c1 caddc) c2 cexp) (co (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co c2 A2 cmul) caddc) c1 caddc)) $;

--| Expand the square of a subtraction. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 10-Jun-2013.)
theorem binom2sub (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) c2 cexp) (co (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co c2 (co A2 B2 cmul) cmul) cmin) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc)) $;

--| Expand the square of a subtraction. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 13-Jun-2013.)
theorem binom2subi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmin) c2 cexp) (co (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co c2 (co A2 B2 cmul) cmul) cmin) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc) $;

--| The cube of a binomial. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Apr-2015.)
theorem binom3 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (co A2 B2 caddc) c3 cexp) (co (co (co A2 c3 cexp) (co c3 (co (co A2 c2 cexp) B2 cmul) cmul) caddc) (co (co c3 (co A2 (co B2 c2 cexp) cmul) cmul) (co B2 c3 cexp) caddc) caddc)) $;

--| An integer is even iff its square is even. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem zesq (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cz) (wb (wcel (co N c2 cdiv) cz) (wcel (co (co N c2 cexp) c2 cdiv) cz)) $;

--| A positive integer is even iff its square is even. (Contributed by NM, 20-Aug-2001.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem nnesq (N: class):
  $ wi (wcel N cn) (wb (wcel (co N c2 cdiv) cn) (wcel (co (co N c2 cexp) c2 cdiv) cn)) $;

--| Bernoulli's inequality, due to Johan Bernoulli (1667-1748). (Contributed by NM, 21-Feb-2005.)
theorem bernneq (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel N cn0) (wbr (cneg c1) A2 cle)) (wbr (co c1 (co A2 N cmul) caddc) (co (co c1 A2 caddc) N cexp) cle) $;

--| Variation of Bernoulli's inequality ~ bernneq . (Contributed by NM, 18-Oct-2007.)
theorem bernneq2 (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel N cn0) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wbr (co (co (co A2 c1 cmin) N cmul) c1 caddc) (co A2 N cexp) cle) $;

--| A corollary of ~ bernneq . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 11-Mar-2014.)
theorem bernneq3 (P N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel P (cfv c2 cuz)) (wcel N cn0)) (wbr N (co P N cexp) clt) $;

--| Exponentiation with a mantissa greater than 1 has no upper bound. (Contributed by NM, 20-Oct-2007.)
theorem expnbnd {k: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr c1 B2 clt)) (wrex k (wbr A2 (co B2 (cv k) cexp) clt) cn) $;

--| The reciprocal of exponentiation with a mantissa greater than 1 has no lower bound. (Contributed by NM, 18-Jul-2008.)
theorem expnlbnd {k: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr c1 B2 clt)) (wrex k (wbr (co c1 (co B2 (cv k) cexp) cdiv) A2 clt) cn) $;

--| The reciprocal of exponentiation with a mantissa greater than 1 has no lower bound. (Contributed by NM, 18-Jul-2008.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 5-Jun-2014.)
theorem expnlbnd2 {j k: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr c1 B2 clt)) (wrex j (wral k (wbr (co c1 (co B2 (cv k) cexp) cdiv) A2 clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cn) $;

--| Value of a complex number raised to the 0th power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem exp0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 cc0 cexp) c1) $;

--| Value of a complex number raised to the first power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem exp1d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 c1 cexp) A2) $;

--| Positive integer exponentiation is 0 iff its mantissa is 0. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem expeq0d (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 N cexp) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 cc0) $;

--| Value of square. Inference version. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem sqvald (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 c2 cexp) (co A2 A2 cmul)) $;

--| Closure of square. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem sqcld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 c2 cexp) cc) $;

--| A number is zero iff its square is zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem sqeq0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 c2 cexp) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 cc0) $;

--| Closure law for nonnegative integer exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem expcld (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 N cexp) cc) $;

--| Value of a complex number raised to a nonnegative integer power plus one. Part of Definition 10-4.1 of [Gleason] p. 134. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem expp1d (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co N c1 caddc) cexp) (co (co A2 N cexp) A2 cmul)) $;

--| Sum of exponents law for nonnegative integer exponentiation. Proposition 10-4.2(a) of [Gleason] p. 135. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem expaddd (ph: wff) (A2 M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co M N caddc) cexp) (co (co A2 M cexp) (co A2 N cexp) cmul)) $;

--| Product of exponents law for positive integer exponentiation. Proposition 10-4.2(b) of [Gleason] p. 135, restricted to nonnegative integer exponents. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem expmuld (ph: wff) (A2 M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co M N cmul) cexp) (co (co A2 M cexp) N cexp)) $;

--| Square of reciprocal. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-Jun-2020.)
theorem sqrecapd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 cap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co c1 A2 cdiv) c2 cexp) (co c1 (co A2 c2 cexp) cdiv)) $;

--| Closure law for integer exponentiation. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-Jun-2020.)
theorem expclzapd (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 cap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 N cexp) cc) $;

--| Nonnegative integer exponentiation is nonzero if its mantissa is nonzero. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-Jun-2020.)
theorem expap0d (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 cap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 N cexp) cc0 cap) $;

--| Value of a complex number raised to a negative power. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-Jun-2020.)
theorem expnegapd (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 cap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (cneg N) cexp) (co c1 (co A2 N cexp) cdiv)) $;

--| Nonnegative integer exponentiation of a reciprocal. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-Jun-2020.)
theorem exprecapd (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 cap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co c1 A2 cdiv) N cexp) (co c1 (co A2 N cexp) cdiv)) $;

--| Value of a nonzero complex number raised to an integer power plus one. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-Jun-2020.)
theorem expp1zapd (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 cap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co N c1 caddc) cexp) (co (co A2 N cexp) A2 cmul)) $;

--| Value of a complex number raised to an integer power minus one. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-Jun-2020.)
theorem expm1apd (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 cap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co N c1 cmin) cexp) (co (co A2 N cexp) A2 cdiv)) $;

--| Exponent subtraction law for nonnegative integer exponentiation. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-Jun-2020.)
theorem expsubapd (ph: wff) (A2 M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 cap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (co M N cmin) cexp) (co (co A2 M cexp) (co A2 N cexp) cdiv)) $;

--| Distribution of square over multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem sqmuld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) c2 cexp) (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) cmul)) $;

--| Distribution of square over division. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 13-Jun-2020.)
theorem sqdivapd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 cc0 cap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) c2 cexp) (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) cdiv)) $;

--| Nonnegative integer exponentiation of a quotient. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 13-Jun-2020.)
theorem expdivapd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 cc0 cap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cdiv) N cexp) (co (co A2 N cexp) (co B2 N cexp) cdiv)) $;

--| Positive integer exponentiation of a product. Proposition 10-4.2(c) of [Gleason] p. 135, restricted to nonnegative integer exponents. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem mulexpd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (co A2 B2 cmul) N cexp) (co (co A2 N cexp) (co B2 N cexp) cmul)) $;

--| Value of zero raised to a positive integer power. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem _0expd (ph: wff) (N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co cc0 N cexp) cc0) $;

--| Closure of exponentiation of reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem reexpcld (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 N cexp) cr) $;

--| Nonnegative integer exponentiation with a nonnegative mantissa is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem expge0d (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (co A2 N cexp) cle) $;

--| Nonnegative integer exponentiation with a nonnegative mantissa is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem expge1d (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 (co A2 N cexp) cle) $;

--| The naturals are closed under squaring. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem nnsqcld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 c2 cexp) cn) $;

--| Closure of exponentiation of nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem nnexpcld (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 N cexp) cn) $;

--| Closure of exponentiation of nonnegative integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem nn0expcld (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 N cexp) cn0) $;

--| Closure law for exponentiation of positive reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem rpexpcld (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 N cexp) crp) $;

--| Closure of exponentiation of reals. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 13-Jun-2020.)
theorem reexpclzapd (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 cap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 N cexp) cr) $;

--| Closure of square in reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem resqcld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 c2 cexp) cr) $;

--| A square of a real is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem sqge0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (co A2 c2 cexp) cle) $;

--| The square of a real apart from zero is positive. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 13-Jun-2020.)
theorem sqgt0apd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 cap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (co A2 c2 cexp) clt) $;

--| Ordering relationship for exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem leexp2ad (ph: wff) (A2 M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr c1 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 M cexp) (co A2 N cexp) cle) $;

--| Ordering relationship for exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem leexp2rd (ph: wff) (A2 M N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N (cfv M cuz)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 c1 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co A2 N cexp) (co A2 M cexp) cle) $;

--| The square function on nonnegative reals is strictly monotonic. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem lt2sqd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) clt)) $;

--| The square function on nonnegative reals is monotonic. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem le2sqd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) cle)) $;

--| The square function is one-to-one for nonnegative reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2016.)
theorem sq11d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Analogue to ~ sq11 but for apartness. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-Aug-2021.)
theorem sq11ap (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle))) (wb (wbr (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) cap) (wbr A2 B2 cap)) $;

--| Extend class notation with function shifter.
term cshi: class;

--| Define a function shifter. This operation offsets the value argument of a function (ordinarily on a subset of ` CC ` ) and produces a new function on ` CC ` . See ~ shftval for its value. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-2005.)
axiom df_shft {x3 y1 z f2: setvar}:
  $ wceq cshi (cmpt2 f2 x3 cvv cc (copab y1 z (wa (wcel (cv y1) cc) (wbr (co (cv y1) (cv x3) cmin) (cv z) (cv f2))))) $;

--| Two ways to write a shifted set ` ( B + A ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem shftlem {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wss B2 cc)) (wceq (crab x3 (wcel (co (cv x3) A2 cmin) B2) cc) (cab x3 (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) (co (cv y1) A2 caddc)) B2))) $;

--| A shift of the upper integers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Nov-2013.)
theorem shftuz {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cz) (wcel B2 cz)) (wceq (crab x3 (wcel (co (cv x3) A2 cmin) (cfv B2 cuz)) cc) (cfv (co B2 A2 caddc) cuz)) $;

--| The value of the sequence shifter operation is a function on ` CC ` . ` A ` is ordinarily an integer. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem shftfvalg {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 F: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel F V)) (wceq (co F A2 cshi) (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) cc) (wbr (co (cv x3) A2 cmin) (cv y1) F)))) $;

--| Existence of the result of applying shift. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 15-Aug-2021.)
theorem ovshftex (A2 F V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel F V) (wcel A2 cc)) (wcel (co F A2 cshi) cvv) $;

--| Value of a fiber of the relation ` F ` . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 15-Aug-2021.)
theorem shftfibg (A2 B2 F V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F V) (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cima (co F A2 cshi) (csn B2)) (cima F (csn (co B2 A2 cmin)))) $;

--| The value of the sequence shifter operation is a function on ` CC ` . ` A ` is ordinarily an integer. (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem shftfval {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co F A2 cshi) (copab x3 y1 (wa (wcel (cv x3) cc) (wbr (co (cv x3) A2 cmin) (cv y1) F)))) $;

--| Domain of a relation shifted by ` A ` . The set on the right is more commonly notated as ` ( dom F + A ) ` (meaning add ` A ` to every element of ` dom F ` ). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem shftdm {x3: setvar} (A2 F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cdm (co F A2 cshi)) (crab x3 (wcel (co (cv x3) A2 cmin) (cdm F)) cc)) $;

--| Value of a fiber of the relation ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 4-Nov-2013.)
theorem shftfib (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cima (co F A2 cshi) (csn B2)) (cima F (csn (co B2 A2 cmin)))) $;

--| Functionality and domain of a sequence shifted by ` A ` . (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Nov-2013.)
theorem shftfn {x3: setvar} (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wfn F B2) (wcel A2 cc)) (wfn (co F A2 cshi) (crab x3 (wcel (co (cv x3) A2 cmin) B2) cc)) $;

--| Value of a sequence shifted by ` A ` . (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 4-Nov-2013.)
theorem shftval (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv B2 (co F A2 cshi)) (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) F)) $;

--| Value of a sequence shifted by ` A - B ` . (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Nov-2013.)
theorem shftval2 (A2 B2 C F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 C caddc) (co F (co A2 B2 cmin) cshi)) (cfv (co B2 C caddc) F)) $;

--| Value of a sequence shifted by ` A - B ` . (Contributed by NM, 20-Jul-2005.)
theorem shftval3 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv A2 (co F (co A2 B2 cmin) cshi)) (cfv B2 F)) $;

--| Value of a sequence shifted by ` -u A ` . (Contributed by NM, 18-Aug-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Nov-2013.)
theorem shftval4 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv B2 (co F (cneg A2) cshi)) (cfv (co A2 B2 caddc) F)) $;

--| Value of a shifted sequence. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Nov-2013.)
theorem shftval5 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv (co B2 A2 caddc) (co F A2 cshi)) (cfv B2 F)) $;

--| Functionality of a shifted sequence. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Nov-2013.)
theorem shftf {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class) (C: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wf B2 C F) (wcel A2 cc)) (wf (crab x3 (wcel (co (cv x3) A2 cmin) B2) cc) C (co F A2 cshi)) $;

--| Composite shift operations. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Nov-2013.)
theorem _2shfti (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (co F A2 cshi) B2 cshi) (co F (co A2 B2 caddc) cshi)) $;

--| Identity law for the shift operation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Nov-2013.)
theorem shftidt2 (F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wceq (co F cc0 cshi) (cres F cc) $;

--| Identity law for the shift operation. (Contributed by NM, 19-Aug-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Nov-2013.)
theorem shftidt (A2 F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 (co F cc0 cshi)) (cfv A2 F)) $;

--| Cancellation law for the shift operation. (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Nov-2013.)
theorem shftcan1 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv B2 (co (co F A2 cshi) (cneg A2) cshi)) (cfv B2 F)) $;

--| Cancellation law for the shift operation. (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 5-Nov-2013.)
theorem shftcan2 (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wcel F cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv B2 (co (co F (cneg A2) cshi) A2 cshi)) (cfv B2 F)) $;

--| Value of a sequence shifted by ` A ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 16-Dec-2017.)
theorem shftvalg (A2 B2 F V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F V) (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv B2 (co F A2 cshi)) (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) F)) $;

--| Value of a sequence shifted by ` -u A ` . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 19-Aug-2021.)
theorem shftval4g (A2 B2 F V: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel F V) (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv B2 (co F (cneg A2) cshi)) (cfv (co A2 B2 caddc) F)) $;

--| Extend class notation to include complex conjugate function.
term ccj: class;

--| Extend class notation to include real part of a complex number.
term cre: class;

--| Extend class notation to include imaginary part of a complex number.
term cim: class;

--| Define the complex conjugate function. See ~ cjcli for its closure and ~ cjval for its value. (Contributed by NM, 9-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Nov-2013.)
axiom df_cj {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq ccj (cmpt x3 cc (crio y1 (wa (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) caddc) cr) (wcel (co ci (co (cv x3) (cv y1) cmin) cmul) cr)) cc)) $;

--| Define a function whose value is the real part of a complex number. See ~ reval for its value, ~ recli for its closure, and ~ replim for its use in decomposing a complex number. (Contributed by NM, 9-May-1999.)
axiom df_re {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cre (cmpt x3 cc (co (co (cv x3) (cfv (cv x3) ccj) caddc) c2 cdiv)) $;

--| Define a function whose value is the imaginary part of a complex number. See ~ imval for its value, ~ imcli for its closure, and ~ replim for its use in decomposing a complex number. (Contributed by NM, 9-May-1999.)
axiom df_im {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cim (cmpt x3 cc (cfv (co (cv x3) ci cdiv) cre)) $;

--| The value of the conjugate of a complex number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Nov-2013.)
theorem cjval {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 ccj) (crio x3 (wa (wcel (co A2 (cv x3) caddc) cr) (wcel (co ci (co A2 (cv x3) cmin) cmul) cr)) cc)) $;

--| The defining property of the complex conjugate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Nov-2013.)
theorem cjth (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wa (wcel (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) caddc) cr) (wcel (co ci (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) cmin) cmul) cr)) $;

--| Domain and codomain of the conjugate function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Nov-2013.)
theorem cjf:
  $ wf cc cc ccj $;

--| The conjugate of a complex number is a complex number (closure law). (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Nov-2013.)
theorem cjcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cfv A2 ccj) cc) $;

--| The value of the real part of a complex number. (Contributed by NM, 9-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Nov-2013.)
theorem reval (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 cre) (co (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) caddc) c2 cdiv)) $;

--| The value of the imaginary part of a complex number. (Contributed by NM, 9-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Nov-2013.)
theorem imval (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 cim) (cfv (co A2 ci cdiv) cre)) $;

--| The imaginary part of a complex number in terms of the real part function. (Contributed by NM, 12-May-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Nov-2013.)
theorem imre (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 cim) (cfv (co (cneg ci) A2 cmul) cre)) $;

--| The real part of a complex number in terms of the imaginary part function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Mar-2015.)
theorem reim (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 cre) (cfv (co ci A2 cmul) cim)) $;

--| The real part of a complex number is real. (Contributed by NM, 9-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Nov-2013.)
theorem recl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cfv A2 cre) cr) $;

--| The imaginary part of a complex number is real. (Contributed by NM, 9-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Nov-2013.)
theorem imcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cfv A2 cim) cr) $;

--| Domain and codomain of the real part function. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 22-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Nov-2013.)
theorem ref:
  $ wf cc cr cre $;

--| Domain and codomain of the imaginary part function. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 22-Oct-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Nov-2013.)
theorem imf:
  $ wf cc cr cim $;

--| The real part of a complex number representation. Definition 10-3.1 of [Gleason] p. 132. (Contributed by NM, 12-May-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Nov-2013.)
theorem crre (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 (co ci B2 cmul) caddc) cre) A2) $;

--| The real part of a complex number representation. Definition 10-3.1 of [Gleason] p. 132. (Contributed by NM, 12-May-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Nov-2013.)
theorem crim (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 (co ci B2 cmul) caddc) cim) B2) $;

--| Reconstruct a complex number from its real and imaginary parts. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Nov-2013.)
theorem replim (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq A2 (co (cfv A2 cre) (co ci (cfv A2 cim) cmul) caddc)) $;

--| Value of the conjugate of a complex number. The value is the real part minus ` _i ` times the imaginary part. Definition 10-3.2 of [Gleason] p. 132. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Nov-2013.)
theorem remim (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 ccj) (co (cfv A2 cre) (co ci (cfv A2 cim) cmul) cmin)) $;

--| The imaginary part of a real number is 0. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Nov-2013.)
theorem reim0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 cim) cc0) $;

--| A number is real iff its imaginary part is 0. (Contributed by NM, 26-Sep-2005.)
theorem reim0b (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wb (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 cim) cc0)) $;

--| A number is real iff it equals its real part. Proposition 10-3.4(f) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 20-Aug-2008.)
theorem rereb (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wb (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 cre) A2)) $;

--| A product with a real multiplier apart from zero is real iff the multiplicand is real. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 14-Jun-2020.)
theorem mulreap (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr B2 cc0 cap)) (wb (wcel A2 cr) (wcel (co B2 A2 cmul) cr)) $;

--| A real number equals its real part. One direction of Proposition 10-3.4(f) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 7-Sep-2007.)
theorem rere (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 cre) A2) $;

--| A number is real iff it equals its complex conjugate. Proposition 10-3.4(f) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 2-Jul-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem cjreb (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wb (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 ccj) A2)) $;

--| Real part of a complex conjugate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem recj (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ccj) cre) (cfv A2 cre)) $;

--| Real part of negative. (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem reneg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (cneg A2) cre) (cneg (cfv A2 cre))) $;

--| Real part distributes over addition. (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem readd (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 caddc) cre) (co (cfv A2 cre) (cfv B2 cre) caddc)) $;

--| Real part distributes over subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-2005.)
theorem resub (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cre) (co (cfv A2 cre) (cfv B2 cre) cmin)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ remul , ~ immul , and ~ cjmul . (Contributed by NM, 28-Jul-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem remullem (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (w3a (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cre) (co (co (cfv A2 cre) (cfv B2 cre) cmul) (co (cfv A2 cim) (cfv B2 cim) cmul) cmin)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cim) (co (co (cfv A2 cre) (cfv B2 cim) cmul) (co (cfv A2 cim) (cfv B2 cre) cmul) caddc)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) ccj) (co (cfv A2 ccj) (cfv B2 ccj) cmul))) $;

--| Real part of a product. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jul-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem remul (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cre) (co (co (cfv A2 cre) (cfv B2 cre) cmul) (co (cfv A2 cim) (cfv B2 cim) cmul) cmin)) $;

--| Real part of a product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem remul2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cre) (co A2 (cfv B2 cre) cmul)) $;

--| Real part of a division. Related to ~ remul2 . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 14-Jun-2020.)
theorem redivap (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr B2 cc0 cap)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cdiv) cre) (co (cfv A2 cre) B2 cdiv)) $;

--| Imaginary part of a complex conjugate. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem imcj (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ccj) cim) (cneg (cfv A2 cim))) $;

--| The imaginary part of a negative number. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem imneg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (cneg A2) cim) (cneg (cfv A2 cim))) $;

--| Imaginary part distributes over addition. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem imadd (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 caddc) cim) (co (cfv A2 cim) (cfv B2 cim) caddc)) $;

--| Imaginary part distributes over subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-2005.)
theorem imsub (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cim) (co (cfv A2 cim) (cfv B2 cim) cmin)) $;

--| Imaginary part of a product. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jul-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem immul (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cim) (co (co (cfv A2 cre) (cfv B2 cim) cmul) (co (cfv A2 cim) (cfv B2 cre) cmul) caddc)) $;

--| Imaginary part of a product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Aug-2014.)
theorem immul2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cim) (co A2 (cfv B2 cim) cmul)) $;

--| Imaginary part of a division. Related to ~ immul2 . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 14-Jun-2020.)
theorem imdivap (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr B2 cc0 cap)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cdiv) cim) (co (cfv A2 cim) B2 cdiv)) $;

--| A real number equals its complex conjugate. Proposition 10-3.4(f) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 8-Oct-1999.)
theorem cjre (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 ccj) A2) $;

--| The conjugate of the conjugate is the original complex number. Proposition 10-3.4(e) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jul-1999.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem cjcj (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ccj) ccj) A2) $;

--| Complex conjugate distributes over addition. Proposition 10-3.4(a) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem cjadd (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 caddc) ccj) (co (cfv A2 ccj) (cfv B2 ccj) caddc)) $;

--| Complex conjugate distributes over multiplication. Proposition 10-3.4(c) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jul-1999.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem cjmul (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) ccj) (co (cfv A2 ccj) (cfv B2 ccj) cmul)) $;

--| Standard inner product on complex numbers. (Contributed by NM, 29-Jul-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem ipcnval (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 (cfv B2 ccj) cmul) cre) (co (co (cfv A2 cre) (cfv B2 cre) cmul) (co (cfv A2 cim) (cfv B2 cim) cmul) caddc)) $;

--| A complex number times its conjugate is real. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem cjmulrcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) cmul) cr) $;

--| A complex number times its conjugate. (Contributed by NM, 1-Feb-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem cjmulval (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) cmul) (co (co (cfv A2 cre) c2 cexp) (co (cfv A2 cim) c2 cexp) caddc)) $;

--| A complex number times its conjugate is nonnegative. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem cjmulge0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wbr cc0 (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) cmul) cle) $;

--| Complex conjugate of negative. (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem cjneg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (cneg A2) ccj) (cneg (cfv A2 ccj))) $;

--| A number plus its conjugate is twice its real part. Compare Proposition 10-3.4(h) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jan-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 14-Jul-2014.)
theorem addcj (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) caddc) (co c2 (cfv A2 cre) cmul)) $;

--| Complex conjugate distributes over subtraction. (Contributed by NM, 28-Apr-2005.)
theorem cjsub (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) ccj) (co (cfv A2 ccj) (cfv B2 ccj) cmin)) $;

--| Complex conjugate of positive integer exponentiation. (Contributed by NM, 7-Jun-2006.)
theorem cjexp (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 N cexp) ccj) (co (cfv A2 ccj) N cexp)) $;

--| The imaginary part of a number in terms of complex conjugate. (Contributed by NM, 30-Apr-2005.)
theorem imval2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 cim) (co (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) cmin) (co c2 ci cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| The real part of zero. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jul-1999.)
theorem re0:
  $ wceq (cfv cc0 cre) cc0 $;

--| The imaginary part of zero. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jul-1999.)
theorem im0:
  $ wceq (cfv cc0 cim) cc0 $;

--| The real part of one. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Jun-2006.)
theorem re1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 cre) c1 $;

--| The imaginary part of one. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Jun-2006.)
theorem im1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 cim) cc0 $;

--| The real part of ` _i ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Jun-2006.)
theorem rei:
  $ wceq (cfv ci cre) cc0 $;

--| The imaginary part of ` _i ` . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 9-Jun-2006.)
theorem imi:
  $ wceq (cfv ci cim) c1 $;

--| The conjugate of zero. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jul-1999.)
theorem cj0:
  $ wceq (cfv cc0 ccj) cc0 $;

--| The complex conjugate of the imaginary unit. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-2005.)
theorem cji:
  $ wceq (cfv ci ccj) (cneg ci) $;

--| The conjugate of a representation of a complex number in terms of real and imaginary parts. (Contributed by NM, 1-Jul-2005.)
theorem cjreim (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 (co ci B2 cmul) caddc) ccj) (co A2 (co ci B2 cmul) cmin)) $;

--| The conjugate of the representation of a complex number in terms of real and imaginary parts. (Contributed by NM, 1-Jul-2005.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem cjreim2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 (co ci B2 cmul) cmin) ccj) (co A2 (co ci B2 cmul) caddc)) $;

--| Complex conjugate is a one-to-one function. (Contributed by NM, 29-Apr-2005.) (Proof shortened by Eric Schmidt, 2-Jul-2009.)
theorem cj11 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 ccj) (cfv B2 ccj)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Complex conjugate and apartness. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 14-Jun-2020.)
theorem cjap (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wb (wbr (cfv A2 ccj) (cfv B2 ccj) cap) (wbr A2 B2 cap)) $;

--| A number is apart from zero iff its complex conjugate is apart from zero. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 14-Jun-2020.)
theorem cjap0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wb (wbr A2 cc0 cap) (wbr (cfv A2 ccj) cc0 cap)) $;

--| A number is nonzero iff its complex conjugate is nonzero. Also see ~ cjap0 which is similar but for apartness. (Contributed by NM, 29-Apr-2005.)
theorem cjne0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wb (wne A2 cc0) (wne (cfv A2 ccj) cc0)) $;

--| Complex conjugate distributes over division. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 14-Jun-2020.)
theorem cjdivap (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wbr B2 cc0 cap)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cdiv) ccj) (co (cfv A2 ccj) (cfv B2 ccj) cdiv)) $;

--| The inverse to the canonical bijection from ` ( RR X. RR ) ` to ` CC ` from ~ cnref1o . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Aug-2014.)
theorem cnrecnv {x3 y1 z: setvar} (F: class x3 y1):
  $ wceq F (cmpt2 x3 y1 cr cr (co (cv x3) (co ci (cv y1) cmul) caddc)) $ >
  $ wceq (ccnv F) (cmpt z cc (cop (cfv (cv z) cre) (cfv (cv z) cim))) $;

--| The real part of a complex number is real (closure law). (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1999.)
theorem recli (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel (cfv A2 cre) cr $;

--| The imaginary part of a complex number is real (closure law). (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1999.)
theorem imcli (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel (cfv A2 cim) cr $;

--| Closure law for complex conjugate. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1999.)
theorem cjcli (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel (cfv A2 ccj) cc $;

--| Construct a complex number from its real and imaginary parts. (Contributed by NM, 1-Oct-1999.)
theorem replimi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq A2 (co (cfv A2 cre) (co ci (cfv A2 cim) cmul) caddc) $;

--| The conjugate of the conjugate is the original complex number. Proposition 10-3.4(e) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1999.)
theorem cjcji (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ccj) ccj) A2 $;

--| A number is real iff its imaginary part is 0. (Contributed by NM, 29-May-1999.)
theorem reim0bi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 cim) cc0) $;

--| A real number equals its real part. Proposition 10-3.4(f) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 27-Oct-1999.)
theorem rerebi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 cre) A2) $;

--| A number is real iff it equals its complex conjugate. Proposition 10-3.4(f) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-1999.)
theorem cjrebi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wb (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 ccj) A2) $;

--| Real part of a complex conjugate. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.)
theorem recji (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ccj) cre) (cfv A2 cre) $;

--| Imaginary part of a complex conjugate. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.)
theorem imcji (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ccj) cim) (cneg (cfv A2 cim)) $;

--| A complex number times its conjugate is real. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-1999.)
theorem cjmulrcli (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) cmul) cr $;

--| A complex number times its conjugate. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.)
theorem cjmulvali (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) cmul) (co (co (cfv A2 cre) c2 cexp) (co (cfv A2 cim) c2 cexp) caddc) $;

--| A complex number times its conjugate is nonnegative. (Contributed by NM, 28-May-1999.)
theorem cjmulge0i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wbr cc0 (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) cmul) cle $;

--| Real part of negative. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-1999.)
theorem renegi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cneg A2) cre) (cneg (cfv A2 cre)) $;

--| Imaginary part of negative. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-1999.)
theorem imnegi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cneg A2) cim) (cneg (cfv A2 cim)) $;

--| Complex conjugate of negative. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-1999.)
theorem cjnegi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cneg A2) ccj) (cneg (cfv A2 ccj)) $;

--| A number plus its conjugate is twice its real part. Compare Proposition 10-3.4(h) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.)
theorem addcji (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) caddc) (co c2 (cfv A2 cre) cmul) $;

--| Real part distributes over addition. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jul-1999.)
theorem readdi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 caddc) cre) (co (cfv A2 cre) (cfv B2 cre) caddc) $;

--| Imaginary part distributes over addition. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jul-1999.)
theorem imaddi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 caddc) cim) (co (cfv A2 cim) (cfv B2 cim) caddc) $;

--| Real part of a product. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jul-1999.)
theorem remuli (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cre) (co (co (cfv A2 cre) (cfv B2 cre) cmul) (co (cfv A2 cim) (cfv B2 cim) cmul) cmin) $;

--| Imaginary part of a product. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jul-1999.)
theorem immuli (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cim) (co (co (cfv A2 cre) (cfv B2 cim) cmul) (co (cfv A2 cim) (cfv B2 cre) cmul) caddc) $;

--| Complex conjugate distributes over addition. Proposition 10-3.4(a) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jul-1999.)
theorem cjaddi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 caddc) ccj) (co (cfv A2 ccj) (cfv B2 ccj) caddc) $;

--| Complex conjugate distributes over multiplication. Proposition 10-3.4(c) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jul-1999.)
theorem cjmuli (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) ccj) (co (cfv A2 ccj) (cfv B2 ccj) cmul) $;

--| Standard inner product on complex numbers. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.)
theorem ipcni (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (co A2 (cfv B2 ccj) cmul) cre) (co (co (cfv A2 cre) (cfv B2 cre) cmul) (co (cfv A2 cim) (cfv B2 cim) cmul) caddc) $;

--| Complex conjugate distributes over division. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 14-Jun-2020.)
theorem cjdivapi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wi (wbr B2 cc0 cap) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cdiv) ccj) (co (cfv A2 ccj) (cfv B2 ccj) cdiv)) $;

--| The real part of a complex number representation. Definition 10-3.1 of [Gleason] p. 132. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1999.)
theorem crrei (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (co A2 (co ci B2 cmul) caddc) cre) A2 $;

--| The imaginary part of a complex number representation. Definition 10-3.1 of [Gleason] p. 132. (Contributed by NM, 10-May-1999.)
theorem crimi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (co A2 (co ci B2 cmul) caddc) cim) B2 $;

--| The real part of a complex number is real (closure law). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem recld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 cre) cr) $;

--| The imaginary part of a complex number is real (closure law). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem imcld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 cim) cr) $;

--| Closure law for complex conjugate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem cjcld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 ccj) cc) $;

--| Construct a complex number from its real and imaginary parts. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem replimd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 (co (cfv A2 cre) (co ci (cfv A2 cim) cmul) caddc)) $;

--| Value of the conjugate of a complex number. The value is the real part minus ` _i ` times the imaginary part. Definition 10-3.2 of [Gleason] p. 132. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem remimd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 ccj) (co (cfv A2 cre) (co ci (cfv A2 cim) cmul) cmin)) $;

--| The conjugate of the conjugate is the original complex number. Proposition 10-3.4(e) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem cjcjd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ccj) ccj) A2) $;

--| A number is real iff its imaginary part is 0. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem reim0bd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 cim) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| A real number equals its real part. Proposition 10-3.4(f) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem rerebd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 cre) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| A number is real iff it equals its complex conjugate. Proposition 10-3.4(f) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem cjrebd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 ccj) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| A number which is nonzero has a complex conjugate which is nonzero. Also see ~ cjap0d which is similar but for apartness. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem cjne0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv A2 ccj) cc0) $;

--| A number which is apart from zero has a complex conjugate which is apart from zero. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Aug-2021.)
theorem cjap0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 cap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv A2 ccj) cc0 cap) $;

--| Real part of a complex conjugate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem recjd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ccj) cre) (cfv A2 cre)) $;

--| Imaginary part of a complex conjugate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem imcjd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ccj) cim) (cneg (cfv A2 cim))) $;

--| A complex number times its conjugate is real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem cjmulrcld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) cmul) cr) $;

--| A complex number times its conjugate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem cjmulvald (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) cmul) (co (co (cfv A2 cre) c2 cexp) (co (cfv A2 cim) c2 cexp) caddc)) $;

--| A complex number times its conjugate is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem cjmulge0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) cmul) cle) $;

--| Real part of negative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem renegd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cneg A2) cre) (cneg (cfv A2 cre))) $;

--| Imaginary part of negative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem imnegd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cneg A2) cim) (cneg (cfv A2 cim))) $;

--| Complex conjugate of negative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem cjnegd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cneg A2) ccj) (cneg (cfv A2 ccj))) $;

--| A number plus its conjugate is twice its real part. Compare Proposition 10-3.4(h) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem addcjd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) caddc) (co c2 (cfv A2 cre) cmul)) $;

--| Complex conjugate of positive integer exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem cjexpd (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 N cexp) ccj) (co (cfv A2 ccj) N cexp)) $;

--| Real part distributes over addition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem readdd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 caddc) cre) (co (cfv A2 cre) (cfv B2 cre) caddc)) $;

--| Imaginary part distributes over addition. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem imaddd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 caddc) cim) (co (cfv A2 cim) (cfv B2 cim) caddc)) $;

--| Real part distributes over subtraction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem resubd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cre) (co (cfv A2 cre) (cfv B2 cre) cmin)) $;

--| Imaginary part distributes over subtraction. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem imsubd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cim) (co (cfv A2 cim) (cfv B2 cim) cmin)) $;

--| Real part of a product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem remuld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cre) (co (co (cfv A2 cre) (cfv B2 cre) cmul) (co (cfv A2 cim) (cfv B2 cim) cmul) cmin)) $;

--| Imaginary part of a product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem immuld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cim) (co (co (cfv A2 cre) (cfv B2 cim) cmul) (co (cfv A2 cim) (cfv B2 cre) cmul) caddc)) $;

--| Complex conjugate distributes over addition. Proposition 10-3.4(a) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem cjaddd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 caddc) ccj) (co (cfv A2 ccj) (cfv B2 ccj) caddc)) $;

--| Complex conjugate distributes over multiplication. Proposition 10-3.4(c) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem cjmuld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) ccj) (co (cfv A2 ccj) (cfv B2 ccj) cmul)) $;

--| Standard inner product on complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem ipcnd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 (cfv B2 ccj) cmul) cre) (co (co (cfv A2 cre) (cfv B2 cre) cmul) (co (cfv A2 cim) (cfv B2 cim) cmul) caddc)) $;

--| Complex conjugate distributes over division. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 15-Jun-2020.)
theorem cjdivapd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 cc0 cap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cdiv) ccj) (co (cfv A2 ccj) (cfv B2 ccj) cdiv)) $;

--| A real number equals its real part. One direction of Proposition 10-3.4(f) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem rered (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 cre) A2) $;

--| The imaginary part of a real number is 0. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem reim0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 cim) cc0) $;

--| A real number equals its complex conjugate. Proposition 10-3.4(f) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem cjred (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 ccj) A2) $;

--| Real part of a product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem remul2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cre) (co A2 (cfv B2 cre) cmul)) $;

--| Imaginary part of a product. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem immul2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cim) (co A2 (cfv B2 cim) cmul)) $;

--| Real part of a division. Related to ~ remul2 . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 15-Jun-2020.)
theorem redivapd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 cap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co B2 A2 cdiv) cre) (co (cfv B2 cre) A2 cdiv)) $;

--| Imaginary part of a division. Related to ~ remul2 . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 15-Jun-2020.)
theorem imdivapd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 cap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co B2 A2 cdiv) cim) (co (cfv B2 cim) A2 cdiv)) $;

--| The real part of a complex number representation. Definition 10-3.1 of [Gleason] p. 132. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem crred (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 (co ci B2 cmul) caddc) cre) A2) $;

--| The imaginary part of a complex number representation. Definition 10-3.1 of [Gleason] p. 132. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem crimd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 (co ci B2 cmul) caddc) cim) B2) $;

--| Two ways to express a reciprocal. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 20-Jul-2021.)
theorem caucvgrelemrec {r: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr A2 cc0 cap)) (wceq (crio r (wceq (co A2 (cv r) cmul) c1) cr) (co c1 A2 cdiv)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ caucvgre . Converting the Cauchy condition. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 20-Jul-2021.)
theorem caucvgrelemcau {k n r: setvar} (ph: wff r) (F: class r):
  $ wi ph (wf cn cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wral k (wa (wbr (cfv (cv n) F) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clt) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (co (cfv (cv n) F) (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv n) cuz)) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wral k (wi (wbr (cv n) (cv k) cltrr) (wa (wbr (cfv (cv n) F) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (crio r (wceq (co (cv n) (cv r) cmul) c1) cr) caddc) cltrr) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (co (cfv (cv n) F) (crio r (wceq (co (cv n) (cv r) cmul) c1) cr) caddc) cltrr))) cn) cn) $;

--| Convergence of real sequences. A Cauchy sequence (as defined here, which has a rate of convergence built in) of real numbers converges to a real number. Specifically on rate of convergence, all terms after the nth term must be within ` 1 / n ` of the nth term. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 19-Jul-2021.)
theorem caucvgre {x3 y1 i j k n: setvar} (ph: wff i j) (F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wral k (wa (wbr (cfv (cv n) F) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clt) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (co (cfv (cv n) F) (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv n) cuz)) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wrex j (wral i (wa (wbr (cfv (cv i) F) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) caddc) clt) (wbr (cv y1) (co (cfv (cv i) F) (cv x3) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cn) crp) cr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvg1n . Rearranging an expression related to the rate of convergence. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 6-Aug-2021.)
theorem cvg1nlemcxze (ph: wff) (A2 C E X Z: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel X crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel E cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co (co (co C c2 cmul) X cdiv) Z cdiv) A2 caddc) E clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co C (co E Z cmul) cdiv) (co X c2 cdiv) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvg1n . The modified sequence ` G ` is a sequence. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 1-Aug-2021.)
theorem cvg1nlemf {j k n: setvar} (ph: wff k n) (C F G Z: class j k n):
  $ wi ph (wf cn cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wral k (wa (wbr (cfv (cv n) F) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (co C (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clt) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (co (cfv (cv n) F) (co C (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv n) cuz)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt j cn (cfv (co (cv j) Z cmul) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C Z clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn cr G) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvg1n . By selecting spaced out terms for the modified sequence ` G ` , the terms are within ` 1 / n ` (without the constant ` C ` ). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 1-Aug-2021.)
theorem cvg1nlemcau {j k n: setvar} (ph: wff j) (C: class j) (F: class) (G: class j k n) (Z: class k n):
  $ wi ph (wf cn cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wral k (wa (wbr (cfv (cv n) F) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (co C (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clt) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (co (cfv (cv n) F) (co C (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv n) cuz)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt j cn (cfv (co (cv j) Z cmul) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C Z clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wral k (wa (wbr (cfv (cv n) G) (co (cfv (cv k) G) (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clt) (wbr (cfv (cv k) G) (co (cfv (cv n) G) (co c1 (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv n) cuz)) cn) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cvg1n . The original sequence ` F ` has a limit (turns out it is the same as the limit of the modified sequence ` G ` ). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 1-Aug-2021.)
theorem cvg1nlemres {x3 y1 i j k n: setvar} (ph: wff) (C: class x3 y1 j) (F: class x3 y1 i) (G: class j) (Z: class x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wf cn cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wral k (wa (wbr (cfv (cv n) F) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (co C (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clt) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (co (cfv (cv n) F) (co C (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv n) cuz)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt j cn (cfv (co (cv j) Z cmul) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel Z cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr C Z clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wrex j (wral i (wa (wbr (cfv (cv i) F) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) caddc) clt) (wbr (cv y1) (co (cfv (cv i) F) (cv x3) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cn) crp) cr) $;

--| Convergence of real sequences. This is a version of ~ caucvgre with a constant multiplier ` C ` on the rate of convergence. That is, all terms after the nth term must be within ` C / n ` of the nth term. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 1-Aug-2021.)
theorem cvg1n {x3 y1 i j k n: setvar} (ph: wff) (C F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wral k (wa (wbr (cfv (cv n) F) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (co C (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clt) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (co (cfv (cv n) F) (co C (cv n) cdiv) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv n) cuz)) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex y1 (wral x3 (wrex j (wral i (wa (wbr (cfv (cv i) F) (co (cv y1) (cv x3) caddc) clt) (wbr (cv y1) (co (cfv (cv i) F) (cv x3) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cn) crp) cr) $;

--| The upper integers are closed under intersection. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 24-Dec-2013.)
theorem uzin2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (crn cuz)) (wcel B2 (crn cuz))) (wcel (cin A2 B2) (crn cuz)) $;

--| Combine two different upper integer properties into one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 25-Dec-2013.)
theorem rexanuz {j k: setvar} (ph ps: wff k):
  $ wb (wrex j (wral k (wa ph ps) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cz) (wa (wrex j (wral k ph (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cz) (wrex j (wral k ps (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cz)) $;

--| Restrict the base of the upper integers set to another upper integers set. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Dec-2013.)
theorem rexuz3 {j k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wcel M cz) (wb (wrex j (wral k ph (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) (wrex j (wral k ph (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cz)) $;

--| Combine two different upper integer properties into one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 26-Dec-2013.)
theorem rexanuz2 {j k: setvar} (ph ps: wff k) (M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wb (wrex j (wral k (wa ph ps) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) (wa (wrex j (wral k ph (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) (wrex j (wral k ps (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z)) $;

--| A version of ~ 19.29 for upper integer quantifiers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Feb-2014.)
theorem r19_29uz {j k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (ps: wff j k) (M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wral k ph Z) (wrex j (wral k ps (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z)) (wrex j (wral k (wa ph ps) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| A version of ~ r19.2m for upper integer quantifiers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Feb-2014.)
theorem r19_2uz {j k: setvar} (ph: wff k) (M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wrex j (wral k ph (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) (wrex k ph Z) $;

--| Lemma for ~ recvguniq . Some of the rearrangements of the expressions. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 8-Aug-2021.)
theorem recvguniqlem (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F K: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 (co (cfv K F) (co (co A2 B2 cmin) c2 cdiv) caddc) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv K F) (co B2 (co (co A2 B2 cmin) c2 cdiv) caddc) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph wfal $;

--| Limits are unique. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 7-Aug-2021.)
theorem recvguniq {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 j) (F: class k) (L M: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (co L (cv x3) caddc) clt) (wbr L (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cv x3) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cn) crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (co M (cv x3) caddc) clt) (wbr M (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cv x3) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cn) crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq L M) $;

--| Extend class notation to include square root of a complex number.
term csqrt: class;

--| Extend class notation to include a function for the absolute value (modulus) of a complex number.
term cabs: class;

--| Define a function whose value is the square root of a nonnegative real number. Defining the square root for complex numbers has one difficult part: choosing between the two roots. The usual way to define a principal square root for all complex numbers relies on excluded middle or something similar. But in the case of a nonnegative real number, we don't have the complications presented for general complex numbers, and we can choose the nonnegative root. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 23-Aug-2020.)
axiom df_rsqrt {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wceq csqrt (cmpt x3 cr (crio y1 (wa (wceq (co (cv y1) c2 cexp) (cv x3)) (wbr cc0 (cv y1) cle)) cr)) $;

--| Define the function for the absolute value (modulus) of a complex number. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jul-1999.)
axiom df_abs {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq cabs (cmpt x3 cc (cfv (co (cv x3) (cfv (cv x3) ccj) cmul) csqrt)) $;

--| Value of square root function. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 23-Aug-2020.)
theorem sqrtrval {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 csqrt) (crio x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) A2) (wbr cc0 (cv x3) cle)) cr)) $;

--| The absolute value (modulus) of a complex number. Proposition 10-3.7(a) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jul-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Nov-2013.)
theorem absval (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) cmul) csqrt)) $;

--| A real number does not lie on the negative imaginary axis. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 8-Jul-2013.)
theorem rennim (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wnel (co ci A2 cmul) crp) $;

--| Lemma for ~ sqrt0 . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 26-Aug-2020.)
theorem sqrt0rlem (A2: class):
  $ wb (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wa (wceq (co A2 c2 cexp) cc0) (wbr cc0 A2 cle))) (wceq A2 cc0) $;

--| Square root of zero. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2013.)
theorem sqrt0:
  $ wceq (cfv cc0 csqrt) cc0 $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . ` 1 + A ` is a positive real (expressed in a way that will help apply ~ iseqf and similar theorems). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 28-Jul-2021.)
theorem resqrexlem1arp {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F N: class y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel N cn)) (wcel (cfv N (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc)))) crp) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . Applying the recursion rule yields a positive real (expressed in a way that will help apply ~ iseqf and similar theorems). (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 28-Jul-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemp1rp {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class) (F: class y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel B2 crp) (wcel C crp))) (wcel (co B2 C (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv))) crp) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . The sequence is a function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro and Jim Kingdon, 27-Jul-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemf {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf cn crp F) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . Initial value. Although this sequence converges to the square root with any positive initial value, this choice makes various steps in the proof of convergence easier. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro and Jim Kingdon, 27-Jul-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemf1 {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c1 F) (co c1 A2 caddc)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . Recursion rule. This sequence is the ancient method for computing square roots, often known as the babylonian method, although known to many ancient cultures. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro and Jim Kingdon, 27-Jul-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemfp1 {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F N: class y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel N cn)) (wceq (cfv (co N c1 caddc) F) (co (co (cfv N F) (co A2 (cfv N F) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . Each element of the sequence is an overestimate. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro and Jim Kingdon, 27-Jul-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemover {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F N: class y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel N cn)) (wbr A2 (co (cfv N F) c2 cexp) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . The sequence is decreasing. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro and Jim Kingdon, 29-Jul-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemdec {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F N: class y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel N cn)) (wbr (cfv (co N c1 caddc) F) (cfv N F) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . The sequence is decreasing. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 31-Jul-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemdecn {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F M N: class y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr N M clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv M F) (cfv N F) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . A (variable) lower bound for each term of the sequence. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro and Jim Kingdon, 29-Jul-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemlo {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F N: class y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel N cn)) (wbr (co c1 (co c2 N cexp) cdiv) (cfv N F) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . Some of the calculations involved in showing that the sequence converges. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro and Jim Kingdon, 29-Jul-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemcalc1 {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F N: class y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel N cn)) (wceq (co (co (cfv (co N c1 caddc) F) c2 cexp) A2 cmin) (co (co (co (co (cfv N F) c2 cexp) A2 cmin) c2 cexp) (co c4 (co (cfv N F) c2 cexp) cmul) cdiv)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . Some of the calculations involved in showing that the sequence converges. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro and Jim Kingdon, 29-Jul-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemcalc2 {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F N: class y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel N cn)) (wbr (co (co (cfv (co N c1 caddc) F) c2 cexp) A2 cmin) (co (co (co (cfv N F) c2 cexp) A2 cmin) c4 cdiv) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . Some of the calculations involved in showing that the sequence converges. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro and Jim Kingdon, 29-Jul-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemcalc3 {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F N: class y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel N cn)) (wbr (co (co (cfv N F) c2 cexp) A2 cmin) (co (co (cfv c1 F) c2 cexp) (co c4 (co N c1 cmin) cexp) cdiv) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . The difference between the squares of two terms of the sequence. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro and Jim Kingdon, 30-Jul-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemnmsq {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class y1 z) (M N: class):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr N M cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (co (cfv N F) c2 cexp) (co (cfv M F) c2 cexp) cmin) (co (co (cfv c1 F) c2 cexp) (co c4 (co N c1 cmin) cexp) cdiv) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . The difference between two terms of the sequence. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro and Jim Kingdon, 31-Jul-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemnm {y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class y1 z) (M N: class):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr N M cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (cfv N F) (cfv M F) cmin) (co (co (co (cfv c1 F) c2 cexp) c2 cmul) (co c2 (co N c1 cmin) cexp) cdiv) clt) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . The sequence has a limit. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 6-Aug-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemcvg {x3 y1 z i j r: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class x3 i j r) (F: class y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex r (wral x3 (wrex j (wral i (wa (wbr (cfv (cv i) F) (co (cv r) (cv x3) caddc) clt) (wbr (cv r) (co (cfv (cv i) F) (cv x3) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cn) crp) cr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . A limit is nonnegative. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 7-Aug-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemgt0 {y1 z e i j: setvar} (ph: wff e) (A2: class e i j) (F: class y1 z i j) (L: class y1 z):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral e (wrex j (wral i (wa (wbr (cfv (cv i) F) (co L (cv e) caddc) clt) (wbr L (co (cfv (cv i) F) (cv e) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cn) crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 L cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . Every term in the sequence is an overestimate compared with the limit ` L ` . Although this theorem is stated in terms of a particular sequence the proof could be adapted for any decreasing convergent sequence. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 9-Aug-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemoverl {y1 z e i j: setvar} (ph: wff e i j) (A2: class e i j) (F K L: class):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral e (wrex j (wral i (wa (wbr (cfv (cv i) F) (co L (cv e) caddc) clt) (wbr L (co (cfv (cv i) F) (cv e) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cn) crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr L (cfv K F) cle) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . The sequence formed by squaring each term of ` F ` converges to ` ( L ^ 2 ) ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro and Jim Kingdon, 8-Aug-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemglsq {x3 y1 z e i j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3 e i j k) (F: class) (G: class x3 y1 z e i j k) (L: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral e (wrex j (wral i (wa (wbr (cfv (cv i) F) (co L (cv e) caddc) clt) (wbr L (co (cfv (cv i) F) (cv e) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cn) crp) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cn (co (cfv (cv x3) F) c2 cexp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral e (wrex j (wral k (wa (wbr (cfv (cv k) G) (co (co L c2 cexp) (cv e) caddc) clt) (wbr (co L c2 cexp) (co (cfv (cv k) G) (cv e) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cn) crp) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . The sequence formed by squaring each term of ` F ` converges to ` A ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro and Jim Kingdon, 8-Aug-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemga {x3 y1 z e i j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3 i) (A2: class x3 e i j k) (F: class y1 z e i) (G L: class x3 y1 z e i j k):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral e (wrex j (wral i (wa (wbr (cfv (cv i) F) (co L (cv e) caddc) clt) (wbr L (co (cfv (cv i) F) (cv e) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cn) crp) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cn (co (cfv (cv x3) F) c2 cexp)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral e (wrex j (wral k (wa (wbr (cfv (cv k) G) (co A2 (cv e) caddc) clt) (wbr A2 (co (cfv (cv k) G) (cv e) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cn) crp) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . The square of a limit is ` A ` . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 7-Aug-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemsqa {y1 z e i j: setvar} (ph: wff e i j) (A2: class i) (F L: class):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel L cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral e (wrex j (wral i (wa (wbr (cfv (cv i) F) (co L (cv e) caddc) clt) (wbr L (co (cfv (cv i) F) (cv e) caddc) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cn) crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co L c2 cexp) A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrex . Existence of square root given a sequence which converges to the square root. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro and Jim Kingdon, 27-Jul-2021.)
theorem resqrexlemex {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (F: class x3):
  $ wceq F (cseq (cmpt2 y1 z crp crp (co (co (cv y1) (co A2 (cv y1) cdiv) caddc) c2 cdiv)) crp (cxp cn (csn (co c1 A2 caddc))) c1) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex x3 (wa (wbr cc0 (cv x3) cle) (wceq (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) A2)) cr) $;

--| Existence of a square root for positive reals. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2013.)
theorem resqrex {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wrex x3 (wa (wbr cc0 (cv x3) cle) (wceq (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) A2)) cr) $;

--| Uniqueness for the square root function. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 10-Aug-2021.)
theorem rsqrmo {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wrmo x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) A2) (wbr cc0 (cv x3) cle)) cr) $;

--| Existence and uniqueness for the real square root function. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 10-Aug-2021.)
theorem rersqreu {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wreu x3 (wa (wceq (co (cv x3) c2 cexp) A2) (wbr cc0 (cv x3) cle)) cr) $;

--| Closure of the square root function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2013.)
theorem resqrtcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wcel (cfv A2 csqrt) cr) $;

--| Lemma for ~ resqrtth . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 10-Aug-2021.)
theorem rersqrtthlem (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wceq (co (cfv A2 csqrt) c2 cexp) A2) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 csqrt) cle)) $;

--| Square root theorem over the reals. Theorem I.35 of [Apostol] p. 29. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2013.)
theorem resqrtth (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wceq (co (cfv A2 csqrt) c2 cexp) A2) $;

--| Square of square root. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2013.)
theorem remsqsqrt (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wceq (co (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv A2 csqrt) cmul) A2) $;

--| The square root function is nonnegative for nonnegative input. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Jul-2013.)
theorem sqrtge0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 csqrt) cle) $;

--| The square root function is positive for positive input. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 10-Jul-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2013.)
theorem sqrtgt0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 clt)) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 csqrt) clt) $;

--| Square root distributes over multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jul-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem sqrtmul (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle))) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) csqrt) (co (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv B2 csqrt) cmul)) $;

--| Square root is monotonic. (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-2005.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem sqrtle (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv B2 csqrt) cle)) $;

--| Square root is strictly monotonic. Closed form of ~ sqrtlti . (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 17-Apr-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem sqrtlt (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle))) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv B2 csqrt) clt)) $;

--| Analogue to ~ sqrt11 but for apartness. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Aug-2021.)
theorem sqrt11ap (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle))) (wb (wbr (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv B2 csqrt) cap) (wbr A2 B2 cap)) $;

--| The square root function is one-to-one. Also see ~ sqrt11ap which would follow easily from this given excluded middle, but which is proved another way without it. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 11-Jun-2013.)
theorem sqrt11 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle))) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv B2 csqrt)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| A square root is zero iff its argument is 0. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jul-1999.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem sqrt00 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 csqrt) cc0) (wceq A2 cc0)) $;

--| The square root of a positive real is a positive real. (Contributed by NM, 22-Feb-2008.)
theorem rpsqrtcl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 crp) (wcel (cfv A2 csqrt) crp) $;

--| Square root distributes over division. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-May-2016.)
theorem sqrtdiv (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wcel B2 crp)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cdiv) csqrt) (co (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv B2 csqrt) cdiv)) $;

--| Relationship between square root and squares. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem sqrtsq2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle))) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 csqrt) B2) (wceq A2 (co B2 c2 cexp))) $;

--| Square root of square. (Contributed by NM, 14-Jan-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem sqrtsq (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 c2 cexp) csqrt) A2) $;

--| Square root of square. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem sqrtmsq (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 A2 cmul) csqrt) A2) $;

--| The square root of 1 is 1. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-1999.)
theorem sqrt1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 csqrt) c1 $;

--| The square root of 4 is 2. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-1999.)
theorem sqrt4:
  $ wceq (cfv c4 csqrt) c2 $;

--| The square root of 9 is 3. (Contributed by NM, 11-May-2004.)
theorem sqrt9:
  $ wceq (cfv c9 csqrt) c3 $;

--| The square root of 2 is bounded by 1 and 2. (Contributed by Roy F. Longton, 8-Aug-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2013.)
theorem sqrt2gt1lt2:
  $ wa (wbr c1 (cfv c2 csqrt) clt) (wbr (cfv c2 csqrt) c2 clt) $;

--| Absolute value of negative. (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2005.)
theorem absneg (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (cneg A2) cabs) (cfv A2 cabs)) $;

--| Real closure of absolute value. (Contributed by NM, 3-Oct-1999.)
theorem abscl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cfv A2 cabs) cr) $;

--| The absolute value of a number and its conjugate are the same. Proposition 10-3.7(b) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 28-Apr-2005.)
theorem abscj (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ccj) cabs) (cfv A2 cabs)) $;

--| Square of value of absolute value function. (Contributed by NM, 16-Jan-2006.)
theorem absvalsq (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co (cfv A2 cabs) c2 cexp) (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) cmul)) $;

--| Square of value of absolute value function. (Contributed by NM, 1-Feb-2007.)
theorem absvalsq2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co (cfv A2 cabs) c2 cexp) (co (co (cfv A2 cre) c2 cexp) (co (cfv A2 cim) c2 cexp) caddc)) $;

--| Square of absolute value of sum. Proposition 10-3.7(g) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jan-2007.)
theorem sqabsadd (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (cfv (co A2 B2 caddc) cabs) c2 cexp) (co (co (co (cfv A2 cabs) c2 cexp) (co (cfv B2 cabs) c2 cexp) caddc) (co c2 (cfv (co A2 (cfv B2 ccj) cmul) cre) cmul) caddc)) $;

--| Square of absolute value of difference. (Contributed by NM, 21-Jan-2007.)
theorem sqabssub (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (co (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) c2 cexp) (co (co (co (cfv A2 cabs) c2 cexp) (co (cfv B2 cabs) c2 cexp) caddc) (co c2 (cfv (co A2 (cfv B2 ccj) cmul) cre) cmul) cmin)) $;

--| Value of absolute value function. Definition 10.36 of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-2005.)
theorem absval2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv (co (co (cfv A2 cre) c2 cexp) (co (cfv A2 cim) c2 cexp) caddc) csqrt)) $;

--| The absolute value of 0. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem abs0:
  $ wceq (cfv cc0 cabs) cc0 $;

--| The absolute value of the imaginary unit. (Contributed by NM, 26-Mar-2005.)
theorem absi:
  $ wceq (cfv ci cabs) c1 $;

--| Absolute value is nonnegative. (Contributed by NM, 20-Nov-2004.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absge0 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 cabs) cle) $;

--| The absolute value of a number apart from zero is a positive real. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Aug-2021.)
theorem absrpclap (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap)) (wcel (cfv A2 cabs) crp) $;

--| The absolute value of a number is apart from zero iff the number is apart from zero. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Aug-2021.)
theorem abs00ap (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wb (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) cc0 cap) (wbr A2 cc0 cap)) $;

--| Strong extensionality for absolute value. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-Aug-2021.)
theorem absext (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wi (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) cap) (wbr A2 B2 cap)) $;

--| The absolute value of a number is zero iff the number is zero. Also see ~ abs00ap which is similar but for apartness. Proposition 10-3.7(c) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 26-Sep-2005.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem abs00 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) cc0) (wceq A2 cc0)) $;

--| A complex number is zero iff its absolute value is zero. Deduction form of ~ abs00 . (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem abs00ad (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) cc0) (wceq A2 cc0)) $;

--| If a complex number is zero, its absolute value is zero. (Contributed by David Moews, 28-Feb-2017.)
theorem abs00bd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) cc0) $;

--| Square of the absolute value of a number that has been decomposed into real and imaginary parts. (Contributed by NM, 1-Feb-2007.)
theorem absreimsq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wceq (co (cfv (co A2 (co ci B2 cmul) caddc) cabs) c2 cexp) (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc)) $;

--| Absolute value of a number that has been decomposed into real and imaginary parts. (Contributed by NM, 14-Jan-2006.)
theorem absreim (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 (co ci B2 cmul) caddc) cabs) (cfv (co (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) caddc) csqrt)) $;

--| Absolute value distributes over multiplication. Proposition 10-3.7(f) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absmul (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cabs) (co (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) cmul)) $;

--| Absolute value distributes over division. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Aug-2021.)
theorem absdivap (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wbr B2 cc0 cap)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cdiv) cabs) (co (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) cdiv)) $;

--| A nonnegative number is its own absolute value. (Contributed by NM, 11-Oct-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) A2) $;

--| The absolute value of 1. Common special case. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 16-Jul-2016.)
theorem abs1:
  $ wceq (cfv c1 cabs) c1 $;

--| A negative number is the negative of its own absolute value. (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2005.)
theorem absnid (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr A2 cc0 cle)) (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) (cneg A2)) $;

--| A real number is less than or equal to its absolute value. (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2005.)
theorem leabs (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wbr A2 (cfv A2 cabs) cle) $;

--| Absolute value of a real number. (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-2005.)
theorem absre (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv (co A2 c2 cexp) csqrt)) $;

--| Square of the absolute value of a real number. (Contributed by NM, 16-Jan-2006.)
theorem absresq (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cr) (wceq (co (cfv A2 cabs) c2 cexp) (co A2 c2 cexp)) $;

--| Absolute value of positive integer exponentiation. (Contributed by NM, 5-Jan-2006.)
theorem absexp (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel N cn0)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 N cexp) cabs) (co (cfv A2 cabs) N cexp)) $;

--| Absolute value of integer exponentiation. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 11-Aug-2021.)
theorem absexpzap (A2 N: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap) (wcel N cz)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 N cexp) cabs) (co (cfv A2 cabs) N cexp)) $;

--| Square can be moved in and out of absolute value. (Contributed by Scott Fenton, 18-Apr-2014.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem abssq (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (co (cfv A2 cabs) c2 cexp) (cfv (co A2 c2 cexp) cabs)) $;

--| The squares of two reals are equal iff their absolute values are equal. (Contributed by NM, 6-Mar-2009.)
theorem sqabs (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wceq (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp)) (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs))) $;

--| The absolute value of a complex number is greater than or equal to the absolute value of its real part. (Contributed by NM, 1-Apr-2005.)
theorem absrele (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wbr (cfv (cfv A2 cre) cabs) (cfv A2 cabs) cle) $;

--| The absolute value of a complex number is greater than or equal to the absolute value of its imaginary part. (Contributed by NM, 17-Mar-2005.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absimle (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wbr (cfv (cfv A2 cim) cabs) (cfv A2 cabs) cle) $;

--| The absolute value of an integer is a nonnegative integer. (Contributed by NM, 27-Feb-2005.)
theorem nn0abscl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cz) (wcel (cfv A2 cabs) cn0) $;

--| The absolute value of an integer is an integer. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 24-Sep-2014.)
theorem zabscl (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cz) (wcel (cfv A2 cabs) cz) $;

--| A number which is less than its absolute value is negative. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-Aug-2021.)
theorem ltabs (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr A2 (cfv A2 cabs) clt)) (wbr A2 cc0 clt) $;

--| Absolute value and 'less than' relation. (Contributed by NM, 6-Apr-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem abslt (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) B2 clt) (wa (wbr (cneg B2) A2 clt) (wbr A2 B2 clt))) $;

--| Absolute value and 'less than or equal to' relation. (Contributed by NM, 6-Apr-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absle (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wb (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) B2 cle) (wa (wbr (cneg B2) A2 cle) (wbr A2 B2 cle))) $;

--| If the absolute value of a complex number is less than a real, its difference from the real is apart from zero. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 12-Aug-2021.)
theorem abssubap0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) B2 clt)) (wbr (co B2 A2 cmin) cc0 cap) $;

--| If the absolute value of a complex number is less than a real, its difference from the real is nonzero. See also ~ abssubap0 which is the same with not equal changed to apart. (Contributed by NM, 2-Nov-2007.)
theorem abssubne0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) B2 clt)) (wne (co B2 A2 cmin) cc0) $;

--| The absolute value of a difference and 'less than' relation. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 18-Sep-2007.)
theorem absdiflt (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wb (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) C clt) (wa (wbr (co B2 C cmin) A2 clt) (wbr A2 (co B2 C caddc) clt))) $;

--| The absolute value of a difference and 'less than or equal to' relation. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 18-Sep-2007.)
theorem absdifle (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wb (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) C cle) (wa (wbr (co B2 C cmin) A2 cle) (wbr A2 (co B2 C caddc) cle))) $;

--| Membership in a symmetric closed real interval. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 16-Nov-2014.)
theorem elicc4abs (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wcel C cr)) (wb (wcel C (co (co A2 B2 cmin) (co A2 B2 caddc) cicc)) (wbr (cfv (co C A2 cmin) cabs) B2 cle)) $;

--| Comparison to a nonnegative number based on comparison to squares. (Contributed by NM, 16-Jan-2006.)
theorem lenegsq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) (wb (wa (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (cneg A2) B2 cle)) (wbr (co A2 c2 cexp) (co B2 c2 cexp) cle)) $;

--| The real part of a number is less than or equal to its absolute value. Proposition 10-3.7(d) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 1-Apr-2005.)
theorem releabs (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wbr (cfv A2 cre) (cfv A2 cabs) cle) $;

--| Reciprocal expressed with a real denominator. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 13-Aug-2021.)
theorem recvalap (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wbr A2 cc0 cap)) (wceq (co c1 A2 cdiv) (co (cfv A2 ccj) (co (cfv A2 cabs) c2 cexp) cdiv)) $;

--| The absolute value function is idempotent. (Contributed by NM, 20-Nov-2004.)
theorem absidm (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 cabs) cabs) (cfv A2 cabs)) $;

--| The absolute value of a number apart from zero is positive. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 13-Aug-2021.)
theorem absgt0ap (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 cc) (wb (wbr A2 cc0 cap) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 cabs) clt)) $;

--| The absolute value of a nonzero integer is a positive integer. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Mar-2011.) (Proof shortened by Andrew Salmon, 25-May-2011.)
theorem nnabscl (N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel N cz) (wne N cc0)) (wcel (cfv N cabs) cn) $;

--| Swapping order of subtraction doesn't change the absolute value. (Contributed by NM, 1-Oct-1999.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem abssub (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs)) $;

--| Absolute value of a nonnegative difference. (Contributed by NM, 14-Feb-2008.)
theorem abssubge0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wceq (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) (co B2 A2 cmin)) $;

--| Absolute value of a nonpositive difference. (Contributed by FL, 3-Jan-2008.)
theorem abssuble0 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) (co B2 A2 cmin)) $;

--| Triangle inequality for absolute value. Proposition 10-3.7(h) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 7-Mar-2005.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem abstri (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 caddc) cabs) (co (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) caddc) cle) $;

--| Absolute value of differences around common element. (Contributed by FL, 9-Oct-2006.)
theorem abs3dif (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) (co (cfv (co A2 C cmin) cabs) (cfv (co C B2 cmin) cabs) caddc) cle) $;

--| Difference of absolute values. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 7-Sep-2007.)
theorem abs2dif (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wbr (co (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) cmin) (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) cle) $;

--| Difference of absolute values. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Apr-2016.)
theorem abs2dif2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) (co (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) caddc) cle) $;

--| Absolute value of difference of absolute values. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 7-Sep-2007.)
theorem abs2difabs (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) cmin) cabs) (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) cle) $;

--| Cancellation law involving the real part of a complex number. (Contributed by NM, 12-May-2005.)
theorem recan {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wb (wral x3 (wceq (cfv (co (cv x3) A2 cmul) cre) (cfv (co (cv x3) B2 cmul) cre)) cc) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Mapping domain and codomain of the absolute value function. (Contributed by NM, 30-Aug-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 7-Nov-2013.)
theorem absf:
  $ wf cc cr cabs $;

--| Lemma involving absolute value of differences. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.)
theorem abs3lem (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel B2 cc)) (wa (wcel C cc) (wcel D cr))) (wi (wa (wbr (cfv (co A2 C cmin) cabs) (co D c2 cdiv) clt) (wbr (cfv (co C B2 cmin) cabs) (co D c2 cdiv) clt)) (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) D clt)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ fzomaxdif . (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Sep-2015.)
theorem fzomaxdiflem (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 (co C D cfzo)) (wcel B2 (co C D cfzo))) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) (wcel (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) (co cc0 (co D C cmin) cfzo)) $;

--| A bound on the separation of two points in a half-open range. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 6-Sep-2015.)
theorem fzomaxdif (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 (co C D cfzo)) (wcel B2 (co C D cfzo))) (wcel (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) (co cc0 (co D C cmin) cfzo)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ cau3 . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Feb-2014.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-May-2014.)
theorem cau3lem {x3 j k m: setvar} (ph: wff) (ps: wff j k) (ch: wff x3 j) (th: wff x3 j m) (ta: wff j k m) (D F G: class j) (Z: class j k m):
  $ wss Z cz $ >
  $ wi ta ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv j) F)) (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv m) F)) (wb ps th) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph ch ps) (wceq (cfv (co (cfv (cv j) F) (cfv (cv k) F) D) G) (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv j) F) D) G)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph th ch) (wceq (cfv (co (cfv (cv m) F) (cfv (cv j) F) D) G) (cfv (co (cfv (cv j) F) (cfv (cv m) F) D) G)) $ >
  $ wi (w3a ph (wa ps th) (wa ch (wcel (cv x3) cr))) (wi (wa (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv j) F) D) G) (co (cv x3) c2 cdiv) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv j) F) (cfv (cv m) F) D) G) (co (cv x3) c2 cdiv) clt)) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv m) F) D) G) (cv x3) clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa ta (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv j) F) D) G) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa ta (wral m (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv m) F) D) G) (cv x3) clt) (cfv (cv k) cuz))) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp)) $;

--| Convert between three-quantifier and four-quantifier versions of the Cauchy criterion. (In particular, the four-quantifier version has no occurrence of ` j ` in the assertion, so it can be used with ~ rexanuz and friends.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 15-Feb-2014.)
theorem cau3 {x3 j k m: setvar} (F: class) (M Z: class m):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wb (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv j) F) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) (wral m (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv m) F) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt) (cfv (cv k) cuz))) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp) $;

--| Change the base of a Cauchy criterion. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2014.)
theorem cau4 {x3 j k: setvar} (F M N W Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq W (cfv N cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wcel N Z) (wb (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv j) F) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv j) F) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) W) crp)) $;

--| A Cauchy sequence of complex numbers is eventually bounded. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 14-Feb-2014.)
theorem caubnd2 {x3 y1 j k: setvar} (F: class) (M: class y1) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv j) F) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp) (wrex y1 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv k) F) cabs) (cv y1) clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) cr) $;

--| Arithmetic-geometric mean inequality for ` n = 2 ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 2-Jul-2014.)
theorem amgm2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wbr cc0 A2 cle)) (wa (wcel B2 cr) (wbr cc0 B2 cle))) (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) csqrt) (co (co A2 B2 caddc) c2 cdiv) cle) $;

--| Square root theorem. Theorem I.35 of [Apostol] p. 29. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2013.)
theorem sqrtthi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wceq (co (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv A2 csqrt) cmul) A2) $;

--| The square root of a nonnegative real is a real. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2013.)
theorem sqrtcli (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wcel (cfv A2 csqrt) cr) $;

--| The square root of a positive real is positive. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2013.)
theorem sqrtgt0i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr cc0 A2 clt) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 csqrt) clt) $;

--| Square root of square. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-1999.)
theorem sqrtmsqi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wceq (cfv (co A2 A2 cmul) csqrt) A2) $;

--| Square root of square. (Contributed by NM, 11-Aug-1999.)
theorem sqrtsqi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wceq (cfv (co A2 c2 cexp) csqrt) A2) $;

--| Square of square root. (Contributed by NM, 11-Aug-1999.)
theorem sqsqrti (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wceq (co (cfv A2 csqrt) c2 cexp) A2) $;

--| The square root of a nonnegative real is nonnegative. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2013.)
theorem sqrtge0i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 csqrt) cle) $;

--| A nonnegative number is its own absolute value. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-1999.)
theorem absidi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) A2) $;

--| A negative number is the negative of its own absolute value. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-1999.)
theorem absnidi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wbr A2 cc0 cle) (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) (cneg A2)) $;

--| A real number is less than or equal to its absolute value. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-1999.)
theorem leabsi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wbr A2 (cfv A2 cabs) cle $;

--| Absolute value of a real number. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-1999.)
theorem absrei (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wceq (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv (co A2 c2 cexp) csqrt) $;

--| The square root of a positive real is a real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2013.)
theorem sqrtpclii (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wbr cc0 A2 clt $ >
  $ wcel (cfv A2 csqrt) cr $;

--| The square root of a positive real is positive. (Contributed by NM, 26-May-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Sep-2013.)
theorem sqrtgt0ii (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wbr cc0 A2 clt $ >
  $ wbr cc0 (cfv A2 csqrt) clt $;

--| The square root function is one-to-one. (Contributed by NM, 27-Jul-1999.)
theorem sqrt11i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv B2 csqrt)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Square root distributes over multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jul-1999.)
theorem sqrtmuli (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) csqrt) (co (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv B2 csqrt) cmul)) $;

--| Square root distributes over multiplication. (Contributed by NM, 30-Jul-1999.)
theorem sqrtmulii (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wbr cc0 A2 cle $ >
  $ wbr cc0 B2 cle $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) csqrt) (co (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv B2 csqrt) cmul) $;

--| Relationship between square root and squares. (Contributed by NM, 31-Jul-1999.)
theorem sqrtmsq2i (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) (wb (wceq (cfv A2 csqrt) B2) (wceq A2 (co B2 B2 cmul))) $;

--| Square root is monotonic. (Contributed by NM, 3-Aug-1999.)
theorem sqrtlei (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv B2 csqrt) cle)) $;

--| Square root is strictly monotonic. (Contributed by Roy F. Longton, 8-Aug-2005.)
theorem sqrtlti (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr cc0 A2 cle) (wbr cc0 B2 cle)) (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv B2 csqrt) clt)) $;

--| Absolute value and 'less than' relation. (Contributed by NM, 6-Apr-2005.)
theorem abslti (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) B2 clt) (wa (wbr (cneg B2) A2 clt) (wbr A2 B2 clt)) $;

--| Absolute value and 'less than or equal to' relation. (Contributed by NM, 6-Apr-2005.)
theorem abslei (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cr $ >
  $ wcel B2 cr $ >
  $ wb (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) B2 cle) (wa (wbr (cneg B2) A2 cle) (wbr A2 B2 cle)) $;

--| Square of value of absolute value function. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.)
theorem absvalsqi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co (cfv A2 cabs) c2 cexp) (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) cmul) $;

--| Square of value of absolute value function. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.)
theorem absvalsq2i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co (cfv A2 cabs) c2 cexp) (co (co (cfv A2 cre) c2 cexp) (co (cfv A2 cim) c2 cexp) caddc) $;

--| Real closure of absolute value. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-1999.)
theorem abscli (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel (cfv A2 cabs) cr $;

--| Absolute value is nonnegative. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-1999.)
theorem absge0i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wbr cc0 (cfv A2 cabs) cle $;

--| Value of absolute value function. Definition 10.36 of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.)
theorem absval2i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv (co (co (cfv A2 cre) c2 cexp) (co (cfv A2 cim) c2 cexp) caddc) csqrt) $;

--| The absolute value of a number is zero iff the number is zero. Proposition 10-3.7(c) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 28-Jul-1999.)
theorem abs00i (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wb (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) cc0) (wceq A2 cc0) $;

--| The absolute value of a nonzero number is positive. Remark in [Apostol] p. 363. (Contributed by NM, 1-Oct-1999.)
theorem absgt0api (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wb (wbr A2 cc0 cap) (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 cabs) clt) $;

--| Absolute value of negative. (Contributed by NM, 2-Aug-1999.)
theorem absnegi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cneg A2) cabs) (cfv A2 cabs) $;

--| The absolute value of a number and its conjugate are the same. Proposition 10-3.7(b) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.)
theorem abscji (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ccj) cabs) (cfv A2 cabs) $;

--| The real part of a number is less than or equal to its absolute value. Proposition 10-3.7(d) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.)
theorem releabsi (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wbr (cfv A2 cre) (cfv A2 cabs) cle $;

--| Swapping order of subtraction doesn't change the absolute value. Example of [Apostol] p. 363. (Contributed by NM, 1-Oct-1999.)
theorem abssubi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) $;

--| Absolute value distributes over multiplication. Proposition 10-3.7(f) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 1-Oct-1999.)
theorem absmuli (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cabs) (co (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) cmul) $;

--| Square of absolute value of sum. Proposition 10-3.7(g) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.)
theorem sqabsaddi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co (cfv (co A2 B2 caddc) cabs) c2 cexp) (co (co (co (cfv A2 cabs) c2 cexp) (co (cfv B2 cabs) c2 cexp) caddc) (co c2 (cfv (co A2 (cfv B2 ccj) cmul) cre) cmul) caddc) $;

--| Square of absolute value of difference. (Contributed by Steve Rodriguez, 20-Jan-2007.)
theorem sqabssubi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wceq (co (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) c2 cexp) (co (co (co (cfv A2 cabs) c2 cexp) (co (cfv B2 cabs) c2 cexp) caddc) (co c2 (cfv (co A2 (cfv B2 ccj) cmul) cre) cmul) cmin) $;

--| Absolute value distributes over division. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 13-Aug-2021.)
theorem absdivapzi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wi (wbr B2 cc0 cap) (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cdiv) cabs) (co (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) cdiv)) $;

--| Triangle inequality for absolute value. Proposition 10-3.7(h) of [Gleason] p. 133. This is Metamath 100 proof #91. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.)
theorem abstrii (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 caddc) cabs) (co (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) caddc) cle $;

--| Absolute value of differences around common element. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.)
theorem abs3difi (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) (co (cfv (co A2 C cmin) cabs) (cfv (co C B2 cmin) cabs) caddc) cle $;

--| Lemma involving absolute value of differences. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.)
theorem abs3lemi (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wcel A2 cc $ >
  $ wcel B2 cc $ >
  $ wcel C cc $ >
  $ wcel D cr $ >
  $ wi (wa (wbr (cfv (co A2 C cmin) cabs) (co D c2 cdiv) clt) (wbr (cfv (co C B2 cmin) cabs) (co D c2 cdiv) clt)) (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) D clt) $;

--| The square root of a positive real is positive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem rpsqrtcld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 csqrt) crp) $;

--| The square root of a positive real is positive. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem sqrtgt0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 csqrt) clt) $;

--| A negative number is the negative of its own absolute value. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absnidd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) (cneg A2)) $;

--| A real number is less than or equal to its absolute value. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem leabsd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 (cfv A2 cabs) cle) $;

--| Absolute value of a real number. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absred (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv (co A2 c2 cexp) csqrt)) $;

--| The square root of a nonnegative real is a real. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem resqrtcld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 csqrt) cr) $;

--| Square root of square. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem sqrtmsqd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 A2 cmul) csqrt) A2) $;

--| Square root of square. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem sqrtsqd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 c2 cexp) csqrt) A2) $;

--| The square root of a nonnegative real is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem sqrtge0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 csqrt) cle) $;

--| A nonnegative number is its own absolute value. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absidd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) A2) $;

--| Square root distributes over division. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem sqrtdivd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cdiv) csqrt) (co (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv B2 csqrt) cdiv)) $;

--| Square root distributes over multiplication. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem sqrtmuld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) csqrt) (co (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv B2 csqrt) cmul)) $;

--| Relationship between square root and squares. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem sqrtsq2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wceq (cfv A2 csqrt) B2) (wceq A2 (co B2 c2 cexp))) $;

--| Square root is monotonic. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem sqrtled (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 cle) (wbr (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv B2 csqrt) cle)) $;

--| Square root is strictly monotonic. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem sqrtltd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr A2 B2 clt) (wbr (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv B2 csqrt) clt)) $;

--| The square root function is one-to-one. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem sqr11d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 csqrt) (cfv B2 csqrt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| Absolute value and 'less than' relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absltd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) B2 clt) (wa (wbr (cneg B2) A2 clt) (wbr A2 B2 clt))) $;

--| Absolute value and 'less than or equal to' relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absled (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cfv A2 cabs) B2 cle) (wa (wbr (cneg B2) A2 cle) (wbr A2 B2 cle))) $;

--| Absolute value of a nonnegative difference. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem abssubge0d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) (co B2 A2 cmin)) $;

--| Absolute value of a nonpositive difference. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem abssuble0d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) (co B2 A2 cmin)) $;

--| The absolute value of a difference and 'less than' relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absdifltd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) C clt) (wa (wbr (co B2 C cmin) A2 clt) (wbr A2 (co B2 C caddc) clt))) $;

--| The absolute value of a difference and 'less than or equal to' relation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absdifled (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) C cle) (wa (wbr (co B2 C cmin) A2 cle) (wbr A2 (co B2 C caddc) cle))) $;

--| Two elements in a half-open interval have separation _strictly_ less than the difference between the endpoints. (Contributed by Stefan O'Rear, 12-Sep-2014.)
theorem icodiamlt (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi (wa (wa (wcel A2 cr) (wcel B2 cr)) (wa (wcel C (co A2 B2 cico)) (wcel D (co A2 B2 cico)))) (wbr (cfv (co C D cmin) cabs) (co B2 A2 cmin) clt) $;

--| Real closure of absolute value. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem abscld (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 cabs) cr) $;

--| Square of value of absolute value function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absvalsqd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv A2 cabs) c2 cexp) (co A2 (cfv A2 ccj) cmul)) $;

--| Square of value of absolute value function. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absvalsq2d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (co (cfv A2 cabs) c2 cexp) (co (co (cfv A2 cre) c2 cexp) (co (cfv A2 cim) c2 cexp) caddc)) $;

--| Absolute value is nonnegative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absge0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 (cfv A2 cabs) cle) $;

--| Value of absolute value function. Definition 10.36 of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absval2d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv (co (co (cfv A2 cre) c2 cexp) (co (cfv A2 cim) c2 cexp) caddc) csqrt)) $;

--| The absolute value of a number is zero iff the number is zero. Proposition 10-3.7(c) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem abs00d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv A2 cabs) cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq A2 cc0) $;

--| The absolute value of a number is zero iff the number is zero. Proposition 10-3.7(c) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absne0d (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne A2 cc0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wne (cfv A2 cabs) cc0) $;

--| The absolute value of a complex number apart from zero is a positive real. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 13-Aug-2021.)
theorem absrpclapd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 cc0 cap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cfv A2 cabs) crp) $;

--| Absolute value of negative. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absnegd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cneg A2) cabs) (cfv A2 cabs)) $;

--| The absolute value of a number and its conjugate are the same. Proposition 10-3.7(b) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem abscjd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (cfv A2 ccj) cabs) (cfv A2 cabs)) $;

--| The real part of a number is less than or equal to its absolute value. Proposition 10-3.7(d) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem releabsd (ph: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv A2 cre) (cfv A2 cabs) cle) $;

--| Absolute value of positive integer exponentiation. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absexpd (ph: wff) (A2 N: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N cn0) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 N cexp) cabs) (co (cfv A2 cabs) N cexp)) $;

--| Swapping order of subtraction doesn't change the absolute value. Example of [Apostol] p. 363. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem abssubd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs)) $;

--| Absolute value distributes over multiplication. Proposition 10-3.7(f) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem absmuld (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cmul) cabs) (co (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) cmul)) $;

--| Absolute value distributes over division. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 13-Aug-2021.)
theorem absdivapd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr B2 cc0 cap) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv (co A2 B2 cdiv) cabs) (co (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) cdiv)) $;

--| Triangle inequality for absolute value. Proposition 10-3.7(h) of [Gleason] p. 133. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem abstrid (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 caddc) cabs) (co (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) caddc) cle) $;

--| Difference of absolute values. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem abs2difd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (co (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) cmin) (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) cle) $;

--| Difference of absolute values. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem abs2dif2d (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) (co (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) caddc) cle) $;

--| Absolute value of difference of absolute values. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem abs2difabsd (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co (cfv A2 cabs) (cfv B2 cabs) cmin) cabs) (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) cle) $;

--| Absolute value of differences around common element. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem abs3difd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) (co (cfv (co A2 C cmin) cabs) (cfv (co C B2 cmin) cabs) caddc) cle) $;

--| Lemma involving absolute value of differences. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 29-May-2016.)
theorem abs3lemd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C D: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel D cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co A2 C cmin) cabs) (co D c2 cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co C B2 cmin) cabs) (co D c2 cdiv) clt) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv (co A2 B2 cmin) cabs) D clt) $;

--| Extend class notation with convergence relation for limits.
term cli: class;

--| Define the limit relation for complex number sequences. See ~ clim for its relational expression. (Contributed by NM, 28-Aug-2005.)
axiom df_clim {x3 y1 f2 j k: setvar}:
  $ wceq cli (copab f2 y1 (wa (wcel (cv y1) cc) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) cc) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) (cv f2)) (cv y1) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cz) crp))) $;

--| The limit relation is a relation. (Contributed by NM, 28-Aug-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem climrel:
  $ wrel cli $;

--| Express the predicate: The limit of complex number sequence ` F ` is ` A ` , or ` F ` converges to ` A ` . This means that for any real ` x ` , no matter how small, there always exists an integer ` j ` such that the absolute difference of any later complex number in the sequence and the limit is less than ` x ` . (Contributed by NM, 28-Aug-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem clim {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 j k) (F: class) (V: class x3 j k):
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cz)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 cli) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wbr (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) cz) crp))) $;

--| Closure of the limit of a sequence of complex numbers. (Contributed by NM, 28-Aug-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-Apr-2015.)
theorem climcl (A2 F: class):
  $ wi (wbr F A2 cli) (wcel A2 cc) $;

--| Express the predicate: The limit of complex number sequence ` F ` is ` A ` , or ` F ` converges to ` A ` , with more general quantifier restrictions than ~ clim . (Contributed by NM, 6-Jan-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem clim2 {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 j k) (F: class) (M: class x3 k) (V: class x3 j k) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 cli) (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wbr (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp))) $;

--| Express the predicate ` F ` converges to ` A ` . (Contributed by NM, 24-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem clim2c {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 j k) (F: class) (M: class x3 k) (V: class x3 j k) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 cli) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp)) $;

--| Express the predicate ` F ` converges to ` 0 ` . (Contributed by NM, 24-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem clim0 {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class x3 j k) (F: class) (M: class x3 k) (V: class x3 j k) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F cc0 cli) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp)) $;

--| Express the predicate ` F ` converges to ` 0 ` . (Contributed by NM, 24-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem clim0c {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class x3 j k) (F: class) (M: class x3 k) (V: class x3 j k) (Z: class x3):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel B2 cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F cc0 cli) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) (cv x3) clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp)) $;

--| Convergence of a sequence of complex numbers. (Contributed by NM, 11-Jan-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem climi {j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class j k) (C F: class) (M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel B2 cc) (wbr (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) C clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| Convergence of a sequence of complex numbers. (Contributed by NM, 11-Jan-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem climi2 {j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class j k) (C F: class) (M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv (co B2 A2 cmin) cabs) C clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| Convergence of a sequence of complex numbers to zero. (Contributed by NM, 11-Jan-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem climi0 {j k: setvar} (ph: wff) (B2: class j k) (C F: class) (M: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) B2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F cc0 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv B2 cabs) C clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) $;

--| An (eventually) constant sequence converges to its value. (Contributed by NM, 28-Aug-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem climconst {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F: class) (M V: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $;

--| A constant sequence converges to its value. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem climconst2 (A2 M Z: class):
  $ wss (cfv M cuz) Z $ >
  $ wcel Z cvv $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel M cz)) (wbr (cxp Z (csn A2)) A2 cli) $;

--| The zero sequence converges to zero. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem climz:
  $ wbr (cxp cz (csn cc0)) cc0 cli $;

--| An infinite sequence of complex numbers converges to at most one limit. (Contributed by NM, 2-Oct-1999.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem climuni (A2 B2 F: class):
  $ wi (wa (wbr F A2 cli) (wbr F B2 cli)) (wceq A2 B2) $;

--| The limit relation is function-like, and with range the complex numbers. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem fclim:
  $ wf (cdm cli) cc cli $;

--| Two ways to express that a function has a limit. (The expression ` ( ~~> `` F ) ` is sometimes useful as a shorthand for "the unique limit of the function ` F ` "). (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Mar-2014.)
theorem climdm (F: class):
  $ wb (wcel F (cdm cli)) (wbr F (cfv F cli) cli) $;

--| An infinite sequence of complex numbers converges to at most one limit. (Contributed by NM, 25-Dec-2005.)
theorem climeu {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi (wbr F A2 cli) (weu x3 (wbr F (cv x3) cli)) $;

--| An infinite sequence of complex numbers converges to at most one limit. (Contributed by NM, 25-Dec-2005.)
theorem climreu {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3) (F: class):
  $ wi (wbr F A2 cli) (wreu x3 (wbr F (cv x3) cli) cc) $;

--| An infinite sequence of complex numbers converges to at most one limit. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jul-2013.)
theorem climmo {x3: setvar} (F: class):
  $ wmo x3 (wbr F (cv x3) cli) $;

--| Two functions that are eventually equal to one another have the same limit. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Nov-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem climeq {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F G: class) (M V W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 cli) (wbr G A2 cli)) $;

--| Exhibit a function ` G ` with the same convergence properties as the not-quite-function ` F ` . (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem climmpt {k: setvar} (A2 F: class) (G M V: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt k Z (cfv (cv k) F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel F V)) (wb (wbr F A2 cli) (wbr G A2 cli)) $;

--| If two sequences converge to each other, they converge to the same limit. (Contributed by NM, 24-Dec-2005.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem _2clim {x3 j k: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3) (F G: class) (M: class x3 k) (V: class x3 j k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G A2 cli) $;

--| A shifted function converges if the original function converges. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 5-Nov-2013.)
theorem climshftlemg (A2 F M V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel F V)) (wi (wbr F A2 cli) (wbr (co F M cshi) A2 cli)) $;

--| A function restricted to upper integers converges iff the original function converges. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jul-2013.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem climres (A2 F M V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel F V)) (wb (wbr (cres F (cfv M cuz)) A2 cli) (wbr F A2 cli)) $;

--| A shifted function converges iff the original function converges. (Contributed by NM, 16-Aug-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem climshft (A2 F M V: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel F V)) (wb (wbr (co F M cshi) A2 cli) (wbr F A2 cli)) $;

--| The zero series converges to zero. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 19-Aug-2021.)
theorem iserclim0 (M: class):
  $ wi (wcel M cz) (wbr (cseq caddc cc (cxp (cfv M cuz) (csn cc0)) M) cc0 cli) $;

--| A shifted function converges iff the original function converges. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 21-Nov-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 6-Feb-2014.)
theorem climshft2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F G K M: class) (W X: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G X) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (co (cv k) K caddc) G) (cfv (cv k) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F A2 cli) (wbr G A2 cli)) $;

--| Convergence to zero of the absolute value is equivalent to convergence to zero. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jul-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem climabs0 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F G: class) (M V W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) G) (cfv (cfv (cv k) F) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wbr F cc0 cli) (wbr G cc0 cli)) $;

--| Image of a limit under a continuous map. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem climcn1 {x3 y1 z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (B2: class x3 y1) (F: class) (G: class x3) (H: class y1 z) (M W: class x3 y1 z k) (Z: class x3 z):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv z) B2)) (wcel (cfv (cv z) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wrex y1 (wral z (wi (wbr (cfv (co (cv z) A2 cmin) cabs) (cv y1) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv A2 F) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) B2) crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) B2) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) H) (cfv (cfv (cv k) G) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H (cfv A2 F) cli) $;

--| Image of a limit under a continuous map, two-arg version. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem climcn2 {x3 y1 z v u k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class) (C D: class x3 y1 z) (F: class) (G: class x3) (H: class x3 u) (K: class y1 z v u) (M W: class x3 y1 z v u k) (Z: class x3 v u):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv u) C) (wcel (cv v) D))) (wcel (co (cv u) (cv v) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H B2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel K W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wrex y1 (wrex z (wral u (wral v (wi (wa (wbr (cfv (co (cv u) A2 cmin) cabs) (cv y1) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cv v) B2 cmin) cabs) (cv z) clt)) (wbr (cfv (co (co (cv u) (cv v) F) (co A2 B2 F) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) D) C) crp) crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) C) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) H) D) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) K) (co (cfv (cv k) G) (cfv (cv k) H) F)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr K (co A2 B2 F) cli) $;

--| Complex number addition is a continuous function. Part of Proposition 14-4.16 of [Gleason] p. 243. (We write out the definition directly because df-cn and df-cncf are not yet available to us. See addcn for the abbreviated version.) (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem addcn2 {y1 z v u: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wrex y1 (wrex z (wral u (wral v (wi (wa (wbr (cfv (co (cv u) B2 cmin) cabs) (cv y1) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cv v) C cmin) cabs) (cv z) clt)) (wbr (cfv (co (co (cv u) (cv v) caddc) (co B2 C caddc) cmin) cabs) A2 clt)) cc) cc) crp) crp) $;

--| Complex number subtraction is a continuous function. Part of Proposition 14-4.16 of [Gleason] p. 243. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem subcn2 {y1 z v u: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wrex y1 (wrex z (wral u (wral v (wi (wa (wbr (cfv (co (cv u) B2 cmin) cabs) (cv y1) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cv v) C cmin) cabs) (cv z) clt)) (wbr (cfv (co (co (cv u) (cv v) cmin) (co B2 C cmin) cmin) cabs) A2 clt)) cc) cc) crp) crp) $;

--| Complex number multiplication is a continuous function. Part of Proposition 14-4.16 of [Gleason] p. 243. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem mulcn2 {y1 z v u: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 crp) (wcel B2 cc) (wcel C cc)) (wrex y1 (wrex z (wral u (wral v (wi (wa (wbr (cfv (co (cv u) B2 cmin) cabs) (cv y1) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cv v) C cmin) cabs) (cv z) clt)) (wbr (cfv (co (co (cv u) (cv v) cmul) (co B2 C cmul) cmin) cabs) A2 clt)) cc) cc) crp) crp) $;

--| A sufficient condition for a function to be continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2014.)
theorem cn1lem {y1 z: setvar} (x3: setvar) (A2: class) (F: class z):
  $ wf cc cc F $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel (cv z) cc) (wcel A2 cc)) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv A2 F) cmin) cabs) (cfv (co (cv z) A2 cmin) cabs) cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wrex y1 (wral z (wi (wbr (cfv (co (cv z) A2 cmin) cabs) (cv y1) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv z) F) (cfv A2 F) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) cc) crp) $;

--| The absolute value function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2014.)
theorem abscn2 {y1 z: setvar} (x3: setvar) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wrex y1 (wral z (wi (wbr (cfv (co (cv z) A2 cmin) cabs) (cv y1) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv z) cabs) (cfv A2 cabs) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) cc) crp) $;

--| The complex conjugate function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2014.)
theorem cjcn2 {y1 z: setvar} (x3: setvar) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wrex y1 (wral z (wi (wbr (cfv (co (cv z) A2 cmin) cabs) (cv y1) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv z) ccj) (cfv A2 ccj) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) cc) crp) $;

--| The real part function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2014.)
theorem recn2 {y1 z: setvar} (x3: setvar) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wrex y1 (wral z (wi (wbr (cfv (co (cv z) A2 cmin) cabs) (cv y1) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv z) cre) (cfv A2 cre) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) cc) crp) $;

--| The imaginary part function is continuous. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2014.)
theorem imcn2 {y1 z: setvar} (x3: setvar) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wrex y1 (wral z (wi (wbr (cfv (co (cv z) A2 cmin) cabs) (cv y1) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv z) cim) (cfv A2 cim) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) cc) crp) $;

--| The limit of a continuous function, theorem form. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2014.)
theorem climcn1lem {x3 y1 z k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2: class) (F: class x3) (G: class y1 z) (H: class) (M: class x3 y1 z) (W: class x3 y1 z k) (Z: class x3 z):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wf cc cc H $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 cc) (wcel (cv x3) crp)) (wrex y1 (wral z (wi (wbr (cfv (co (cv z) A2 cmin) cabs) (cv y1) clt) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv z) H) (cfv A2 H) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) cc) crp) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) G) (cfv (cfv (cv k) F) H)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G (cfv A2 H) cli) $;

--| Limit of the absolute value of a sequence. Proposition 12-2.4(c) of [Gleason] p. 172. (Contributed by NM, 7-Jun-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2014.)
theorem climabs {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F G M: class) (W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) G) (cfv (cfv (cv k) F) cabs)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G (cfv A2 cabs) cli) $;

--| Limit of the complex conjugate of a sequence. Proposition 12-2.4(c) of [Gleason] p. 172. (Contributed by NM, 7-Jun-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2014.)
theorem climcj {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F G M: class) (W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) G) (cfv (cfv (cv k) F) ccj)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G (cfv A2 ccj) cli) $;

--| Limit of the real part of a sequence. Proposition 12-2.4(c) of [Gleason] p. 172. (Contributed by NM, 7-Jun-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2014.)
theorem climre {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F G M: class) (W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) G) (cfv (cfv (cv k) F) cre)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G (cfv A2 cre) cli) $;

--| Limit of the imaginary part of a sequence. Proposition 12-2.4(c) of [Gleason] p. 172. (Contributed by NM, 7-Jun-2006.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2014.)
theorem climim {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F G M: class) (W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) G) (cfv (cfv (cv k) F) cim)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G (cfv A2 cim) cli) $;

--| The limit of a convergent real sequence is real. Corollary 12-2.5 of [Gleason] p. 172. (Contributed by NM, 10-Sep-2005.)
theorem climrecl {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $;

--| A nonnegative sequence converges to a nonnegative number. (Contributed by NM, 11-Sep-2005.)
theorem climge0 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv k) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $;

--| Limit of the sum of two converging sequences. Proposition 12-2.1(a) of [Gleason] p. 168. (Contributed by NM, 24-Sep-2005.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 31-Jan-2014.)
theorem climadd {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G H M: class) (X: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G B2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) H) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H (co A2 B2 caddc) cli) $;

--| Limit of the product of two converging sequences. Proposition 12-2.1(c) of [Gleason] p. 168. (Contributed by NM, 27-Dec-2005.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 1-Feb-2014.)
theorem climmul {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G H M: class) (X: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G B2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) H) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H (co A2 B2 cmul) cli) $;

--| Limit of the difference of two converging sequences. Proposition 12-2.1(b) of [Gleason] p. 168. (Contributed by NM, 4-Aug-2007.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 1-Feb-2014.)
theorem climsub {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G H M: class) (X: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel H X) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G B2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) H) (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr H (co A2 B2 cmin) cli) $;

--| Limit of a constant ` C ` added to each term of a sequence. (Contributed by NM, 24-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2014.)
theorem climaddc1 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 C F G M: class) (W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) G) (co (cfv (cv k) F) C caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G (co A2 C caddc) cli) $;

--| Limit of a constant ` C ` added to each term of a sequence. (Contributed by NM, 24-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2014.)
theorem climaddc2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 C F G M: class) (W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) G) (co C (cfv (cv k) F) caddc)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G (co C A2 caddc) cli) $;

--| Limit of a sequence multiplied by a constant ` C ` . Corollary 12-2.2 of [Gleason] p. 171. (Contributed by NM, 24-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2014.)
theorem climmulc2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 C F G M: class) (W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) G) (co C (cfv (cv k) F) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G (co C A2 cmul) cli) $;

--| Limit of a constant ` C ` subtracted from each term of a sequence. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2014.)
theorem climsubc1 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 C F G M: class) (W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) G) (co (cfv (cv k) F) C cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G (co A2 C cmin) cli) $;

--| Limit of a constant ` C ` minus each term of a sequence. (Contributed by NM, 24-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 9-Feb-2014.)
theorem climsubc2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 C F G M: class) (W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) G) (co C (cfv (cv k) F) cmin)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G (co C A2 cmin) cli) $;

--| Comparison of the limits of two sequences. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 10-Sep-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-Feb-2014.)
theorem climle {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G B2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $;

--| Convergence of a sequence sandwiched between another converging sequence and its limit. (Contributed by NM, 6-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2014.)
theorem climsqz {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F G M: class) (W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wbr (cfv (cv k) G) A2 cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G A2 cli) $;

--| Convergence of a sequence sandwiched between another converging sequence and its limit. (Contributed by NM, 14-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 3-Feb-2014.)
theorem climsqz2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F G M: class) (W: class k) (Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel G W) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wbr (cfv (cv k) G) (cfv (cv k) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wbr A2 (cfv (cv k) G) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr G A2 cli) $;

--| The limit of an infinite series with an initial segment removed. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 20-Aug-2021.)
theorem clim2iser {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F M N Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc cc F M) A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc cc F (co N c1 caddc)) (co A2 (cfv N (cseq caddc cc F M)) cmin) cli) $;

--| The limit of an infinite series with an initial segment added. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 21-Aug-2021.)
theorem clim2iser2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F M N Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc cc F (co N c1 caddc)) A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc cc F M) (co A2 (cfv N (cseq caddc cc F M)) caddc) cli) $;

--| An infinite series converges, if and only if the series does with initial terms removed. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 9-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Apr-2014.)
theorem iiserex {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F M N Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N Z) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wcel (cseq caddc cc F M) (cdm cli)) (wcel (cseq caddc cc F N) (cdm cli))) $;

--| Multiplication of an infinite series by a constant. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 14-Nov-2007.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-Feb-2014.)
theorem iisermulc2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 C F G M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc cc F M) A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wceq (cfv (cv k) G) (co C (cfv (cv k) F) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc cc G M) (co C A2 cmul) cli) $;

--| Comparison of a constant to the limit of a sequence. (Contributed by NM, 28-Feb-2008.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 1-Feb-2014.)
theorem climlec2 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cr) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F B2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wbr A2 (cfv (cv k) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $;

--| Comparison of the limits of two infinite series. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Aug-2021.)
theorem iserile {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 B2 F G M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc cc F M) A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc cc G M) B2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) G) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv k) G) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr A2 B2 cle) $;

--| The limit of an infinite series of nonnegative reals is nonnegative. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Aug-2021.)
theorem iserige0 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc cc F M) A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv k) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr cc0 A2 cle) $;

--| The limit of a monotonic sequence is an upper bound. (Contributed by NM, 18-Mar-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 10-Feb-2014.)
theorem climub {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F M N Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wbr (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv N F) A2 cle) $;

--| The partial sums of a converging infinite series with nonnegative terms are bounded by its limit. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Aug-2021.)
theorem climserile {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (A2 F M N Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel N Z) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cseq caddc cc F M) A2 cli) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cr) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wbr cc0 (cfv (cv k) F) cle) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr (cfv N (cseq caddc cc F M)) A2 cle) $;

--| A converging sequence of complex numbers is a Cauchy sequence. The converse would require excluded middle or a different definition of Cauchy sequence (for example, fixing a rate of convergence as in ~ climcvg1n ). Theorem 12-5.3 of [Gleason] p. 180 (necessity part). (Contributed by NM, 16-Apr-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 26-Apr-2014.)
theorem climcau {x3 j k: setvar} (F M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel F (cdm cli))) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv j) F) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp) $;

--| A Cauchy sequence of real numbers converges, existence version. The rate of convergence is fixed: all terms after the nth term must be within ` C / n ` of the nth term, where ` C ` is a constant multiplier. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 23-Aug-2021.)
theorem climrecvg1n {k n: setvar} (ph: wff) (C F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn cr F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wral k (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv n) F) cmin) cabs) (co C (cv n) cdiv) clt) (cfv (cv n) cuz)) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm cli)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ climcvg1n . We construct sequences of the real and imaginary parts of each term of ` F ` , show those converge, and use that to show that ` F ` converges. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 24-Aug-2021.)
theorem climcvg1nlem {x3 k n: setvar} (ph: wff) (C: class x3) (F: class n) (G: class x3) (H: class) (J: class x3 n):
  $ wi ph (wf cn cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wral k (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv n) F) cmin) cabs) (co C (cv n) cdiv) clt) (cfv (cv n) cuz)) cn) $ >
  $ wceq G (cmpt x3 cn (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cre)) $ >
  $ wceq H (cmpt x3 cn (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) cim)) $ >
  $ wceq J (cmpt x3 cn (co ci (cfv (cv x3) H) cmul)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm cli)) $;

--| A Cauchy sequence of complex numbers converges, existence version. The rate of convergence is fixed: all terms after the nth term must be within ` C / n ` of the nth term, where ` C ` is a constant multiplier. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 23-Aug-2021.)
theorem climcvg1n {k n: setvar} (ph: wff) (C F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn cc F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel C crp) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral n (wral k (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv n) F) cmin) cabs) (co C (cv n) cdiv) clt) (cfv (cv n) cuz)) cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F (cdm cli)) $;

--| A converging sequence of complex numbers is a Cauchy sequence. This is like ~ climcau but adds the part that ` ( F `` k ) ` is complex. (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 24-Aug-2021.)
theorem climcaucn {x3 j k: setvar} (F M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cz) (wcel F (cdm cli))) (wral x3 (wrex j (wral k (wa (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) (wbr (cfv (co (cfv (cv k) F) (cfv (cv j) F) cmin) cabs) (cv x3) clt)) (cfv (cv j) cuz)) Z) crp) $;

--| If an infinite series converges, its underlying sequence converges to zero. (Contributed by NM, 2-Sep-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 16-Feb-2014.)
theorem serif0 {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F M V Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel F V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel (cseq caddc cc F M) (cdm cli)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) Z)) (wcel (cfv (cv k) F) cc) $ >
  $ wi ph (wbr F cc0 cli) $;

--| Extend class notation to include finite summations. (An underscore was added to the ASCII token in order to facilitate set.mm text searches, since "sum" is a commonly used word in comments.)
term csu {k: setvar} (A2: class) (B2: class k): class;

--| Define the sum of a series with an index set of integers ` A ` . ` k ` is normally a free variable in ` B ` , i.e. ` B ` can be thought of as ` B ( k ) ` . This definition is the result of a collection of discussions over the most general definition for a sum that does not need the index set to have a specified ordering. This definition is in two parts, one for finite sums and one for subsets of the upper integers. When summing over a subset of the upper integers, we extend the index set to the upper integers by adding zero outside the domain, and then sum the set in order, setting the result to the limit of the partial sums, if it exists. This means that conditionally convergent sums can be evaluated meaningfully. For finite sums, we are explicitly order-independent, by picking any bijection to a 1-based finite sequence and summing in the induced order. These two methods of summation produce the same result on their common region of definition (i.e. finite subsets of the upper integers). Examples: ` sum_ k e. { 1 , 2 , 4 } ` ` k ` means ` 1 + 2 + 4 = 7 ` , and ` sum_ k e. NN ` ` ( 1 / ( 2 ^ k ) ) = 1 ` means 1/2 + 1/4 + 1/8 + ... = 1. (Contributed by NM, 11-Dec-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jun-2019.)
axiom df_sum {x3 f2 k m n: setvar} (A2 B2: class k):
  $ wceq (csu k A2 B2) (cio x3 (wo (wrex m (wa (wss A2 (cfv (cv m) cuz)) (wbr (cseq caddc cc (cmpt n cz (cif (wcel (cv n) A2) (csb k (cv n) B2) cc0)) (cv m)) (cv x3) cli)) cz) (wrex m (wex f2 (wa (wf1o (co c1 (cv m) cfz) A2 (cv f2)) (wceq (cv x3) (cfv (cv m) (cseq caddc cc (cmpt n cn (csb k (cfv (cv n) (cv f2)) B2)) c1))))) cn))) $;

--| Equality theorem for a sum. (Contributed by NM, 11-Dec-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jun-2019.)
theorem sumeq1 {k: setvar} (A2 B2 C: class k):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wceq (csu k A2 C) (csu k B2 C)) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for sum. (Contributed by NM, 11-Dec-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jun-2019.)
theorem nfsum1 {k: setvar} (A2 B2: class k):
  $ wnfc k A2 $ >
  $ wnfc k (csu k A2 B2) $;

--| Bound-variable hypothesis builder for sum: if ` x ` is (effectively) not free in ` A ` and ` B ` , it is not free in ` sum_ k e. A B ` . (Contributed by NM, 11-Dec-2005.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 13-Jun-2019.)
theorem nfsum {x3 k: setvar} (A2 B2: class x3 k):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 (csu k A2 B2) $;

--| Lemma concerning rationality of square root of 2. The core of the proof - if ` A / B = sqrt ( 2 ) ` , then ` A ` and ` B ` are even, so ` A / 2 ` and ` B / 2 ` are smaller representatives, which is absurd by the method of infinite descent (here implemented by strong induction). (Contributed by NM, 20-Aug-2001.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem sqr2irrlem (ph: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 cn) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv c2 csqrt) (co A2 B2 cdiv)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wa (wcel (co A2 c2 cdiv) cz) (wcel (co B2 c2 cdiv) cn)) $;

--| The square root of 2 is not rational. That is, for any rational number, ` ( sqrt `` 2 ) ` does not equal it. However, if we were to say "the square root of 2 is irrational" that would mean something stronger: "for any rational number, ` ( sqrt `` 2 ) ` is apart from it" (the two statements are equivalent given excluded middle). We do not prove irrationality in this stronger sense here. The proof's core is proven in ~ sqr2irrlem , which shows that if ` A / B = sqrt ( 2 ) ` , then ` A ` and ` B ` are even, so ` A / 2 ` and ` B / 2 ` are smaller representatives, which is absurd. (Contributed by NM, 8-Jan-2002.) (Proof shortened by Mario Carneiro, 12-Sep-2015.)
theorem sqrt2irr:
  $ wnel (cfv c2 csqrt) cq $;

--| The square root of 2 exists and is a real number. (Contributed by NM, 3-Dec-2004.)
theorem sqrt2re:
  $ wcel (cfv c2 csqrt) cr $;

--| A strictly-decreasing nonnegative integer sequence with initial term ` N ` reaches zero by the ` N ` th term. Deduction version. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 31-Mar-2011.)
theorem nn0seqcvgd {k: setvar} (ph: wff) (F N: class):
  $ wi ph (wf cn0 cn0 F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq N (cfv cc0 F)) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv k) cn0)) (wi (wne (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) cc0) (wbr (cfv (co (cv k) c1 caddc) F) (cfv (cv k) F) clt)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv N F) cc0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ialgr0 . Expressing ~ algrflemg in a form suitable for theorems such as ~ iseq1 or ~ iseqfn . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Jul-2021.)
theorem ialgrlem1st (ph: wff) (x3 y1: setvar) (S F: class):
  $ wi ph (wf S S F) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wa (wcel (cv x3) S) (wcel (cv y1) S))) (wcel (co (cv x3) (cv y1) (ccom F c1st)) S) $;

--| Lemma for ~ ialgr0 . Closure of a constant function, in a form suitable for theorems such as ~ iseq1 or ~ iseqfn . (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 22-Jul-2021.)
theorem ialgrlemconst (ph: wff) (x3: setvar) (A2 S M Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel (cv x3) (cfv M cuz))) (wcel (cfv (cv x3) (cxp Z (csn A2))) S) $;

--| The value of the algorithm iterator ` R ` at ` 0 ` is the initial state ` A ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 31-Mar-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2014.)
theorem ialgr0 (ph: wff) (A2 R S F M V Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq R (cseq (ccom F c1st) S (cxp Z (csn A2)) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf S S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wceq (cfv M R) A2) $;

--| An algorithm is a step function ` F : S --> S ` on a state space ` S ` . An algorithm acts on an initial state ` A e. S ` by iteratively applying ` F ` to give ` A ` , ` ( F `` A ) ` , ` ( F `` ( F `` A ) ) ` and so on. An algorithm is said to halt if a fixed point of ` F ` is reached after a finite number of iterations. The algorithm iterator ` R : NN0 --> S ` "runs" the algorithm ` F ` so that ` ( R `` k ) ` is the state after ` k ` iterations of ` F ` on the initial state ` A ` . Domain and codomain of the algorithm iterator ` R ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 31-Mar-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 28-May-2014.)
theorem ialgrf (ph: wff) (A2 R S F M V Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq R (cseq (ccom F c1st) S (cxp Z (csn A2)) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf S S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf Z S R) $;

--| The value of the algorithm iterator ` R ` at ` ( K + 1 ) ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 31-Mar-2011.) (Revised by Mario Carneiro, 27-Dec-2014.)
theorem ialgrp1 (ph: wff) (A2 R S F K M V Z: class):
  $ wceq Z (cfv M cuz) $ >
  $ wceq R (cseq (ccom F c1st) S (cxp Z (csn A2)) M) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel M cz) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 S) $ >
  $ wi ph (wf S S F) $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel S V) $ >
  $ wi (wa ph (wcel K Z)) (wceq (cfv (co K c1 caddc) R) (cfv (cfv K R) F)) $;

--| If ` I ` is an invariant of ` F ` , its value is unchanged after any number of iterations of ` F ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 31-Mar-2011.)
theorem ialginv {x3: setvar} (A2: class x3) (R S F I: class) (K V: class x3):
  $ wceq R (cseq (ccom F c1st) S (cxp cn0 (csn A2)) cc0) $ >
  $ wf S S F $ >
  $ wfn I S $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv x3) S) (wceq (cfv (cfv (cv x3) F) I) (cfv (cv x3) I)) $ >
  $ wcel S V $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 S) (wcel K cn0)) (wceq (cfv (cfv K R) I) (cfv (cfv cc0 R) I)) $;

--| One way to prove that an algorithm halts is to construct a countdown function ` C : S --> NN0 ` whose value is guaranteed to decrease for each iteration of ` F ` until it reaches ` 0 ` . That is, if ` X e. S ` is not a fixed point of ` F ` , then ` ( C `` ( F `` X ) ) < ( C `` X ) ` . If ` C ` is a countdown function for algorithm ` F ` , the sequence ` ( C `` ( R `` k ) ) ` reaches ` 0 ` after at most ` N ` steps, where ` N ` is the value of ` C ` for the initial state ` A ` . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 22-Jun-2011.)
theorem ialgcvg {z: setvar} (A2: class z) (C R S F: class) (N V: class z):
  $ wf S S F $ >
  $ wceq R (cseq (ccom F c1st) S (cxp cn0 (csn A2)) cc0) $ >
  $ wf S cn0 C $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv z) S) (wi (wne (cfv (cfv (cv z) F) C) cc0) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv z) F) C) (cfv (cv z) C) clt)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 C) $ >
  $ wcel S V $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 S) (wceq (cfv (cfv N R) C) cc0) $;

--| Lemma for ~ algcvgb . (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 31-Mar-2011.)
theorem algcvgblem (M N: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel M cn0) (wcel N cn0)) (wb (wi (wne N cc0) (wbr N M clt)) (wa (wi (wne M cc0) (wbr N M clt)) (wi (wceq M cc0) (wceq N cc0)))) $;

--| Two ways of expressing that ` C ` is a countdown function for algorithm ` F ` . The first is used in these theorems. The second states the condition more intuitively as a conjunction: if the countdown function's value is currently nonzero, it must decrease at the next step; if it has reached zero, it must remain zero at the next step. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 31-Mar-2011.)
theorem algcvgb (C S F X: class):
  $ wf S S F $ >
  $ wf S cn0 C $ >
  $ wi (wcel X S) (wb (wi (wne (cfv (cfv X F) C) cc0) (wbr (cfv (cfv X F) C) (cfv X C) clt)) (wa (wi (wne (cfv X C) cc0) (wbr (cfv (cfv X F) C) (cfv X C) clt)) (wi (wceq (cfv X C) cc0) (wceq (cfv (cfv X F) C) cc0)))) $;

--| The countdown function ` C ` remains ` 0 ` after ` N ` steps. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 22-Jun-2011.)
theorem ialgcvga {z: setvar} (A2: class z) (C R S F: class) (K N V: class z):
  $ wf S S F $ >
  $ wceq R (cseq (ccom F c1st) S (cxp cn0 (csn A2)) cc0) $ >
  $ wf S cn0 C $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv z) S) (wi (wne (cfv (cfv (cv z) F) C) cc0) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv z) F) C) (cfv (cv z) C) clt)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 C) $ >
  $ wcel S V $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 S) (wi (wcel K (cfv N cuz)) (wceq (cfv (cfv K R) C) cc0)) $;

--| If ` F ` reaches a fixed point when the countdown function ` C ` reaches ` 0 ` , ` F ` remains fixed after ` N ` steps. (Contributed by Paul Chapman, 22-Jun-2011.)
theorem ialgfx {z: setvar} (A2: class z) (C R S F K N: class) (V: class z):
  $ wf S S F $ >
  $ wceq R (cseq (ccom F c1st) S (cxp cn0 (csn A2)) cc0) $ >
  $ wf S cn0 C $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv z) S) (wi (wne (cfv (cfv (cv z) F) C) cc0) (wbr (cfv (cfv (cv z) F) C) (cfv (cv z) C) clt)) $ >
  $ wceq N (cfv A2 C) $ >
  $ wcel S V $ >
  $ wi (wcel (cv z) S) (wi (wceq (cfv (cv z) C) cc0) (wceq (cfv (cv z) F) (cv z))) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 S) (wi (wcel K (cfv N cuz)) (wceq (cfv K R) (cfv N R))) $;

--| Unless there is a reason to diverge, we follow the conventions of the Metamath Proof Explorer (aka "set.mm"). This list of conventions is intended to be read in conjunction with the corresponding conventions in the Metamath Proof Explorer, and only the differences are described below. <HTML><br></HTML> <HTML><ul> <li><b>Minimizing axioms and the axiom of choice.</b> We prefer proofs that depend on fewer and/or weaker axioms, even if the proofs are longer. In particular, our choice of IZF (Intuitionistic Zermelo-Fraenkel) over CZF (Constructive Zermelo-Fraenkel, a weaker system) was just an expedient choice because IZF is easier to formalize in metamath. You can find some development using CZF in BJ's mathbox starting at ~ ax-bd0 (and the section header just above it). As for the axiom of choice, the full axiom of choice implies excluded middle as seen at ~ acexmid , although some authors will use countable choice or dependent choice. For example, countable choice or excluded middle is needed to show that the Cauchy reals coincide with the Dedekind reals - Corollary 11.4.3 of [HoTT], p. (varies).</li> <li><b>Junk/undefined results.</b> Much of the discussion of this topic in the Metamath Proof Explorer applies except that certain techniques are not available to us. For example, the Metamath Proof Explorer will often say "if a function is evaluated within its domain, a certain result follows; if the function is evaluated outside its domain, the same result follows. Since the function must be evaluated within its domain or outside it, the result follows unconditionally" (the use of excluded middle in this argument is perhaps obvious when stated this way). For this reason, we generally need to prove we are evaluating functions within their domains and avoid the reverse closure theorems of the Metamath Proof Explorer.</li> <li><b>Bibliography references.</b> The bibliography for the Intuitionistic Logic Explorer is separate from the one for the Metamath Proof Explorer but feel free to copy-paste a citation in either direction in order to cite it.</li> </ul></HTML> <HTML><b>Label naming conventions</b></HTML> Here are a few of the label naming conventions: <HTML><ul> <li><b>Suffixes.</b> We follow the conventions of the Metamath Proof Explorer with a few additions. A biconditional in set.mm which is an implication in iset.mm should have a "r" (for the reverse direction), or "i"/"im" (for the forward direction) appended. A theorem in set.mm which has a decidability condition added should add "dc" to the theorem name. A theorem in set.mm where "nonempty class" is changed to "inhabited class" should add "m" (for member) to the theorem name.</li> </ul></HTML> <HTML> The following table shows some commonly-used abbreviations in labels which are not found in the Metamath Proof Explorer, in alphabetical order. For each abbreviation we provide a mnenomic to help you remember it, the source theorem/assumption defining it, an expression showing what it looks like, whether or not it is a "syntax fragment" (an abbreviation that indicates a particular kind of syntax), and hyperlinks to label examples that use the abbreviation. The abbreviation is bolded if there is a df-NAME definition but the label fragment is not NAME. <table border="1" id="naming-abbreviation-table"> <tr><th>Abbreviation</th><th>Mnenomic</th><th>Source</th> <th>Expression</th><th>Syntax?</th><th>Example(s)</th></tr> <tr><td>ap</td><td>apart</td><td> ~ df-ap </td> <td> </td><td>Yes</td><td> ~ apadd1 , ~ apne </td></tr> </table> </HTML> <li><b>Community.</b> The Metamath mailing list also covers the Intuitionistic Logic Explorer and is at: ~ https://groups.google.com/forum/#!forum/metamath . </li> </ul></HTML> (Contributed by Jim Kingdon, 24-Feb-2020.)
theorem conventions (ph: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ ph $;

--| Example for ~ ax-io . Example by David A. Wheeler. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem ex_or:
  $ wo (wceq c2 c3) (wceq c4 c4) $;

--| Example for ~ ax-ia1 . Example by David A. Wheeler. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 9-May-2015.)
theorem ex_an:
  $ wa (wceq c2 c2) (wceq c3 c3) $;

--| Example for ~ df-fl . Example by David A. Wheeler. (Contributed by Mario Carneiro, 18-Jun-2015.)
theorem ex_fl:
  $ wa (wceq (cfv (co c3 c2 cdiv) cfl) c1) (wceq (cfv (cneg (co c3 c2 cdiv)) cfl) (cneg c2)) $;

--| Example for ~ df-ceil . (Contributed by AV, 4-Sep-2021.)
theorem ex_ceil:
  $ wa (wceq (cfv (co c3 c2 cdiv) cceil) c2) (wceq (cfv (cneg (co c3 c2 cdiv)) cceil) (cneg c1)) $;

--| (_This theorem is a dummy placeholder for these guidelines. The name of this theorem, "mathbox", is hard-coded into the Metamath program to identify the start of the mathbox section for web page generation_.) A "mathbox" is a user-contributed section that is maintained by its contributor independently from the main part of set.mm. _For contributors_: By making a contribution, you agree to release it into the public domain, according to the statement at the beginning of set.mm. Mathboxes are provided to help keep your work synchronized with changes in set.mm, but they shouldn't be depended on as a permanent archive. If you want to preserve your original contribution, it is your responsibility to keep your own copy of it along with the version of set.mm that works with it. _Guidelines_: 1. If at all possible, please use only 0-ary class constants for new definitions. 2. Try to follow the style of the rest of set.mm. Each $p and $a statement must be immediately preceded with the comment that will be shown on its web page description. The metamath program command "write source set.mm /rewrap" will take care of wrapping comment lines and indentation conventions. All mathbox content will be on public display and should hopefully reflect the overall quality of the website. 3. Before submitting a revised mathbox, please make sure it verifies against the current set.mm. 4. Mathboxes should be independent i.e. the proofs should verify with all other mathboxes removed. If you need a theorem from another mathbox, that is fine (and encouraged), but let me know, so I can move the theorem to the main section. One way avoid undesired accidental use of other mathbox theorems is to develop your mathbox using a modified set.mm that has mathboxes removed. _Notes_: 1. We may decide to move some theorems to the main part of set.mm for general use. 2. We may make changes to mathboxes to maintain the overall quality of set.mm. Normally we will let you know if a change might impact what you are working on. 3. If you use theorems from another user's mathbox, we don't provide assurance that they are based on correct or consistent $a statements. (If you find such a problem, please let us know so it can be corrected.) (Contributed by NM, 20-Feb-2007.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem mathbox (ph: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ ph $;

--| Heyting's formal system Axiom #1 from [Heyting] p. 127. (Contributed by MM, 11-Aug-2018.)
theorem ax1hfs (ph: wff):
  $ wi ph (wa ph ph) $;

--| Double negation of excluded middle. Intuitionistic logic refutes the negation of excluded middle (but, of course, does not prove excluded middle) for any formula. (Contributed by BJ, 9-Oct-2019.)
theorem nnexmid (ph: wff):
  $ wn (wn (wo ph (wn ph))) $;

--| Double negation of decidability of a formula. Intuitionistic logic refutes undecidability (but, of course, does not prove decidability) of any formula. (Contributed by BJ, 9-Oct-2019.)
theorem nndc (ph: wff):
  $ wn (wn (wdc ph)) $;

--| Decidability of a proposition is decidable if and only if that proposition is decidable. ` DECID ` is idempotent. (Contributed by BJ, 9-Oct-2019.)
theorem dcdc (ph: wff):
  $ wb (wdc (wdc ph)) (wdc ph) $;

--| Existential generalization. (Contributed by BJ, 8-Dec-2019.) Proof modification is discouraged because there are shorter proofs, but using less basic results (like ~ exlimiv and ~ 19.9ht or ~ 19.23ht ). (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_ex {x3: setvar} (ph: wff):
  $ wi (wex x3 ph) ph $;

--| Closed form of ~ hbal (copied from set.mm). (Contributed by BJ, 2-May-2019.)
theorem bj_hbalt {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal y1 (wi ph (wal x3 ph))) (wi (wal y1 ph) (wal x3 (wal y1 ph))) $;

--| Closed form of ~ nfal (copied from set.mm). (Contributed by BJ, 2-May-2019.)
theorem bj_nfalt {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wnf y1 ph)) (wnf y1 (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ spim . (Contributed by BJ, 17-Oct-2019.)
theorem spimd {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ps ch))) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wal x3 ps) ch) $;

--| Double substitution, as in ~ spim . (Contributed by BJ, 17-Oct-2019.)
theorem _2spim {x3 y1 z t: setvar} (ps ch: wff x3 y1 z t):
  $ wnf x3 ch $ >
  $ wnf z ch $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv z) (cv t))) (wi ps ch) $ >
  $ wi (wal z (wal x3 ps)) ch $;

--| Implicit substitution of ` y ` for ` x ` and ` t ` for ` z ` into a theorem. (Contributed by BJ, 17-Oct-2019.)
theorem ch2var {x3 y1 z t: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1 z t):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf z ps $ >
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv z) (cv t))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Version of ~ ch2var with non-freeness hypotheses replaced by DV conditions. (Contributed by BJ, 17-Oct-2019.)
theorem ch2varv {x3 y1 z t: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z t) (ps: wff y1 t):
  $ wi (wa (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wceq (cv z) (cv t))) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ ph $ >
  $ ps $;

--| Lemma for ~ bj-vtoclgf . (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_exlimmp {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi ch ph $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi ch (wi ph ps))) (wi (wex x3 ch) ps) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bj-vtoclgf . (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_exlimmpi {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi ch ph $ >
  $ wi ch (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wex x3 ch) ps $;

--| A strengthening of ~ sbiedh (same proof). (Contributed by BJ, 16-Dec-2019.)
theorem bj_sbimedh {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ph (wal x3 ph) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi ch (wal x3 ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wsb x3 y1 ps) ch) $;

--| A strengthening of ~ sbieh (same proof). (Contributed by BJ, 16-Dec-2019.)
theorem bj_sbimeh {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi ps (wal x3 ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wsb x3 y1 ph) ps $;

--| A strengthening of ~ sbie (same proof). (Contributed by BJ, 16-Dec-2019.)
theorem bj_sbime {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wsb x3 y1 ph) ps $;

--| Weakening two hypotheses of ~ vtoclgf . (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.)
theorem bj_vtoclgft {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2 V: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) ph $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wi ph ps))) (wi (wcel A2 V) ps) $;

--| Weakening two hypotheses of ~ vtoclgf . (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.)
theorem bj_vtoclgf {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2 V: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) ph $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) ps $;

--| Lemma for ~ elabgf . (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.)
theorem elabgf0 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wb (wcel A2 (cab x3 ph)) ph) $;

--| One implication of ~ elabgf , in closed form. (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.)
theorem elabgft1 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wi ph ps))) (wi (wcel A2 (cab x3 ph)) ps) $;

--| One implication of ~ elabgf . (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.)
theorem elabgf1 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cab x3 ph)) ps $;

--| One implication of ~ elabgf . (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.)
theorem elabgf2 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wi ps ph) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 B2) (wi ps (wcel A2 (cab x3 ph))) $;

--| One implication of ~ elabf . (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.)
theorem elabf1 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cab x3 ph)) ps $;

--| One implication of ~ elabf . (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.)
theorem elabf2 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wi ps ph) $ >
  $ wi ps (wcel A2 (cab x3 ph)) $;

--| One implication of ~ elab . (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.)
theorem elab1 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 (cab x3 ph)) ps $;

--| One implication of ~ elab . (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.)
theorem elab2a {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff) (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wi ps ph) $ >
  $ wi ps (wcel A2 (cab x3 ph)) $;

--| One implication of ~ elabg . (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.)
theorem elabg2 {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff) (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wi ps ph) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi ps (wcel A2 (cab x3 ph))) $;

--| Restricted specialization, generalized. Weakens a hypothesis of ~ rspccv and seems to have a shorter proof. (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.)
theorem bj_rspgt {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wi ph ps))) (wi (wral x3 ph B2) (wi (wcel A2 B2) ps)) $;

--| Restricted specialization, generalized. Weakens a hypothesis of ~ rspccv and seems to have a shorter proof. (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.)
theorem bj_rspg {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2 B2: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnfc x3 B2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 ph B2) (wi (wcel A2 B2) ps) $;

--| Rule used to change bound variables, using implicit substitution. (Contributed by BJ, 22-Nov-2019.)
theorem cbvrald {x3 y1: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3 y1) (A2: class):
  $ wnf x3 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf y1 ps) $ >
  $ wi ph (wnf x3 ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ps ch)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wral x3 ps A2) (wral y1 ch A2)) $;

--| Version of ~ intss1 using a class abstraction and explicit substitution. (Contributed by BJ, 29-Nov-2019.)
theorem bj_intabssel {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 V: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (wsbc x3 ph A2) (wss (cint (cab x3 ph)) A2)) $;

--| Version of ~ intss1 using a class abstraction and implicit substitution. Closed form of ~ intmin3 . (Contributed by BJ, 29-Nov-2019.)
theorem bj_intabssel1 {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2 V: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wi ps ph) $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi ps (wss (cint (cab x3 ph)) A2)) $;

--| Version of ~ elssuni using a class abstraction and explicit substitution. (Contributed by BJ, 29-Nov-2019.)
theorem bj_elssuniab {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2 V: class x3):
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (wsbc x3 ph A2) (wss A2 (cuni (cab x3 ph)))) $;

--| If two converse inclusions are characterized each by a formula, then equality is characterized by the conjunction of these formulas. (Contributed by BJ, 30-Nov-2019.)
theorem bj_sseq (ph ps ch: wff) (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi ph (wb ps (wss A2 B2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb ch (wss B2 A2)) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wa ps ch) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| Syntax for the predicate ` Bdd ` .
term wbd (ph: wff): wff;

--| If two formulas are equivalent, then boundedness of one implies boundedness of the other. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
axiom ax_bd0 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wi (wbd ph) (wbd ps) $;

--| An implication between two bounded formulas is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 25-Sep-2019.)
axiom ax_bdim (ph ps: wff):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd ps $ >
  $ wbd (wi ph ps) $;

--| The conjunction of two bounded formulas is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 25-Sep-2019.)
axiom ax_bdan (ph ps: wff):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd ps $ >
  $ wbd (wa ph ps) $;

--| The disjunction of two bounded formulas is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 25-Sep-2019.)
axiom ax_bdor (ph ps: wff):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd ps $ >
  $ wbd (wo ph ps) $;

--| The negation of a bounded formula is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 25-Sep-2019.)
axiom ax_bdn (ph: wff):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd (wn ph) $;

--| A bounded universal quantification of a bounded formula is bounded. Note the DV condition on ` x , y ` . (Contributed by BJ, 25-Sep-2019.)
axiom ax_bdal {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (y1: setvar):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd (wral x3 ph (cv y1)) $;

--| A bounded existential quantification of a bounded formula is bounded. Note the DV condition on ` x , y ` . (Contributed by BJ, 25-Sep-2019.)
axiom ax_bdex {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (y1: setvar):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd (wrex x3 ph (cv y1)) $;

--| An atomic formula is bounded (equality predicate). (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
axiom ax_bdeq (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wbd (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| An atomic formula is bounded (equality predicate). (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
axiom ax_bdeq_b (x3: setvar):
  $ wbd (wceq (cv x3) (cv x3)) $;

--| An atomic formula is bounded (membership predicate). (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
axiom ax_bdel (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wbd (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| An atomic formula is bounded (membership predicate). (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
axiom ax_bdel_b (x3: setvar):
  $ wbd (wcel (cv x3) (cv x3)) $;

--| A formula resulting from proper substitution in a bounded formula is bounded. This probably cannot be proved from the other axioms, since neither the definiens in ~ df-sb , nor probably any other equivalent formula, is syntactically bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
axiom ax_bdsb {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd (wsb x3 y1 ph) $;

--| Equality property for the predicate ` Bdd ` . (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdeq (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wbd ph) (wbd ps) $;

--| A formula equivalent to a bounded one is bounded. See also ~ bd0r . (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bd0 (ph ps: wff):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wbd ps $;

--| A formula equivalent to a bounded one is bounded. Stated with a commuted (compared with ~ bd0 ) biconditional in the hypothesis, to work better with definitions ( ` ps ` is the definiendum that one wants to prove bounded). (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bd0r (ph ps: wff):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wb ps ph $ >
  $ wbd ps $;

--| A biconditional between two bounded formulas is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdbi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd ps $ >
  $ wbd (wb ph ps) $;

--| Stability of a bounded formula is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdstab (ph: wff):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd (wstab ph) $;

--| Decidability of a bounded formula is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bddc (ph: wff):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd (wdc ph) $;

--| A disjunction of three bounded formulas is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bd3or (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd ps $ >
  $ wbd ch $ >
  $ wbd (w3o ph ps ch) $;

--| A conjunction of three bounded formulas is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bd3an (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd ps $ >
  $ wbd ch $ >
  $ wbd (w3a ph ps ch) $;

--| A truth (a (closed) theorem) is a bounded formula. (Contributed by BJ, 6-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdth (ph: wff):
  $ ph $ >
  $ wbd ph $;

--| The truth value ` T. ` is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdtru:
  $ wbd wtru $;

--| The truth value ` F. ` is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdfal:
  $ wbd wfal $;

--| A falsity is a bounded formula. (Contributed by BJ, 6-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdnth (ph: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wbd ph $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ bdnth not using ~ bdfal . Then, ~ bdfal can be proved from this theorem, using ~ fal . The total number of proof steps would be 17 (for ~ bdnthALT ) + 3 = 20, which is more than 8 (for ~ bdfal ) + 9 (for ~ bdnth ) = 17. (Contributed by BJ, 6-Oct-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem bdnthALT (ph: wff):
  $ wn ph $ >
  $ wbd ph $;

--| The exclusive disjunction of two bounded formulas is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdxor (ph ps: wff):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd ps $ >
  $ wbd (wxo ph ps) $;

--| Boundedness of a membership formula. (Contributed by BJ, 8-Dec-2019.)
theorem bj_bdcel {y1: setvar} (x3: setvar) (A2: class):
  $ wbd (wceq (cv y1) A2) $ >
  $ wbd (wcel A2 (cv x3)) $;

--| Membership in a class defined by class abstraction using a bounded formula, is a bounded formula. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdab {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd (wcel (cv x3) (cab y1 ph)) $;

--| Conditional equality of a bounded formula is a bounded formula. (Contributed by BJ, 16-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdcdeq {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd (wcdeq x3 y1 ph) $;

--| Syntax for the predicate ` Bdd_ ` .
term wbdc (A2: class): wff;

--| Define a bounded class as one such that membership in this class is a bounded formula. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
axiom df_bdc {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wbdc A2) (wal x3 (wbd (wcel (cv x3) A2))) $;

--| Equality property for the predicate ` Bdd_ ` . (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdceq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wb (wbdc A2) (wbdc B2) $;

--| A class equal to a bounded one is bounded. Note the use of ~ ax-ext . See also ~ bdceqir . (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdceqi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wceq A2 B2 $ >
  $ wbdc B2 $;

--| A class equal to a bounded one is bounded. Stated with a commuted (compared with ~ bdceqi ) equality in the hypothesis, to work better with definitions ( ` B ` is the definiendum that one wants to prove bounded; see comment of ~ bd0r ). (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdceqir (A2 B2: class):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wceq B2 A2 $ >
  $ wbdc B2 $;

--| The belonging of a setvar in a bounded class is a bounded formula. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdel {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wbdc A2) (wbd (wcel (cv x3) A2)) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ bdel . Its converse is ~ bdelir . (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdeli {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wbd (wcel (cv x3) A2) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ df-bdc . Its converse is ~ bdeli . (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdelir {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wbd (wcel (cv x3) A2) $ >
  $ wbdc A2 $;

--| A setvar is a bounded class. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdcv (x3: setvar):
  $ wbdc (cv x3) $;

--| A class defined by class abstraction using a bounded formula is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 6-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdcab {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbdc (cab x3 ph) $;

--| A formula which defines (by class abstraction) a bounded class is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 6-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdph {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wbdc (cab x3 ph) $ >
  $ wbd ph $;

--| Boundedness of a formula resulting from implicit substitution in a bounded formula. Note that the proof does not use ~ ax-bdsb ; therefore, using implicit instead of explicit substitution when boundedness is important, one might avoid using ~ ax-bdsb . (Contributed by BJ, 19-Nov-2019.)
theorem bds {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps: wff y1):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wb ph ps) $ >
  $ wbd ps $;

--| A class defined by restricted abstraction from a bounded class and a bounded formula is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdcrab {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbdc (crab x3 ph A2) $;

--| Inequality of two setvars is a bounded formula. (Contributed by BJ, 16-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdne (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wbd (wne (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| Non-membership of a setvar in a bounded formula is a bounded formula. (Contributed by BJ, 16-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdnel {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wbd (wnel (cv x3) A2) $;

--| Boundedness of existential uniqueness. Remark regarding restricted quantifiers: the formula ` A. x e. A ph ` need not be bounded even if ` A ` and ` ph ` are. Indeed, ` _V ` is bounded by ~ bdcvv , and ` |- ( A. x e. _V ph <-> A. x ph ) ` (in minimal propositional calculus), so by ~ bd0 , if ` A. x e. _V ph ` were bounded when ` ph ` is bounded, then ` A. x ph ` would be bounded as well when ` ph ` is bounded, which is not the case. The same remark holds with ` E. , E! , E* ` . (Contributed by BJ, 16-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdreu {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (y1: setvar):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd (wreu x3 ph (cv y1)) $;

--| Boundedness of existential at-most-one. (Contributed by BJ, 16-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdrmo {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (y1: setvar):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd (wrmo x3 ph (cv y1)) $;

--| The universal class is bounded. The formulation may sound strange, but recall that here, "bounded" means " ` Delta0 ` ". (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdcvv:
  $ wbdc cvv $;

--| A formula resulting from proper substitution of a setvar for a setvar in a bounded formula is bounded. See also ~ bdsbcALT . (Contributed by BJ, 16-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdsbc {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd (wsbc x3 ph (cv y1)) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ bdsbc . (Contributed by BJ, 16-Oct-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem bdsbcALT {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbd (wsbc x3 ph (cv y1)) $;

--| A class resulting from proper substitution of a setvar for a setvar in a bounded class is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 16-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdccsb {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class x3 y1):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wbdc (csb x3 (cv y1) A2) $;

--| The difference of two bounded classes is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdcdif (A2 B2: class):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wbdc B2 $ >
  $ wbdc (cdif A2 B2) $;

--| The union of two bounded classes is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdcun (A2 B2: class):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wbdc B2 $ >
  $ wbdc (cun A2 B2) $;

--| The intersection of two bounded classes is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdcin (A2 B2: class):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wbdc B2 $ >
  $ wbdc (cin A2 B2) $;

--| The inclusion of a setvar in a bounded class is a bounded formula. Note: apparently, we cannot prove from the present axioms that equality of two bounded classes is a bounded formula. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdss (x3: setvar) (A2: class):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wbd (wss (cv x3) A2) $;

--| The empty class is bounded. See also ~ bdcnulALT . (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdcnul:
  $ wbdc c0 $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ bdcnul . Similarly, for the next few theorems proving boundedness of a class, one can either use their definition followed by ~ bdceqir , or use the corresponding characterizations of its elements followed by ~ bdelir . (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem bdcnulALT:
  $ wbdc c0 $;

--| Boundedness of the formula expressing that a setvar is equal to the empty class. (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.)
theorem bdeq0 (x3: setvar):
  $ wbd (wceq (cv x3) c0) $;

--| Boundedness of the formula "the empty set belongs to the setvar ` x ` ". (Contributed by BJ, 30-Nov-2019.)
theorem bj_bd0el (x3: setvar):
  $ wbd (wcel c0 (cv x3)) $;

--| The power class of a bounded class is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 3-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdcpw (A2: class):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wbdc (cpw A2) $;

--| The singleton of a setvar is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 16-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdcsn (x3: setvar):
  $ wbdc (csn (cv x3)) $;

--| The pair of two setvars is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 16-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdcpr (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wbdc (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| The unordered triple of three setvars is bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 16-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdctp (x3 y1 z: setvar):
  $ wbdc (ctp (cv x3) (cv y1) (cv z)) $;

--| Inclusion of a singleton of a setvar in a bounded class is a bounded formula. (Contributed by BJ, 16-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdsnss {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wbd (wss (csn (cv x3)) A2) $;

--| Equality of a setvar with a singleton of a setvar is a bounded formula. (Contributed by BJ, 16-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdvsn {y1: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wbd (wceq (cv x3) (csn (cv y1))) $;

--| The ordered pair of two setvars is a bounded class. (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.)
theorem bdop {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wbdc (cop (cv x3) (cv y1)) $;

--| The union of a setvar is a bounded class. (Contributed by BJ, 15-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdcuni (x3: setvar):
  $ wbdc (cuni (cv x3)) $;

--| The intersection of a setvar is a bounded class. (Contributed by BJ, 16-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdcint (x3: setvar):
  $ wbdc (cint (cv x3)) $;

--| The indexed union of a bounded class with a setvar indexing set is a bounded class. (Contributed by BJ, 16-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdciun {x3: setvar} (y1: setvar) (A2: class x3):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wbdc (ciun x3 (cv y1) A2) $;

--| The indexed intersection of a bounded class with a setvar indexing set is a bounded class. (Contributed by BJ, 16-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdciin {x3: setvar} (y1: setvar) (A2: class x3):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wbdc (ciin x3 (cv y1) A2) $;

--| The successor of a setvar is a bounded class. (Contributed by BJ, 16-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdcsuc (x3: setvar):
  $ wbdc (csuc (cv x3)) $;

--| Boundedness of the formula expressing that a setvar is equal to the successor of another. (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.)
theorem bdeqsuc {y1: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wbd (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) $;

--| Boundedness of the formula "the successor of the setvar ` x ` belongs to the setvar ` y ` ". (Contributed by BJ, 30-Nov-2019.)
theorem bj_bdsucel {x3: setvar} (y1: setvar):
  $ wbd (wcel (csuc (cv x3)) (cv y1)) $;

--| A class given by a restricted definition binder is bounded, under the given hypotheses. (Contributed by BJ, 24-Nov-2019.)
theorem bdcriota {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wreu x3 ph (cv y1) $ >
  $ wbdc (crio x3 ph (cv y1)) $;

--| Axiom scheme of bounded (or restricted, or ` Delta0 ` ) separation. It is stated with all possible disjoint variable conditions, to show that this weak form is sufficient. For the full axiom of separation, see ~ ax-sep . (Contributed by BJ, 5-Oct-2019.)
axiom ax_bdsep {x3 a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wal a (wex b (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv b)) (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv a)) ph)))) $;

--| Version of ~ ax-bdsep without initial universal quantifier. (Contributed by BJ, 5-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdsep1 {x3 a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wex b (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv b)) (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv a)) ph))) $;

--| Version of ~ ax-bdsep with one DV condition removed and without initial universal quantifier. Use ~ bdsep1 when sufficient. (Contributed by BJ, 5-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdsep2 {x3 a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 a):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wex b (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv b)) (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv a)) ph))) $;

--| Closed form of ~ bdsepnf . Version of ~ ax-bdsep with one DV condition removed, the other DV condition replaced by a non-freeness antecedent, and without initial universal quantifier. Use ~ bdsep1 when sufficient. (Contributed by BJ, 19-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdsepnft {x3 a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 a b):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wnf b ph)) (wex b (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv b)) (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv a)) ph)))) $;

--| Version of ~ ax-bdsep with one DV condition removed, the other DV condition replaced by a non-freeness hypothesis, and without initial universal quantifier. See also ~ bdsepnfALT . Use ~ bdsep1 when sufficient. (Contributed by BJ, 5-Oct-2019.)
theorem bdsepnf {x3 a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 a b):
  $ wnf b ph $ >
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wex b (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv b)) (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv a)) ph))) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ bdsepnf , not using ~ bdsepnft . (Contributed by BJ, 5-Oct-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem bdsepnfALT {x3 a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 a b):
  $ wnf b ph $ >
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wex b (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv b)) (wa (wcel (cv x3) (cv a)) ph))) $;

--| Closed form of the version of ~ zfauscl for bounded formulas using bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 13-Nov-2019.)
theorem bdzfauscl {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wex y1 (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wa (wcel (cv x3) A2) ph)))) $;

--| Bounded version of ~ bm1.3ii . (Contributed by BJ, 5-Oct-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bdbm1_3ii {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff y1):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wex x3 (wal y1 (wi ph (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))) $ >
  $ wex x3 (wal y1 (wb (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) ph)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bj-axempty and ~ bj-axempty2 . (Contributed by BJ, 25-Oct-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) Use ~ ax-nul instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem bj_axemptylem {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wal y1 (wi (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)) wfal)) $;

--| Axiom of the empty set from bounded separation. It is provable from bounded separation since the intuitionistic FOL used in iset.mm assumes a non-empty universe. See ~ axnul . (Contributed by BJ, 25-Oct-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) Use ~ ax-nul instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem bj_axempty {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wral y1 wfal (cv x3)) $;

--| Axiom of the empty set from bounded separation, alternate version to ~ bj-axempty . (Contributed by BJ, 27-Oct-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) Use ~ ax-nul instead. (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem bj_axempty2 {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wal y1 (wn (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))) $;

--| ~ nalset from bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 18-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_nalset {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wn (wex x3 (wal y1 (wcel (cv y1) (cv x3)))) $;

--| ~ vprc from bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 18-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_vprc:
  $ wn (wcel cvv cvv) $;

--| ~ nvel from bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 18-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_nvel (A2: class):
  $ wn (wcel cvv A2) $;

--| ~ vnex from bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 18-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_vnex {x3: setvar}:
  $ wn (wex x3 (wceq (cv x3) cvv)) $;

--| Bounded version of ~ inex1 . (Contributed by BJ, 13-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bdinex1 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wbdc B2 $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cin A2 B2) cvv $;

--| Bounded version of ~ inex2 . (Contributed by BJ, 13-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bdinex2 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wbdc B2 $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cin B2 A2) cvv $;

--| Bounded version of ~ inex1g . (Contributed by BJ, 13-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bdinex1g (A2 B2 V: class):
  $ wbdc B2 $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cin A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| Bounded version of ~ ssex . (Contributed by BJ, 13-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bdssex (A2 B2: class):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wi (wss A2 B2) (wcel A2 cvv) $;

--| Bounded version of ~ ssexi . (Contributed by BJ, 13-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bdssexi (A2 B2: class):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wss A2 B2 $ >
  $ wcel A2 cvv $;

--| Bounded version of ~ ssexg . (Contributed by BJ, 13-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bdssexg (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wi (wa (wss A2 B2) (wcel B2 C)) (wcel A2 cvv) $;

--| Bounded version of ~ ssexd . (Contributed by BJ, 13-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bdssexd (ph: wff) (A2 B2 C: class):
  $ wi ph (wcel B2 C) $ >
  $ wi ph (wss A2 B2) $ >
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wi ph (wcel A2 cvv) $;

--| Bounded version of ~ rabexg . (Contributed by BJ, 19-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bdrabexg {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (crab x3 ph A2) cvv) $;

--| The intersection of two sets is a set, from bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 19-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_inex (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wcel (cin A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| ~ intexr from bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 18-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_intexr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel (cint A2) cvv) (wne A2 c0) $;

--| ~ intnexr from bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 18-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_intnexr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wceq (cint A2) cvv) (wn (wcel (cint A2) cvv)) $;

--| Proof of ~ zfpair2 using only bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 5-Oct-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_zfpair2 (x3 y1: setvar):
  $ wcel (cpr (cv x3) (cv y1)) cvv $;

--| Proof of ~ prexg using only bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 5-Oct-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_prexg (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wcel (cpr A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| ~ snexg from bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 5-Oct-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_snexg (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (csn A2) cvv) $;

--| ~ snex from bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 5-Oct-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_snex (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (csn A2) cvv $;

--| If a class is a set, then it is a member of a set. (Copied from set.mm.) (Contributed by BJ, 3-Apr-2019.)
theorem bj_sels {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wex x3 (wcel A2 (cv x3))) $;

--| ~ axun2 from bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 15-Oct-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_axun2 {y1 z w: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wex y1 (wal z (wb (wcel (cv z) (cv y1)) (wex w (wa (wcel (cv z) (cv w)) (wcel (cv w) (cv x3)))))) $;

--| ~ uniex2 from bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 15-Oct-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_uniex2 {y1: setvar} (x3: setvar):
  $ wex y1 (wceq (cv y1) (cuni (cv x3))) $;

--| ~ uniex from bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 13-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_uniex (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cuni A2) cvv $;

--| ~ uniexg from bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 13-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_uniexg (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (cuni A2) cvv) $;

--| ~ unex from bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 13-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_unex (A2 B2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel B2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (cun A2 B2) cvv $;

--| Bounded version of ~ unexb . (Contributed by BJ, 13-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bdunexb (A2 B2: class):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wbdc B2 $ >
  $ wb (wa (wcel A2 cvv) (wcel B2 cvv)) (wcel (cun A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| ~ unexg from bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 13-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_unexg (A2 B2 V W: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 V) (wcel B2 W)) (wcel (cun A2 B2) cvv) $;

--| ~ sucexg from bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 13-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_sucexg (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wcel (csuc A2) cvv) $;

--| ~ sucex from bounded separation. (Contributed by BJ, 13-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_sucex (A2: class):
  $ wcel A2 cvv $ >
  $ wcel (csuc A2) cvv $;

--| Axiom for ` Delta0 ` -classical logic. (Contributed by BJ, 2-Jan-2020.)
axiom ax_bj_d0cl (ph: wff):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wdc ph $;

--| Equivalence property for negation. TODO: minimize all theorems using ~ notbid and ~ notbii . (Contributed by BJ, 27-Jan-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_notbi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wb ph ps) (wb (wn ph) (wn ps)) $;

--| Inference associated with ~ bj-notbi . (Contributed by BJ, 27-Jan-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_notbii (ph ps: wff):
  $ wb ph ps $ >
  $ wb (wn ph) (wn ps) $;

--| Deduction form of ~ bj-notbi . (Contributed by BJ, 27-Jan-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_notbid (ph ps ch: wff):
  $ wi ph (wb ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wb (wn ps) (wn ch)) $;

--| Equivalence property for ` DECID ` . TODO: solve conflict with ~ dcbi ; minimize ~ dcbii and ~ dcbid with it, as well as theorems using those. (Contributed by BJ, 27-Jan-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_dcbi (ph ps: wff):
  $ wi (wb ph ps) (wb (wdc ph) (wdc ps)) $;

--| ` Delta0 ` -classical logic and separation implies classical logic. (Contributed by BJ, 2-Jan-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_d0clsepcl (ph: wff):
  $ wdc ph $;

--| Syntax for inductive classes.
term wind (A2: class): wff;

--| Define the property of being an inductive class. (Contributed by BJ, 30-Nov-2019.)
axiom df_bj_ind {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wind A2) (wa (wcel c0 A2) (wral x3 (wcel (csuc (cv x3)) A2) A2)) $;

--| A direct consequence of the definition of ` Ind ` . (Contributed by BJ, 30-Nov-2019.)
theorem bj_indsuc (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wind A2) (wi (wcel B2 A2) (wcel (csuc B2) A2)) $;

--| Equality property for ` Ind ` . (Contributed by BJ, 30-Nov-2019.)
theorem bj_indeq (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wceq A2 B2) (wb (wind A2) (wind B2)) $;

--| Boundedness of the formula "the setvar ` x ` is an inductive class". (Contributed by BJ, 30-Nov-2019.)
theorem bj_bdind (x3: setvar):
  $ wbd (wind (cv x3)) $;

--| The property of being an inductive class is closed under intersections. (Contributed by BJ, 30-Nov-2019.)
theorem bj_indint {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wind (cint (crab x3 (wind (cv x3)) A2)) $;

--| If ` A ` is inductive and ` B ` is "inductive in ` A ` ", then ` ( A i^i B ) ` is inductive. (Contributed by BJ, 25-Oct-2020.)
theorem bj_indind {x3: setvar} (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wind A2) (wa (wcel c0 B2) (wral x3 (wi (wcel (cv x3) B2) (wcel (csuc (cv x3)) B2)) A2))) (wind (cin A2 B2)) $;

--| Alternate definition of ` _om ` , as the intersection of all the inductive sets. Proposal: make this the definition. (Contributed by BJ, 30-Nov-2019.)
theorem bj_dfom {x3: setvar}:
  $ wceq com (cint (cab x3 (wind (cv x3)))) $;

--| ` _om ` is an inductive class. (Contributed by BJ, 30-Nov-2019.)
theorem bj_omind:
  $ wind com $;

--| ` _om ` is included in all the inductive sets (but for the moment, we cannot prove that it is included in all the inductive classes). (Contributed by BJ, 30-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_omssind (A2 V: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (wind A2) (wss com A2)) $;

--| A characterization of subclasses of ` _om ` . (Contributed by BJ, 30-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_ssom {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wal x3 (wi (wind (cv x3)) (wss A2 (cv x3)))) (wss A2 com) $;

--| A set is equal to ` _om ` if and only if it is the smallest inductive set. (Contributed by BJ, 30-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_om {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wb (wceq A2 com) (wa (wind A2) (wal x3 (wi (wind (cv x3)) (wss A2 (cv x3)))))) $;

--| Two formulations of the axiom of infinity (see ~ ax-infvn and ~ bj-omex ) . (Contributed by BJ, 30-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_2inf {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wb (wcel com cvv) (wex x3 (wa (wind (cv x3)) (wal y1 (wi (wind (cv y1)) (wss (cv x3) (cv y1)))))) $;

--| Constructive proof of ~ peano2 . Temporary note: another possibility is to simply replace ~ sucexg with ~ bj-sucexg in the proof of ~ peano2 . (Contributed by BJ, 18-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_peano2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wcel (csuc A2) com) $;

--| Version of ~ peano5 when ` _om i^i A ` is assumed to be a set, allowing a proof from the core axioms of CZF. (Contributed by BJ, 19-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem peano5set {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel (cin com A2) V) (wi (wa (wcel c0 A2) (wral x3 (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (csuc (cv x3)) A2)) com)) (wss com A2)) $;

--| Obsolete version of ~ peano5set as of 26-Oct-2020. (Contributed by BJ, 19-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem peano5setOLD {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel (cin com A2) V) (wi (wa (wcel c0 A2) (wral x3 (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (csuc (cv x3)) A2)) com)) (wss com A2)) $;

--| Axiom of infinity in a constructive setting. This asserts the existence of the special set we want (the set of natural numbers), instead of the existence of a set with some properties ( ~ ax-iinf ) from which one then proves, using full separation, that the wanted set exists ( ~ omex ). "vn" is for "Von Neumann". (Contributed by BJ, 14-Nov-2019.)
axiom ax_infvn {x3 y1: setvar}:
  $ wex x3 (wa (wind (cv x3)) (wal y1 (wi (wind (cv y1)) (wss (cv x3) (cv y1))))) $;

--| Proof of ~ omex from ~ ax-infvn . (Contributed by BJ, 14-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_omex:
  $ wcel com cvv $;

--| Bounded version of ~ peano5 . (Contributed by BJ, 19-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bdpeano5 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wi (wa (wcel c0 A2) (wral x3 (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (csuc (cv x3)) A2)) com)) (wss com A2) $;

--| Version of ~ peano5 when ` A ` is assumed to be a set, allowing a proof from the core axioms of CZF. (Contributed by BJ, 19-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem speano5 {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (w3a (wcel A2 V) (wcel c0 A2) (wral x3 (wi (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wcel (csuc (cv x3)) A2)) com)) (wss com A2) $;

--| Bounded induction (principle of induction when ` A ` is assumed to be a set) allowing a proof from basic constructive axioms. See ~ find for a nonconstructive proof of the general case. See ~ bdfind for a proof when ` A ` is assumed to be bounded. (Contributed by BJ, 22-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem findset {x3: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (w3a (wss A2 com) (wcel c0 A2) (wral x3 (wcel (csuc (cv x3)) A2) A2)) (wceq A2 com)) $;

--| Bounded induction (principle of induction when ` A ` is assumed to be bounded), proved from basic constructive axioms. See ~ find for a nonconstructive proof of the general case. See ~ findset for a proof when ` A ` is assumed to be a set. (Contributed by BJ, 22-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bdfind {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wi (w3a (wss A2 com) (wcel c0 A2) (wral x3 (wcel (csuc (cv x3)) A2) A2)) (wceq A2 com) $;

--| Bounded induction (principle of induction for bounded formulas), using implicit substitutions (the biconditional versions of the hypotheses are implicit substitutions, and we have weakened them to implications). Constructive proof (from CZF). See ~ finds for a proof of full induction in IZF. From this version, it is easy to prove bounded versions of ~ finds , ~ finds2 , ~ finds1 . (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_bdfindis {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch th: wff x3 y1):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf x3 ch $ >
  $ wnf x3 th $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wi ps ph) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) (wi th ph) $ >
  $ wi (wa ps (wral y1 (wi ch th) com)) (wral x3 ph com) $;

--| Version of ~ bj-bdfindis using a class term in the consequent. Constructive proof (from CZF). See the comment of ~ bj-bdfindis for explanations. (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_bdfindisg {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch th ta: wff x3 y1) (A2: class x3 y1):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf x3 ch $ >
  $ wnf x3 th $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wi ps ph) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) (wi th ph) $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 ta $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wi ph ta) $ >
  $ wi (wa ps (wral y1 (wi ch th) com)) (wi (wcel A2 com) ta) $;

--| Bounded induction (principle of induction for bounded formulas), using explicit substitutions. Constructive proof (from CZF). See the comment of ~ bj-bdfindis for explanations. From this version, it is easy to prove the bounded version of ~ findes . (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_bdfindes {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wi (wa (wsbc x3 ph c0) (wral x3 (wi ph (wsbc x3 ph (csuc (cv x3)))) com)) (wral x3 ph com) $;

--| Constructive proof of a variant of ~ nn0suc . For a constructive proof of ~ nn0suc , see ~ bj-nn0suc . (Contributed by BJ, 19-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_nn0suc0 {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wo (wceq A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (csuc (cv x3))) A2)) $;

--| A natural number is a transitive set. (Contributed by BJ, 22-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_nntrans (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wi (wcel B2 A2) (wss B2 A2)) $;

--| A natural number is a transitive set. (Contributed by BJ, 22-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_nntrans2 (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wtr A2) $;

--| A natural number does not belong to itself. Version of ~ elirr for natural numbers, which does not require ~ ax-setind . (Contributed by BJ, 24-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_nnelirr (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wn (wcel A2 A2)) $;

--| A version of ~ en2lp for natural numbers, which does not require ~ ax-setind . Note: using this theorem and ~ bj-nnelirr , one can remove dependency on ~ ax-setind from ~ nntri2 and ~ nndcel ; one can actually remove more dependencies from these. (Contributed by BJ, 28-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_nnen2lp (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wn (wa (wcel A2 B2) (wcel B2 A2))) $;

--| Remove from ~ peano4 dependency on ~ ax-setind . Therefore, it only requires core constructive axioms (albeit more of them). (Contributed by BJ, 28-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_peano4 (A2 B2: class):
  $ wi (wa (wcel A2 com) (wcel B2 com)) (wb (wceq (csuc A2) (csuc B2)) (wceq A2 B2)) $;

--| The set ` _om ` is transitive. A natural number is included in ` _om ` . Constructive proof of ~ elnn . The idea is to use bounded induction with the formula ` x C_ _om ` . This formula, in a logic with terms, is bounded. So in our logic without terms, we need to temporarily replace it with ` x C_ a ` and then deduce the original claim. (Contributed by BJ, 29-Dec-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_omtrans (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wss A2 com) $;

--| The set ` _om ` is transitive. (Contributed by BJ, 29-Dec-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_omtrans2:
  $ wtr com $;

--| A natural number is an ordinal. Constructive proof of ~ nnord . Can also be proved from ~ bj-nnelon if the latter is proved from ~ bj-omssonALT . (Contributed by BJ, 27-Oct-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_nnord (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (word A2) $;

--| A natural number is an ordinal. Constructive proof of ~ nnon . Can also be proved from ~ bj-omssonALT . (Contributed by BJ, 27-Oct-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_nnelon (A2: class):
  $ wi (wcel A2 com) (wcel A2 con0) $;

--| The set ` _om ` is an ordinal. Constructive proof of ~ ordom . (Contributed by BJ, 29-Dec-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_omord:
  $ word com $;

--| The set ` _om ` is an ordinal. Constructive proof of ~ omelon . (Contributed by BJ, 29-Dec-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_omelon:
  $ wcel com con0 $;

--| Constructive proof of ~ omsson . See also ~ bj-omssonALT . (Contributed by BJ, 27-Oct-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.
theorem bj_omsson:
  $ wss com con0 $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ bj-omsson . (Contributed by BJ, 27-Oct-2020.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem bj_omssonALT:
  $ wss com con0 $;

--| Proof of (biconditional form of) ~ nn0suc from the core axioms of CZF. See also ~ bj-nn0sucALT . As a characterization of the elements of ` _om ` , this could be labeled "elom". (Contributed by BJ, 19-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_nn0suc {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 com) (wo (wceq A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (csuc (cv x3))) com)) $;

--| Axiom of set-induction with a DV condition replaced with a non-freeness hypothesis (Contributed by BJ, 22-Nov-2019.)
theorem setindft {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wnf y1 ph)) (wi (wal x3 (wi (wral y1 (wsb x3 y1 ph) (cv x3)) ph)) (wal x3 ph)) $;

--| Axiom of set-induction with a DV condition replaced with a non-freeness hypothesis (Contributed by BJ, 22-Nov-2019.)
theorem setindf {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wi (wal x3 (wi (wral y1 (wsb x3 y1 ph) (cv x3)) ph)) (wal x3 ph) $;

--| Axiom of set induction using implicit substitutions. (Contributed by BJ, 22-Nov-2019.)
theorem setindis {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps ch: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf x3 ch $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ch ph) $ >
  $ wi (wal y1 (wi (wral z ps (cv y1)) ch)) (wal x3 ph) $;

--| Axiom of bounded set induction. (Contributed by BJ, 28-Nov-2019.)
axiom ax_bdsetind {y1 a: setvar} (ph: wff a):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wi (wal a (wi (wral y1 (wsb a y1 ph) (cv a)) ph)) (wal a ph) $;

--| Axiom of bounded set induction using implicit substitutions. (Contributed by BJ, 22-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bdsetindis {x3 y1 z: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1) (ps ch: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wbd ph $ >
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf x3 ch $ >
  $ wnf y1 ph $ >
  $ wnf y1 ps $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv z)) (wi ph ps) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ch ph) $ >
  $ wi (wal y1 (wi (wral z ps (cv y1)) ch)) (wal x3 ph) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bj-inf2vn . Remark: unoptimized proof (have to use more deduction style). (Contributed by BJ, 8-Dec-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_inf2vnlem1 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wo (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) A2)))) (wind A2) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bj-inf2vnlem3 and ~ bj-inf2vnlem4 . Remark: unoptimized proof (have to use more deduction style). (Contributed by BJ, 8-Dec-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_inf2vnlem2 {x3 y1 u t: setvar} (A2 Z: class):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wo (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) A2)) A2) (wi (wind Z) (wal u (wi (wral t (wi (wcel (cv t) A2) (wcel (cv t) Z)) (cv u)) (wi (wcel (cv u) A2) (wcel (cv u) Z))))) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bj-inf2vn . (Contributed by BJ, 8-Dec-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_inf2vnlem3 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 Z: class):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wbdc Z $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wo (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) A2)) A2) (wi (wind Z) (wss A2 Z)) $;

--| Lemma for ~ bj-inf2vn2 . (Contributed by BJ, 8-Dec-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_inf2vnlem4 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2 Z: class):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wo (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) A2)) A2) (wi (wind Z) (wss A2 Z)) $;

--| A sufficient condition for ` _om ` to be a set. See ~ bj-inf2vn2 for the unbounded version from full set induction. (Contributed by BJ, 8-Dec-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_inf2vn {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wbdc A2 $ >
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wo (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) A2)))) (wceq A2 com)) $;

--| A sufficient condition for ` _om ` to be a set; unbounded version of ~ bj-inf2vn . (Contributed by BJ, 8-Dec-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_inf2vn2 {x3 y1: setvar} (A2: class) (V: class x3 y1):
  $ wi (wcel A2 V) (wi (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) A2) (wo (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) A2)))) (wceq A2 com)) $;

--| Another axiom of infinity in a constructive setting (see ~ ax-infvn ). (Contributed by BJ, 14-Nov-2019.) (New usage is discouraged.)
axiom ax_inf2 {x3 y1 a: setvar}:
  $ wex a (wal x3 (wb (wcel (cv x3) (cv a)) (wo (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wrex y1 (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) (cv a))))) $;

--| Using bounded set induction and the strong axiom of infinity, ` _om ` is a set, that is, we recover ~ ax-infvn (see ~ bj-2inf for the equivalence of the latter with ~ bj-omex ). (Contributed by BJ, 8-Dec-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem bj_omex2:
  $ wcel com cvv $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ bj-nn0suc , also constructive but from ~ ax-inf2 , hence requiring ~ ax-bdsetind . (Contributed by BJ, 8-Dec-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem bj_nn0sucALT {x3: setvar} (A2: class):
  $ wb (wcel A2 com) (wo (wceq A2 c0) (wrex x3 (wceq A2 (csuc (cv x3))) com)) $;

--| Principle of induction, using implicit substitutions (the biconditional versions of the hypotheses are implicit substitutions, and we have weakened them to implications). Constructive proof (from CZF). See ~ bj-bdfindis for a bounded version not requiring ~ ax-setind . See ~ finds for a proof in IZF. From this version, it is easy to prove of ~ finds , ~ finds2 , ~ finds1 . (Contributed by BJ, 22-Dec-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_findis {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch th: wff x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf x3 ch $ >
  $ wnf x3 th $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wi ps ph) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) (wi th ph) $ >
  $ wi (wa ps (wral y1 (wi ch th) com)) (wral x3 ph com) $;

--| Version of ~ bj-findis using a class term in the consequent. Constructive proof (from CZF). See the comment of ~ bj-findis for explanations. (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_findisg {x3 y1: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (ps ch th ta: wff x3 y1) (A2: class x3 y1):
  $ wnf x3 ps $ >
  $ wnf x3 ch $ >
  $ wnf x3 th $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) c0) (wi ps ph) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (cv y1)) (wi ph ch) $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) (csuc (cv y1))) (wi th ph) $ >
  $ wnfc x3 A2 $ >
  $ wnf x3 ta $ >
  $ wi (wceq (cv x3) A2) (wi ph ta) $ >
  $ wi (wa ps (wral y1 (wi ch th) com)) (wi (wcel A2 com) ta) $;

--| Principle of induction, using explicit substitutions. Constructive proof (from CZF). See the comment of ~ bj-findis for explanations. From this version, it is easy to prove ~ findes . (Contributed by BJ, 21-Nov-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.)
theorem bj_findes {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3):
  $ wi (wa (wsbc x3 ph c0) (wral x3 (wi ph (wsbc x3 ph (csuc (cv x3)))) com)) (wral x3 ph com) $;

--| Axiom scheme of strong collection. It is stated with all possible disjoint variable conditions, to show that this weak form is sufficient. (Contributed by BJ, 5-Oct-2019.)
axiom ax_strcoll {x3 y1 a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1):
  $ wal a (wi (wral x3 (wex y1 ph) (cv a)) (wex b (wal y1 (wb (wcel (cv y1) (cv b)) (wrex x3 ph (cv a)))))) $;

--| Version of ~ ax-strcoll with one DV condition removed and without initial universal quantifier. (Contributed by BJ, 5-Oct-2019.)
theorem strcoll2 {x3 y1 a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 a):
  $ wi (wral x3 (wex y1 ph) (cv a)) (wex b (wal y1 (wb (wcel (cv y1) (cv b)) (wrex x3 ph (cv a))))) $;

--| Closed form of ~ strcollnf . Version of ~ ax-strcoll with one DV condition removed, the other DV condition replaced by a non-freeness antecedent, and without initial universal quantifier. (Contributed by BJ, 21-Oct-2019.)
theorem strcollnft {x3 y1 a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 a b):
  $ wi (wal x3 (wal y1 (wnf b ph))) (wi (wral x3 (wex y1 ph) (cv a)) (wex b (wal y1 (wb (wcel (cv y1) (cv b)) (wrex x3 ph (cv a)))))) $;

--| Version of ~ ax-strcoll with one DV condition removed, the other DV condition replaced by a non-freeness hypothesis, and without initial universal quantifier. (Contributed by BJ, 21-Oct-2019.)
theorem strcollnf {x3 y1 a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 a b):
  $ wnf b ph $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wex y1 ph) (cv a)) (wex b (wal y1 (wb (wcel (cv y1) (cv b)) (wrex x3 ph (cv a))))) $;

--| Alternate proof of ~ strcollnf , not using ~ strcollnft . (Contributed by BJ, 5-Oct-2019.) (Proof modification is discouraged.) (New usage is discouraged.)
theorem strcollnfALT {x3 y1 a b: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 a b):
  $ wnf b ph $ >
  $ wi (wral x3 (wex y1 ph) (cv a)) (wex b (wal y1 (wb (wcel (cv y1) (cv b)) (wrex x3 ph (cv a))))) $;

--| Axiom scheme of subset collection. It is stated with all possible disjoint variable conditions, to show that this weak form is sufficient. (Contributed by BJ, 5-Oct-2019.)
axiom ax_sscoll {x3 y1 z a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z):
  $ wal a (wal b (wex c (wal z (wi (wral x3 (wrex y1 ph (cv b)) (cv a)) (wrex d (wal y1 (wb (wcel (cv y1) (cv d)) (wrex x3 ph (cv a)))) (cv c)))))) $;

--| Version of ~ ax-sscoll with two DV conditions removed and without initial universal quantifiers. (Contributed by BJ, 5-Oct-2019.)
theorem sscoll2 {x3 y1 z a b c d: setvar} (ph: wff x3 y1 z a b):
  $ wex c (wal z (wi (wral x3 (wrex y1 ph (cv b)) (cv a)) (wrex d (wal y1 (wb (wcel (cv y1) (cv d)) (wrex x3 ph (cv a)))) (cv c)))) $;

--| Extend wff definition to include "all some" applied to a top-level implication, which means ` ps ` is true whenever ` ph ` is true, and there is at least least one ` x ` where ` ph ` is true. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 20-Oct-2018.)
term walsi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3): wff;

--| Extend wff definition to include "all some" applied to a class, which means ` ph ` is true for all ` x ` in ` A ` , and there is at least one ` x ` in ` A ` . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 20-Oct-2018.)
term walsc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3): wff;

--| Define "all some" applied to a top-level implication, which means ` ps ` is true whenever ` ph ` is true and there is at least one ` x ` where ` ph ` is true. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 20-Oct-2018.)
axiom df_alsi {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3):
  $ wb (walsi x3 ph ps) (wa (wal x3 (wi ph ps)) (wex x3 ph)) $;

--| Define "all some" applied to a class, which means ` ph ` is true for all ` x ` in ` A ` and there is at least one ` x ` in ` A ` . (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 20-Oct-2018.)
axiom df_alsc {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wb (walsc x3 ph A2) (wa (wral x3 ph A2) (wex x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2))) $;

--| There is an equivalence between the two "all some" forms. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 22-Oct-2018.)
theorem alsconv {x3: setvar} (ph: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wb (walsi x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2) ph) (walsc x3 ph A2) $;

--| Deduction rule: Given "all some" applied to a top-level inference, you can extract the "for all" part. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 20-Oct-2018.)
theorem alsi1d {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (walsi x3 ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wal x3 (wi ps ch)) $;

--| Deduction rule: Given "all some" applied to a top-level inference, you can extract the "exists" part. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 20-Oct-2018.)
theorem alsi2d {x3: setvar} (ph ps ch: wff x3):
  $ wi ph (walsi x3 ps ch) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 ps) $;

--| Deduction rule: Given "all some" applied to a class, you can extract the "for all" part. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 20-Oct-2018.)
theorem alsc1d {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (walsc x3 ps A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wral x3 ps A2) $;

--| Deduction rule: Given "all some" applied to a class, you can extract the "there exists" part. (Contributed by David A. Wheeler, 20-Oct-2018.)
theorem alsc2d {x3: setvar} (ph ps: wff x3) (A2: class x3):
  $ wi ph (walsc x3 ps A2) $ >
  $ wi ph (wex x3 (wcel (cv x3) A2)) $;

